/* Exact-source presentation outlines. Question records and source passages remain unchanged. */
window.L.studyOutlineData={"cards":{"g1-1a":{"source":"<p>Lecture 1b gives the sequence: <b>observe → describe → ask a question → make a hypothesis → test the hypothesis → adjust it with new data → build a model (interpretation)</b>. On Sep 1 the professor replayed it for continental drift: observe matching coastlines, ask why, propose a hypothesis, predict what else must be true (matching rocks, fossils, glacial scratches), then collect data. A hypothesis that survives many tests can become a theory; one that fails is revised.</p><p>The key move is the <b>prediction</b>: “If this hypothesis is right, what else must I find?” Data that could come out either way is what makes a test.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The research method is a loop, not a checklist</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lecture 1b gives the sequence<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Observe → describe → ask a question → make a hypothesis → test the hypothesis → adjust it with new data → build a model (interpretation)</b>.</li></ul></li><li>On Sep 1 the professor replayed it for continental drift<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Observe matching coastlines, ask why, propose a hypothesis, predict what else must be true (matching rocks, fossils, glacial scratches), then collect data.</li></ul></li><li>A hypothesis that survives many tests can become a theory.</li><li>One that fails is revised.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The key move is the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">prediction</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>“If this hypothesis is right, what else must I find?”</li></ul></li><li>Data that could come out either way is what makes a test.</li></ul></section>"},"g1-1b":{"source":"<p>An <b>observation</b> is what you can see or measure directly: “the cliff exposes tan, brown, and yellowish layers,” “blocks sit on top of the cliff.” An <b>inference</b> is an interpretation of cause or history: “the blocks fell from a higher ledge,” “the layers were deposited in water.” Good geology states observations first, then infers, and keeps the two separate so an inference can be tested.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observation vs. inference</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An <mark class=\"pilot-term\">observation</mark> is what you can see or measure directly<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>“The cliff exposes tan, brown, and yellowish layers.”</li><li>“Blocks sit on top of the cliff.”</li></ul></li><li>An <mark class=\"pilot-term\">inference</mark> is an interpretation of cause or history<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>“The blocks fell from a higher ledge.”</li><li>“The layers were deposited in water.”</li></ul></li><li>Good geology states observations first, then infers, and keeps the two separate so an inference can be tested.</li></ul></section>"},"g1-1c":{"source":"<p>ILEAs use <b>Predict → Draw → Justify → Research → Revise understanding</b>. The syllabus says ILEAs are graded mainly on the quality of reasoning; observations, logical deductions, and hypothesis testing; not only on a single right answer. That is also the habit the investigation section of Exam 1 rewards.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">ILEA cycle (in-class assignments)</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>ILEAs use <b>Predict → Draw → Justify → Research → Revise understanding</b>.</li><li>The syllabus says ILEAs are graded mainly on the quality of reasoning.</li><li>Observations, logical deductions, and hypothesis testing.</li><li>Not only on a single right answer.</li><li>That is also the habit the investigation section of Exam 1 rewards.</li></ul></section>"},"g1-2a":{"source":"<p>The Lecture 1b landscape labels how geology affects where people live: <b>landslides</b> on slopes, <b>volcanic eruptions</b>, <b>earthquakes along faults</b>, the <b>type of soil</b>, <b>slopes too steep</b> to build on, and <b>streams</b> that are at once a <b>flood hazard, a water supply, and a source of soil nutrients</b>. The same feature can be both a resource and a hazard.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read a landscape for hazards and resources</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Lecture 1b landscape labels how geology affects where people live<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Landslides</b> on slopes, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">volcanic eruptions</mark>, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">earthquakes along faults</mark>, the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">type of soil</mark>, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">slopes too steep</mark> to build on, and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">streams</mark> that are at once a <b>flood hazard, a water supply, and a source of soil nutrients</b>.</li></ul></li><li>The same feature can be both a resource and a hazard.</li></ul></section>"},"g1-2b":{"source":"<p>Slide 7 contrasts <b>iron mines in very old rocks</b>, which record a change in Earth's early atmosphere, with <b>copper mines in much younger rocks</b> related to <b>mountain building in the West</b>. The lesson: where a resource is found depends on when and how its host rocks formed.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Resources reflect geologic history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Slide 7 contrasts <b>iron mines in very old rocks</b>, which record a change in Earth's early atmosphere, with <b>copper mines in much younger rocks</b> related to <b>mountain building in the West</b>.</li><li>The lesson<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Where a resource is found depends on when and how its host rocks formed.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g1-3a":{"source":"<p>Around Australia, the brown–blue boundary is only the <b>current shoreline</b>. The <b>shallow seafloor around the land is the continental shelf</b>, and the <b>outer edge of the shelf is the edge of the continent</b>. Some parts of the seafloor look simple and others complex, meaning they had different histories. On Sep 1 the professor used shelves again: South America's east coast has a broad shallow shelf, while the west coast drops into a trench.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Where does a continent really end?</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Around Australia, the brown–blue boundary is only the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">current shoreline</mark>.</li><li>The <b>shallow seafloor around the land is the continental shelf</b>, and the <b>outer edge of the shelf is the edge of the continent</b>.</li><li>Some parts of the seafloor look simple and others complex, meaning they had different histories.</li><li>On Sep 1 the professor used shelves again<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>South America's east coast has a broad shallow shelf, while the west coast drops into a trench.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g1-3b":{"source":"<p>Two globes compare <b>glaciers and ice sheets 28,000 years ago and today</b>: ice once covered far more of the Northern Hemisphere, so climate has changed. The Jurassic scene (about 160 million years ago) asks what evidence ancient animals leave; bones, teeth, shells, tracks, and other fossils preserved in rocks. Past conditions are reconstructed from such evidence, not observed directly.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Clues about past climate and life</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Two globes compare <b>glaciers and ice sheets 28,000 years ago and today</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Ice once covered far more of the Northern Hemisphere, so climate has changed.</li></ul></li><li>The Jurassic scene (about 160 million years ago) asks what evidence ancient animals leave.</li><li>Bones, teeth, shells, tracks, and other fossils preserved in rocks.</li><li>Past conditions are reconstructed from such evidence, not observed directly.</li></ul></section>"},"g1-4a":{"source":"<ul><li><b>Crust</b>: the thin upper layer, in two types: <b>continental</b> and <b>oceanic</b>.</li><li><b>Mantle</b>: the <b>thickest</b> layer.</li><li><b>Core</b>: the deepest layer, made of <b>iron-nickel</b>: <b>molten outer core</b>, <b>solid inner core</b>.</li></ul><p>Iron-nickel meteorites (slide 13) are used as evidence for what a metallic core is made of. The Sep 8 lecture added typical rocks: continental crust like granite (quartz, feldspar), oceanic crust like basalt, and an upper mantle dominated by olivine.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Composition layers: crust, mantle, core</h4><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Crust</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The thin upper layer, in two types</li><li><b>Continental</b> and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">oceanic</mark>.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Mantle</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The <mark class=\"pilot-term\">thickest</mark> layer.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Core</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The deepest layer, made of <mark class=\"pilot-term\">iron-nickel</mark></li><li><b>Molten outer core</b>, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">solid inner core</mark>.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Iron-nickel meteorites (slide 13) are used as evidence for what a metallic core is made of.</li><li>The Sep 8 lecture added typical rocks<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Continental crust like granite (quartz, feldspar), oceanic crust like basalt, and an upper mantle dominated by olivine.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g1-4b":{"source":"<p>Earth can also be divided by <b>strength</b>. The <b>lithosphere</b> is the stronger, rigid outer shell: the <b>crust plus the uppermost mantle</b>. Beneath it is the <b>asthenosphere</b>: <b>hot and weak, but mostly solid</b>. Plates are pieces of lithosphere moving over the asthenosphere (Chapter 3).</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Strength layers: lithosphere and asthenosphere</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Earth can also be divided by <mark class=\"pilot-term\">strength</mark>.</li><li>The <mark class=\"pilot-term\">lithosphere</mark> is the stronger, rigid outer shell<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The <b>crust plus the uppermost mantle</b>.</li></ul></li><li>Beneath it is the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">asthenosphere</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Hot and weak, but mostly solid</b>.</li></ul></li><li>Plates are pieces of lithosphere moving over the asthenosphere (Chapter 3).</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-guide-T5":{"source":"<p><b>Composition:</b> crust, mantle, core. Oceanic crust is more mafic; continental crust is generally more felsic. The core is dominated by iron and nickel; its outer part is liquid and inner part solid.</p><p><b>Mechanical behavior:</b> the lithosphere is rigid crust plus uppermost mantle. The underlying asthenosphere is hotter, weak, and mostly solid; it can flow over geologic time. <b>LAB = lithosphere–asthenosphere boundary.</b> It is not the crust–mantle boundary.</p><p><b>Isostasy:</b> equally dense thicker blocks float higher and extend deeper; equally thick denser blocks sit lower. Thick continental crust helps explain the higher elevation of the Denver region compared with the Gulf Coast. The wooden-block analogy illustrates buoyancy, not a wholly liquid mantle.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Exam 1 focus: Earth layers, the LAB, and isostasy</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Composition</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Crust, mantle, core.</li></ul></li><li>Oceanic crust is more mafic.</li><li>Continental crust is generally more felsic.</li><li>The core is dominated by iron and nickel.</li><li>Its outer part is liquid and inner part solid.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Mechanical behavior</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The lithosphere is rigid crust plus uppermost mantle.</li></ul></li><li>The underlying asthenosphere is hotter, weak, and mostly solid.</li><li>It can flow over geologic time.</li><li><b>LAB = lithosphere–asthenosphere boundary.</b></li><li>It is not the crust–mantle boundary.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Isostasy</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Equally dense thicker blocks float higher and extend deeper.</li><li>Equally thick denser blocks sit lower.</li></ul></li><li>Thick continental crust helps explain the higher elevation of the Denver region compared with the Gulf Coast.</li><li>The wooden-block analogy illustrates buoyancy, not a wholly liquid mantle.</li></ul></section>"},"g1-5a":{"source":"<p>Think of wooden blocks floating in water. <b>Thicker blocks ride higher</b>; <b>denser blocks sit lower</b>. Crust floats on the mantle in the same way, so <b>regions with thick crust stand higher than regions with thin crust</b>, and <b>continental crust stands higher than oceanic crust</b> (oceanic crust is thinner and denser). This relationship between crustal thickness and elevation is <b>isostasy</b>. Some mountains are also built on top of the crust.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Floating blocks explain elevation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Think of wooden blocks floating in water.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Thicker blocks ride higher</mark>.</li><li><b>Denser blocks sit lower</b>.</li><li>Crust floats on the mantle in the same way, so <b>regions with thick crust stand higher than regions with thin crust</b>, and <b>continental crust stands higher than oceanic crust</b> (oceanic crust is thinner and denser).</li><li>This relationship between crustal thickness and elevation is <mark class=\"pilot-term\">isostasy</mark>.</li><li>Some mountains are also built on top of the crust.</li></ul></section>"},"g1-6a":{"source":"<table><tr><th>From outside / at the surface</th><th>From inside Earth</th></tr><tr><td>Sun's energy; gravity from the Sun and Moon; atmospheric pressure; wind and ocean currents; Earth's gravity pulling material downhill</td><td>Forces within Earth; radioactive decay (heat); heat transfer from the interior</td></tr></table><p>Chapter 5 returns to internal heat: most of Earth's internal heat today comes from radioactive decay.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What drives change: inside vs. outside Earth</h4><table><tr><th>From outside / at the surface</th><th>From inside Earth</th></tr><tr><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Sun's energy.</li><li>Gravity from the Sun and Moon.</li><li>Atmospheric pressure.</li><li>Wind and ocean currents.</li><li>Earth's gravity pulling material downhill.</li></ul></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Forces within Earth.</li><li>Radioactive decay (heat).</li><li>Heat transfer from the interior.</li></ul></td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chapter 5 returns to internal heat<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Most of Earth's internal heat today comes from radioactive decay.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g1-6b":{"source":"<p>Slide 18 labels: water vapor, drops, and ice crystals; <b>precipitation</b>; <b>wind</b>; <b>evaporation</b>; the atmosphere <b>blocks some light and UV</b>; the Sun's energy is converted to <b>infrared</b>, some of which the atmosphere blocks; <b>land and sea absorb heat</b>.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What the atmosphere does</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Slide 18 labels<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Water vapor, drops, and ice crystals.</li><li><b>Precipitation</b>.</li><li><b>Wind</b>.</li><li><b>Evaporation</b>.</li><li>The atmosphere <b>blocks some light and UV</b>.</li><li>The Sun's energy is converted to <mark class=\"pilot-term\">infrared</mark>, some of which the atmosphere blocks.</li><li><b>Land and sea absorb heat</b>.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g1-7a":{"source":"<p>Rivers, glaciers, steep mountain fronts, sand dunes, beaches, offshore areas, and lakes are normal surface environments. <b>Rocks formed in normal surface environments are sedimentary rocks.</b> Each environment leaves a recognizable deposit: angular rock debris below a steep mountain front, well-sorted sand in dunes, rounded pebbles on a beach.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Surface environments → sedimentary rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rivers, glaciers, steep mountain fronts, sand dunes, beaches, offshore areas, and lakes are normal surface environments.</li><li><b>Rocks formed in normal surface environments are sedimentary rocks.</b></li><li>Each environment leaves a recognizable deposit<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Angular rock debris below a steep mountain front, well-sorted sand in dunes, rounded pebbles on a beach.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g1-7b":{"source":"<p>Magma can <b>erupt as lava</b>, <b>explode as ash</b>, or <b>solidify at depth</b>. <b>Rock formed from magma is igneous rock.</b> (This is the last slide covered in class on Aug 25.)</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Rocks from magma: igneous</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Magma can <mark class=\"pilot-term\">erupt as lava</mark>, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">explode as ash</mark>, or <mark class=\"pilot-term\">solidify at depth</mark>.</li><li><b>Rock formed from magma is igneous rock.</b> (This is the last slide covered in class on Aug 25.).</li></ul></section>"},"g1-7c":{"source":"<p><span class=\"badge flag\">Rock cycle reviewed Sep 24</span> Rocks <b>changed by heat and pressure</b> are <b>metamorphic</b>; minerals <b>precipitated from hot water</b> form <b>hydrothermal</b> rock. The rock-cycle diagram links processes: <b>weathering → erosion and transport → deposition → burial → deformation and metamorphism → melting → solidification → uplift</b>, with many shortcuts. Earth's four spheres are the <b>lithosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere, and atmosphere</b>.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Metamorphic rocks, rock cycle, and Earth’s spheres</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><span class=\"badge flag\">Rock cycle reviewed Sep 24</span> Rocks <b>changed by heat and pressure</b> are <mark class=\"pilot-term\">metamorphic</mark>.</li><li>Minerals <mark class=\"pilot-term\">precipitated from hot water</mark> form <mark class=\"pilot-term\">hydrothermal</mark> rock.</li><li>The rock-cycle diagram links processes<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Weathering → erosion and transport → deposition → burial → deformation and metamorphism → melting → solidification → uplift</b>, with many shortcuts.</li></ul></li><li>Earth's four spheres are the <b>lithosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere, and atmosphere</b>.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-guide-T1":{"source":"<p><b>Igneous</b> rock forms when magma or lava cools and crystallizes. <b>Sedimentary</b> rock forms from sediment through deposition and lithification (compaction and cementation), or from material precipitated from water. <b>Metamorphic</b> rock is changed by heat, pressure, and fluids while remaining solid.</p><p>Practice the verbs: weathering breaks rock down; erosion removes it; transport moves it; deposition drops it. Burial can compact sediment or lead to metamorphism. Melting produces magma; cooling produces igneous rock. Uplift exposes rock to surface processes.</p><p><b>There is no mandatory starting point or single loop.</b> Granite can become metamorphic directly, or weather into sediment. Sedimentary rock can be buried and eventually melt.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Exam 1 focus: Rock cycle and its many paths</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Igneous</mark> rock forms when magma or lava cools and crystallizes.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Sedimentary</mark> rock forms from sediment through deposition and lithification (compaction and cementation), or from material precipitated from water.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Metamorphic</mark> rock is changed by heat, pressure, and fluids while remaining solid.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Practice the verbs<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Weathering breaks rock down.</li><li>Erosion removes it.</li><li>Transport moves it.</li><li>Deposition drops it.</li></ul></li><li>Burial can compact sediment or lead to metamorphism.</li><li>Melting produces magma.</li><li>Cooling produces igneous rock.</li><li>Uplift exposes rock to surface processes.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>There is no mandatory starting point or single loop.</b></li><li>Granite can become metamorphic directly, or weather into sediment.</li><li>Sedimentary rock can be buried and eventually melt.</li></ul></section>"},"g2-1a":{"source":"<p>Lecture 2's labeled hillside gives a practical checklist: <b>different parts of the hill; various colors; ledges that reflect layers; color that is only a stain on the outside of the rock; fractures; loose pieces vs. solid bedrock; loose pieces covering reddish rocks on the slope; rounded vs. angular corners</b>. Colored overlays group similar features into main rock units (slide 4).</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">A checklist for looking at a landscape</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lecture 2's labeled hillside gives a practical checklist<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Different parts of the hill</b>.</li><li><b>Various colors</b>.</li><li><b>Ledges that reflect layers</b>.</li><li><b>Color that is only a stain on the outside of the rock</b>.</li><li><b>Fractures</b>.</li><li><b>Loose pieces vs. solid bedrock</b>.</li><li><b>Loose pieces covering reddish rocks on the slope</b>.</li><li><b>Rounded vs. angular corners</b>.</li></ul></li><li>Colored overlays group similar features into main rock units (slide 4).</li></ul></section>"},"g2-1b":{"source":"<p>A field sketch of the mesa (slide 6) keeps the ledges, cliffs, fractures, layers, and loose material, labels them, and drops distracting detail such as individual bushes. A sketch is a model: it selects information. Exam “investigation” questions often show a simplified diagram instead of a photo, so practice moving between the two.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Sketches emphasize what matters</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A field sketch of the mesa (slide 6) keeps the ledges, cliffs, fractures, layers, and loose material, labels them, and drops distracting detail such as individual bushes.</li><li>A sketch is a model<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>It selects information.</li></ul></li><li>Exam “investigation” questions often show a simplified diagram instead of a photo, so practice moving between the two.</li></ul></section>"},"g2-1c":{"source":"<p>Slide 8 asks which modern setting made deposits most like an outcrop full of <b>rounded pebbles</b>: a <b>steep mountain front</b> (angular, broken pieces that have not traveled far) or a <b>river with pebbles</b> (rounded by transport). Rounded clasts match the river. Present-day processes are the key for reading past ones.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use modern environments to interpret old rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Slide 8 asks which modern setting made deposits most like an outcrop full of <mark class=\"pilot-term\">rounded pebbles</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A <mark class=\"pilot-term\">steep mountain front</mark> (angular, broken pieces that have not traveled far) or a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">river with pebbles</mark> (rounded by transport).</li></ul></li><li>Rounded clasts match the river.</li><li>Present-day processes are the key for reading past ones.</li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-mediterranean":{"source":"<ul><li>The supplied question pairs buried salt deposits with wind-deposited sand from about six million years ago. In this course case, the combination supports strong evaporation and exposed, desert-like conditions rather than an unchanged deep sea.</li><li>Evaporation removes water and concentrates dissolved salts; wind-deposited sand supplies a separate clue about the depositional setting. Use both clues, not merely the modern location beneath seawater.</li><li>Atlantic water enters through the <b>Strait of Gibraltar</b>, replenishing the Mediterranean despite its evaporative deficit. The gateway matters to the water budget.</li><li>This is the interpretation asked by the supplied Connect case. The complete Messinian basin history is more complex; these clues alone do not specify every drying or refilling stage.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect case: a changing Mediterranean</h4><ul><li>The supplied question pairs buried salt deposits with wind-deposited sand from about six million years ago. In this course case, the combination supports strong evaporation and exposed, desert-like conditions rather than an unchanged deep sea.</li><li>Evaporation removes water and concentrates dissolved salts.</li><li>Wind-deposited sand supplies a separate clue about the depositional setting.</li><li>Use both clues, not merely the modern location beneath seawater.</li><li>Atlantic water enters through the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Strait of Gibraltar</mark>, replenishing the Mediterranean despite its evaporative deficit. The gateway matters to the water budget.</li><li>This is the interpretation asked by the supplied Connect case.</li><li>The complete Messinian basin history is more complex.</li><li>These clues alone do not specify every drying or refilling stage.</li></ul></section>"},"g2-2a":{"source":"<p>Three models show one landscape through time: a broad flat-topped <b>mesa</b>, eroded into a narrower <b>butte</b>, then into small <b>knobs</b>. The resistant cap protects the rock below until erosion removes it. Order: <b>mesa → butte → knobs</b>.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Erosion shrinks a mesa into a butte, then knobs</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Three models show one landscape through time<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A broad flat-topped <mark class=\"pilot-term\">mesa</mark>, eroded into a narrower <mark class=\"pilot-term\">butte</mark>, then into small <mark class=\"pilot-term\">knobs</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>The resistant cap protects the rock below until erosion removes it.</li><li>Order<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Mesa → butte → knobs</b>.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g2-2b":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Rule</th><th>What you see</th></tr><tr><td>Position of layers (superposition)</td><td>Lower layers were deposited first; the youngest layer is on top.</td><td>Undisturbed stack of layers</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-cutting relations</td><td>A feature that cuts across rocks is younger than the rocks it cuts.</td><td>A fault or dike through layers</td></tr><tr><td>Pieces of older rock (inclusions / clasts)</td><td>A unit that contains pieces of another unit is younger than those pieces.</td><td>Pebbles of unit X inside unit Y</td></tr><tr><td>Contact effects (baking)</td><td>Magma bakes the older rocks it touches; the baked rock is older than the intrusion.</td><td>A changed rim next to an intrusion</td></tr></table><p>These give <b>relative</b> order (older/younger), not ages in years.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Four principles for ordering events</h4><table><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Rule</th><th>What you see</th></tr><tr><td>Position of layers (superposition)</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Lower layers were deposited first.</li><li>The youngest layer is on top.</li></ul></td><td>Undisturbed stack of layers</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-cutting relations</td><td>A feature that cuts across rocks is younger than the rocks it cuts.</td><td>A fault or dike through layers</td></tr><tr><td>Pieces of older rock (inclusions / clasts)</td><td>A unit that contains pieces of another unit is younger than those pieces.</td><td>Pebbles of unit X inside unit Y</td></tr><tr><td>Contact effects (baking)</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Magma bakes the older rocks it touches.</li><li>The baked rock is older than the intrusion.</li></ul></td><td>A changed rim next to an intrusion</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>These give <mark class=\"pilot-term\">relative</mark> order (older/younger), not ages in years.</li></ul></section>"},"g2-2c":{"source":"<p>Slide 14 shows a <b>lower tilted unit</b>, a <b>middle sandy unit</b>, and an <b>upper coarse unit</b>. Superposition puts the lower unit first. The middle unit lies flat on top of tilted layers, so <b>tilting (and erosion of the tilted beds) happened after the lower unit formed but before the middle unit was deposited</b>. The upper coarse unit is youngest. Always list events, not just rock units: deposition, tilting, faulting, intrusion, erosion.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Putting a sequence together</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Slide 14 shows a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">lower tilted unit</mark>, a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">middle sandy unit</mark>, and an <mark class=\"pilot-term\">upper coarse unit</mark>.</li><li>Superposition puts the lower unit first.</li><li>The middle unit lies flat on top of tilted layers, so <b>tilting (and erosion of the tilted beds) happened after the lower unit formed but before the middle unit was deposited</b>.</li><li>The upper coarse unit is youngest.</li><li>Always list events, not just rock units<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Deposition, tilting, faulting, intrusion, erosion.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g2-3a":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Map</th><th>Shows</th></tr><tr><td>Topographic map</td><td>Elevation using <b>contour lines</b> (lines of equal elevation)</td></tr><tr><td>Shaded-relief map</td><td>The shape of the land as if lit from one side</td></tr><tr><td>Satellite image</td><td>What the surface looks like from space (color, vegetation, dark lava)</td></tr><tr><td>Geologic map</td><td><b>Types and ages of rocks and features</b> (e.g., faults)</td></tr></table><p>The SP Crater area in northern Arizona (slides 15–16) appears on all four: small volcanoes, a dark young lava flow from SP Crater, light-colored sedimentary rocks, and linear features that are faults.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Four kinds of maps and what each is for</h4><table><tr><th>Map</th><th>Shows</th></tr><tr><td>Topographic map</td><td>Elevation using <mark class=\"pilot-term\">contour lines</mark> (lines of equal elevation)</td></tr><tr><td>Shaded-relief map</td><td>The shape of the land as if lit from one side</td></tr><tr><td>Satellite image</td><td>What the surface looks like from space (color, vegetation, dark lava)</td></tr><tr><td>Geologic map</td><td><b>Types and ages of rocks and features</b> (e.g., faults)</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The SP Crater area in northern Arizona (slides 15–16) appears on all four<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Small volcanoes, a dark young lava flow from SP Crater, light-colored sedimentary rocks, and linear features that are faults.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g2-3b":{"source":"<ul><li><b>Elevation</b>: height above sea level at one point.</li><li><b>Relief</b>: the <b>difference in elevation between two places</b> (subtract).</li><li><b>Steepness of slope</b>: how fast elevation changes over horizontal distance.</li></ul><p>Example: a peak at 2,600 m and a valley floor at 900 m have <b>1,700 m of relief</b> between them. Use the generator below for more.</p><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"relief\">Practice relief calculations (5)</button>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Elevation, relief, and slope are different measurements</h4><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Elevation</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Height above sea level at one point.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Relief</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The <b>difference in elevation between two places</b> (subtract).</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Steepness of slope</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>How fast elevation changes over horizontal distance.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Example<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A peak at 2,600 m and a valley floor at 900 m have <mark class=\"pilot-term\">1,700 m of relief</mark> between them.</li></ul></li><li>Use the generator below for more.</li></ul><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"relief\">Practice relief calculations (5)</button></section>"},"g2-3c":{"source":"<ul><li><b>Cross section</b>: a vertical slice showing rock units and structures at depth.</li><li><b>Block diagram</b>: a 3-D block: map view on top plus cross sections on the sides.</li><li><b>Stratigraphic section</b>: a single column listing the layers from bottom (oldest) to top.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Three ways to show what is underground</h4><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Cross section</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A vertical slice showing rock units and structures at depth.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Block diagram</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A 3-D block</li><li>Map view on top plus cross sections on the sides.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Stratigraphic section</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A single column listing the layers from bottom (oldest) to top.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-contours":{"source":"<ul><li><b>Index contours</b> are the heavier contour lines, usually labeled with elevation. Intermediate contours are thinner.</li><li>The <b>contour interval</b> is an elevation difference between neighboring contours, not a special kind of line.</li><li>On one map with a fixed contour interval, tightly spaced contours indicate steeper ground; widely spaced contours indicate gentler ground. A heavy line alone does not mean a steep slope.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Index contours, interval, and slope</h4><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Index contours</mark> are the heavier contour lines, usually labeled with elevation. Intermediate contours are thinner.</li><li>The <mark class=\"pilot-term\">contour interval</mark> is an elevation difference between neighboring contours, not a special kind of line.</li><li>On one map with a fixed contour interval, tightly spaced contours indicate steeper ground.</li><li>Widely spaced contours indicate gentler ground.</li><li>A heavy line alone does not mean a steep slope.</li></ul></section>"},"g2-4a":{"source":"<p><b>Quantitative data</b> are numeric measurements (a crater 1.2 km wide; a layer 3 m thick). <b>Qualitative data</b> are descriptions in words or sketches (angular blocks on the rim; a gray, fine-grained layer). Both are observations; neither is automatically better.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Two kinds of data</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Quantitative data</mark> are numeric measurements (a crater 1.2 km wide; a layer 3 m thick).</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Qualitative data</mark> are descriptions in words or sketches (angular blocks on the rim; a gray, fine-grained layer).</li><li>Both are observations.</li><li>Neither is automatically better.</li></ul></section>"},"g2-4b":{"source":"<p>Four main parts, based on fossils (slide 23): <b>Precambrian</b> (before shells and hard parts) → <b>Paleozoic</b> (appearance of fish, plants, insects, reptiles) → <b>Mesozoic</b> (dinosaurs and first flowering plants) → <b>Cenozoic</b> (most recent; lots of mammals). If Earth history were squeezed into one year, the Precambrian fills most of the calendar and the other three crowd into the final weeks.</p><p>Periods, oldest to youngest: <b>Cambrian, Ordovician, Silurian, Devonian, Mississippian, Pennsylvanian, Permian</b> (Paleozoic); <b>Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous</b> (Mesozoic); <b>Paleogene, Neogene, Quaternary</b> (Cenozoic). Boundaries on the lecture chart: Paleozoic begins 541 Ma, Mesozoic 252 Ma, Cenozoic 66 Ma.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The geologic timescale</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Four main parts, based on fossils (slide 23)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Precambrian</mark> (before shells and hard parts) → <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Paleozoic</mark> (appearance of fish, plants, insects, reptiles) → <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Mesozoic</mark> (dinosaurs and first flowering plants) → <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Cenozoic</mark> (most recent; lots of mammals).</li></ul></li><li>If Earth history were squeezed into one year, the Precambrian fills most of the calendar and the other three crowd into the final weeks.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Periods, oldest to youngest<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Cambrian, Ordovician, Silurian, Devonian, Mississippian, Pennsylvanian, Permian</b> (Paleozoic).</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous</mark> (Mesozoic).</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Paleogene, Neogene, Quaternary</mark> (Cenozoic).</li></ul></li><li>Boundaries on the lecture chart<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Paleozoic begins 541 Ma, Mesozoic 252 Ma, Cenozoic 66 Ma.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g2-5a":{"source":"<p>The gasoline case (slide 25) is the template: observation → question → proposed explanations → <b>predictions for each</b> → collect data → conclusion. For the crater (slides 26–29) the models are a <b>volcanic explosion</b>, a <b>rising mass of salt</b>, and a <b>meteoroid impact</b>. Observations: the rim has <b>angular blocks of fractured rock</b>, and the crater sits in <b>sedimentary layers</b>.</p><p>A useful test is one where the models predict <i>different</i> results. Reasoning from each model (lab inference, not stated on the slides): a volcanic origin predicts volcanic rock (lava, ash) at the crater; rising salt predicts salt at depth and layers pushed <i>up</i> into a dome rather than a pit; an impact predicts shattered, ejected rock and possibly meteorite fragments, with no volcanic rock or salt required.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep several explanations alive, then test their predictions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The gasoline case (slide 25) is the template<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Observation → question → proposed explanations → <mark class=\"pilot-term\">predictions for each</mark> → collect data → conclusion.</li></ul></li><li>For the crater (slides 26–29) the models are a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">volcanic explosion</mark>, a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">rising mass of salt</mark>, and a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">meteoroid impact</mark>.</li><li>Observations<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The rim has <b>angular blocks of fractured rock</b>, and the crater sits in <mark class=\"pilot-term\">sedimentary layers</mark>.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A useful test is one where the models predict <i>different</i> results.</li><li>Reasoning from each model (lab inference, not stated on the slides)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A volcanic origin predicts volcanic rock (lava, ash) at the crater.</li><li>Rising salt predicts salt at depth and layers pushed <i>up</i> into a dome rather than a pit.</li><li>An impact predicts shattered, ejected rock and possibly meteorite fragments, with no volcanic rock or salt required.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g2-5b":{"source":"<p>Upheaval Dome is a <b>circular feature</b> where <b>layers bend upward around the dome</b>, surrounded by <b>flat-lying sedimentary layers</b>. The stratigraphic column lists units A (upper sandstone) down to G (sandstone with pebbles); by superposition <b>G is oldest and A youngest</b>. Possible explanations: <b>rising salt, rising magma, meteoroid impact</b>. The slide asks for predictions from each and what information would narrow the origin; it does not hand you the answer.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Upheaval Dome</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Upheaval Dome is a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">circular feature</mark> where <b>layers bend upward around the dome</b>, surrounded by <mark class=\"pilot-term\">flat-lying sedimentary layers</mark>.</li><li>The stratigraphic column lists units A (upper sandstone) down to G (sandstone with pebbles).</li><li>By superposition <b>G is oldest and A youngest</b>.</li><li>Possible explanations<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Rising salt, rising magma, meteoroid impact</b>.</li></ul></li><li>The slide asks for predictions from each and what information would narrow the origin.</li><li>It does not hand you the answer.</li></ul></section>"},"g3-1a":{"source":"<p>Slide 10 names the main features: <b>mid-ocean ridges</b> (undersea mountain ranges), <b>deep ocean trenches</b>, <b>oceanic fracture zones</b> (linear steps in the seafloor), <b>continental shelves</b>, <b>linear island and seamount chains</b>, <b>submerged ridges and island arcs</b>, <b>oceanic plateaus</b>, and continents.</p><p>Close-ups: <b>South America</b> has a trench, the Andes, and a low-relief interior on the west-to-east path, with a broad shelf only on the east; the <b>South Atlantic</b> has a central ridge with zigzags, fracture zones, shelves on both sides and no trenches; <b>Japan</b> has curving ridges (island arc) beside trenches; <b>Tibet</b> is the highest broad plateau, bordered by the Himalaya.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The features plate tectonics has to explain</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Slide 10 names the main features<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Mid-ocean ridges</b> (undersea mountain ranges), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">deep ocean trenches</mark>, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">oceanic fracture zones</mark> (linear steps in the seafloor), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">continental shelves</mark>, <b>linear island and seamount chains</b>, <b>submerged ridges and island arcs</b>, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">oceanic plateaus</mark>, and continents.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Close-ups<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">South America</mark> has a trench, the Andes, and a low-relief interior on the west-to-east path, with a broad shelf only on the east.</li><li>The <mark class=\"pilot-term\">South Atlantic</mark> has a central ridge with zigzags, fracture zones, shelves on both sides and no trenches.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Japan</mark> has curving ridges (island arc) beside trenches.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Tibet</mark> is the highest broad plateau, bordered by the Himalaya.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g3-1b":{"source":"<p>On Sep 1 the professor explained the colors: <b>dark blue = deep water</b> (around 3–5 km or more), <b>light blue = shallow water</b> (a mid-ocean ridge rises to within roughly 2 km of the surface; shelves are shallower still). Land images are often <b>false color</b> from satellite data, so a color can stand for a measurement rather than what your eye would see. Water depth is <b>bathymetry</b>.</p><p>Example from the Pacific Northwest (slide 3): a linear <b>step in the seafloor</b> separates shallower water to the north from much deeper water to the south; a fracture zone.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reading depth on the maps</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On Sep 1 the professor explained the colors<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Dark blue = deep water</b> (around 3–5 km or more), <b>light blue = shallow water</b> (a mid-ocean ridge rises to within roughly 2 km of the surface; shelves are shallower still).</li></ul></li><li>Land images are often <mark class=\"pilot-term\">false color</mark> from satellite data, so a color can stand for a measurement rather than what your eye would see.</li><li>Water depth is <mark class=\"pilot-term\">bathymetry</mark>.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Example from the Pacific Northwest (slide 3)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A linear <mark class=\"pilot-term\">step in the seafloor</mark> separates shallower water to the north from much deeper water to the south.</li><li>A fracture zone.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g3-2a":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Observation</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr><tr><td>Shapes of continents match across the Atlantic (Brazil's corner fits Africa's indentation)</td><td>They were once joined, then moved apart; the <b>continental drift</b> hypothesis (Wegener)</td></tr><tr><td>Fossils of the same land creatures (e.g., <i>Mesosaurus</i>) on continents now separated by oceans</td><td>Continents were joined so creatures could move between them</td></tr><tr><td>Glacial scratches (striations/grooves) record ice flowing from directions that are now ocean</td><td>With continents restored, ice sheets flowed outward from a center</td></tr><tr><td>Similar rocks and structures on matching coasts (Sep 1)</td><td>Same geology once continuous</td></tr></table><p>The joined southern continents are called <b>Gondwana</b> (Sep 1).</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observation → hypothesis → tests</h4><table><tr><th>Observation</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr><tr><td>Shapes of continents match across the Atlantic (Brazil's corner fits Africa's indentation)</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>They were once joined, then moved apart.</li><li>The <mark class=\"pilot-term\">continental drift</mark> hypothesis (Wegener).</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>Fossils of the same land creatures (e.g., <i>Mesosaurus</i>) on continents now separated by oceans</td><td>Continents were joined so creatures could move between them</td></tr><tr><td>Glacial scratches (striations/grooves) record ice flowing from directions that are now ocean</td><td>With continents restored, ice sheets flowed outward from a center</td></tr><tr><td>Similar rocks and structures on matching coasts (Sep 1)</td><td>Same geology once continuous</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The joined southern continents are called <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Gondwana</mark> (Sep 1).</li></ul></section>"},"g3-2b":{"source":"<p>Wegener could not explain <b>what force moves continents</b>. His suggestions (tidal pull of the Moon, continents plowing through ocean crust) were shown by calculation to be far too weak. Slide 15 adds that some geologists were unfamiliar with data from other parts of the world. Drift was largely rejected from the 1930s until new seafloor data in the 1950s–60s revived it as <b>plate tectonics</b>, which supplies a mechanism (seafloor spreading, ridge push, slab pull, mantle convection).</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The weakness: no mechanism</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wegener could not explain <mark class=\"pilot-term\">what force moves continents</mark>.</li><li>His suggestions (tidal pull of the Moon, continents plowing through ocean crust) were shown by calculation to be far too weak.</li><li>Slide 15 adds that some geologists were unfamiliar with data from other parts of the world.</li><li>Drift was largely rejected from the 1930s until new seafloor data in the 1950s–60s revived it as <mark class=\"pilot-term\">plate tectonics</mark>, which supplies a mechanism (seafloor spreading, ridge push, slab pull, mantle convection).</li></ul></section>"},"g3-3a":{"source":"<p>Earthquakes are concentrated in <b>narrow belts</b>: along mid-ocean ridges, trenches, and mountain belts, and are sparse in continental interiors. Volcanoes follow similar belts (ridges, island arcs next to trenches, mountain belts next to trenches), plus isolated oceanic islands (Hawaii) and the Red Sea/East Africa. Earthquakes, volcanism, and mountain building together are <b>tectonic activity</b>. <b>Belts of tectonic activity divide the lithosphere into tectonic plates</b>; plate interiors are relatively quiet.</p><p>Sep 1: the professor counted <b>7 major plates and 6 smaller ones (13 total)</b> on the lecture map (e.g., Pacific, North American, South American, African, Eurasian, Australian-Indian, Antarctic; Nazca, Cocos, Caribbean, Arabian, Philippine…). Counts differ between maps; know how plates are defined, not only the number.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Earthquakes, volcanoes, and mountains line up</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Earthquakes are concentrated in <mark class=\"pilot-term\">narrow belts</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Along mid-ocean ridges, trenches, and mountain belts, and are sparse in continental interiors.</li></ul></li><li>Volcanoes follow similar belts (ridges, island arcs next to trenches, mountain belts next to trenches), plus isolated oceanic islands (Hawaii) and the Red Sea/East Africa.</li><li>Earthquakes, volcanism, and mountain building together are <mark class=\"pilot-term\">tectonic activity</mark>.</li><li><b>Belts of tectonic activity divide the lithosphere into tectonic plates</b>.</li><li>Plate interiors are relatively quiet.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sep 1<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The professor counted <b>7 major plates and 6 smaller ones (13 total)</b> on the lecture map (e.g., Pacific, North American, South American, African, Eurasian, Australian-Indian, Antarctic; Nazca, Cocos, Caribbean, Arabian, Philippine…).</li></ul></li><li>Counts differ between maps.</li><li>Know how plates are defined, not only the number.</li></ul></section>"},"g3-3b":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Relative motion</th><th>Boundary</th><th>Typical features</th></tr><tr><td>Move apart</td><td><b>Divergent</b></td><td>Mid-ocean ridge, continental rift</td></tr><tr><td>Move toward each other</td><td><b>Convergent</b></td><td>Trench + island arc or volcanic mountain belt; collision mountains</td></tr><tr><td>Slide horizontally past</td><td><b>Transform</b></td><td>Offsets between ridge segments; e.g., San Andreas</td></tr></table>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Boundaries are defined by relative motion</h4><table><tr><th>Relative motion</th><th>Boundary</th><th>Typical features</th></tr><tr><td>Move apart</td><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Divergent</mark></td><td>Mid-ocean ridge, continental rift</td></tr><tr><td>Move toward each other</td><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Convergent</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Trench + island arc or volcanic mountain belt.</li><li>Collision mountains.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>Slide horizontally past</td><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Transform</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Offsets between ridge segments.</li><li>E.g., San Andreas.</li></ul></td></tr></table></section>"},"exam1-guide-T4":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Relative motion</th><th>Boundary</th><th>Crustal budget</th></tr><tr><td>Apart</td><td>Divergent / constructive</td><td>Creates oceanic crust at ridges</td></tr><tr><td>Together</td><td>Convergent / destructive in subduction</td><td>Consumes oceanic lithosphere at trenches</td></tr><tr><td>Slide past</td><td>Transform / conservative</td><td>Neither creates nor consumes crust</td></tr></table><p>Count connected plate regions, not arrows, continents, or boundary lines. Relative motion determines boundary type. At a ridge, crust is added; at a subduction zone, oceanic lithosphere is consumed. Practice identifying these processes from the arrows and features shown.</p><p><b>Red Sea:</b> a young opening ocean basin. <b>Atlantic:</b> a mature spreading basin. <b>Pacific:</b> extensive subduction around its margins. This is a teaching comparison, not a single guaranteed future path for all oceans.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Exam 1 focus: Read plate maps and boundary types</h4><table><tr><th>Relative motion</th><th>Boundary</th><th>Crustal budget</th></tr><tr><td>Apart</td><td>Divergent / constructive</td><td>Creates oceanic crust at ridges</td></tr><tr><td>Together</td><td>Convergent / destructive in subduction</td><td>Consumes oceanic lithosphere at trenches</td></tr><tr><td>Slide past</td><td>Transform / conservative</td><td>Neither creates nor consumes crust</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Count connected plate regions, not arrows, continents, or boundary lines.</li><li>Relative motion determines boundary type.</li><li>At a ridge, crust is added.</li><li>At a subduction zone, oceanic lithosphere is consumed.</li><li>Practice identifying these processes from the arrows and features shown.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Red Sea</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A young opening ocean basin.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Atlantic</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A mature spreading basin.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Pacific</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Extensive subduction around its margins.</li></ul></li><li>This is a teaching comparison, not a single guaranteed future path for all oceans.</li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-tectonics":{"source":"<ul><li><b>Tectonics</b> concerns the origin and arrangement of large structural features of Earth and the processes that deform and move the crust. Plate tectonics explains those features through interacting lithospheric plates.</li><li>Mineralogy studies minerals; sedimentology studies sediments and their deposition. Match the scale and subject of a question to the field.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Name the field as well as the process</h4><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Tectonics</mark> concerns the origin and arrangement of large structural features of Earth and the processes that deform and move the crust. Plate tectonics explains those features through interacting lithospheric plates.</li><li>Mineralogy studies minerals.</li><li>Sedimentology studies sediments and their deposition.</li><li>Match the scale and subject of a question to the field.</li></ul></section>"},"g3-4a":{"source":"<ol><li>Plates move apart (<b>seafloor spreading</b>), leaving a <b>narrow trough or rift</b> along the crest.</li><li><b>Asthenosphere rises and melts</b>.</li><li><b>Magma rises through fractures</b>, erupts or solidifies at depth, and <b>forms new oceanic crust</b>.</li><li>The <b>ridge stands high because the rocks are hot and the lithosphere is thin</b>; hot material expands (Sep 1).</li><li>Crust gets <b>older and colder away from the ridge</b>.</li></ol><p>Earthquakes happen as blocks drop into the rift; volcanism builds new crust (Sep 1).</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What happens at a mid-ocean ridge</h4><ol><li>Plates move apart (<mark class=\"pilot-term\">seafloor spreading</mark>), leaving a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">narrow trough or rift</mark> along the crest.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Asthenosphere rises and melts</mark>.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Magma rises through fractures</mark>, erupts or solidifies at depth, and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">forms new oceanic crust</mark>.</li><li>The <b>ridge stands high because the rocks are hot and the lithosphere is thin</b>.</li><li>Hot material expands (Sep 1).</li><li>Crust gets <b>older and colder away from the ridge</b>.</li></ol><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Earthquakes happen as blocks drop into the rift.</li><li>Volcanism builds new crust (Sep 1).</li></ul></section>"},"g3-4b":{"source":"<p><b>1.</b> Rising mantle causes <b>initial uplift</b>. <b>2.</b> <b>Stretching and faulting form a rift</b> (example: <b>East African Rift</b>; the Sep 1 lecture also named the Rio Grande rift); melting forms magma. <b>3.</b> Rifting can lead to <b>seafloor spreading and a new ocean basin</b> (example: <b>Red Sea</b>: a young ocean). <b>4.</b> The <b>ocean widens</b> with spreading (example: <b>modern Atlantic</b>). On Sep 1 the professor added that as the Atlantic grows, the Pacific shrinks.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Rifting a continent, stage by stage</h4><ol class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rising mantle causes <mark class=\"pilot-term\">initial uplift</mark>.</li><li>Stretching and faulting form a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">rift</mark>.<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The East African Rift is an example. The Sep 1 lecture also named the Rio Grande rift.</li><li>Melting forms magma.</li></ul></li><li>Rifting can lead to <mark class=\"pilot-term\">seafloor spreading</mark> and a new ocean basin.<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The Red Sea is an example of a young ocean.</li></ul></li><li>The ocean widens with spreading.<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The modern Atlantic is an example.</li></ul></li></ol><p>On Sep 1 the professor added that as the Atlantic grows, the Pacific shrinks.</p></section>"},"g3-5a":{"source":"<p>When two oceanic plates converge, <b>one moves down (subduction)</b>. On Sep 1 the professor explained which one: the <b>older, colder, denser plate sinks</b>. Features: a <b>trench</b> where the plate bends down; an <b>accretionary prism</b> of scraped-off material; the <b>slab releases water</b>; <b>water causes melting of the mantle</b> above the slab; <b>magma rises</b> through mantle and crust; <b>eruptions form a volcanic island arc</b> (e.g., Japan).</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Ocean-ocean convergence: trench and island arc</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>When two oceanic plates converge, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">one moves down (subduction)</mark>.</li><li>On Sep 1 the professor explained which one<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The <b>older, colder, denser plate sinks</b>.</li></ul></li><li>Features<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A <mark class=\"pilot-term\">trench</mark> where the plate bends down.</li><li>An <mark class=\"pilot-term\">accretionary prism</mark> of scraped-off material.</li><li>The <mark class=\"pilot-term\">slab releases water</mark>.</li><li><b>Water causes melting of the mantle</b> above the slab.</li><li><b>Magma rises</b> through mantle and crust.</li><li><b>Eruptions form a volcanic island arc</b> (e.g., Japan).</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g3-5b":{"source":"<p>Oceanic plates are being <b>subducted on both sides of the Pacific</b>, while new crust forms at the <b>East Pacific Rise</b>. Where subduction is beneath <b>oceanic</b> plates you get <b>island arcs</b> (e.g., Japan, west Pacific). Where it is beneath <b>continental</b> plates you get <b>mountain belts with volcanoes</b> (e.g., Andes, east Pacific).</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why the Pacific is ringed by volcanoes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Oceanic plates are being <b>subducted on both sides of the Pacific</b>, while new crust forms at the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">East Pacific Rise</mark>.</li><li>Where subduction is beneath <mark class=\"pilot-term\">oceanic</mark> plates you get <mark class=\"pilot-term\">island arcs</mark> (e.g., Japan, west Pacific).</li><li>Where it is beneath <mark class=\"pilot-term\">continental</mark> plates you get <mark class=\"pilot-term\">mountain belts with volcanoes</mark> (e.g., Andes, east Pacific).</li></ul></section>"},"g3-5c":{"source":"<p>Subduction of the oceanic part of a plate brings two continents together until they <b>collide</b>. <b>Continental plate is buoyant, so subduction ends</b>. Results: a <b>wide zone of deformation</b>, <b>pieces sliced off</b>, <b>thick crust = high elevation</b>, and <b>few volcanoes</b>. The Himalaya and Tibetan Plateau are the example; the peaks are still rising and the region has many large earthquakes (Sep 1).</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Continent-continent collision</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Subduction of the oceanic part of a plate brings two continents together until they <mark class=\"pilot-term\">collide</mark>.</li><li><b>Continental plate is buoyant, so subduction ends</b>.</li><li>Results<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A <mark class=\"pilot-term\">wide zone of deformation</mark>, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">pieces sliced off</mark>, <b>thick crust = high elevation</b>, and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">few volcanoes</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>The Himalaya and Tibetan Plateau are the example.</li><li>The peaks are still rising and the region has many large earthquakes (Sep 1).</li></ul></section>"},"g3-6a":{"source":"<p>On a <b>transform boundary</b>, plates move <b>horizontally past one another</b>. Most transforms <b>link spreading segments</b> of a mid-ocean ridge, which is why ridges zigzag. On Sep 8 the professor drew the distinction that trips people up: the offset between two ridge segments is an <b>active transform fault</b>; its continuation beyond the segments is a <b>fracture zone</b>: an <b>inactive</b> scar where both sides now move the same direction. Where three boundaries meet is a <b>triple junction</b> (e.g., the Mendocino triple junction off northern California).</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transforms link other boundaries</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">transform boundary</mark>, plates move <mark class=\"pilot-term\">horizontally past one another</mark>.</li><li>Most transforms <mark class=\"pilot-term\">link spreading segments</mark> of a mid-ocean ridge, which is why ridges zigzag.</li><li>On Sep 8 the professor drew the distinction that trips people up<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The offset between two ridge segments is an <mark class=\"pilot-term\">active transform fault</mark>.</li><li>Its continuation beyond the segments is a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">fracture zone</mark></li><li>An <mark class=\"pilot-term\">inactive</mark> scar where both sides now move the same direction.</li></ul></li><li>Where three boundaries meet is a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">triple junction</mark> (e.g., the Mendocino triple junction off northern California).</li></ul></section>"},"g3-6b":{"source":"<p>The type of boundary depends on how the boundary is <b>oriented relative to the motion</b>. Where the Pacific–North America boundary runs parallel to plate motion it is a transform (the <b>Queen Charlotte fault</b>); where it bends, the same motion becomes <b>convergent</b> and the Pacific plate is subducted beneath North America (slide 38). Transforms link two spreading centers or a spreading center with a subduction zone.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Same plates, different boundary types</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The type of boundary depends on how the boundary is <b>oriented relative to the motion</b>.</li><li>Where the Pacific–North America boundary runs parallel to plate motion it is a transform (the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Queen Charlotte fault</mark>).</li><li>Where it bends, the same motion becomes <mark class=\"pilot-term\">convergent</mark> and the Pacific plate is subducted beneath North America (slide 38).</li><li>Transforms link two spreading centers or a spreading center with a subduction zone.</li></ul></section>"},"g3-7a":{"source":"<p>Slide 34 names <b>ridge push</b> (the elevated ridge pushes plates outward), <b>slab pull</b> (the sinking slab pulls the rest of the plate), and <b>other forces, such as convection in the mantle</b>. This is the mechanism Wegener lacked.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Driving forces</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Slide 34 names <mark class=\"pilot-term\">ridge push</mark> (the elevated ridge pushes plates outward), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">slab pull</mark> (the sinking slab pulls the rest of the plate), and <b>other forces, such as convection in the mantle</b>.</li><li>This is the mechanism Wegener lacked.</li></ul></section>"},"g3-7b":{"source":"<p>Plates move <b>centimeters per year</b>: about fingernail-growth speed; and some move faster than others. On direct-measurement maps, <b>arrows show direction</b>, <b>longer arrows mean faster</b>, and some arrows curve because plates rotate on a sphere. Over millions of years, cm/yr adds up to thousands of kilometers.</p><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"hotspotRate\">Practice rate calculations (5)</button>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Rates are centimeters per year</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Plates move <mark class=\"pilot-term\">centimeters per year</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>About fingernail-growth speed.</li><li>And some move faster than others.</li></ul></li><li>On direct-measurement maps, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">arrows show direction</mark>, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">longer arrows mean faster</mark>, and some arrows curve because plates rotate on a sphere.</li><li>Over millions of years, cm/yr adds up to thousands of kilometers.</li></ul><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"hotspotRate\">Practice rate calculations (5)</button></section>"},"cx1-g-rate":{"source":"<ul><li>A <b>rate</b> expresses how much a quantity changes per unit time. Plate speed uses distance divided by elapsed time.</li><li>A distance alone describes how far; a duration alone describes how long. Neither is a rate until they are related, for example centimeters per year.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">A rate is change divided by elapsed time</h4><ul><li>A <mark class=\"pilot-term\">rate</mark> expresses how much a quantity changes per unit time. Plate speed uses distance divided by elapsed time.</li><li>A distance alone describes how far.</li><li>A duration alone describes how long.</li><li>Neither is a rate until they are related, for example centimeters per year.</li></ul></section>"},"g3-8a":{"source":"<p>Earth's magnetic field comes from <b>convection currents in the liquid outer core</b> and <b>reverses</b> over time (normal vs. reversed polarity). New crust at a ridge records the field as it cools. As spreading continues, each stripe is carried away and split, producing <b>stripes that are symmetric (mirror images) on both sides of the ridge</b>.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Magnetic stripes: a tape recorder at the ridge</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Earth's magnetic field comes from <b>convection currents in the liquid outer core</b> and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">reverses</mark> over time (normal vs. reversed polarity).</li><li>New crust at a ridge records the field as it cools.</li><li>As spreading continues, each stripe is carried away and split, producing <b>stripes that are symmetric (mirror images) on both sides of the ridge</b>.</li></ul></section>"},"g3-8b":{"source":"<p>Drilling showed <b>volcanic rocks of the oceanic crust are youngest near the ridge</b> (just formed) and <b>older with distance</b>, and <b>sediment thickens away from the ridge</b> because older seafloor has had more time to collect it. Both are predictions of seafloor spreading that passed the test.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Drill cores: older crust, thicker sediment</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Drilling showed <b>volcanic rocks of the oceanic crust are youngest near the ridge</b> (just formed) and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">older with distance</mark>, and <b>sediment thickens away from the ridge</b> because older seafloor has had more time to collect it.</li><li>Both are predictions of seafloor spreading that passed the test.</li></ul></section>"},"g3-8c":{"source":"<p>A <b>volcano forms over a hot spot</b>; as the <b>plate moves</b>, the volcano is carried away and <b>becomes inactive</b>, and a new one forms over the hot spot. The <b>plate subsides as it cools</b>, so old islands sink to become <b>seamounts</b>. Result: a <b>line of islands and seamounts</b> (e.g., Hawaii) that gets <b>older away from the active end</b>, pointing in the direction the plate moved away from.</p><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"hotspotRate\">Practice hot-spot rate problems (5)</button>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Hot spots make age-progressive chains</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <b>volcano forms over a hot spot</b>.</li><li>As the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">plate moves</mark>, the volcano is carried away and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">becomes inactive</mark>, and a new one forms over the hot spot.</li><li>The <b>plate subsides as it cools</b>, so old islands sink to become <mark class=\"pilot-term\">seamounts</mark>.</li><li>Result<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A <b>line of islands and seamounts</b> (e.g., Hawaii) that gets <b>older away from the active end</b>, pointing in the direction the plate moved away from.</li></ul></li></ul><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"hotspotRate\">Practice hot-spot rate problems (5)</button></section>"},"g3-8d":{"source":"<p>West to east: an offshore <b>trench</b> → the <b>Andes</b> (mountains and volcanoes over a subduction zone) → a <b>low-relief interior</b> → the east coast, which is a <b>passive margin (not a plate boundary)</b> with a broad shelf → the <b>Mid-Atlantic Ridge</b>, where spreading occurs. South America and the western South Atlantic ride on the same plate.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why South America is lopsided</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>West to east<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>An offshore <mark class=\"pilot-term\">trench</mark> → the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Andes</mark> (mountains and volcanoes over a subduction zone) → a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">low-relief interior</mark> → the east coast, which is a <b>passive margin (not a plate boundary)</b> with a broad shelf → the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Mid-Atlantic Ridge</mark>, where spreading occurs.</li></ul></li><li>South America and the western South Atlantic ride on the same plate.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-guide-T3":{"source":"<p>Wegener linked continental fits, fossils, rocks, and past climates, but lacked a convincing driving mechanism. Seafloor spreading supplied a testable process: new basalt forms at a ridge and records magnetic polarity as it cools. Matching reversal patterns on both sides support spreading.</p><p>Away from an undisturbed ridge, oceanic lithosphere is generally <b>older, cooler, denser, thicker, and lower</b>. The mantle part of the lithosphere thickens as it cools. Sediment cover also generally thickens with age.</p><p><b>Keep three thicknesses separate:</b> oceanic crust stays roughly similar in thickness; cooling mantle lithosphere thickens; sediment can accumulate on top.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Exam 1 focus: Drift, spreading, stripes, and oceanic plate aging</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wegener linked continental fits, fossils, rocks, and past climates, but lacked a convincing driving mechanism.</li><li>Seafloor spreading supplied a testable process<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>New basalt forms at a ridge and records magnetic polarity as it cools.</li></ul></li><li>Matching reversal patterns on both sides support spreading.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Away from an undisturbed ridge, oceanic lithosphere is generally <b>older, cooler, denser, thicker, and lower</b>.</li><li>The mantle part of the lithosphere thickens as it cools.</li><li>Sediment cover also generally thickens with age.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Keep three thicknesses separate</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Oceanic crust stays roughly similar in thickness.</li><li>Cooling mantle lithosphere thickens.</li><li>Sediment can accumulate on top.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g4-1a":{"source":"<p>A mineral is a <b>naturally occurring, inorganic, crystalline solid with a relatively consistent (specific) chemical composition</b>. Fail any one and it is not a mineral:</p><table><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Is</th><th>Is not</th></tr><tr><td>Natural</td><td>Natural diamond</td><td>Lab-grown (synthetic) diamond</td></tr><tr><td>Inorganic</td><td>Calcite crystal</td><td>A shell made by a creature, even of the same composition</td></tr><tr><td>Solid</td><td>Ice</td><td>Liquid water; liquid mercury</td></tr><tr><td>Ordered internal structure (crystalline)</td><td>Calcite</td><td>Volcanic glass (obsidian): atoms arranged randomly</td></tr><tr><td>Specific composition</td><td>Halite, NaCl, whatever the sample size</td><td>Conglomerate: different parts, different compositions</td></tr></table><p>“Minerals” on a vitamin label are chemical elements, not geologic minerals.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Five tests for “mineral”</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A mineral is a <b>naturally occurring, inorganic, crystalline solid with a relatively consistent (specific) chemical composition</b>.</li><li>Fail any one and it is not a mineral:.</li></ul><table><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Is</th><th>Is not</th></tr><tr><td>Natural</td><td>Natural diamond</td><td>Lab-grown (synthetic) diamond</td></tr><tr><td>Inorganic</td><td>Calcite crystal</td><td>A shell made by a creature, even of the same composition</td></tr><tr><td>Solid</td><td>Ice</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Liquid water.</li><li>Liquid mercury.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>Ordered internal structure (crystalline)</td><td>Calcite</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Volcanic glass (obsidian)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Atoms arranged randomly.</li></ul></li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>Specific composition</td><td>Halite, NaCl, whatever the sample size</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Conglomerate<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Different parts, different compositions.</li></ul></li></ul></td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>“Minerals” on a vitamin label are chemical elements, not geologic minerals.</li></ul></section>"},"g4-1b":{"source":"<p>The professor's zoom (Sep 8): <b>landscape → outcrop → hand specimen → minerals → atoms</b>. <b>Rocks are made of minerals, and minerals are made of chemical elements.</b> Most rocks contain several minerals (granite: quartz, feldspar, and dark minerals); some contain one (limestone can be nearly all calcite; sandstone can be all quartz). A few minerals are one element (<b>native elements</b>, e.g., diamond = carbon). Very thin slices (<b>thin sections</b>, under 30 micrometers per the lecture) let light pass for microscope identification.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Levels of scale</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The professor's zoom (Sep 8)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Landscape → outcrop → hand specimen → minerals → atoms</b>.</li></ul></li><li><b>Rocks are made of minerals, and minerals are made of chemical elements.</b></li><li>Most rocks contain several minerals (granite: quartz, feldspar, and dark minerals).</li><li>Some contain one (limestone can be nearly all calcite; sandstone can be all quartz).</li><li>A few minerals are one element (<mark class=\"pilot-term\">native elements</mark>, e.g., diamond = carbon).</li><li>Very thin slices (<mark class=\"pilot-term\">thin sections</mark>, under 30 micrometers per the lecture) let light pass for microscope identification.</li></ul></section>"},"g4-1c":{"source":"<p>The professor's shortcut (Sep 10): in a <b>crystalline</b> rock the minerals <b>grew</b> in place, interlocking as they competed for space; in a <b>clastic</b> rock the pieces were <b>produced</b> from pre-existing rocks by weathering and then deposited. Crystalline rocks typically form by crystallizing magma, metamorphism, or precipitation from water (hot, or evaporating). Clastic rocks mostly form at the surface (dunes, rivers, beaches), though explosive eruptions also make clastic volcanic rocks.</p><p>Four things to observe in either: <b>types of minerals, sizes, shapes, and layering</b>.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Crystalline vs. clastic</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The professor's shortcut (Sep 10)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>In a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">crystalline</mark> rock the minerals <mark class=\"pilot-term\">grew</mark> in place, interlocking as they competed for space.</li><li>In a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">clastic</mark> rock the pieces were <mark class=\"pilot-term\">produced</mark> from pre-existing rocks by weathering and then deposited.</li></ul></li><li>Crystalline rocks typically form by crystallizing magma, metamorphism, or precipitation from water (hot, or evaporating).</li><li>Clastic rocks mostly form at the surface (dunes, rivers, beaches), though explosive eruptions also make clastic volcanic rocks.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Four things to observe in either<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Types of minerals, sizes, shapes, and layering</b>.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-peninsular":{"source":"<ul><li>The Peninsular Ranges of southern California and Baja California expose <b>granite and metamorphic rocks formed at depth</b>. Granite crystallized from magma in the crust; the metamorphic rocks record burial, heating, and deformation.</li><li>Later uplift and erosion exposed these rocks. Do not confuse the mountain bedrock with loose sand and gravel transported into nearby lowlands.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect location: the Peninsular Ranges</h4><ul><li>The Peninsular Ranges of southern California and Baja California expose <b>granite and metamorphic rocks formed at depth</b>.</li><li>Granite crystallized from magma in the crust.</li><li>The metamorphic rocks record burial, heating, and deformation.</li><li>Later uplift and erosion exposed these rocks. Do not confuse the mountain bedrock with loose sand and gravel transported into nearby lowlands.</li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-grains":{"source":"<ul><li>For the introductory crystal/clast description, inspect <b>size, shape, and composition</b>, as well as how the grains fit together.</li><li>Density can help identify a mineral, but it is not the visible crystal/clast attribute requested by this rock-description task. Do not swap a mineral test for a texture observation.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Describe the grains before naming the rock</h4><ul><li>For the introductory crystal/clast description, inspect <mark class=\"pilot-term\">size, shape, and composition</mark>, as well as how the grains fit together.</li><li>Density can help identify a mineral, but it is not the visible crystal/clast attribute requested by this rock-description task. Do not swap a mineral test for a texture observation.</li></ul></section>"},"g4-2a":{"source":"<ul><li><b>Crystal shape</b>: cubes, prisms, hexagons; reflects the atomic arrangement when growth is unobstructed.</li><li><b>Cleavage vs. fracture</b>: breaking along flat planes (cleavage) vs. irregular or curved surfaces (fracture).</li><li><b>Color</b>: useful but <b>not reliable</b>: quartz comes in many colors because of trace elements.</li><li><b>Luster</b>: how light bounces off: <b>metallic</b> or <b>nonmetallic</b> (glassy, pearly, silky, resinous, earthy).</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What you can see without a test</h4><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Crystal shape</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Cubes, prisms, hexagons.</li><li>Reflects the atomic arrangement when growth is unobstructed.</li></ul></li><li><b>Cleavage vs. fracture</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Breaking along flat planes (cleavage) vs. irregular or curved surfaces (fracture).</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Color</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Useful but <b>not reliable</b></li><li>Quartz comes in many colors because of trace elements.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Luster</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>How light bounces off</li><li><b>Metallic</b> or <mark class=\"pilot-term\">nonmetallic</mark> (glassy, pearly, silky, resinous, earthy).</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g4-2b":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Test</th><th>How</th><th>Classic result</th></tr><tr><td>Hardness</td><td>Scratch test; a harder material scratches a softer one</td><td>Mohs scale 1–10</td></tr><tr><td>Streak</td><td>Rub on unglazed porcelain</td><td>Hematite leaves a <b>reddish</b> streak</td></tr><tr><td>Effervescence</td><td>Drop of dilute HCl</td><td><b>Calcite</b> fizzes strongly (limestone)</td></tr><tr><td>Magnetism</td><td>Magnet or compass</td><td><b>Magnetite</b> is the strongest natural magnet</td></tr><tr><td>Density / specific gravity</td><td>Heft test; ratio to water</td><td>Galena feels heavy; typical rock ≈ 2.7</td></tr></table><p><b>Mohs scale:</b> 1 talc, 2 gypsum, 3 calcite, 4 fluorite, 5 apatite, 6 K-feldspar, 7 quartz, 8 topaz, 9 corundum, 10 diamond. Reference objects: fingernail 2.5, copper wire 3.5, window glass or knife blade 5.5. The numbers are <b>relative ranks</b>, not equal steps (diamond is far harder than corundum).</p><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"hardness\">Practice scratch-test reasoning (5)</button> <button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"mineralId\">Practice mineral ID from properties (5)</button>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Five simple tests</h4><table><tr><th>Test</th><th>How</th><th>Classic result</th></tr><tr><td>Hardness</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Scratch test.</li><li>A harder material scratches a softer one.</li></ul></td><td>Mohs scale 1–10</td></tr><tr><td>Streak</td><td>Rub on unglazed porcelain</td><td>Hematite leaves a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">reddish</mark> streak</td></tr><tr><td>Effervescence</td><td>Drop of dilute HCl</td><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Calcite</mark> fizzes strongly (limestone)</td></tr><tr><td>Magnetism</td><td>Magnet or compass</td><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Magnetite</mark> is the strongest natural magnet</td></tr><tr><td>Density / specific gravity</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Heft test.</li><li>Ratio to water.</li></ul></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Galena feels heavy.</li><li>Typical rock ≈ 2.7.</li></ul></td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Mohs scale</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>1 Talc, 2 gypsum, 3 calcite, 4 fluorite, 5 apatite, 6 K-feldspar, 7 quartz, 8 topaz, 9 corundum, 10 diamond.</li></ul></li><li>Reference objects<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Fingernail 2.5, copper wire 3.5, window glass or knife blade 5.5. The numbers are <mark class=\"pilot-term\">relative ranks</mark>, not equal steps (diamond is far harder than corundum).</li></ul></li></ul><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"hardness\">Practice scratch-test reasoning (5)</button> <button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"mineralId\">Practice mineral ID from properties (5)</button></section>"},"cx1-g-mineralogist":{"source":"<ul><li>A <b>mineralogist</b> studies minerals: their composition, crystal structure, properties, and occurrence. Quartz and halite are geological minerals.</li><li>Nutritional uses of “minerals” refer to chemical elements needed by the body. That usage is not the geological definition of a naturally occurring crystalline solid.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What a mineralogist studies</h4><ul><li>A <mark class=\"pilot-term\">mineralogist</mark> studies minerals<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Their composition, crystal structure, properties, and occurrence.</li></ul></li><li>Quartz and halite are geological minerals.</li><li>Nutritional uses of “minerals” refer to chemical elements needed by the body. That usage is not the geological definition of a naturally occurring crystalline solid.</li></ul></section>"},"g4-3a":{"source":"<p>Halite (NaCl): sodium and chlorine alternate in three perpendicular directions, a <b>cubic</b> arrangement, so halite grows and cleaves into cubes. Common atomic arrangements are the <b>cube</b>, the <b>tetrahedron</b> (one atom surrounded by four, a pyramid with a triangular base), and the <b>octahedron</b> (two four-sided pyramids joined base to base). Well-formed crystals grow <b>unimpeded</b>, usually into open space filled with water or magma; crystals that grow <b>around earlier crystals</b> take irregular shapes.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Shape reflects the arrangement of atoms</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Halite (NaCl)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Sodium and chlorine alternate in three perpendicular directions, a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">cubic</mark> arrangement, so halite grows and cleaves into cubes.</li></ul></li><li>Common atomic arrangements are the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">cube</mark>, the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">tetrahedron</mark> (one atom surrounded by four, a pyramid with a triangular base), and the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">octahedron</mark> (two four-sided pyramids joined base to base).</li><li>Well-formed crystals grow <mark class=\"pilot-term\">unimpeded</mark>, usually into open space filled with water or magma.</li><li>Crystals that grow <mark class=\"pilot-term\">around earlier crystals</mark> take irregular shapes.</li></ul></section>"},"g4-3b":{"source":"<p>A mineral cleaves where bonds are weakest, along parallel planes. If no such planes exist, it fractures.</p><table><tr><th>Cleavage</th><th>Result</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>One direction</td><td>Thin sheets</td><td>Micas (biotite, muscovite)</td></tr><tr><td>Two at ~90°</td><td>Right-angle steps</td><td>Pyroxene</td></tr><tr><td>Two not at 90° (~60°/120°)</td><td>Non-square corners</td><td>Amphibole</td></tr><tr><td>Three at 90°</td><td>Cubes, stair steps</td><td>Halite (also galena)</td></tr><tr><td>Three not at 90°</td><td>Rhombs</td><td>Calcite</td></tr><tr><td>None</td><td>Conchoidal fracture</td><td>Quartz</td></tr></table>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Cleavage follows the weakest bonds</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A mineral cleaves where bonds are weakest, along parallel planes.</li><li>If no such planes exist, it fractures.</li></ul><table><tr><th>Cleavage</th><th>Result</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>One direction</td><td>Thin sheets</td><td>Micas (biotite, muscovite)</td></tr><tr><td>Two at ~90°</td><td>Right-angle steps</td><td>Pyroxene</td></tr><tr><td>Two not at 90° (~60°/120°)</td><td>Non-square corners</td><td>Amphibole</td></tr><tr><td>Three at 90°</td><td>Cubes, stair steps</td><td>Halite (also galena)</td></tr><tr><td>Three not at 90°</td><td>Rhombs</td><td>Calcite</td></tr><tr><td>None</td><td>Conchoidal fracture</td><td>Quartz</td></tr></table></section>"},"g4-4a":{"source":"<p>An atom has a nucleus of <b>protons (+)</b> and <b>neutrons (no charge)</b> surrounded by <b>electrons (−)</b>. The number of protons is the <b>atomic number</b>. Atoms are most stable with a full outer shell: sodium (1 outer electron) tends to <b>give</b> one; chlorine (7 outer electrons) tends to <b>take</b> one. An atom that gains or loses electrons is an <b>ion</b>: positive ions are <b>cations</b>, negative ions are <b>anions</b>.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Atoms and ions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An atom has a nucleus of <mark class=\"pilot-term\">protons (+)</mark> and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">neutrons (no charge)</mark> surrounded by <mark class=\"pilot-term\">electrons (−)</mark>.</li><li>The number of protons is the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">atomic number</mark>.</li><li>Atoms are most stable with a full outer shell<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Sodium (1 outer electron) tends to <mark class=\"pilot-term\">give</mark> one.</li><li>Chlorine (7 outer electrons) tends to <mark class=\"pilot-term\">take</mark> one.</li></ul></li><li>An atom that gains or loses electrons is an <mark class=\"pilot-term\">ion</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Positive ions are <mark class=\"pilot-term\">cations</mark>, negative ions are <mark class=\"pilot-term\">anions</mark>.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g4-4b":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Bond</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Strength</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>Covalent</td><td>Atoms <b>share</b> electrons</td><td>Strongest</td><td>Diamond; Si–O inside a tetrahedron; water molecule</td></tr><tr><td>Ionic</td><td>Electron <b>transferred</b>; opposite charges attract</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Halite, fluorite</td></tr><tr><td>Metallic</td><td>Electrons shared freely among many atoms</td><td>Low</td><td>Gold, copper (pliable, conduct electricity)</td></tr><tr><td>Intermolecular</td><td>Weak attraction between molecules or sheets</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Between sheets of mica, clay, graphite</td></tr></table><p><b>Diamond vs. graphite</b> (Sep 10): both are pure carbon. Diamond's carbon atoms form a 3-D covalent framework; hardest mineral (10). Graphite has strong covalent sheets held by weak intermolecular forces, so sheets slide; soft enough for pencils.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Four ways atoms hold together</h4><table><tr><th>Bond</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Strength</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>Covalent</td><td>Atoms <mark class=\"pilot-term\">share</mark> electrons</td><td>Strongest</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Diamond.</li><li>Si–O inside a tetrahedron.</li><li>Water molecule.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>Ionic</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Electron <mark class=\"pilot-term\">transferred</mark>.</li><li>Opposite charges attract.</li></ul></td><td>Moderate</td><td>Halite, fluorite</td></tr><tr><td>Metallic</td><td>Electrons shared freely among many atoms</td><td>Low</td><td>Gold, copper (pliable, conduct electricity)</td></tr><tr><td>Intermolecular</td><td>Weak attraction between molecules or sheets</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Between sheets of mica, clay, graphite</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Diamond vs. graphite</b> (Sep 10)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Both are pure carbon.</li></ul></li><li>Diamond's carbon atoms form a 3-D covalent framework.</li><li>Hardest mineral (10).</li><li>Graphite has strong covalent sheets held by weak intermolecular forces, so sheets slide.</li><li>Soft enough for pencils.</li></ul></section>"},"g4-4c":{"source":"<p>A water molecule is <b>polar</b>: the oxygen side is slightly negative, the hydrogen side slightly positive. That polarity pulls Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions out of a halite crystal, so halite <b>dissolves</b>. When salty water <b>evaporates</b>, the ions become concentrated and bond again, so halite <b>precipitates</b>. Ice is a mineral; its hydrogen-bonded structure spaces molecules farther apart than in liquid water, so <b>ice is less dense and floats</b>. <span class=\"small muted\">(Textbook section; not discussed in the supplied recordings.)</span></p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Water, dissolving, and ice (textbook 4.13)</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A water molecule is <mark class=\"pilot-term\">polar</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The oxygen side is slightly negative, the hydrogen side slightly positive.</li></ul></li><li>That polarity pulls Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions out of a halite crystal, so halite <mark class=\"pilot-term\">dissolves</mark>.</li><li>When salty water <mark class=\"pilot-term\">evaporates</mark>, the ions become concentrated and bond again, so halite <mark class=\"pilot-term\">precipitates</mark>.</li><li>Ice is a mineral.</li><li>Its hydrogen-bonded structure spaces molecules farther apart than in liquid water, so <b>ice is less dense and floats</b>. <span class=\"small muted\">(Textbook section; not discussed in the supplied recordings.)</span>.</li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-element-families":{"source":"<ul><li>Transition metals and noble gases are categories of chemical elements. <b>Sulfides</b> are a mineral family involving sulfur combined with metals; they are not a column of the periodic table.</li><li>In the course’s bond classification, silicon and oxygen within a silicate tetrahedron share electrons in <b>covalent bonds</b>. Metallic bonding instead involves mobile electrons; ionic bonding emphasizes attraction between oppositely charged ions.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Element categories are not mineral families</h4><ul><li>Transition metals and noble gases are categories of chemical elements.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Sulfides</mark> are a mineral family involving sulfur combined with metals.</li><li>They are not a column of the periodic table.</li><li>In the course’s bond classification, silicon and oxygen within a silicate tetrahedron share electrons in <mark class=\"pilot-term\">covalent bonds</mark>.</li><li>Metallic bonding instead involves mobile electrons.</li><li>Ionic bonding emphasizes attraction between oppositely charged ions.</li></ul></section>"},"g4-5a":{"source":"<p><b>Oxygen</b> is the most abundant element in the crust and <b>silicon</b> is second; together they make up about <b>three-quarters of the crust by weight</b> (textbook: 74%). The next most abundant are aluminum, iron, calcium, sodium, potassium, and magnesium. Because O and Si are so common and bond readily, <b>silicates are the largest and most common mineral family</b>: over 90% of the minerals in the crust. Everything else (carbonates, oxides, sulfides, sulfates, halides, native elements) is grouped as <b>non-silicates</b>.</p><p>For the <b>whole Earth</b>, iron is far more abundant than in the crust because the core is iron-nickel.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why most minerals are silicates</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Oxygen</mark> is the most abundant element in the crust and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">silicon</mark> is second.</li><li>Together they make up about <b>three-quarters of the crust by weight</b> (textbook: 74%).</li><li>The next most abundant are aluminum, iron, calcium, sodium, potassium, and magnesium.</li><li>Because O and Si are so common and bond readily, <b>silicates are the largest and most common mineral family</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Over 90% of the minerals in the crust.</li></ul></li><li>Everything else (carbonates, oxides, sulfides, sulfates, halides, native elements) is grouped as <mark class=\"pilot-term\">non-silicates</mark>.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>For the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">whole Earth</mark>, iron is far more abundant than in the crust because the core is iron-nickel.</li></ul></section>"},"g4-5b":{"source":"<p>One small <b>silicon</b> atom sits in the center of <b>four oxygen</b> atoms arranged as a <b>tetrahedron</b>. Charge check (the professor did this on Sep 10): Si is +4, four O are −2 each (−8), so the unit carries a net <b>−4</b> charge. It must bond to <b>cations</b> (Fe, Mg, Ca, Na, K, Al) or share oxygens with other tetrahedra to become neutral.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The building block: SiO₄ tetrahedron</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>One small <mark class=\"pilot-term\">silicon</mark> atom sits in the center of <mark class=\"pilot-term\">four oxygen</mark> atoms arranged as a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">tetrahedron</mark>.</li><li>Charge check (the professor did this on Sep 10)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Si is +4, four O are −2 each (−8), so the unit carries a net <mark class=\"pilot-term\">−4</mark> charge.</li></ul></li><li>It must bond to <mark class=\"pilot-term\">cations</mark> (Fe, Mg, Ca, Na, K, Al) or share oxygens with other tetrahedra to become neutral.</li></ul></section>"},"g4-5c":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Oxygens shared</th><th>Example</th><th>Cleavage</th></tr><tr><td>Independent (isolated) tetrahedra</td><td>0</td><td><b>Olivine</b></td><td>None (bonds similar in all directions)</td></tr><tr><td>Single chains</td><td>2</td><td><b>Pyroxene</b></td><td>Two, near 90°</td></tr><tr><td>Double chains</td><td>2 and 3 (alternating)</td><td><b>Amphibole</b></td><td>Two, ~60°/120°</td></tr><tr><td>Sheets</td><td>3</td><td><b>Micas</b>, clays</td><td>One (sheets)</td></tr><tr><td>Framework</td><td>All 4</td><td><b>Quartz</b>, feldspars</td><td>Quartz: none; feldspar: two</td></tr></table>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">How tetrahedra link; and what that does to cleavage</h4><table><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Oxygens shared</th><th>Example</th><th>Cleavage</th></tr><tr><td>Independent (isolated) tetrahedra</td><td>0</td><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Olivine</mark></td><td>None (bonds similar in all directions)</td></tr><tr><td>Single chains</td><td>2</td><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Pyroxene</mark></td><td>Two, near 90°</td></tr><tr><td>Double chains</td><td>2 and 3 (alternating)</td><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Amphibole</mark></td><td>Two, ~60°/120°</td></tr><tr><td>Sheets</td><td>3</td><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Micas</mark>, clays</td><td>One (sheets)</td></tr><tr><td>Framework</td><td>All 4</td><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Quartz</mark>, feldspars</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Quartz<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>None.</li><li>Feldspar</li><li>Two.</li></ul></li></ul></td></tr></table></section>"},"g4-5d":{"source":"<p><b>Light / felsic</b> (upper continental crust): <b>quartz</b> (SiO₂, hardness 7, no cleavage, conchoidal fracture, clear to many colors), <b>K-feldspar</b> (pink to cream, two cleavages), <b>plagioclase</b> (white to gray; Na-rich to Ca-rich; straight striations), <b>muscovite</b> (clear-silvery mica, one cleavage).</p><p><b>Dark / mafic</b> (Mg- and Fe-rich; oceanic crust, mantle, lower crust): <b>amphibole</b> (long crystals, ~60/120° cleavage), <b>pyroxene</b> (stubby crystals, ~90° cleavage), <b>olivine</b> (olive green, no cleavage; most common mineral of the upper mantle), <b>garnet</b> (often deep red, 12-sided), <b>biotite</b> (black mica, one cleavage).</p><p><b>Clay minerals</b> are sheet silicates with weaker bonds; they mostly form by weathering.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Common light (felsic) and dark (mafic) silicates</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Light / felsic</mark> (upper continental crust)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Quartz</b> (SiO₂, hardness 7, no cleavage, conchoidal fracture, clear to many colors), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">K-feldspar</mark> (pink to cream, two cleavages), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">plagioclase</mark> (white to gray; Na-rich to Ca-rich; straight striations), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">muscovite</mark> (clear-silvery mica, one cleavage).</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Dark / mafic</mark> (Mg- and Fe-rich; oceanic crust, mantle, lower crust)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Amphibole</b> (long crystals, ~60/120° cleavage), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">pyroxene</mark> (stubby crystals, ~90° cleavage), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">olivine</mark> (olive green, no cleavage; most common mineral of the upper mantle), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">garnet</mark> (often deep red, 12-sided), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">biotite</mark> (black mica, one cleavage).</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Clay minerals</mark> are sheet silicates with weaker bonds.</li><li>They mostly form by weathering.</li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-reservoir":{"source":"<ul><li><b>Earth’s crust:</b> oxygen and silicon are the two most abundant elements. This supports the abundance of silicate minerals.</li><li><b>Whole Earth:</b> the supplied abundance figure ranks iron, oxygen, and silicon as the three most abundant elements. Including the iron-rich core changes the answer.</li><li>Always identify the requested reservoir and number of elements before answering. A correct crustal answer does not answer a whole-Earth question.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the reservoir: crust versus whole Earth</h4><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Earth’s crust</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Oxygen and silicon are the two most abundant elements.</li></ul></li><li>This supports the abundance of silicate minerals.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Whole Earth</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The supplied abundance figure ranks iron, oxygen, and silicon as the three most abundant elements.</li></ul></li><li>Including the iron-rich core changes the answer.</li><li>Always identify the requested reservoir and number of elements before answering. A correct crustal answer does not answer a whole-Earth question.</li></ul></section>"},"g4-6a":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Family</th><th>Key chemistry</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>Carbonates</td><td>Metal + CO₃</td><td>Calcite CaCO₃ (fizzes in HCl; limestone), dolomite CaMg(CO₃)₂</td></tr><tr><td>Oxides</td><td>Metal + O</td><td>Hematite Fe₂O₃ (red streak), magnetite Fe₃O₄ (magnetic)</td></tr><tr><td>Sulfides</td><td>Metal + S (no oxygen)</td><td>Pyrite FeS₂ (“fool's gold”), galena PbS (heavy, cubic), chalcopyrite</td></tr><tr><td>Sulfates</td><td>Metal + SO₄ (sulfur bonded to oxygen)</td><td>Gypsum (scratched by fingernail; wallboard)</td></tr><tr><td>Halides</td><td>Metal + Cl or F</td><td>Halite NaCl, fluorite</td></tr><tr><td>Native elements</td><td>One element</td><td>Gold, copper, sulfur, diamond, graphite</td></tr></table><p>Sep 10 tip: if the formula has no Si-O group, it is a non-silicate; sulfi<b>d</b>e = metal + sulfur; sulf<b>ate</b> = metal + sulfur <b>and oxygen</b>.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Non-silicate families: read the formula</h4><table><tr><th>Family</th><th>Key chemistry</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>Carbonates</td><td>Metal + CO₃</td><td>Calcite CaCO₃ (fizzes in HCl; limestone), dolomite CaMg(CO₃)₂</td></tr><tr><td>Oxides</td><td>Metal + O</td><td>Hematite Fe₂O₃ (red streak), magnetite Fe₃O₄ (magnetic)</td></tr><tr><td>Sulfides</td><td>Metal + S (no oxygen)</td><td>Pyrite FeS₂ (“fool's gold”), galena PbS (heavy, cubic), chalcopyrite</td></tr><tr><td>Sulfates</td><td>Metal + SO₄ (sulfur bonded to oxygen)</td><td>Gypsum (scratched by fingernail; wallboard)</td></tr><tr><td>Halides</td><td>Metal + Cl or F</td><td>Halite NaCl, fluorite</td></tr><tr><td>Native elements</td><td>One element</td><td>Gold, copper, sulfur, diamond, graphite</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sep 10 tip<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>If the formula has no Si-O group, it is a non-silicate.</li><li>Sulfi<mark class=\"pilot-term\">d</mark>e = metal + sulfur.</li><li>Sulf<mark class=\"pilot-term\">ate</mark> = metal + sulfur <mark class=\"pilot-term\">and oxygen</mark>.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g4-6b":{"source":"<ul><li><b>Continental crust:</b> much of it granite-like; quartz and feldspar; sedimentary cover with quartz, clays, feldspar, carbonates.</li><li><b>Oceanic crust:</b> basalt and gabbro; plagioclase, pyroxene, olivine.</li><li><b>Upper mantle:</b> mostly olivine and pyroxene.</li><li><b>Lower mantle:</b> high-pressure Fe-Mg silicates and oxides.</li><li><b>Core:</b> iron and nickel (outer molten, inner solid).</li></ul><p>Sep 10 example: sand at the <b>Mississippi River mouth</b> is mostly <b>silicate</b> grains (quartz, feldspar, etc.) carried from the Rocky Mountains, while <b>white Florida beach sand</b> includes <b>shell fragments; carbonate</b>, a non-silicate.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Which minerals where</h4><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Continental crust</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Much of it granite-like.</li><li>Quartz and feldspar.</li><li>Sedimentary cover with quartz, clays, feldspar, carbonates.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Oceanic crust</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Basalt and gabbro.</li><li>Plagioclase, pyroxene, olivine.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Upper mantle</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Mostly olivine and pyroxene.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Lower mantle</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>High-pressure Fe-Mg silicates and oxides.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Core</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Iron and nickel (outer molten, inner solid).</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sep 10 example<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Sand at the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Mississippi River mouth</mark> is mostly <mark class=\"pilot-term\">silicate</mark> grains (quartz, feldspar, etc.) carried from the Rocky Mountains, while <mark class=\"pilot-term\">white Florida beach sand</mark> includes <b>shell fragments</b>.</li><li><b>Carbonate</b>, a non-silicate.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-mineral-rarity":{"source":"<ul><li>Most mineral species are <b>uncommon to rare</b>; a much smaller set of common rock-forming minerals accounts for most ordinary rocks.</li><li>Do not confuse the number of distinct mineral species with their abundance in rocks. The quiz’s historical count is not a current census or a required numerical target.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Many mineral species, few common rock formers</h4><ul><li>Most mineral species are <mark class=\"pilot-term\">uncommon to rare</mark>.</li><li>A much smaller set of common rock-forming minerals accounts for most ordinary rocks.</li><li>Do not confuse the number of distinct mineral species with their abundance in rocks. The quiz’s historical count is not a current census or a required numerical target.</li></ul></section>"},"g5-1a":{"source":"<p>Texture = the sizes, shapes, and arrangement of the pieces. The first question is always: <b>can I see the crystals without a lens?</b></p><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>What you see</th><th>What it records</th></tr><tr><td><b>Phaneritic</b> (coarse)</td><td>Crystals visible to the unaided eye</td><td>Slow cooling at depth</td></tr><tr><td><b>Aphanitic</b> (fine)</td><td>Crystals too small to see; may include ash or glass</td><td>Fast cooling at or near the surface</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pegmatite</b></td><td>Crystals centimeters to meters long</td><td>Water-rich magma lets atoms migrate far and fast; forms at moderate to deep levels, near chamber sides and top</td></tr><tr><td><b>Glassy</b></td><td>No crystals at all</td><td>Cooled too fast for crystals to form</td></tr></table><p>Textbook 5.8 shows the same felsic minerals as coarse granite (slow), medium granitic rock (medium), and fine rhyolite (very fast). Same ingredients, different cooling history.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Grain size is a cooling clock</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Texture = the sizes, shapes, and arrangement of the pieces.</li><li>The first question is always<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Can I see the crystals without a lens?</b></li></ul></li></ul><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>What you see</th><th>What it records</th></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Phaneritic</mark> (coarse)</td><td>Crystals visible to the unaided eye</td><td>Slow cooling at depth</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Aphanitic</mark> (fine)</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Crystals too small to see.</li><li>May include ash or glass.</li></ul></td><td>Fast cooling at or near the surface</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Pegmatite</mark></td><td>Crystals centimeters to meters long</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Water-rich magma lets atoms migrate far and fast.</li><li>Forms at moderate to deep levels, near chamber sides and top.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Glassy</mark></td><td>No crystals at all</td><td>Cooled too fast for crystals to form</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Textbook 5.8 shows the same felsic minerals as coarse granite (slow), medium granitic rock (medium), and fine rhyolite (very fast).</li><li>Same ingredients, different cooling history.</li></ul></section>"},"g5-1b":{"source":"<ul><li><b>Porphyritic</b>: larger crystals (<b>phenocrysts</b>) in a finer matrix. Two-stage history: slow growth in a subsurface chamber, then rapid cooling after rising to near or on the surface.</li><li><b>Vesicular</b>: holes (<b>vesicles</b>) left by gas bubbles. Bubbles form only at <b>low pressure</b>, on or very near the surface. Most volcanic ash is <b>broken vesicle walls</b>.</li><li><b>Welded</b>: hot ash and pumice compacted by overlying material into flattened lenses. Typical of <b>pyroclastic flows</b> (hot when deposited). Ash that fell from a column cooled first, so it is usually <b>nonwelded</b>.</li><li><b>Volcanic breccia</b>: angular fragments in finer matrix; from explosions, lava that breaks while flowing, or volcanic mudflows and landslides.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Special textures and their settings</h4><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Porphyritic</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Larger crystals (<mark class=\"pilot-term\">phenocrysts</mark>) in a finer matrix.</li></ul></li><li>Two-stage history<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Slow growth in a subsurface chamber, then rapid cooling after rising to near or on the surface.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Vesicular</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Holes (<mark class=\"pilot-term\">vesicles</mark>) left by gas bubbles.</li></ul></li><li>Bubbles form only at <mark class=\"pilot-term\">low pressure</mark>, on or very near the surface.</li><li>Most volcanic ash is <mark class=\"pilot-term\">broken vesicle walls</mark>.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Welded</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Hot ash and pumice compacted by overlying material into flattened lenses.</li></ul></li><li>Typical of <mark class=\"pilot-term\">pyroclastic flows</mark> (hot when deposited).</li><li>Ash that fell from a column cooled first, so it is usually <mark class=\"pilot-term\">nonwelded</mark>.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Volcanic breccia</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Angular fragments in finer matrix.</li><li>From explosions, lava that breaks while flowing, or volcanic mudflows and landslides.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g5-2a":{"source":"<p>Name an igneous rock with two questions: <b>what is it made of</b> (column) and <b>how big are the crystals</b> (row). This is the chart projected in class (your photo IMG_5737 shows textbook figure 05.03.b1), redrawn:</p><div data-hook=\"igclass\"></div><table><tr><th></th><th>Felsic</th><th>Intermediate</th><th>Mafic</th><th>Ultramafic</th></tr><tr><td>Coarse (intrusive)</td><td>Granite</td><td>Diorite</td><td>Gabbro</td><td>Peridotite</td></tr><tr><td>Fine (volcanic)</td><td>Rhyolite</td><td>Andesite</td><td>Basalt</td><td>Komatiite (rare, very old ultramafic lava)</td></tr><tr><td>Silica</td><td>~70–77%</td><td>~60%</td><td>~44–50%</td><td>lowest</td></tr><tr><td>Key minerals</td><td>Quartz, K-feldspar, Na-plagioclase, biotite, muscovite</td><td>Plagioclase, amphibole, biotite ± pyroxene</td><td>Ca-plagioclase, pyroxene ± olivine</td><td>Olivine, pyroxene</td></tr></table><p>Left to right: <b>silica drops</b>, <b>Mg, Fe, Ca rise</b>, the rock <b>darkens</b>. The upper mantle is peridotite. Granodiorite sits between granite and diorite.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The two-axis chart</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Name an igneous rock with two questions<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>What is it made of</b> (column) and <b>how big are the crystals</b> (row).</li></ul></li><li>This is the chart projected in class (your photo IMG_5737 shows textbook figure 05.03.b1), redrawn:.</li></ul><div data-hook=\"igclass\"></div><table><tr><th></th><th>Felsic</th><th>Intermediate</th><th>Mafic</th><th>Ultramafic</th></tr><tr><td>Coarse (intrusive)</td><td>Granite</td><td>Diorite</td><td>Gabbro</td><td>Peridotite</td></tr><tr><td>Fine (volcanic)</td><td>Rhyolite</td><td>Andesite</td><td>Basalt</td><td>Komatiite (rare, very old ultramafic lava)</td></tr><tr><td>Silica</td><td>~70–77%</td><td>~60%</td><td>~44–50%</td><td>lowest</td></tr><tr><td>Key minerals</td><td>Quartz, K-feldspar, Na-plagioclase, biotite, muscovite</td><td>Plagioclase, amphibole, biotite ± pyroxene</td><td>Ca-plagioclase, pyroxene ± olivine</td><td>Olivine, pyroxene</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Left to right<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Silica drops</b>, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Mg, Fe, Ca rise</mark>, the rock <mark class=\"pilot-term\">darkens</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>The upper mantle is peridotite.</li><li>Granodiorite sits between granite and diorite.</li></ul></section>"},"g5-2b":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Rock</th><th>Defining feature</th><th>Usual composition</th></tr><tr><td><b>Obsidian</b></td><td>Shiny volcanic glass</td><td>Mostly felsic (rhyolite)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pumice</b></td><td>Light-colored, full of vesicles; silica-rich magma traps gas</td><td>Felsic to intermediate</td></tr><tr><td><b>Scoria</b></td><td>Dark or reddish, many vesicles</td><td>Basalt or andesite</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tuff</b></td><td>Ash, pumice, crystals, fragments (welded or nonwelded)</td><td>Mostly felsic to intermediate</td></tr><tr><td><b>Volcanic breccia</b></td><td>Angular fragments in finer matrix</td><td>Almost any</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pegmatite</b></td><td>Crystals &gt; several cm</td><td>Any, but mostly granitic</td></tr></table>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Rocks named for a texture</h4><table><tr><th>Rock</th><th>Defining feature</th><th>Usual composition</th></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Obsidian</mark></td><td>Shiny volcanic glass</td><td>Mostly felsic (rhyolite)</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Pumice</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Light-colored, full of vesicles.</li><li>Silica-rich magma traps gas.</li></ul></td><td>Felsic to intermediate</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Scoria</mark></td><td>Dark or reddish, many vesicles</td><td>Basalt or andesite</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Tuff</mark></td><td>Ash, pumice, crystals, fragments (welded or nonwelded)</td><td>Mostly felsic to intermediate</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Volcanic breccia</mark></td><td>Angular fragments in finer matrix</td><td>Almost any</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Pegmatite</mark></td><td>Crystals &gt; several cm</td><td>Any, but mostly granitic</td></tr></table></section>"},"g5-2c":{"source":"<p>Pairs across each row share a composition; only the grain size differs. Look for: visible crystals (coarse) vs. none (fine); light vs. dark overall color; green olivine in ultramafic rock.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Photo lineup: the chart in real rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Pairs across each row share a composition.</li><li>Only the grain size differs.</li><li>Look for<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Visible crystals (coarse) vs. none (fine).</li><li>Light vs. dark overall color.</li><li>Green olivine in ultramafic rock.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-bandelier":{"source":"<ul><li>Bandelier’s cliff dwellings occupy cliffs made from <b>compacted volcanic ash</b>, associated with the caldera-forming eruptions in the Jemez Mountains.</li><li>The rock category is <b>tuff</b>: consolidated volcanic ash. It is not granite crystallized slowly at depth or sandstone made from ordinary transported sand grains.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect location: Bandelier’s cliff dwellings</h4><ul><li>Bandelier’s cliff dwellings occupy cliffs made from <mark class=\"pilot-term\">compacted volcanic ash</mark>, associated with the caldera-forming eruptions in the Jemez Mountains.</li><li>The rock category is <mark class=\"pilot-term\">tuff</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Consolidated volcanic ash.</li></ul></li><li>It is not granite crystallized slowly at depth or sandstone made from ordinary transported sand grains.</li></ul></section>"},"g5-3a":{"source":"<ul><li>Early-Earth events (impacts, core formation) account for only about <b>20%</b> of today's internal heat.</li><li>Most comes from <b>radioactive decay</b> of unstable atoms such as uranium and potassium; decay in the <b>mantle</b> supplies most of Earth's thermal energy. The textbook describes alpha, beta, and gamma decay.</li><li>Heat moves by <b>conduction</b> (direct contact), <b>radiant transfer</b> (thermal radiation), and <b>convection</b> (flow of a warm, less dense liquid or weak solid). Plate tectonics involves conduction and convection: rising asthenosphere at ridges, cooling lithosphere by conduction, sinking slabs.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why the interior is still hot</h4><ul><li>Early-Earth events (impacts, core formation) account for only about <mark class=\"pilot-term\">20%</mark> of today's internal heat.</li><li>Most comes from <mark class=\"pilot-term\">radioactive decay</mark> of unstable atoms such as uranium and potassium.</li><li>Decay in the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">mantle</mark> supplies most of Earth's thermal energy.</li><li>The textbook describes alpha, beta, and gamma decay.</li><li>Heat moves by <mark class=\"pilot-term\">conduction</mark> (direct contact), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">radiant transfer</mark> (thermal radiation), and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">convection</mark> (flow of a warm, less dense liquid or weak solid).</li><li>Plate tectonics involves conduction and convection<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Rising asthenosphere at ridges, cooling lithosphere by conduction, sinking slabs.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g5-3b":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Place</th><th>Temperature</th></tr><tr><td>Surface rocks</td><td>~10–35 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Crust gradient</td><td><b>+20–40 °C per km</b> (the geothermal gradient)</td></tr><tr><td>Lower crust</td><td>~900 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Mantle</td><td>~1,400–1,500 °C, yet mostly solid because <b>high pressure</b> keeps it solid</td></tr><tr><td>Core</td><td>~3,000–5,000 °C (hottest)</td></tr></table><p>Sep 15: the professor framed melting as a competition between <b>temperature</b> (drives melting) and <b>pressure</b> (holds atoms in a solid). Pressure is proportional to depth.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Numbers worth carrying</h4><table><tr><th>Place</th><th>Temperature</th></tr><tr><td>Surface rocks</td><td>~10–35 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Crust gradient</td><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">+20–40 °C per km</mark> (the geothermal gradient)</td></tr><tr><td>Lower crust</td><td>~900 °C</td></tr><tr><td>Mantle</td><td>~1,400–1,500 °C, yet mostly solid because <mark class=\"pilot-term\">high pressure</mark> keeps it solid</td></tr><tr><td>Core</td><td>~3,000–5,000 °C (hottest)</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sep 15<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The professor framed melting as a competition between <mark class=\"pilot-term\">temperature</mark> (drives melting) and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">pressure</mark> (holds atoms in a solid).</li></ul></li><li>Pressure is proportional to depth.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-guide-T15":{"source":"<p><b>Convection:</b> heat carried by moving material—warm, less dense material rises and cooler, denser material sinks. The boiling-pot circulation is the review analogy.</p><p><b>Conduction:</b> heat moves through material or across contact without bulk movement—magma heats its wall rock; heat passes through the pot wall. <b>Radiation:</b> heat carried by electromagnetic waves—hot lava emits thermal radiation.</p><p>Plume ascent carries heat by convection; transfer from magma into neighboring crust can occur by conduction. Distinguish heat transfer from the separate pressure decrease that causes decompression melting.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Exam 1 focus: Convection, conduction, and radiation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Convection</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Heat carried by moving material—warm, less dense material rises and cooler, denser material sinks.</li></ul></li><li>The boiling-pot circulation is the review analogy.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Conduction</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Heat moves through material or across contact without bulk movement—magma heats its wall rock.</li><li>Heat passes through the pot wall.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Radiation</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Heat carried by electromagnetic waves—hot lava emits thermal radiation.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Plume ascent carries heat by convection.</li><li>Transfer from magma into neighboring crust can occur by conduction.</li><li>Distinguish heat transfer from the separate pressure decrease that causes decompression melting.</li></ul></section>"},"g5-4a":{"source":"<p>Temperature runs left to right (hotter →); pressure runs <b>top to bottom</b> (deeper ↓). The <b>melting curve (solidus)</b> separates solid (left) from liquid (right). A rock melts when it moves from the solid field across the curve. Three ways to get there:</p><ol><li><b>Heating</b>: same pressure, higher temperature (move right).</li><li><b>Decompression</b>: already-hot rock rises fast enough that it does not cool; pressure drops and the rock crosses the curve (move up). The rock must be hot and rise fast.</li><li><b>Adding water</b>: temperature and pressure unchanged; water shifts the curve to lower temperature, <b>by as much as 500 °C</b>, so the rock is suddenly in the liquid field.</li></ol><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"pt\">Practice P-T paths (5)</button>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The graph the professor walked through (Sep 15)</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Temperature runs left to right (hotter →).</li><li>Pressure runs <mark class=\"pilot-term\">top to bottom</mark> (deeper ↓).</li><li>The <mark class=\"pilot-term\">melting curve (solidus)</mark> separates solid (left) from liquid (right).</li><li>A rock melts when it moves from the solid field across the curve.</li><li>Three ways to get there:.</li></ul><ol><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Heating</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Same pressure, higher temperature (move right).</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Decompression</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Already-hot rock rises fast enough that it does not cool.</li><li>Pressure drops and the rock crosses the curve (move up).</li></ul></li><li>The rock must be hot and rise fast.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Adding water</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Temperature and pressure unchanged.</li><li>Water shifts the curve to lower temperature, <b>by as much as 500 °C</b>, so the rock is suddenly in the liquid field.</li></ul></li></ol><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"pt\">Practice P-T paths (5)</button></section>"},"g5-4b":{"source":"<p>Melting starts <b>40–150 km</b> down in the source area. Complete melting is rare. <b>Felsic minerals melt at lower temperatures</b> than mafic ones, so the first melt is <b>more felsic than the source</b>.</p><ul><li>Mantle (ultramafic) → partial melting → mostly <b>mafic</b> magma.</li><li>Mafic source → partial melting → can yield <b>intermediate</b> magma.</li><li>Continental crust (felsic source) → <b>felsic</b> magma.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Partial melting makes magma more felsic than its source</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Melting starts <mark class=\"pilot-term\">40–150 km</mark> down in the source area.</li><li>Complete melting is rare.</li><li><b>Felsic minerals melt at lower temperatures</b> than mafic ones, so the first melt is <b>more felsic than the source</b>.</li></ul><ul><li>Mantle (ultramafic) → partial melting → mostly <mark class=\"pilot-term\">mafic</mark> magma.</li><li>Mafic source → partial melting → can yield <mark class=\"pilot-term\">intermediate</mark> magma.</li><li>Continental crust (felsic source) → <mark class=\"pilot-term\">felsic</mark> magma.</li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-vibration":{"source":"<ul><li>Atoms and bonds in a solid are <b>vibrating</b>. Being solid does not mean being motionless at the atomic scale.</li><li>Increasing temperature increases vibration. Melting begins when the structure can no longer remain intact under the prevailing conditions; higher pressure generally favors the solid in the course’s introductory melting model.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">A solid can be hot and vibrating</h4><ul><li>Atoms and bonds in a solid are <mark class=\"pilot-term\">vibrating</mark>. Being solid does not mean being motionless at the atomic scale.</li><li>Increasing temperature increases vibration.</li><li>Melting begins when the structure can no longer remain intact under the prevailing conditions.</li><li>Higher pressure generally favors the solid in the course’s introductory melting model.</li></ul></section>"},"g5-5a":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Process</th><th>What happens</th><th>Result</th></tr><tr><td><b>Crystallization</b></td><td>Mafic minerals crystallize first</td><td>Remaining magma becomes more felsic</td></tr><tr><td><b>Crystal settling</b></td><td>Heavy mafic crystals sink; light felsic crystals may float</td><td>Chamber bottom more mafic, top more felsic</td></tr><tr><td><b>Assimilation</b></td><td>Hot magma melts wall rocks and incorporates them</td><td>Composition shifts toward the wall rock</td></tr><tr><td><b>Magma mixing</b></td><td>Two magmas meet and blend</td><td>Intermediate between the two (irregular contacts show both were molten)</td></tr></table>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Changing a magma after it forms</h4><table><tr><th>Process</th><th>What happens</th><th>Result</th></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Crystallization</mark></td><td>Mafic minerals crystallize first</td><td>Remaining magma becomes more felsic</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Crystal settling</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Heavy mafic crystals sink.</li><li>Light felsic crystals may float.</li></ul></td><td>Chamber bottom more mafic, top more felsic</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Assimilation</mark></td><td>Hot magma melts wall rocks and incorporates them</td><td>Composition shifts toward the wall rock</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Magma mixing</mark></td><td>Two magmas meet and blend</td><td>Intermediate between the two (irregular contacts show both were molten)</td></tr></table></section>"},"g5-5b":{"source":"<p>Melt occupies about <b>10% more volume</b> than the rock it came from, so it is <b>less dense</b> and rises. It moves by <b>filling fractures</b> (magma pressure pries them open; tectonic stretching controls their orientation → <b>dikes</b>) or by <b>blocks of roof and wall rock breaking off</b> and sinking into the magma, making room above (the Sep 15 lecture, as transcribed, calls this <b>stoping</b>).</p><p>Four controls on how high it gets: <b>magma pressure</b> from the weight of overlying rock; <b>density</b> (mafic magma may stall at the base of the less dense crust); <b>gas pressure</b> (bubbles form at shallow depth, lowering density, speeding ascent); <b>tectonic stress</b> (opens steep paths or traps magma). <b>Most magma solidifies at depth.</b></p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Getting to the surface is hard</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Melt occupies about <mark class=\"pilot-term\">10% more volume</mark> than the rock it came from, so it is <mark class=\"pilot-term\">less dense</mark> and rises.</li><li>It moves by <mark class=\"pilot-term\">filling fractures</mark> (magma pressure pries them open; tectonic stretching controls their orientation → <mark class=\"pilot-term\">dikes</mark>) or by <b>blocks of roof and wall rock breaking off</b> and sinking into the magma, making room above (the Sep 15 lecture, as transcribed, calls this <mark class=\"pilot-term\">stoping</mark>).</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Four controls on how high it gets<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Magma pressure</b> from the weight of overlying rock.</li><li><b>Density</b> (mafic magma may stall at the base of the less dense crust).</li><li><b>Gas pressure</b> (bubbles form at shallow depth, lowering density, speeding ascent).</li><li><b>Tectonic stress</b> (opens steep paths or traps magma).</li></ul></li><li><b>Most magma solidifies at depth.</b></li></ul></section>"},"g5-5c":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>More viscous (piles up)</th><th>Less viscous (spreads thin, travels far)</th></tr><tr><td><b>Temperature</b> (most important)</td><td>Cooler</td><td>Hotter</td></tr><tr><td>Composition</td><td>Felsic: many long Si-O chains</td><td>Mafic: fewer, shorter chains</td></tr><tr><td>Dissolved water</td><td>Less water</td><td>More water (breaks chains)</td></tr><tr><td>Crystal content</td><td>Many crystals</td><td>Few crystals</td></tr></table><p>Textbook breakfast analogy: orange juice ≈ hot, crystal-poor basalt; thick oatmeal ≈ crystal-rich felsic magma.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Viscosity: resistance to flow</h4><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>More viscous (piles up)</th><th>Less viscous (spreads thin, travels far)</th></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Temperature</mark> (most important)</td><td>Cooler</td><td>Hotter</td></tr><tr><td>Composition</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Felsic<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Many long Si-O chains.</li></ul></li></ul></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Mafic<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Fewer, shorter chains.</li></ul></li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>Dissolved water</td><td>Less water</td><td>More water (breaks chains)</td></tr><tr><td>Crystal content</td><td>Many crystals</td><td>Few crystals</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Textbook breakfast analogy<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Orange juice ≈ hot, crystal-poor basalt.</li><li>Thick oatmeal ≈ crystal-rich felsic magma.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-magma-controls":{"source":"<ul><li>In the introductory thermal comparison, hotter mafic magma is more able to melt lower-melting felsic wall rock than cooler felsic magma is to melt mafic rock. Actual assimilation also depends on heat supply and contact conditions.</li><li>If mafic magma assimilates silica-rich felsic wall rock, that added material shifts the mixture toward a more felsic composition. Assimilation means incorporation of wall rock, not removal of settled crystals.</li><li>For otherwise comparable magma, <b>higher temperature lowers viscosity</b> and permits easier flow. Composition and crystal content are additional controls, so keep them fixed when testing temperature alone.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Track composition and temperature separately</h4><ul><li>In the introductory thermal comparison, hotter mafic magma is more able to melt lower-melting felsic wall rock than cooler felsic magma is to melt mafic rock. Actual assimilation also depends on heat supply and contact conditions.</li><li>If mafic magma assimilates silica-rich felsic wall rock, that added material shifts the mixture toward a more felsic composition. Assimilation means incorporation of wall rock, not removal of settled crystals.</li><li>For otherwise comparable magma, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">higher temperature lowers viscosity</mark> and permits easier flow. Composition and crystal content are additional controls, so keep them fixed when testing temperature alone.</li></ul></section>"},"g5-6a":{"source":"<p>Magma cools by <b>conduction</b> into wall rocks (which may be metamorphosed or melted), <b>radiation and conduction</b> at the surface, <b>releasing gases</b>, and <b>convection of groundwater</b> heated by the magma (possibly the main cooling path for some magmas). Solidification starts at different temperatures: <b>mafic &gt; 1,100 °C</b>, <b>intermediate ~950 °C</b>, <b>felsic ~800 °C</b>. A rising magma's path on a temperature-depth graph: melt at depth → rise → cool across the melting curve (crystallize) → later uplift to the surface.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Losing heat</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Magma cools by <mark class=\"pilot-term\">conduction</mark> into wall rocks (which may be metamorphosed or melted), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">radiation and conduction</mark> at the surface, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">releasing gases</mark>, and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">convection of groundwater</mark> heated by the magma (possibly the main cooling path for some magmas).</li><li>Solidification starts at different temperatures<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Mafic &gt; 1,100 °C</b>, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">intermediate ~950 °C</mark>, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">felsic ~800 °C</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>A rising magma's path on a temperature-depth graph<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Melt at depth → rise → cool across the melting curve (crystallize) → later uplift to the surface.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g5-6b":{"source":"<p>Minerals crystallize in the <b>reverse</b> of the order they melt: <b>mafic first, felsic last</b>.</p><ul><li><b>Discontinuous side</b>: olivine → pyroxene → amphibole → biotite.</li><li><b>Continuous side</b>: plagioclase from <b>Ca-rich</b> (high T) to <b>Na-rich</b> (low T).</li><li><b>Last</b>: K-feldspar, muscovite, quartz.</li></ul><p>Consequences: early crystals grow unimpeded and have good shapes; late crystals fill the gaps (irregular). Removing early mafic crystals leaves the melt <b>less mafic</b> (MgO falls, SiO₂ rises). Felsic magmas lack the Mg to grow olivine; mafic magmas lack the Si to grow much quartz.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bowen's reaction series</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Minerals crystallize in the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">reverse</mark> of the order they melt<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Mafic first, felsic last</b>.</li></ul></li></ul><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Discontinuous side</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Olivine → pyroxene → amphibole → biotite.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Continuous side</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Plagioclase from <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Ca-rich</mark> (high T) to <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Na-rich</mark> (low T).</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Last</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>K-feldspar, muscovite, quartz.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Consequences<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Early crystals grow unimpeded and have good shapes.</li><li>Late crystals fill the gaps (irregular).</li></ul></li><li>Removing early mafic crystals leaves the melt <mark class=\"pilot-term\">less mafic</mark> (MgO falls, SiO₂ rises).</li><li>Felsic magmas lack the Mg to grow olivine.</li><li>Mafic magmas lack the Si to grow much quartz.</li></ul></section>"},"g5-7a":{"source":"<p class=\"small muted\">Sep 24 review explicitly covers melting mechanisms and their tectonic settings. Additional details in this section are broader textbook study.</p><p>Plates separate; solid asthenosphere rises; pressure drops → <b>decompression melting</b> → mafic magma. Oceanic crust, top to bottom: <b>pillow basalt</b> (lava erupted into water) → <b>sheeted dikes</b> (parallel to the rift, perpendicular to spreading) → <b>gabbro</b> (solidified chambers, sometimes layered by crystal settling) → ultramafic mantle that was partially melted. Slices of this sequence exposed on land are called <b>ophiolites</b>.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Mid-ocean ridges: ~60% of Earth's magma</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sep 24 review explicitly covers melting mechanisms and their tectonic settings.</li><li>Additional details in this section are broader textbook study.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Plates separate.</li><li>Solid asthenosphere rises.</li><li>Pressure drops → <mark class=\"pilot-term\">decompression melting</mark> → mafic magma.</li><li>Oceanic crust, top to bottom<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Pillow basalt</b> (lava erupted into water) → <mark class=\"pilot-term\">sheeted dikes</mark> (parallel to the rift, perpendicular to spreading) → <mark class=\"pilot-term\">gabbro</mark> (solidified chambers, sometimes layered by crystal settling) → ultramafic mantle that was partially melted.</li></ul></li><li>Slices of this sequence exposed on land are called <mark class=\"pilot-term\">ophiolites</mark>.</li></ul></section>"},"g5-7b":{"source":"<p class=\"small muted\">Sep 24 review explicitly covers melting mechanisms and their tectonic settings. Additional details in this section are broader textbook study.</p><p>The sinking slab heats up; water-bearing minerals (such as mica) break down; water rises into the overlying mantle and <b>lowers its melting temperature</b> → mafic magma. Rising through thick continental crust, magma heats and partly melts it, producing <b>intermediate or felsic</b> magma. Most subduction magma never erupts; what does erupts is mostly <b>andesite</b>, in <b>island arcs</b> (ocean-ocean) or <b>mountain-belt volcanoes</b> (ocean-continent).</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Subduction zones: ~one-fifth of Earth's magma</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sep 24 review explicitly covers melting mechanisms and their tectonic settings.</li><li>Additional details in this section are broader textbook study.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The sinking slab heats up.</li><li>Water-bearing minerals (such as mica) break down.</li><li>Water rises into the overlying mantle and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">lowers its melting temperature</mark> → mafic magma.</li><li>Rising through thick continental crust, magma heats and partly melts it, producing <mark class=\"pilot-term\">intermediate or felsic</mark> magma.</li><li>Most subduction magma never erupts.</li><li>What does erupts is mostly <mark class=\"pilot-term\">andesite</mark>, in <mark class=\"pilot-term\">island arcs</mark> (ocean-ocean) or <mark class=\"pilot-term\">mountain-belt volcanoes</mark> (ocean-continent).</li></ul></section>"},"g5-7c":{"source":"<p class=\"small muted\">Sep 24 review explicitly covers melting mechanisms and their tectonic settings. Additional details in this section are broader textbook study.</p><p>Most hot spots are the surface expression of a <b>mantle plume</b>: hot, less dense <b>solid</b> mantle rising like a lava-lamp blob (some may start at the core-mantle boundary). In oceans, plume melting plus decompression builds large volcanoes (Hawaii); a plate moving over a fixed plume makes a <b>chain of islands and seamounts</b>. In continents, plume heat makes mafic magma in the mantle and can melt crust to make <b>felsic</b> magma; the two may mix. Example: the <b>White Mountain batholith</b> (New Hampshire), interpreted as hot-spot related.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Hot spots</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sep 24 review explicitly covers melting mechanisms and their tectonic settings.</li><li>Additional details in this section are broader textbook study.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Most hot spots are the surface expression of a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">mantle plume</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Hot, less dense <mark class=\"pilot-term\">solid</mark> mantle rising like a lava-lamp blob (some may start at the core-mantle boundary).</li></ul></li><li>In oceans, plume melting plus decompression builds large volcanoes (Hawaii).</li><li>A plate moving over a fixed plume makes a <b>chain of islands and seamounts</b>.</li><li>In continents, plume heat makes mafic magma in the mantle and can melt crust to make <mark class=\"pilot-term\">felsic</mark> magma.</li><li>The two may mix.</li><li>Example<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The <mark class=\"pilot-term\">White Mountain batholith</mark> (New Hampshire), interpreted as hot-spot related.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"cx1-g-ridge-cooling":{"source":"<ul><li>Magma retained at depth beneath a ridge loses heat more slowly than lava erupted into cold seawater. Slower cooling allows larger crystals; rapid cooling produces finer material.</li><li>This explains the contrast between coarse intrusive gabbro below and fine-grained basalt above without requiring different original magma compositions.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Depth controls cooling at a ridge</h4><ul><li>Magma retained at depth beneath a ridge loses heat more slowly than lava erupted into cold seawater.</li><li>Slower cooling allows larger crystals.</li><li>Rapid cooling produces finer material.</li><li>This explains the contrast between coarse intrusive gabbro below and fine-grained basalt above without requiring different original magma compositions.</li></ul></section>"},"g5-8a":{"source":"<p class=\"small muted\">Sep 24 review explicitly covers melting mechanisms and their tectonic settings. Additional details in this section are broader textbook study.</p><ul><li><b>Extrusive / volcanic</b>: solidified on the surface (lava or ash).</li><li><b>Intrusive</b>: solidified below the surface. <b>Plutonic</b> ≈ intrusive at considerable depth (more than several km).</li><li>A solidified magma chamber is a <b>pluton</b>; a smaller one with steep sides is a <b>stock</b>; one or more contiguous plutons covering a huge region is a <b>batholith</b> (e.g., the <b>Sierra Nevada batholith</b>, gray granite solidified at depth then uplifted). Most batholiths form from many magma pulses over a long time.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Where the rock solidified</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sep 24 review explicitly covers melting mechanisms and their tectonic settings.</li><li>Additional details in this section are broader textbook study.</li></ul><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Extrusive / volcanic</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Solidified on the surface (lava or ash).</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Intrusive</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Solidified below the surface.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Plutonic</mark> ≈ intrusive at considerable depth (more than several km).</li><li>A solidified magma chamber is a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">pluton</mark>.</li><li>A smaller one with steep sides is a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">stock</mark>.</li><li>One or more contiguous plutons covering a huge region is a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">batholith</mark> (e.g., the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Sierra Nevada batholith</mark>, gray granite solidified at depth then uplifted).</li><li>Most batholiths form from many magma pulses over a long time.</li></ul></section>"},"g5-8b":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Geometry</th></tr><tr><td><b>Dike</b></td><td>Sheet that <b>cuts across</b> layers; usually steep</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sill</b></td><td>Sheet <b>parallel</b> to layers; usually subhorizontal; lifts rocks above it</td></tr><tr><td><b>Laccolith</b></td><td>Sill that inflates into a lump, <b>doming</b> the layers above</td></tr><tr><td><b>Volcanic neck</b></td><td>Resistant solidified conduit left after erosion (Shiprock, NM: breccia, formed beneath an explosion crater)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Columnar joints</b></td><td>Five- or six-sided columns from contraction during cooling; <b>perpendicular</b> to the cooling unit (vertical in a flat flow or sill, horizontal in a vertical dike)</td></tr></table>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Small intrusions: read the geometry</h4><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Geometry</th></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Dike</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Sheet that <mark class=\"pilot-term\">cuts across</mark> layers.</li><li>Usually steep.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Sill</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Sheet <mark class=\"pilot-term\">parallel</mark> to layers.</li><li>Usually subhorizontal.</li><li>Lifts rocks above it.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Laccolith</mark></td><td>Sill that inflates into a lump, <mark class=\"pilot-term\">doming</mark> the layers above</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Volcanic neck</mark></td><td>Resistant solidified conduit left after erosion (Shiprock, NM: breccia, formed beneath an explosion crater)</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Columnar joints</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Five- or six-sided columns from contraction during cooling.</li><li><b>Perpendicular</b> to the cooling unit (vertical in a flat flow or sill, horizontal in a vertical dike).</li></ul></td></tr></table></section>"},"g6-1a":{"source":"<p class=\"small muted\">Textbook source. Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity; other details here remain broader course material.</p><p>A <b>volcano</b> is a <b>vent</b> where magma and other volcanic products erupt onto the surface. Most have a <b>crater</b>; magma also erupts from <b>fissures</b> (linear cracks) and <b>calderas</b> (huge circular depressions). Clues of recent activity: fresh ash on slopes, unweathered flows without developed soil.</p><p><b>Not every hill of volcanic rock is a volcano.</b> A <b>mesa</b> capped by a lava flow is an eroded remnant of a once-extensive flow; it did not form over a vent. The old fissure may show up as a <b>dike</b> nearby.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">A volcano is a vent</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Textbook source.</li><li>Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity.</li><li>Other details here remain broader course material.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <mark class=\"pilot-term\">volcano</mark> is a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">vent</mark> where magma and other volcanic products erupt onto the surface.</li><li>Most have a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">crater</mark>.</li><li>Magma also erupts from <mark class=\"pilot-term\">fissures</mark> (linear cracks) and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">calderas</mark> (huge circular depressions).</li><li>Clues of recent activity<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Fresh ash on slopes, unweathered flows without developed soil.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Not every hill of volcanic rock is a volcano.</b></li><li>A <mark class=\"pilot-term\">mesa</mark> capped by a lava flow is an eroded remnant of a once-extensive flow.</li><li>It did not form over a vent.</li><li>The old fissure may show up as a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">dike</mark> nearby.</li></ul></section>"},"g6-1b":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Shape and size</th><th>Made of</th><th>Built by</th></tr><tr><td><b>Scoria (cinder) cone</b></td><td>Small cone, summit crater; usually ≤ 300 m high</td><td>Loose basaltic (less often andesitic) scoria, bombs</td><td>One episode, months to years</td></tr><tr><td><b>Shield volcano</b></td><td>Broad, gentle slopes; can be tens of km wide (largest type)</td><td>Mostly basaltic lava flows</td><td>Many eruptions</td></tr><tr><td><b>Composite volcano</b> (stratovolcano)</td><td>Symmetrical, moderately steep, thousands of m high</td><td>Interlayered lava, pyroclastic deposits, mudflows; mostly andesite</td><td>Many eruptions over a long time</td></tr><tr><td><b>Volcanic dome</b></td><td>Dome, up to hundreds of m high</td><td>Viscous felsic or intermediate lava piled over the vent</td><td>Usually one episode</td></tr></table><p>Smallest: scoria cones and domes. Largest: shields, then composites.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Four volcano types that build hills and mountains</h4><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Shape and size</th><th>Made of</th><th>Built by</th></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Scoria (cinder) cone</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Small cone, summit crater.</li><li>Usually ≤ 300 m high.</li></ul></td><td>Loose basaltic (less often andesitic) scoria, bombs</td><td>One episode, months to years</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Shield volcano</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Broad, gentle slopes.</li><li>Can be tens of km wide (largest type).</li></ul></td><td>Mostly basaltic lava flows</td><td>Many eruptions</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Composite volcano</mark> (stratovolcano)</td><td>Symmetrical, moderately steep, thousands of m high</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Interlayered lava, pyroclastic deposits, mudflows.</li><li>Mostly andesite.</li></ul></td><td>Many eruptions over a long time</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Volcanic dome</mark></td><td>Dome, up to hundreds of m high</td><td>Viscous felsic or intermediate lava piled over the vent</td><td>Usually one episode</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Smallest<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Scoria cones and domes.</li></ul></li><li>Largest<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Shields, then composites.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"exam1-guide-T14":{"source":"<p><b>Viscosity</b> is resistance to flow. More silica and lower temperature generally increase viscosity. Fluid basalt spreads to build broad <b>shield volcanoes</b>. More viscous magma and alternating eruption products build steeper <b>composite volcanoes</b> such as Mount Fuji and Mount St. Helens.</p><p><b>Cinder/scoria cones</b> are small, steep accumulations of loose fragments around a vent; their steep slopes do not mean the magma must be felsic. Gas escape, composition, temperature, and crystals all influence eruption behavior.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Exam 1 focus: Viscosity and volcano shape</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Viscosity</mark> is resistance to flow.</li><li>More silica and lower temperature generally increase viscosity.</li><li>Fluid basalt spreads to build broad <mark class=\"pilot-term\">shield volcanoes</mark>.</li><li>More viscous magma and alternating eruption products build steeper <mark class=\"pilot-term\">composite volcanoes</mark> such as Mount Fuji and Mount St. Helens.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Cinder/scoria cones</mark> are small, steep accumulations of loose fragments around a vent.</li><li>Their steep slopes do not mean the magma must be felsic.</li><li>Gas escape, composition, temperature, and crystals all influence eruption behavior.</li></ul></section>"},"g6-2a":{"source":"<p class=\"small muted\">Textbook source. Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity; other details here remain broader course material.</p><ul><li><b>Lava flow</b>: magma flows away from the vent.</li><li><b>Lava dome</b>: highly viscous (silica-rich) lava piles up around the vent.</li><li><b>Lava fountain</b>: gas-rich, low-viscosity (basaltic) lava sprayed into the air.</li><li><b>Tephra</b>: ash, pumice, and fragments thrown into the air; tephra sand-sized or smaller is <b>volcanic ash</b>.</li></ul><p><b>Eruption column</b> vs. <b>pyroclastic flow</b>: the difference is mainly <b>gas content</b>. Lots of gas carries tephra high into the atmosphere; less gas cannot support the column, which collapses into a hot, dense cloud that races downhill at <b>&gt; 100 km/h</b> and <b>&gt; 500 °C</b>.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Ways magma comes out</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Textbook source.</li><li>Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity.</li><li>Other details here remain broader course material.</li></ul><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Lava flow</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Magma flows away from the vent.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Lava dome</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Highly viscous (silica-rich) lava piles up around the vent.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Lava fountain</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Gas-rich, low-viscosity (basaltic) lava sprayed into the air.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Tephra</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Ash, pumice, and fragments thrown into the air.</li><li>Tephra sand-sized or smaller is <mark class=\"pilot-term\">volcanic ash</mark>.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Eruption column</mark> vs. <mark class=\"pilot-term\">pyroclastic flow</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The difference is mainly <mark class=\"pilot-term\">gas content</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>Lots of gas carries tephra high into the atmosphere.</li><li>Less gas cannot support the column, which collapses into a hot, dense cloud that races downhill at <mark class=\"pilot-term\">&gt; 100 km/h</mark> and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">&gt; 500 °C</mark>.</li></ul></section>"},"g6-2b":{"source":"<p>Magma holds dissolved H₂O, CO₂, and SO₂. Like opening a soda, dropping pressure near the surface lets bubbles form. In <b>viscous felsic</b> magma, bubbles cannot escape; gas pressure builds and the eruption is <b>explosive</b>. In <b>fluid basaltic</b> magma, bubbles escape easily; eruptions are <b>fairly nonexplosive</b> (lava fountains are small, local explosions). The textbook's chain: <b>composition → viscosity → gas trapped or released → eruption style → volcano shape, rock types, hazards</b>.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Gas + viscosity = explosiveness</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Magma holds dissolved H₂O, CO₂, and SO₂.</li><li>Like opening a soda, dropping pressure near the surface lets bubbles form.</li><li>In <mark class=\"pilot-term\">viscous felsic</mark> magma, bubbles cannot escape.</li><li>Gas pressure builds and the eruption is <mark class=\"pilot-term\">explosive</mark>.</li><li>In <mark class=\"pilot-term\">fluid basaltic</mark> magma, bubbles escape easily.</li><li>Eruptions are <mark class=\"pilot-term\">fairly nonexplosive</mark> (lava fountains are small, local explosions).</li><li>The textbook's chain<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Composition → viscosity → gas trapped or released → eruption style → volcano shape, rock types, hazards</b>.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g6-3a":{"source":"<p class=\"small muted\">Textbook source. Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity; other details here remain broader course material.</p><p>Early: gas-rich magma makes a <b>lava fountain</b>; falling clots build a <b>scoria cone</b> (months to years, ≤ ~300 m). Later: degassed magma pours out as <b>lava flows</b>, often from the base of the cone.</p><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it is</th></tr><tr><td>Vesicular basalt</td><td>Trapped gas bubbles</td></tr><tr><td>Nonvesicular basalt</td><td>Magma low in gas (never had it or lost it)</td></tr><tr><td>Volcanic bombs</td><td>Large blobs or blocks ejected</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lava tube</b></td><td>Solid roof insulates flowing lava, which travels farther; drained tube = cave</td></tr><tr><td><b>Aa</b></td><td>Rough, jagged, broken-up surface</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pahoehoe</b></td><td>Smooth, ropy surface; grows as tongues, often tube-fed</td></tr></table>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">A basaltic eruption, start to finish</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Textbook source.</li><li>Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity.</li><li>Other details here remain broader course material.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Early<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Gas-rich magma makes a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">lava fountain</mark>.</li><li>Falling clots build a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">scoria cone</mark> (months to years, ≤ ~300 m).</li></ul></li><li>Later<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Degassed magma pours out as <mark class=\"pilot-term\">lava flows</mark>, often from the base of the cone.</li></ul></li></ul><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it is</th></tr><tr><td>Vesicular basalt</td><td>Trapped gas bubbles</td></tr><tr><td>Nonvesicular basalt</td><td>Magma low in gas (never had it or lost it)</td></tr><tr><td>Volcanic bombs</td><td>Large blobs or blocks ejected</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Lava tube</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Solid roof insulates flowing lava, which travels farther.</li><li>Drained tube = cave.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Aa</mark></td><td>Rough, jagged, broken-up surface</td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Pahoehoe</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Smooth, ropy surface.</li><li>Grows as tongues, often tube-fed.</li></ul></td></tr></table></section>"},"g6-3b":{"source":"<p>Built by many low-viscosity basaltic flows, so slopes are <b>gentle</b>. The largest form over <b>hot spots</b> on oceanic plates. <b>Mauna Loa</b> is the world's largest volcano (about 9,000 m above the seafloor). <b>Kilauea</b> is probably the most active; its summit <b>caldera</b> formed when magma withdrew and the ground collapsed. Eruptions come from <b>fissures</b> fed by sheetlike magma bodies. Lava entering the sea adds new land; lava erupted into water forms <b>pillows</b>.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Shield volcanoes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Built by many low-viscosity basaltic flows, so slopes are <mark class=\"pilot-term\">gentle</mark>.</li><li>The largest form over <mark class=\"pilot-term\">hot spots</mark> on oceanic plates.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Mauna Loa</mark> is the world's largest volcano (about 9,000 m above the seafloor).</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Kilauea</mark> is probably the most active.</li><li>Its summit <mark class=\"pilot-term\">caldera</mark> formed when magma withdrew and the ground collapsed.</li><li>Eruptions come from <mark class=\"pilot-term\">fissures</mark> fed by sheetlike magma bodies.</li><li>Lava entering the sea adds new land.</li><li>Lava erupted into water forms <mark class=\"pilot-term\">pillows</mark>.</li></ul></section>"},"g6-3c":{"source":"<p>Vast, commonly km-thick stacks of basalt flows fed by <b>long fissures</b>; single flows can exceed 1,000 km³. Most probably begin when a <b>mantle plume</b> first reaches the lithosphere.</p><ul><li><b>Columbia Plateau</b> (WA, OR, ID): 300+ flows, 17–5 Ma; linked to the hot spot now under Yellowstone.</li><li><b>Siberian Traps</b>: 252 Ma, same time as a major extinction.</li><li><b>Deccan Traps</b> (India): about 65–66 Ma, same time as the dinosaur extinction.</li></ul><p>Climate effects: SO₂ aerosols reflect sunlight (cooling); CO₂ warms. Which dominates is unclear.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Flood basalts: the largest eruptions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Vast, commonly km-thick stacks of basalt flows fed by <mark class=\"pilot-term\">long fissures</mark>.</li><li>Single flows can exceed 1,000 km³.</li><li>Most probably begin when a <mark class=\"pilot-term\">mantle plume</mark> first reaches the lithosphere.</li></ul><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Columbia Plateau</mark> (WA, OR, ID)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>300+ Flows, 17–5 Ma.</li><li>Linked to the hot spot now under Yellowstone.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Siberian Traps</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>252 Ma, same time as a major extinction.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Deccan Traps</mark> (India)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>About 65–66 Ma, same time as the dinosaur extinction.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Climate effects<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>SO₂ aerosols reflect sunlight (cooling).</li><li>CO₂ warms.</li></ul></li><li>Which dominates is unclear.</li></ul></section>"},"g6-4a":{"source":"<p class=\"small muted\">Textbook source. Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity; other details here remain broader course material.</p><p>A <b>hazard</b> is a potentially dangerous situation or event (a lava flow that could erupt). <b>Risk</b> is an assessment of whether that hazard could affect society: lives, property, jobs, crops, climate. The same lava flow is a high risk for a subdivision in its path (Royal Gardens, Kilauea) and almost no risk in an uninhabited desert.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Hazard is the danger; risk is the consequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Textbook source.</li><li>Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity.</li><li>Other details here remain broader course material.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <mark class=\"pilot-term\">hazard</mark> is a potentially dangerous situation or event (a lava flow that could erupt).</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Risk</mark> is an assessment of whether that hazard could affect society<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Lives, property, jobs, crops, climate.</li></ul></li><li>The same lava flow is a high risk for a subdivision in its path (Royal Gardens, Kilauea) and almost no risk in an uninhabited desert.</li></ul></section>"},"g6-4b":{"source":"<ul><li><b>Falling objects</b>: scoria and <b>bombs</b> near the vent.</li><li><b>Ash</b>: buries structures, breathing problems; Eyjafjallajökull (Iceland, 2010) shut down European air travel.</li><li><b>Gases</b>: CO₂ asphyxiates; H₂S paralyzes; SO₂, HCl, and fluorine compounds kill crops and livestock and poison water.</li><li><b>Lava flows</b>: usually slow enough to walk away from, but burn and bury everything in their path.</li><li><b>Floods</b>: eruptions under ice release catastrophic meltwater floods (<b>jökulhlaup</b>, Iceland 1996).</li><li><b>Climate</b>: Laki (Iceland, 1783), the largest recorded eruption (16 km³), released SO₂; aerosols dimmed sunlight, followed by crop failure and famine.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What basaltic eruptions do</h4><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Falling objects</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Scoria and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">bombs</mark> near the vent.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Ash</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Buries structures, breathing problems.</li><li>Eyjafjallajökull (Iceland, 2010) shut down European air travel.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Gases</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>CO₂ asphyxiates.</li><li>H₂S paralyzes.</li><li>SO₂, HCl, and fluorine compounds kill crops and livestock and poison water.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Lava flows</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Usually slow enough to walk away from, but burn and bury everything in their path.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Floods</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Eruptions under ice release catastrophic meltwater floods (<mark class=\"pilot-term\">jökulhlaup</mark>, Iceland 1996).</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Climate</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Laki (Iceland, 1783), the largest recorded eruption (16 km³), released SO₂.</li><li>Aerosols dimmed sunlight, followed by crop failure and famine.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g6-5a":{"source":"<p class=\"small muted\">Textbook source. Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity; other details here remain broader course material.</p><p>Also called <b>stratovolcanoes</b>; common above <b>subduction zones</b> (Ring of Fire). Built over long periods from <b>interlayered</b> lava flows, pyroclastic flows, tephra falls, and mudflows, cut by dikes and sills. Most common rock: <b>andesite</b>. Hazards: tall eruption columns (ash drifts hundreds of km), <b>pyroclastic flows</b> (most violent), and <b>lahars</b>, volcanic mudflows made when rain or snowmelt mixes with loose ash and rock on steep slopes.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Composite volcanoes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Textbook source.</li><li>Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity.</li><li>Other details here remain broader course material.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Also called <mark class=\"pilot-term\">stratovolcanoes</mark>.</li><li>Common above <mark class=\"pilot-term\">subduction zones</mark> (Ring of Fire).</li><li>Built over long periods from <mark class=\"pilot-term\">interlayered</mark> lava flows, pyroclastic flows, tephra falls, and mudflows, cut by dikes and sills.</li><li>Most common rock<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Andesite</b>.</li></ul></li><li>Hazards<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Tall eruption columns (ash drifts hundreds of km), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">pyroclastic flows</mark> (most violent), and <mark class=\"pilot-term\">lahars</mark>, volcanic mudflows made when rain or snowmelt mixes with loose ash and rock on steep slopes.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g6-5b":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Event</th><th>What happened</th></tr><tr><td><b>Vesuvius, AD 79</b></td><td>Earthquakes and an eruption column dropped tephra on Pompeii, then six pyroclastic flows (three hit Pompeii); ~20,000 (Pompeii) and ~5,000 (Herculaneum) died. Naples (4+ million) sits in the zone today.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Mount Pelée, 1902</b> (Martinique)</td><td>Warning signs ignored; a pyroclastic flow at ~500 km/h hit St. Pierre; almost all ~30,000 residents died, mostly by breathing hot gas and ash.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Mount St. Helens, 1980</b></td><td>A <b>bulge</b> grew on the north flank from rising magma; an earthquake triggered a massive <b>landslide</b> of the bulge, dropping the pressure on the magma → <b>lateral blast</b> and an eruption column.</td></tr></table>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Three composite-volcano disasters</h4><table><tr><th>Event</th><th>What happened</th></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Vesuvius, AD 79</mark></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Earthquakes and an eruption column dropped tephra on Pompeii, then six pyroclastic flows (three hit Pompeii).</li><li>~20,000 (Pompeii) and ~5,000 (Herculaneum) died.</li><li>Naples (4+ million) sits in the zone today.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Mount Pelée, 1902</mark> (Martinique)</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Warning signs ignored.</li><li>A pyroclastic flow at ~500 km/h hit St. Pierre.</li><li>Almost all ~30,000 residents died, mostly by breathing hot gas and ash.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><b>Mount St. Helens, 1980</b></td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A <mark class=\"pilot-term\">bulge</mark> grew on the north flank from rising magma.</li><li>An earthquake triggered a massive <mark class=\"pilot-term\">landslide</mark> of the bulge, dropping the pressure on the magma → <mark class=\"pilot-term\">lateral blast</mark> and an eruption column.</li></ul></td></tr></table></section>"},"g6-5c":{"source":"<p>Viscous andesite to rhyolite piles up over the vent; surfaces are rubble of angular blocks. Domes <b>grow</b> from inside (inflation) or by thick lava breaking out. Domes are <b>destroyed</b> by <b>collapse</b> of steep flanks (small pyroclastic flows) or by <b>explosion</b> when solidified magma plugs the conduit and trapped gas builds up. <b>Mount Unzen</b> (Japan): a dome-collapse pyroclastic flow killed 43 journalists and volcanologists. Dome rocks include <b>obsidian</b> with flow bands.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Volcanic domes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Viscous andesite to rhyolite piles up over the vent.</li><li>Surfaces are rubble of angular blocks.</li><li>Domes <mark class=\"pilot-term\">grow</mark> from inside (inflation) or by thick lava breaking out.</li><li>Domes are <mark class=\"pilot-term\">destroyed</mark> by <mark class=\"pilot-term\">collapse</mark> of steep flanks (small pyroclastic flows) or by <mark class=\"pilot-term\">explosion</mark> when solidified magma plugs the conduit and trapped gas builds up.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Mount Unzen</mark> (Japan)<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A dome-collapse pyroclastic flow killed 43 journalists and volcanologists.</li></ul></li><li>Dome rocks include <mark class=\"pilot-term\">obsidian</mark> with flow bands.</li></ul></section>"},"g6-6a":{"source":"<p class=\"small muted\">Textbook source. Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity; other details here remain broader course material.</p><p>A <b>caldera</b> is a large basin-shaped volcanic depression with steep walls. Formation happens <b>at the same time</b> as the eruption:</p><ol><li><b>Felsic magma</b>, mostly from crustal melting, accumulates in a shallow chamber.</li><li>Eruptions begin; the chamber empties and its <b>roof subsides</b> along curved fractures.</li><li>Eruption columns and pyroclastic flows; much tephra falls back in, making thick <b>tuff</b>; wall landslides add blocks.</li><li>Late magma builds <b>domes</b> inside; sediment and lakes may fill the basin.</li></ol><p>Example: <b>Valles Caldera</b> (NM), ~22 km across, ~2 million years old.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Collapse while erupting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Textbook source.</li><li>Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity.</li><li>Other details here remain broader course material.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <mark class=\"pilot-term\">caldera</mark> is a large basin-shaped volcanic depression with steep walls.</li><li>Formation happens <mark class=\"pilot-term\">at the same time</mark> as the eruption:.</li></ul><ol><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Felsic magma</mark>, mostly from crustal melting, accumulates in a shallow chamber.</li><li>Eruptions begin.</li><li>The chamber empties and its <mark class=\"pilot-term\">roof subsides</mark> along curved fractures.</li><li>Eruption columns and pyroclastic flows.</li><li>Much tephra falls back in, making thick <mark class=\"pilot-term\">tuff</mark>.</li><li>Wall landslides add blocks.</li><li>Late magma builds <mark class=\"pilot-term\">domes</mark> inside.</li><li>Sediment and lakes may fill the basin.</li></ol><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Example<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Valles Caldera</mark> (NM), ~22 km across, ~2 million years old.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g6-6b":{"source":"<ul><li><b>Crater Lake</b> (Mount Mazama), ~7,700 years ago; eruption > 50× the 1980 St. Helens eruption; Wizard Island is a later scoria cone.</li><li><b>Santorini (Thera)</b>, ~3,500 years ago; collapse probably generated a destructive wave toward Minoan Crete.</li><li><b>Krakatau, 1883</b>: caldera collapse, pyroclastic flows, huge waves; almost 40,000 deaths; loudest recorded sound. Anak Krakatau grew from 1927.</li><li><b>Yellowstone</b>: three huge caldera eruptions in the last 2.1 million years; youngest caldera 640,000 years ago; ash from northern Mexico to southern Canada.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Caldera disasters and Yellowstone</h4><ul><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Crater Lake</mark> (Mount Mazama), ~7,700 years ago.</li><li>Eruption &gt; 50× the 1980 St. Helens eruption.</li><li>Wizard Island is a later scoria cone.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Santorini (Thera)</mark>, ~3,500 years ago.</li><li>Collapse probably generated a destructive wave toward Minoan Crete.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Krakatau, 1883</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Caldera collapse, pyroclastic flows, huge waves.</li><li>Almost 40,000 deaths.</li><li>Loudest recorded sound.</li></ul></li><li>Anak Krakatau grew from 1927.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Yellowstone</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Three huge caldera eruptions in the last 2.1 million years.</li><li>Youngest caldera 640,000 years ago.</li><li>Ash from northern Mexico to southern Canada.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g6-7a":{"source":"<p class=\"small muted\">Textbook source. Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity; other details here remain broader course material.</p><p><b>What type of volcano?</b> Shape (steep = viscous, explosive, landslide-prone; gentle = basaltic), rock type (welded tuff, rhyolite, andesite = more dangerous than basalt), age and history (fresh shape, dated layers, oral histories).</p><p><b>Where is the risk highest?</b></p><ol><li><b>Proximity</b>: the biggest factor.</li><li><b>Valleys</b>: channel lava, small pyroclastic flows, and mudflows; ridges are safer.</li><li><b>Wind direction</b>: ash and pumice go downwind (prevailing wind).</li><li><b>Particulars</b>: steep sides, a dome that could collapse.</li></ol><p><b>Armero, 1985</b>: an eruption of Nevado del Ruiz (Colombia) melted snow and ice; a mudflow down a valley killed more than 20,000 people in a town that geologists had warned about.</p><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"hazard\">Practice village-risk maps (5)</button>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Assessing danger</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Textbook source.</li><li>Sep 24 review emphasizes volcano types, examples, and viscosity.</li><li>Other details here remain broader course material.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>What type of volcano?</b></li><li>Shape (steep = viscous, explosive, landslide-prone; gentle = basaltic), rock type (welded tuff, rhyolite, andesite = more dangerous than basalt), age and history (fresh shape, dated layers, oral histories).</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Where is the risk highest?</b></li></ul><ol><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Proximity</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The biggest factor.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Valleys</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Channel lava, small pyroclastic flows, and mudflows.</li><li>Ridges are safer.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Wind direction</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Ash and pumice go downwind (prevailing wind).</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Particulars</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Steep sides, a dome that could collapse.</li></ul></li></ol><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Armero, 1985</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>An eruption of Nevado del Ruiz (Colombia) melted snow and ice.</li><li>A mudflow down a valley killed more than 20,000 people in a town that geologists had warned about.</li></ul></li></ul><button class=\"btn small\" data-guidegen=\"hazard\">Practice village-risk maps (5)</button></section>"},"g6-7b":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Instrument</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr><tr><td>Ground shaking</td><td>Seismometers</td><td>Moving magma breaks rock; rhythmic tremor can precede eruptions (Pinatubo 1991)</td></tr><tr><td>Gas output</td><td>SO₂ measurements (ground, aircraft)</td><td>Rising, degassing magma; SO₂ rose sharply days before Pinatubo</td></tr><tr><td>Heat flow</td><td>Thermal instruments, satellites</td><td>Magma warming the volcano</td></tr><tr><td>Ground deformation</td><td>GPS, tiltmeters, precise surveying, satellite radar</td><td>Magma inflating the mountain</td></tr></table>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Monitoring</h4><table><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Instrument</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr><tr><td>Ground shaking</td><td>Seismometers</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Moving magma breaks rock.</li><li>Rhythmic tremor can precede eruptions (Pinatubo 1991).</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>Gas output</td><td>SO₂ measurements (ground, aircraft)</td><td><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Rising, degassing magma.</li><li>SO₂ rose sharply days before Pinatubo.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>Heat flow</td><td>Thermal instruments, satellites</td><td>Magma warming the volcano</td></tr><tr><td>Ground deformation</td><td>GPS, tiltmeters, precise surveying, satellite radar</td><td>Magma inflating the mountain</td></tr></table></section>"},"g6-7c":{"source":"<p>An <b>andesitic composite volcano</b> above the <b>Cascadia subduction zone</b> (Juan de Fuca plate subducting eastward beneath North America: ocean-continent). At least <b>11</b> significant pyroclastic eruptions in 10,000 years; most recent <b>1820</b>. The USGS considers its <b>mudflows the greatest volcanic hazard in the Cascades</b>: melted ice cap or big landslides send slurries down valleys toward Tacoma's suburbs, some built on a mudflow only about 600 years old. Ash would probably go east with prevailing winds.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Mount Rainier</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An <mark class=\"pilot-term\">andesitic composite volcano</mark> above the <mark class=\"pilot-term\">Cascadia subduction zone</mark> (Juan de Fuca plate subducting eastward beneath North America: ocean-continent).</li><li>At least <mark class=\"pilot-term\">11</mark> significant pyroclastic eruptions in 10,000 years.</li><li>Most recent <mark class=\"pilot-term\">1820</mark>. The USGS considers its <b>mudflows the greatest volcanic hazard in the Cascades</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Melted ice cap or big landslides send slurries down valleys toward Tacoma's suburbs, some built on a mudflow only about 600 years old.</li></ul></li><li>Ash would probably go east with prevailing winds.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-1a":{"source":"<p>Begin with the setting, then ask what transports sediment and where it loses energy. Steep mountain streams can move gravel and boulders. A stream can split and rejoin into braided channels; on gentler terrain, meandering channels flood adjacent flat land. <b>Fluvial</b> means stream or river processes. Floodplains accumulate mud and sand during floods.</p><p>A <b>delta</b> grows where a stream enters standing water and slows. Wind selectively moves sand and finer particles; dunes commonly contain well-sorted sand. Glaciers carry a wide size range and release sediment as they melt. Lakes combine active shorelines with quieter deeper water. Wetlands preserve plant material where saturation limits oxidation.</p><p>Use several clues together: grain size, sorting, sedimentary structures, and plant or shell remains. A single sandstone does not uniquely identify a desert. Compare modern environments with the original overview before interpreting an ancient rock.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Streams and floodplains</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Begin with the setting, then ask what transports sediment and where it loses energy.</li><li>Steep mountain streams can move gravel and boulders.</li><li>A stream can split and rejoin into braided channels.</li><li>On gentler terrain, meandering channels flood adjacent flat land.</li><li><b>Fluvial</b> means stream or river processes.</li><li>Floodplains accumulate mud and sand during floods.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Other land environments</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <b>delta</b> grows where a stream enters standing water and slows.</li><li>Wind selectively moves sand and finer particles.</li><li>Dunes commonly contain well-sorted sand.</li><li>Glaciers carry a wide size range and release sediment as they melt.</li><li>Lakes combine active shorelines with quieter deeper water.</li><li>Wetlands preserve plant material where saturation limits oxidation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Clues for identifying an environment</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Use several clues together<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Grain size, sorting, sedimentary structures, and plant or shell remains.</li></ul></li><li>A single sandstone does not uniquely identify a desert.</li><li>Compare modern environments with the original overview before interpreting an ancient rock.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-intro":{"source":"<p>The chapter introduction follows the <b>Fraser River</b> through southwestern British Columbia. Mountain streams, lakes, glaciers, and tributaries supply sediment to the river. Its branching and rejoining channels illustrate a braided river. At the Strait of Georgia, slowing flow disperses sediment and builds a delta near Vancouver.</p><p>Follow one grain through this system: weathering produces it, erosion removes it, a stream transports it, and decreasing flow allows deposition. Coarse and fine material respond differently. The delta can grow seaward as material accumulates. An ancient deposit must be interpreted from textures and structures, because the modern river and coastline may no longer exist.</p><p>Vancouver also illustrates the human importance of sediment. Broad sediment-covered plains provide land for building and agriculture; pore spaces hold groundwater. This supplied example connects environment, process, preserved rock, and resources.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow the Fraser River</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The chapter introduction follows the <b>Fraser River</b> through southwestern British Columbia.</li><li>Mountain streams, lakes, glaciers, and tributaries supply sediment to the river.</li><li>Its branching and rejoining channels illustrate a braided river.</li><li>At the Strait of Georgia, slowing flow disperses sediment and builds a delta near Vancouver.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Track one sediment grain</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Follow one grain through this system<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Weathering produces it, erosion removes it, a stream transports it, and decreasing flow allows deposition.</li></ul></li><li>Coarse and fine material respond differently.</li><li>The delta can grow seaward as material accumulates.</li><li>An ancient deposit must be interpreted from textures and structures, because the modern river and coastline may no longer exist.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why sediment matters</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Vancouver also illustrates the human importance of sediment.</li><li>Broad sediment-covered plains provide land for building and agriculture.</li><li>Pore spaces hold groundwater.</li><li>This supplied example connects environment, process, preserved rock, and resources.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-connect-settings":{"source":"<h3>Connect place, sediment, and transport</h3><ul><li><b>Lettered landscape:</b> A mountains; B glacial streams; C braided river; D delta; E lake. At D, flowing water meets standing water and loses transport capacity.</li><li><b>Meandering river:</b> the winding channel and flat floodplain fit mud and sand redistributed by floodwaters.</li><li><b>Beach:</b> the supplied shore has sand and shell pieces. Beach sediment varies between coasts.</li><li><b>Reef:</b> marine organisms build a structure and supply coral and shell material, commonly in shallow, warm, clear water.</li><li><b>Lagoon:</b> sheltered water behind a barrier is commonly shallow and calm; sand, mud, and coral fragments can mix.</li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T1\">Practice environments</button> <button class=\"btn\" data-e2-label=\"E2T1\">Practice environment figures</button></p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect place, sediment, and transport</h4><ul><li><b>Lettered landscape</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A mountains.</li><li>B glacial streams.</li><li>C braided river.</li><li>D delta.</li><li>E lake.</li></ul></li><li>At D, flowing water meets standing water and loses transport capacity.</li><li><b>Meandering river</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The winding channel and flat floodplain fit mud and sand redistributed by floodwaters.</li></ul></li><li><b>Beach</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The supplied shore has sand and shell pieces.</li></ul></li><li>Beach sediment varies between coasts.</li><li><b>Reef</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Marine organisms build a structure and supply coral and shell material, commonly in shallow, warm, clear water.</li></ul></li><li><b>Lagoon</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Sheltered water behind a barrier is commonly shallow and calm.</li><li>Sand, mud, and coral fragments can mix.</li></ul></li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T1\">Practice environments</button> <button class=\"btn\" data-e2-label=\"E2T1\">Practice environment figures</button></p></section>"},"g-connect-fluvial":{"source":"<h4>Stream and river language</h4><ul><li><b>Fluvial</b> means related to streams and rivers. It describes an agent and its processes, not one specific grain size.</li><li>A fluvial system can move gravel in a strong channel and deposit mud when floodwater spreads out and slows.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Stream and river language</h4><ul><li><b>Fluvial</b> means related to streams and rivers. It describes an agent and its processes, not one specific grain size.</li><li>A fluvial system can move gravel in a strong channel and deposit mud when floodwater spreads out and slows.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-1-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Mountain source to stream</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A sedimentary environment is a place where material is produced, moved or deposited. Steep mountain slopes release large angular blocks.</li><li>Mountain streams can carry coarse sediment through bedrock canyons, and channels may split and rejoin as braided streams beyond the mountains. Fluvial describes river processes and deposits; it does not name one grain size.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Meanders and deltas</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On gentler terrain, a meandering stream is bordered by a floodplain. Floodwater spreads mud and sand across that surface.</li><li>At a river mouth, the current enters standing lake or ocean water and slows. Sediment accumulates into a delta that builds outward. A floodplain lies alongside a channel; a delta grows at its mouth.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Wind and ice select differently</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wind transports sand and finer particles. Finer material can travel onward, leaving dunes composed mainly of well-sorted sand.</li><li>A glacier instead carries a wide mixture, from ground rock flour to large blocks. Melting releases both water and sediment. Different agents can therefore produce quite different deposits from the same mountain source.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Lakes and wetlands</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A lake contains several environments, from wave-worked shorelines to quieter central water. The shoreline may be sandy or rocky depending on the material available. In wetlands, saturated ground supports abundant vegetation.</li><li>Water can limit exposure to oxygen, helping plant material become an important part of the accumulating sediment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Interpret a collection of clues</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Topography, water and local geology control which environments develop. To interpret an ancient deposit, combine grain size, sorting, structures and biological remains.</li><li>Angular blocks beside a cliff support a slope setting; rounded gravel in a channel supports stream transport. One sand layer alone cannot distinguish a desert, river or beach.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A river slows on entering a lake → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Delta</mark>.</li><li>Fine material spreads beyond a flooded channel → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Floodplain</mark>.</li><li>Wind concentrates similar sand sizes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sand dune</mark>.</li><li>Moving ice releases a broad sediment mixture → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Glacial deposit</mark>.</li><li>Waterlogged ground preserves plants → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Wetland</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p1-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p1-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.1, PDF page 1\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.1 · PDF p1 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p2-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p2-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.1, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.1 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.1, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.1 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Mountain source to stream</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A sedimentary environment is a place where material is produced, moved or deposited. Steep mountain slopes release large angular blocks.</li><li>Mountain streams can carry coarse sediment through bedrock canyons, and channels may split and rejoin as braided streams beyond the mountains.</li><li>Fluvial describes river processes and deposits.</li><li>It does not name one grain size.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Meanders and deltas</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On gentler terrain, a meandering stream is bordered by a floodplain. Floodwater spreads mud and sand across that surface.</li><li>At a river mouth, the current enters standing lake or ocean water and slows.</li><li>Sediment accumulates into a delta that builds outward.</li><li>A floodplain lies alongside a channel.</li><li>A delta grows at its mouth.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Wind and ice select differently</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wind transports sand and finer particles. Finer material can travel onward, leaving dunes composed mainly of well-sorted sand.</li><li>A glacier instead carries a wide mixture, from ground rock flour to large blocks. Melting releases both water and sediment. Different agents can therefore produce quite different deposits from the same mountain source.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Lakes and wetlands</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A lake contains several environments, from wave-worked shorelines to quieter central water. The shoreline may be sandy or rocky depending on the material available. In wetlands, saturated ground supports abundant vegetation.</li><li>Water can limit exposure to oxygen, helping plant material become an important part of the accumulating sediment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Interpret a collection of clues</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Topography, water and local geology control which environments develop. To interpret an ancient deposit, combine grain size, sorting, structures and biological remains.</li><li>Angular blocks beside a cliff support a slope setting.</li><li>Rounded gravel in a channel supports stream transport.</li><li>One sand layer alone cannot distinguish a desert, river or beach.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A river slows on entering a lake → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Delta</mark>.</li><li>Fine material spreads beyond a flooded channel → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Floodplain</mark>.</li><li>Wind concentrates similar sand sizes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sand dune</mark>.</li><li>Moving ice releases a broad sediment mixture → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Glacial deposit</mark>.</li><li>Waterlogged ground preserves plants → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Wetland</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p1-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p1-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.1, PDF page 1\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.1 · PDF p1 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p2-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p2-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.1, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.1 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.1-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.1, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.1 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-2a":{"source":"<p>Beaches accumulate sediment moved by waves and currents; their grains depend on local supply. A <b>lagoon</b> is sheltered water behind a reef or barrier island. A <b>reef</b> is constructed by organisms, commonly in shallow, warm, clear water. A barrier island is a long, narrow sand body separated from shore by lagoon or marsh.</p><p>Continental shelves and slopes receive sand, mud, and carbonate sediment. Material can move downslope in a <b>turbidity current</b>, a sediment-laden water flow. Deep seafloor commonly receives fine dust and the remains of small marine organisms. Tidal flats alternate between flooding and exposure; coastal dunes form when wind moves shoreline sand inland.</p><p>Distinguish a sheltered lagoon from open shelf water and a river delta from a reef. Ocean depth alone does not determine the sediment: supply, water clarity, organisms, and transport also matter.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Beaches, lagoons, reefs, and barriers</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Beaches accumulate sediment moved by waves and currents.</li><li>Their grains depend on local supply.</li><li>A <b>lagoon</b> is sheltered water behind a reef or barrier island.</li><li>A <b>reef</b> is constructed by organisms, commonly in shallow, warm, clear water.</li><li>A barrier island is a long, narrow sand body separated from shore by lagoon or marsh.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Offshore transport and deposition</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continental shelves and slopes receive sand, mud, and carbonate sediment.</li><li>Material can move downslope in a <b>turbidity current</b>, a sediment-laden water flow.</li><li>Deep seafloor commonly receives fine dust and the remains of small marine organisms.</li><li>Tidal flats alternate between flooding and exposure.</li><li>Coastal dunes form when wind moves shoreline sand inland.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Interpret the whole setting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Distinguish a sheltered lagoon from open shelf water and a river delta from a reef.</li><li>Ocean depth alone does not determine the sediment<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Supply, water clarity, organisms, and transport also matter.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g7-2-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Beach and barrier</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A coastline receives sediment from local erosion and from rivers, waves and currents. A beach can contain sand, shells or rounded gravel; not every coast has a sandy beach.</li><li>Long narrow accumulations of sand can form barrier islands. Shallow lagoons or saltwater marshes commonly separate those islands from the mainland.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Lagoon and reef</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A lagoon is sheltered water behind a reef or island. Its nearshore sediment may come from land, while coral and shell debris can enter from a reef.</li><li>In suitable shallow, warm, clear water, organisms build reefs from their hard parts. The reef reduces the wave energy reaching the protected shoreline.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Shelf, slope and turbidity current</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continental shelves and slopes accumulate mud, sand and carbonate material. When sediment becomes unstable, a dense mixture of water and sediment can move downslope as a turbidity current.</li><li>Submarine canyons may funnel this material toward deeper water. Quiet offshore settling and sudden downslope transport can occur in the same basin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deep water and exposed flats</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Deep seafloor is cold and dark. Far from land, fine windblown dust and microscopic marine remains commonly dominate the sediment.</li><li>Along a shoreline, tidal flats alternate between flooding and exposure. In hot dry settings, evaporation can leave salt and other minerals. Depth alone does not explain every deposit.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect neighboring environments</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wind can carry beach sand inland to coastal dunes and can return it toward the water when wind direction changes. Deltas extend offshore, where unstable fronts can deliver sediment to deeper water.</li><li>Read the whole system: sediment supply, water clarity, organisms, wave protection and transport all help distinguish neighboring environments.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sheltered water behind a barrier or reef → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Lagoon</mark>.</li><li>Organisms construct a carbonate framework → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Reef</mark>.</li><li>Dense sediment-laden water moves downslope → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Turbidity current</mark>.</li><li>Long narrow coastal sand body → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Barrier island</mark>.</li><li>Coastal land is flooded and exposed by tides → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Tidal flat</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.2-p1-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.2-p1-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.2, PDF page 1\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.2 · PDF p1 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Beach and barrier</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A coastline receives sediment from local erosion and from rivers, waves and currents.</li><li>A beach can contain sand, shells or rounded gravel.</li><li>Not every coast has a sandy beach.</li><li>Long narrow accumulations of sand can form barrier islands. Shallow lagoons or saltwater marshes commonly separate those islands from the mainland.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Lagoon and reef</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A lagoon is sheltered water behind a reef or island. Its nearshore sediment may come from land, while coral and shell debris can enter from a reef.</li><li>In suitable shallow, warm, clear water, organisms build reefs from their hard parts. The reef reduces the wave energy reaching the protected shoreline.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Shelf, slope and turbidity current</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continental shelves and slopes accumulate mud, sand and carbonate material. When sediment becomes unstable, a dense mixture of water and sediment can move downslope as a turbidity current.</li><li>Submarine canyons may funnel this material toward deeper water. Quiet offshore settling and sudden downslope transport can occur in the same basin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deep water and exposed flats</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Deep seafloor is cold and dark. Far from land, fine windblown dust and microscopic marine remains commonly dominate the sediment.</li><li>Along a shoreline, tidal flats alternate between flooding and exposure. In hot dry settings, evaporation can leave salt and other minerals. Depth alone does not explain every deposit.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect neighboring environments</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wind can carry beach sand inland to coastal dunes and can return it toward the water when wind direction changes. Deltas extend offshore, where unstable fronts can deliver sediment to deeper water.</li><li>Read the whole system<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Sediment supply, water clarity, organisms, wave protection and transport all help distinguish neighboring environments.</li></ul></li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sheltered water behind a barrier or reef → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Lagoon</mark>.</li><li>Organisms construct a carbonate framework → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Reef</mark>.</li><li>Dense sediment-laden water moves downslope → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Turbidity current</mark>.</li><li>Long narrow coastal sand body → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Barrier island</mark>.</li><li>Coastal land is flooded and exposed by tides → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Tidal flat</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.2-p1-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.2-p1-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.2, PDF page 1\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.2 · PDF p1 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-3a":{"source":"<p><b>Physical weathering</b> breaks rock without requiring a chemical change. Near-surface fracturing, frost or mineral wedging, thermal expansion, and biological activity can loosen pieces. Expansion on unloading and roots growing in cracks are physical mechanisms. Thermal expansion is included in the textbook with a caution about its relative importance.</p><p><b>Chemical weathering</b> changes minerals. Dissolution carries soluble material away; oxidation combines susceptible material with oxygen; hydrolysis commonly turns silicate minerals into clay and dissolved products. Organisms can produce acids and drive chemical reactions.</p><p>Starting composition and environmental conditions determine the products. Resistant quartz can survive transport while less stable minerals break down. Physical breakup exposes fresh surfaces to chemical reactions, so both processes can work together. Weathering produces material; erosion removes it, and transport carries it elsewhere.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Physical weathering</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Physical weathering</b> breaks rock without requiring a chemical change.</li><li>Near-surface fracturing, frost or mineral wedging, thermal expansion, and biological activity can loosen pieces.</li><li>Expansion on unloading and roots growing in cracks are physical mechanisms.</li><li>Thermal expansion is included in the textbook with a caution about its relative importance.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Chemical weathering</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Chemical weathering</b> changes minerals.</li><li>Dissolution carries soluble material away.</li><li>Oxidation combines susceptible material with oxygen.</li><li>hydrolysis commonly turns silicate minerals into clay and dissolved products.</li><li>Organisms can produce acids and drive chemical reactions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Products and interacting processes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Starting composition and environmental conditions determine the products.</li><li>Resistant quartz can survive transport while less stable minerals break down.</li><li>Physical breakup exposes fresh surfaces to chemical reactions, so both processes can work together.</li><li>Weathering produces material.</li><li>Erosion removes it, and transport carries it elsewhere.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-susceptibility":{"source":"<h4>Compare under the same conditions</h4><ul><li>Fractures increase exposed surface area and let water, air, and organisms enter the rock. More fractured portions commonly weather faster.</li><li>Mineral composition matters: soluble material is vulnerable to dissolution; quartz-rich material is much less soluble.</li></ul><h4>Separate the evidence</h4><ul><li>A susceptibility comparison needs both rock properties and environmental conditions. Do not assume every mineral dissolves at the same rate.</li><li>Physical breakup makes clasts; dissolution carries material away as dissolved ions. Dissolved ions are not clasts.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare under the same conditions</h4><ul><li>Fractures increase exposed surface area and let water, air, and organisms enter the rock. More fractured portions commonly weather faster.</li><li>Mineral composition matters<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Soluble material is vulnerable to dissolution.</li><li>Quartz-rich material is much less soluble.</li></ul></li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate the evidence</h4><ul><li>A susceptibility comparison needs both rock properties and environmental conditions. Do not assume every mineral dissolves at the same rate.</li><li>Physical breakup makes clasts.</li><li>Dissolution carries material away as dissolved ions.</li><li>Dissolved ions are not clasts.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-3-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Break the rock physically</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Physical weathering breaks rock apart without requiring a change in its minerals. Near-surface fracturing can accompany unloading or a fall from a cliff.</li><li>Freezing water and growing salt crystals widen cracks.</li><li>Roots can pry fractures apart, while burrowing exposes material.</li><li>Thermal expansion can contribute, but this textbook explicitly qualifies its relative importance.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Change the minerals chemically</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chemical weathering changes or removes minerals.</li><li>Dissolution puts soluble components into water. Oxidation forms oxides, including reddish iron oxides. Hydrolysis reacts water with silicate minerals and commonly produces clay plus dissolved material.</li><li>Acids and other biological reactions can also break minerals down. These are chemical changes, not simply the rounding of a stone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Different minerals leave different products</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Starting composition matters. Soluble limestone can develop pits and grooves as water removes material.</li><li>In granite, feldspar may alter to clay or survive as grains, resistant quartz commonly remains as sand, and dark minerals can yield clay and iron oxides.</li><li>One original rock can therefore supply several sediment sizes and compositions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Fractures expose new surfaces</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fracturing makes a rock weaker and increases the surface available to water, air and organisms.</li><li>Physical breakup can accelerate chemical reactions, and chemical alteration can weaken rock for further breakup. Highly fractured parts of a unit may erode into notches or lower terrain.</li><li>The two forms of weathering often reinforce each other.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transport changes the fragments</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>After weathering loosens material, streams, waves, wind, ice and gravity can move it.</li><li>Collisions break clasts, and abrasion scrapes surfaces and rounds exposed corners. Moving sediment can also scour bedrock and create more fragments.</li><li>Keep the steps distinct: weathering produces loose or dissolved material, erosion removes it, and transport carries it elsewhere.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Water freezes and widens a crack → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Physical weathering</mark>.</li><li>Feldspar reacts to form clay → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hydrolysis</mark>.</li><li>Mineral components enter solution → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dissolution</mark>.</li><li>Iron-bearing material reacts with oxygen → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Oxidation</mark>.</li><li>Moving grains scrape a channel → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Abrasion</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.3, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.3 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.3, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.3 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.3, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.3 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Break the rock physically</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Physical weathering breaks rock apart without requiring a change in its minerals. Near-surface fracturing can accompany unloading or a fall from a cliff.</li><li>Freezing water and growing salt crystals widen cracks.</li><li>Roots can pry fractures apart, while burrowing exposes material.</li><li>Thermal expansion can contribute, but this textbook explicitly qualifies its relative importance.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Change the minerals chemically</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chemical weathering changes or removes minerals.</li><li>Dissolution puts soluble components into water. Oxidation forms oxides, including reddish iron oxides. Hydrolysis reacts water with silicate minerals and commonly produces clay plus dissolved material.</li><li>Acids and other biological reactions can also break minerals down. These are chemical changes, not simply the rounding of a stone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Different minerals leave different products</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Starting composition matters. Soluble limestone can develop pits and grooves as water removes material.</li><li>In granite, feldspar may alter to clay or survive as grains, resistant quartz commonly remains as sand, and dark minerals can yield clay and iron oxides.</li><li>One original rock can therefore supply several sediment sizes and compositions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Fractures expose new surfaces</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fracturing makes a rock weaker and increases the surface available to water, air and organisms.</li><li>Physical breakup can accelerate chemical reactions, and chemical alteration can weaken rock for further breakup. Highly fractured parts of a unit may erode into notches or lower terrain.</li><li>The two forms of weathering often reinforce each other.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transport changes the fragments</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>After weathering loosens material, streams, waves, wind, ice and gravity can move it.</li><li>Collisions break clasts, and abrasion scrapes surfaces and rounds exposed corners. Moving sediment can also scour bedrock and create more fragments.</li><li>Keep the steps distinct<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Weathering produces loose or dissolved material, erosion removes it, and transport carries it elsewhere.</li></ul></li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Water freezes and widens a crack → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Physical weathering</mark>.</li><li>Feldspar reacts to form clay → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hydrolysis</mark>.</li><li>Mineral components enter solution → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dissolution</mark>.</li><li>Iron-bearing material reacts with oxygen → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Oxidation</mark>.</li><li>Moving grains scrape a channel → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Abrasion</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.3, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.3 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.3, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.3 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.3-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.3, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.3 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-4a":{"source":"<p>Describe <b>size</b>, <b>roundness</b>, and <b>sorting</b> independently. Gravel includes granules, pebbles, cobbles, and boulders; sand is smaller than gravel; silt and clay together are mud. The grain-size table in the original page is the authority for exact boundaries. Clay can refer to a particle size or a mineral group.</p><p>Angular grains have sharp edges; abrasion during transport can round them. Sorting describes the range of sizes. Well-sorted sediment has a narrow range; poorly sorted sediment mixes sizes. Rounding and sorting can develop during repeated transport, but do not infer a unique travel distance from either alone.</p><p>The material transported depends on the agent, current strength, clast size and density, and available sediment. Wind selectively moves sand and finer particles; glaciers can carry a broad mixture. Strong water currents can move larger clasts than weak currents. Observe all three properties before assigning a rock name or environment.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Grain size</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Describe <b>size</b>, <b>roundness</b>, and <b>sorting</b> independently.</li><li>Gravel includes granules, pebbles, cobbles, and boulders.</li><li>Sand is smaller than gravel.</li><li>silt and clay together are mud.</li><li>The grain-size table in the original page is the authority for exact boundaries.</li><li>Clay can refer to a particle size or a mineral group.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Roundness and sorting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Angular grains have sharp edges.</li><li>Abrasion during transport can round them.</li><li>Sorting describes the range of sizes.</li><li>Well-sorted sediment has a narrow range.</li><li>Poorly sorted sediment mixes sizes.</li><li>Rounding and sorting can develop during repeated transport, but do not infer a unique travel distance from either alone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transport controls</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The material transported depends on the agent, current strength, clast size and density, and available sediment.</li><li>Wind selectively moves sand and finer particles.</li><li>Glaciers can carry a broad mixture.</li><li>Strong water currents can move larger clasts than weak currents.</li><li>Observe all three properties before assigning a rock name or environment.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-clasts":{"source":"<h4>What is being classified?</h4><ul><li>A <b>clast</b> is a fragment of rock or mineral. Grain size and mineral composition answer different questions.</li><li>Largest to smallest: <b>boulder → cobble → pebble → granule → sand → silt → clay</b>. The four largest classes are gravel; silt and clay together are mud.</li></ul><h4>Keep size separate from composition</h4><ul><li><b>Clay-sized</b> describes very small particles, regardless of their mineral identity. <b>Clay minerals</b> names a mineral group.</li><li>Many clay-sized grains are clay minerals, but size alone does not identify a mineral. A very small quartz particle is still quartz.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What is being classified?</h4><ul><li>A <b>clast</b> is a fragment of rock or mineral. Grain size and mineral composition answer different questions.</li><li>Largest to smallest<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Boulder → cobble → pebble → granule → sand → silt → clay</b>.</li></ul></li><li>The four largest classes are gravel.</li><li>Silt and clay together are mud.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep size separate from composition</h4><ul><li><b>Clay-sized</b> describes very small particles, regardless of their mineral identity. <b>Clay minerals</b> names a mineral group.</li><li>Many clay-sized grains are clay minerals, but size alone does not identify a mineral. A very small quartz particle is still quartz.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-4-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Name the particle sizes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Gravel includes granules, pebbles, cobbles and boulders. Sand is finer than gravel, while silt and clay together make mud.</li><li>The supplied size table gives the exact boundaries. Clay is both a size term and a mineral-group name, so a clay-sized particle and a clay mineral describe different properties.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Roundness concerns edges</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Angular clasts have sharp edges and corners. Repeated collisions and abrasion tend to remove those exposed corners, producing smaller, rounder pieces.</li><li>Shape is distinct from size: a large cobble can be rounded, and a tiny sand grain can remain angular. Rounding does not by itself specify one transport distance.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Sorting concerns the size range</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Poorly sorted sediment mixes many sizes. Moderately sorted sediment has a more limited range, and well-sorted sediment concentrates similar sizes.</li><li>Wind-built dunes commonly contain well-sorted sand because wind cannot supply gravel and finer particles may move onward. A mixture can be rounded and still poorly sorted.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow transport from the source</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Near a bedrock source, sediment commonly starts coarse, angular and poorly sorted. Transport can break it, round it and separate sizes.</li><li>Farther along a stream or repeatedly worked by waves, finer and better-sorted material may remain. These are useful tendencies whose interpretation still depends on source rocks and available sediment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Check four transport controls</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The textbook identifies four controls: slope steepness, current strength, sediment supply and the transporting agent. Strong turbulent water can move larger clasts than weaker flow, but it cannot carry a size the source does not provide.</li><li>Glaciers can move large mixtures, while wind is selective. Describe size, roundness and sorting before interpreting the setting.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Range of particle sizes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sorting</mark>.</li><li>Sharpness of edges and corners → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Roundness</mark>.</li><li>Slope, current, supply and agent → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Controls on transport</mark>.</li><li>Silt and clay together → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Mud</mark>.</li><li>Loose rock fragments → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Clastic or detrital sediment</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p6-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p6-Im3.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Name the particle sizes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Gravel includes granules, pebbles, cobbles and boulders. Sand is finer than gravel, while silt and clay together make mud.</li><li>The supplied size table gives the exact boundaries. Clay is both a size term and a mineral-group name, so a clay-sized particle and a clay mineral describe different properties.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Roundness concerns edges</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Angular clasts have sharp edges and corners. Repeated collisions and abrasion tend to remove those exposed corners, producing smaller, rounder pieces.</li><li>Shape is distinct from size<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A large cobble can be rounded, and a tiny sand grain can remain angular.</li></ul></li><li>Rounding does not by itself specify one transport distance.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Sorting concerns the size range</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Poorly sorted sediment mixes many sizes. Moderately sorted sediment has a more limited range, and well-sorted sediment concentrates similar sizes.</li><li>Wind-built dunes commonly contain well-sorted sand because wind cannot supply gravel and finer particles may move onward. A mixture can be rounded and still poorly sorted.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow transport from the source</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Near a bedrock source, sediment commonly starts coarse, angular and poorly sorted. Transport can break it, round it and separate sizes.</li><li>Farther along a stream or repeatedly worked by waves, finer and better-sorted material may remain. These are useful tendencies whose interpretation still depends on source rocks and available sediment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Check four transport controls</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The textbook identifies four controls<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Slope steepness, current strength, sediment supply and the transporting agent.</li></ul></li><li>Strong turbulent water can move larger clasts than weaker flow, but it cannot carry a size the source does not provide.</li><li>Glaciers can move large mixtures, while wind is selective. Describe size, roundness and sorting before interpreting the setting.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Range of particle sizes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sorting</mark>.</li><li>Sharpness of edges and corners → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Roundness</mark>.</li><li>Slope, current, supply and agent → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Controls on transport</mark>.</li><li>Silt and clay together → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Mud</mark>.</li><li>Loose rock fragments → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Clastic or detrital sediment</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p6-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.4-p6-Im3.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.4, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.4 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-5a":{"source":"<p>Classify a clastic rock by the particles preserved within it. Rounded gravel forms <b>conglomerate</b>; angular gravel forms <b>breccia</b>. Sand forms sandstone; silt forms siltstone. Fine clay-rich sediment can form shale, whose aligned grains allow thin sheets to split apart. Quartz sandstone is quartz rich; arkose contains abundant feldspar; graywacke contains mixed grains and rock fragments.</p><p><b>Lithification</b> changes loose sediment into rock. Compaction moves grains closer together and expels pore fluids. Cementation precipitates mineral material in the pores and binds grains. Compaction is particularly substantial in initially water-rich mud. Common cements include carbonate, silica, and iron-bearing material.</p><p>Composition, grain size, and roundness carry different information. Sandstone is a size-based name; feldspar content adds a compositional distinction. A red stain may be cement or oxidation rather than the original grains. Read the clast chart and the compaction sequence separately.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Name the clastic rock</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Classify a clastic rock by the particles preserved within it.</li><li>Rounded gravel forms <b>conglomerate</b>.</li><li>angular gravel forms <b>breccia</b>.</li><li>Sand forms sandstone.</li><li>silt forms siltstone.</li><li>Fine clay-rich sediment can form shale, whose aligned grains allow thin sheets to split apart.</li><li>Quartz sandstone is quartz rich.</li><li>Arkose contains abundant feldspar.</li><li>graywacke contains mixed grains and rock fragments.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Lithification: compaction and cementation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Lithification</b> changes loose sediment into rock.</li><li>Compaction moves grains closer together and expels pore fluids.</li><li>Cementation precipitates mineral material in the pores and binds grains.</li><li>Compaction is particularly substantial in initially water-rich mud.</li><li>Common cements include carbonate, silica, and iron-bearing material.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate texture from composition</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Composition, grain size, and roundness carry different information.</li><li>Sandstone is a size-based name.</li><li>Feldspar content adds a compositional distinction.</li><li>A red stain may be cement or oxidation rather than the original grains.</li><li>Read the clast chart and the compaction sequence separately.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-connect-photo-evidence":{"source":"<h3>Separate texture, composition, and environment</h3><ul><li><b>A:</b> visible sand-sized grains support sandstone.</li><li><b>B:</b> the supplied fine-grained layered example supports siltstone or shale. Do not force an exact name when the source allows both.</li><li><b>C:</b> chalk is very fine carbonate made largely from microorganism remains. Pale color alone does not identify it.</li><li>Alternating sandstone and shale can record deep-water turbidity currents separated by quiet mud deposition.</li></ul><h3>Keep the two columns separate</h3><ul><li>The plateau basal unit contains rounded gravel, wood, and tracks: river/floodplain evidence.</li><li>The separate assessment column begins with well-sorted sandstone and large cross beds: dune evidence. Similar color does not make these the same environment.</li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T2\">Practice rock evidence</button></p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate texture, composition, and environment</h4><ul><li><b>A</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Visible sand-sized grains support sandstone.</li></ul></li><li><b>B</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The supplied fine-grained layered example supports siltstone or shale.</li></ul></li><li>Do not force an exact name when the source allows both.</li><li><b>C</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Chalk is very fine carbonate made largely from microorganism remains.</li></ul></li><li>Pale color alone does not identify it.</li><li>Alternating sandstone and shale can record deep-water turbidity currents separated by quiet mud deposition.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the two columns separate</h4><ul><li>The plateau basal unit contains rounded gravel, wood, and tracks<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>River/floodplain evidence.</li></ul></li><li>The separate assessment column begins with well-sorted sandstone and large cross beds<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Dune evidence.</li></ul></li><li>Similar color does not make these the same environment.</li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T2\">Practice rock evidence</button></p></section>"},"g7-5-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Classify gravel and sand</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lithification turns loose sediment into rock. Rounded gravel produces conglomerate; angular gravel produces breccia.</li><li>Sand-sized grains make sandstone. These names describe the preserved clasts, rather than a unique depositional setting. Grain composition and roundness refine the description after the main size-based classification.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish sandstone varieties</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Arkose contains at least twenty-five percent sand-sized feldspar in this course source. Preserved feldspar commonly supports rapid burial after derivation from crystalline rocks.</li><li>Quartz sandstone contains mostly quartz, reflecting a quartz-rich source or destruction of weaker grains. Graywacke contains a mixture of mineral and rock-fragment compositions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the finest rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Siltstone contains silt-sized particles, commonly quartz. Shale contains very fine, aligned clay-rich material and splits into thin sheets or chips.</li><li>That splitting behavior matters when distinguishing shale from other mudrocks. Use the source photographs with the texture descriptions, rather than choosing a rock from color alone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compact grains and expel water</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Burial adds weight. During compaction, grains move closer together, pore space shrinks, and water or air is expelled.</li><li>Platy clay particles can rotate toward parallel orientations as a layer thins. The grains remain solid: compaction is packing, not melting, and some pore space can remain even after burial.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Cement the remaining pores</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Water moving through connected pores carries dissolved components. New minerals precipitate around grains and bind them as cement.</li><li>Four common cements here are calcite, silica, clay minerals and iron oxides. Silica can make a resistant rock, calcite can redissolve, and iron oxides commonly redden it. Cementation and compaction are different contributions to lithification.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rounded gravel-sized clasts → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Conglomerate</mark>.</li><li>Angular gravel-sized clasts → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Breccia</mark>.</li><li>Sand with at least 25% feldspar → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Arkose</mark>.</li><li>Mostly quartz sand grains → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Quartz sandstone</mark>.</li><li>Quartz, feldspar, mica and rock-fragment mixture → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Graywacke</mark>.</li><li>Compaction of platy clay-rich sediment with thin splitting → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Shale</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.5, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.5 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.5, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.5 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.5, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.5 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.5, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.5 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Classify gravel and sand</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lithification turns loose sediment into rock.</li><li>Rounded gravel produces conglomerate.</li><li>Angular gravel produces breccia.</li><li>Sand-sized grains make sandstone. These names describe the preserved clasts, rather than a unique depositional setting. Grain composition and roundness refine the description after the main size-based classification.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish sandstone varieties</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Arkose contains at least twenty-five percent sand-sized feldspar in this course source. Preserved feldspar commonly supports rapid burial after derivation from crystalline rocks.</li><li>Quartz sandstone contains mostly quartz, reflecting a quartz-rich source or destruction of weaker grains. Graywacke contains a mixture of mineral and rock-fragment compositions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the finest rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Siltstone contains silt-sized particles, commonly quartz. Shale contains very fine, aligned clay-rich material and splits into thin sheets or chips.</li><li>That splitting behavior matters when distinguishing shale from other mudrocks. Use the source photographs with the texture descriptions, rather than choosing a rock from color alone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compact grains and expel water</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Burial adds weight. During compaction, grains move closer together, pore space shrinks, and water or air is expelled.</li><li>Platy clay particles can rotate toward parallel orientations as a layer thins.</li><li>The grains remain solid<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Compaction is packing, not melting, and some pore space can remain even after burial.</li></ul></li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Cement the remaining pores</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Water moving through connected pores carries dissolved components. New minerals precipitate around grains and bind them as cement.</li><li>Four common cements here are calcite, silica, clay minerals and iron oxides. Silica can make a resistant rock, calcite can redissolve, and iron oxides commonly redden it. Cementation and compaction are different contributions to lithification.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rounded gravel-sized clasts → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Conglomerate</mark>.</li><li>Angular gravel-sized clasts → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Breccia</mark>.</li><li>Sand with at least 25% feldspar → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Arkose</mark>.</li><li>Mostly quartz sand grains → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Quartz sandstone</mark>.</li><li>Quartz, feldspar, mica and rock-fragment mixture → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Graywacke</mark>.</li><li>Compaction of platy clay-rich sediment with thin splitting → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Shale</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.5, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.5 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.5, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.5 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.5, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.5 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.5-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.5, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.5 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-6a":{"source":"<p>Some sedimentary rocks form from dissolved substances or organic material rather than chiefly from transported rock fragments. Evaporation concentrates dissolved ions and can precipitate <b>evaporites</b>, including rock salt and gypsum. Chemical precipitation also forms minerals where water chemistry changes.</p><p>Organisms extract dissolved substances to build shells and skeletons. Accumulation of carbonate remains can form limestone; silica-rich remains can contribute to chert. Plant material accumulates in waterlogged environments and, after burial and alteration, can form coal. These origins overlap: a biochemical rock can include fragments of organisms, yet its material originated through biological extraction from water.</p><p>Use the dominant material and formation process to interpret the rock. Halite is a mineral; rock salt is a rock composed mostly of halite. Coal records organic accumulation and preservation, whereas an evaporite records concentration and precipitation from water.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Chemical precipitation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Some sedimentary rocks form from dissolved substances or organic material rather than chiefly from transported rock fragments.</li><li>Evaporation concentrates dissolved ions and can precipitate <b>evaporites</b>, including rock salt and gypsum.</li><li>Chemical precipitation also forms minerals where water chemistry changes.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Biological and organic origins</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Organisms extract dissolved substances to build shells and skeletons.</li><li>Accumulation of carbonate remains can form limestone.</li><li>Silica-rich remains can contribute to chert.</li><li>Plant material accumulates in waterlogged environments and, after burial and alteration, can form coal.</li><li>These origins overlap<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A biochemical rock can include fragments of organisms, yet its material originated through biological extraction from water.</li></ul></li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the dominant material</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Use the dominant material and formation process to interpret the rock.</li><li>Halite is a mineral.</li><li>Rock salt is a rock composed mostly of halite.</li><li>Coal records organic accumulation and preservation, whereas an evaporite records concentration and precipitation from water.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-6-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Precipitate dissolved material</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Nonclastic sediment can form from dissolved components instead of fragments broken from older rock. As salty lake or sea water evaporates, minerals such as halite and gypsum can precipitate.</li><li>Other chemical reactions also make minerals. Evaporation removes water; the dissolved material is left behind and becomes concentrated before precipitation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Construct carbonate with chemistry and life</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Calcium-rich spring water can deposit layered calcite as travertine. Organisms also extract dissolved chemicals to build shells and skeletons.</li><li>Accumulated carbonate remains can become limestone. Chemical and biological processes may work together, so the contrast concerns the stated formation pathway rather than an absolute separation of all natural examples.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish chalk and dolostone</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chalk is a soft, very fine-grained limestone made from microscopic calcium-carbonate remains.</li><li>Dolostone can form when magnesium-bearing groundwater reacts with limestone, replacing calcite with dolomite. This changes the mineral composition of an existing carbonate rock; it is different from simply compacting an untouched pile of shell fragments.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Silica and iron make other rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chert is silica rich. It can form from accumulated microscopic silica shells or from chemical precipitation, including nodules in carbonate rock.</li><li>Iron formation contains thin layers rich in iron minerals, commonly with quartz, and much of it formed early in Earth history. Similar layering therefore does not mean identical composition or origin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Preserve plants as peat and coal</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wetlands can accumulate plant debris faster than it is completely destroyed. Peat retains much of the original plant texture and pore space.</li><li>With burial, compaction and heating, peat loses water and oxygen and becomes coal. Carbon-rich coal records plant material; it is not an evaporite and is not made from a pile of quartz sand.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Halite and gypsum left as water evaporates → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Evaporite deposits</mark>.</li><li>Layered calcite precipitated around a spring → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Travertine</mark>.</li><li>Microscopic carbonate remains accumulate → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Chalk</mark>.</li><li>Magnesium-bearing fluid replaces limestone minerals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dolostone</mark>.</li><li>Silica-rich shells accumulate or silica precipitates → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Chert</mark>.</li><li>Plant debris is buried, compacted and heated → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Coal</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Precipitate dissolved material</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Nonclastic sediment can form from dissolved components instead of fragments broken from older rock. As salty lake or sea water evaporates, minerals such as halite and gypsum can precipitate.</li><li>Other chemical reactions also make minerals.</li><li>Evaporation removes water.</li><li>The dissolved material is left behind and becomes concentrated before precipitation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Construct carbonate with chemistry and life</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Calcium-rich spring water can deposit layered calcite as travertine. Organisms also extract dissolved chemicals to build shells and skeletons.</li><li>Accumulated carbonate remains can become limestone. Chemical and biological processes may work together, so the contrast concerns the stated formation pathway rather than an absolute separation of all natural examples.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish chalk and dolostone</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chalk is a soft, very fine-grained limestone made from microscopic calcium-carbonate remains.</li><li>Dolostone can form when magnesium-bearing groundwater reacts with limestone, replacing calcite with dolomite.</li><li>This changes the mineral composition of an existing carbonate rock.</li><li>It is different from simply compacting an untouched pile of shell fragments.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Silica and iron make other rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chert is silica rich. It can form from accumulated microscopic silica shells or from chemical precipitation, including nodules in carbonate rock.</li><li>Iron formation contains thin layers rich in iron minerals, commonly with quartz, and much of it formed early in Earth history. Similar layering therefore does not mean identical composition or origin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Preserve plants as peat and coal</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wetlands can accumulate plant debris faster than it is completely destroyed. Peat retains much of the original plant texture and pore space.</li><li>With burial, compaction and heating, peat loses water and oxygen and becomes coal.</li><li>Carbon-rich coal records plant material.</li><li>It is not an evaporite and is not made from a pile of quartz sand.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Halite and gypsum left as water evaporates → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Evaporite deposits</mark>.</li><li>Layered calcite precipitated around a spring → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Travertine</mark>.</li><li>Microscopic carbonate remains accumulate → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Chalk</mark>.</li><li>Magnesium-bearing fluid replaces limestone minerals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dolostone</mark>.</li><li>Silica-rich shells accumulate or silica precipitates → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Chert</mark>.</li><li>Plant debris is buried, compacted and heated → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Coal</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.6-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.6, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.6 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-7a":{"source":"<p>Beds preserve changes in sediment supply, current strength, environment, or sea level. A boundary can mark erosion or a pause in deposition; not every layer represents an equal amount of time. Layer thickness and composition must be interpreted with other evidence.</p><p><b>Graded bedding</b> commonly places coarse grains at the bottom and successively finer grains above, recording waning current strength. A settling jar models this sorting. <b>Cross beds</b> are inclined layers within a larger bed, formed as dunes or ripples migrate. Their shape can preserve flow direction. Large cross beds commonly occur in wind-blown dunes; smaller examples also occur in streams and shorelines.</p><p>Separate a graded bed from a whole sequence that changes upward. One event can produce a graded bed; a succession of environments can produce a longer fining- or coarsening-upward sequence. Observe contacts, internal layers, and grain-size trends before explaining the process.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What beds record</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Beds preserve changes in sediment supply, current strength, environment, or sea level.</li><li>A boundary can mark erosion or a pause in deposition.</li><li>Not every layer represents an equal amount of time.</li><li>Layer thickness and composition must be interpreted with other evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Graded beds and cross beds</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Graded bedding</b> commonly places coarse grains at the bottom and successively finer grains above, recording waning current strength.</li><li>A settling jar models this sorting.</li><li><b>Cross beds</b> are inclined layers within a larger bed, formed as dunes or ripples migrate.</li><li>Their shape can preserve flow direction.</li><li>Large cross beds commonly occur in wind-blown dunes.</li><li>Smaller examples also occur in streams and shorelines.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">One bed versus a longer sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Separate a graded bed from a whole sequence that changes upward.</li><li>One event can produce a graded bed.</li><li>A succession of environments can produce a longer fining- or coarsening-upward sequence.</li><li>Observe contacts, internal layers, and grain-size trends before explaining the process.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-7-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate formation, bed and contact</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A distinctive laterally traceable rock unit can be called a formation. A formation may contain many individual beds.</li><li>Bedding can be defined by grain size, composition or color, and the boundary between rock units is a contact. Contacts may be sharp, gradational or scoured; a sharp boundary alone does not establish its cause.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Change conditions between beds</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Layers can record a discrete flood, changes in current strength or direction, a new sediment supply, changing sea level or changing climate.</li><li>Each explanation needs the actual evidence. Different colors do not automatically mean different climates, and a thick bed does not supply a universal amount of elapsed time.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Settle a normally graded bed</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shake mixed sediment in water and let it settle. Coarser particles settle first, with progressively finer material above.</li><li>A normally graded bed therefore becomes finer upward as transport energy wanes or suspended sediment settles. Read the variation within one bed. Water left above a jar deposit is not another sedimentary layer.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Move a dune and retain cross beds</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A current moves grains over a dune or ripple crest. Grains accumulate on its down-current slope, and migrating dunes preserve inclined layers called cross beds.</li><li>Cross beds can form in water as well as in wind. Large dune-scale sets and other clues support a desert interpretation; inclined layers alone do not prove one.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare complete structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Parallel beds commonly form as layers accumulate on one another. A scoured contact instead records erosion before the next deposit.</li><li>Keep parallel bedding, cross bedding and grading distinct: they concern layer orientation, inclined internal deposits and an upward grain-size change. Compare the actual source photograph before identifying the process.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Distinctive laterally traceable rock unit → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Formation</mark>.</li><li>Boundary between rock units → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Contact</mark>.</li><li>Coarse base grades to finer sediment above → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Normally graded bed</mark>.</li><li>Inclined internal layers left by migrating dunes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cross beds</mark>.</li><li>Layers accumulate with similar orientations → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Parallel beds</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.7, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.7 · PDF p5 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p8-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p8-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.7, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.7 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p9-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p9-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.7, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.7 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate formation, bed and contact</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A distinctive laterally traceable rock unit can be called a formation. A formation may contain many individual beds.</li><li>Bedding can be defined by grain size, composition or color, and the boundary between rock units is a contact.</li><li>Contacts may be sharp, gradational or scoured.</li><li>A sharp boundary alone does not establish its cause.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Change conditions between beds</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Layers can record a discrete flood, changes in current strength or direction, a new sediment supply, changing sea level or changing climate.</li><li>Each explanation needs the actual evidence. Different colors do not automatically mean different climates, and a thick bed does not supply a universal amount of elapsed time.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Settle a normally graded bed</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shake mixed sediment in water and let it settle. Coarser particles settle first, with progressively finer material above.</li><li>A normally graded bed therefore becomes finer upward as transport energy wanes or suspended sediment settles. Read the variation within one bed. Water left above a jar deposit is not another sedimentary layer.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Move a dune and retain cross beds</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A current moves grains over a dune or ripple crest. Grains accumulate on its down-current slope, and migrating dunes preserve inclined layers called cross beds.</li><li>Cross beds can form in water as well as in wind.</li><li>Large dune-scale sets and other clues support a desert interpretation.</li><li>Inclined layers alone do not prove one.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare complete structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Parallel beds commonly form as layers accumulate on one another. A scoured contact instead records erosion before the next deposit.</li><li>Keep parallel bedding, cross bedding and grading distinct<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>They concern layer orientation, inclined internal deposits and an upward grain-size change.</li></ul></li><li>Compare the actual source photograph before identifying the process.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Distinctive laterally traceable rock unit → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Formation</mark>.</li><li>Boundary between rock units → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Contact</mark>.</li><li>Coarse base grades to finer sediment above → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Normally graded bed</mark>.</li><li>Inclined internal layers left by migrating dunes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cross beds</mark>.</li><li>Layers accumulate with similar orientations → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Parallel beds</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.7, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.7 · PDF p5 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p8-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p8-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.7, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.7 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p9-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.7-p9-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.7, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.7 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-8a":{"source":"<p>Both rocks contain large clasts, so start by describing <b>roundness</b>. Breccia has angular fragments and commonly poor sorting. It forms where material is broken and accumulates with limited abrasion, including steep slopes, talus, landslides, and debris-flow deposits. Angular fragments alone do not prove one specific origin.</p><p>Conglomerate contains rounded coarse clasts and finer matrix. Streams, beaches, and other energetic environments can transport coarse material and round it. Alluvial fans can preserve a mixture of stream and debris-flow deposits; environments are not defined by a single rock name.</p><p>Compare size distribution, clast support versus matrix, bedding, and association with neighboring deposits. Grain rounding supports abrasion, while the coarse grain size supports transport or accumulation capable of handling large pieces.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Breccia: angular clasts</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Both rocks contain large clasts, so start by describing <b>roundness</b>.</li><li>Breccia has angular fragments and commonly poor sorting.</li><li>It forms where material is broken and accumulates with limited abrasion, including steep slopes, talus, landslides, and debris-flow deposits.</li><li>Angular fragments alone do not prove one specific origin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Conglomerate: rounded clasts</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Conglomerate contains rounded coarse clasts and finer matrix.</li><li>Streams, beaches, and other energetic environments can transport coarse material and round it.</li><li>Alluvial fans can preserve a mixture of stream and debris-flow deposits.</li><li>Environments are not defined by a single rock name.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the supporting clues</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compare size distribution, clast support versus matrix, bedding, and association with neighboring deposits.</li><li>Grain rounding supports abrasion, while the coarse grain size supports transport or accumulation capable of handling large pieces.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-8-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the angular jumble</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Breccia contains angular large clasts in a finer matrix and is commonly poorly sorted. Massive lenses or wedges may have little visible bedding because material arrived in a sudden chaotic event.</li><li>Some fragments remain shattered but close together. These observations support limited rounding, but do not identify one unique environment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deposit breccia in several ways</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Mudflows and debris flows carry mixtures down steep slopes. Landslides shatter rock during movement.</li><li>Streams can build coarse alluvial fans at a mountain front, and glaciers can leave mixtures of large fragments and fine rock flour.</li><li>Breccia is compatible with several of these settings, so inspect matrix, geometry and surrounding deposits.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Round gravel into conglomerate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Conglomerate has rounded gravel-sized clasts in sand or mud. Some beds have many touching clasts and little matrix; others contain scattered gravel within finer material.</li><li>Rounding usually records abrasion and reworking. Smaller gravel can reflect weaker flow, a finer source, long abrasion or a combination of those factors.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transport gravel in rivers and waves</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Energetic streams and braided channels can deposit coarse gravel. Repeated wave pounding can round beach stones without moving them a great distance away.</li><li>Rounded clasts therefore do not supply one exact travel distance. Match the rock with channel forms, shoreline evidence and other observations before choosing its environment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Carry coarse material offshore</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Conglomerate can also occur in deltas and underwater flows near continental slopes. Large clasts show that an effective transporting or moving process was available; the setting need not have been dry land.</li><li>Breccia and conglomerate share coarse grain size, while angular versus rounded clasts distinguish their names.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Angular shattered rock in a chaotic deposit → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Landslide breccia</mark>.</li><li>Muddy slurry carries large fragments down a canyon → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Debris-flow deposit</mark>.</li><li>Fan-shaped deposit at a mountain front → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Alluvial fan</mark>.</li><li>Rounded gravel in migrating channels → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Stream conglomerate</mark>.</li><li>Repeated waves round stones in place → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Beach reworking</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.8, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.8 · PDF p5 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.8, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.8 · PDF p5 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.8, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.8 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p10-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p10-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.8, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.8 · PDF p10 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the angular jumble</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Breccia contains angular large clasts in a finer matrix and is commonly poorly sorted. Massive lenses or wedges may have little visible bedding because material arrived in a sudden chaotic event.</li><li>Some fragments remain shattered but close together. These observations support limited rounding, but do not identify one unique environment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deposit breccia in several ways</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Mudflows and debris flows carry mixtures down steep slopes. Landslides shatter rock during movement.</li><li>Streams can build coarse alluvial fans at a mountain front, and glaciers can leave mixtures of large fragments and fine rock flour.</li><li>Breccia is compatible with several of these settings, so inspect matrix, geometry and surrounding deposits.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Round gravel into conglomerate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Conglomerate has rounded gravel-sized clasts in sand or mud.</li><li>Some beds have many touching clasts and little matrix.</li><li>Others contain scattered gravel within finer material.</li><li>Rounding usually records abrasion and reworking. Smaller gravel can reflect weaker flow, a finer source, long abrasion or a combination of those factors.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transport gravel in rivers and waves</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Energetic streams and braided channels can deposit coarse gravel. Repeated wave pounding can round beach stones without moving them a great distance away.</li><li>Rounded clasts therefore do not supply one exact travel distance. Match the rock with channel forms, shoreline evidence and other observations before choosing its environment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Carry coarse material offshore</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Conglomerate can also occur in deltas and underwater flows near continental slopes.</li><li>Large clasts show that an effective transporting or moving process was available.</li><li>The setting need not have been dry land.</li><li>Breccia and conglomerate share coarse grain size, while angular versus rounded clasts distinguish their names.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Angular shattered rock in a chaotic deposit → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Landslide breccia</mark>.</li><li>Muddy slurry carries large fragments down a canyon → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Debris-flow deposit</mark>.</li><li>Fan-shaped deposit at a mountain front → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Alluvial fan</mark>.</li><li>Rounded gravel in migrating channels → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Stream conglomerate</mark>.</li><li>Repeated waves round stones in place → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Beach reworking</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.8, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.8 · PDF p5 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.8, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.8 · PDF p5 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.8, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.8 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p10-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.8-p10-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.8, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.8 · PDF p10 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-9a":{"source":"<p>Sandstone preserves sand-sized particles but can form in many environments. Wind dunes tend to be well sorted and can preserve large cross beds. Stream deposits may include channels, gravel, variable sorting, and smaller cross beds. Beach sand is repeatedly reworked by waves, but its composition depends on the local supply.</p><p>Deltas and offshore shelves accumulate sand carried from land. Turbidity currents move sediment into deeper settings. Compare bedding, fossils, grain composition, and adjacent units to distinguish these possibilities. Sand-sized particles alone do not identify a beach or desert.</p><p>Sandstone commonly forms resistant cliffs or ledges where strongly cemented. The landscape depends on cement, fractures, climate, and neighboring rocks. A visible sand grain gives a textural observation; the environment remains an interpretation that needs several supporting attributes.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Dunes, streams, and beaches</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sandstone preserves sand-sized particles but can form in many environments.</li><li>Wind dunes tend to be well sorted and can preserve large cross beds.</li><li>Stream deposits may include channels, gravel, variable sorting, and smaller cross beds.</li><li>Beach sand is repeatedly reworked by waves, but its composition depends on the local supply.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deltas, shelves, and deeper water</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Deltas and offshore shelves accumulate sand carried from land.</li><li>Turbidity currents move sediment into deeper settings.</li><li>Compare bedding, fossils, grain composition, and adjacent units to distinguish these possibilities.</li><li>Sand-sized particles alone do not identify a beach or desert.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Rock resistance and interpretation limits</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sandstone commonly forms resistant cliffs or ledges where strongly cemented.</li><li>The landscape depends on cement, fractures, climate, and neighboring rocks.</li><li>A visible sand grain gives a textural observation.</li><li>The environment remains an interpretation that needs several supporting attributes.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-9-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize sand-dominated rock</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sandstone is dominated by sand-sized grains, commonly quartz but sometimes feldspar, volcanic fragments or other minerals. It may contain some silt or gravel while remaining sandstone.</li><li>Grains can be angular or rounded. Parallel layers, cross beds and massive-looking intervals record different patterns of deposition and preservation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare desert dunes and streams</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Desert dunes commonly produce well-sorted sand and large cross beds. Iron-oxide cement can give terrestrial sandstone a red color.</li><li>Streams deposit sand in channels and during floods, commonly interlayered with finer floodplain sediment. Use structures and associated deposits together; neither red color nor sandstone alone proves desert dunes.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deposit sand on a coast</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Beaches obtain sand from local rocks, reefs, rivers, wind and coastal currents. Shells or gravel may accompany it, although energetic waves can destroy fragile shells.</li><li>Wind moves some beach sand into coastal dunes. Deltas also deliver sand through channels and shallow water, so several neighboring coastal settings can produce sandstone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build a delta over marine mud</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The source outcrop changes upward from marine shale with thin sand beds to thicker sandstone with less shale. A delta advanced over the earlier marine mud.</li><li>This records a shift toward sandier, more landward conditions at that location. Read the whole vertical sequence rather than interpreting the upper sandstone in isolation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Carry sand beyond the shoreline</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sand can remain on a continental shelf or move into deeper water. A turbidity current carries a slurry downslope and can leave a graded sand bed among quieter marine mud deposits.</li><li>Sand near volcanoes may be rich in dark volcanic grains. Deep-water sandstone is therefore compatible with continental sediment delivered by a sudden current.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Well-sorted sand with large cross-bed sets → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dune deposit</mark>.</li><li>Discrete sand layers among floodplain fines → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Stream deposit</mark>.</li><li>Sand, local shells and wave-reworked gravel → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Beach deposit</mark>.</li><li>Sand advances over earlier marine mud → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Delta progradation</mark>.</li><li>Graded sand beds among deep-water mud → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Turbidity-current deposits</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.9, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.9 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.9, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.9 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.9, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.9 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p8-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p8-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.9, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.9 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize sand-dominated rock</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sandstone is dominated by sand-sized grains, commonly quartz but sometimes feldspar, volcanic fragments or other minerals. It may contain some silt or gravel while remaining sandstone.</li><li>Grains can be angular or rounded. Parallel layers, cross beds and massive-looking intervals record different patterns of deposition and preservation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare desert dunes and streams</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Desert dunes commonly produce well-sorted sand and large cross beds. Iron-oxide cement can give terrestrial sandstone a red color.</li><li>Streams deposit sand in channels and during floods, commonly interlayered with finer floodplain sediment.</li><li>Use structures and associated deposits together.</li><li>Neither red color nor sandstone alone proves desert dunes.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deposit sand on a coast</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Beaches obtain sand from local rocks, reefs, rivers, wind and coastal currents. Shells or gravel may accompany it, although energetic waves can destroy fragile shells.</li><li>Wind moves some beach sand into coastal dunes. Deltas also deliver sand through channels and shallow water, so several neighboring coastal settings can produce sandstone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build a delta over marine mud</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The source outcrop changes upward from marine shale with thin sand beds to thicker sandstone with less shale. A delta advanced over the earlier marine mud.</li><li>This records a shift toward sandier, more landward conditions at that location. Read the whole vertical sequence rather than interpreting the upper sandstone in isolation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Carry sand beyond the shoreline</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sand can remain on a continental shelf or move into deeper water. A turbidity current carries a slurry downslope and can leave a graded sand bed among quieter marine mud deposits.</li><li>Sand near volcanoes may be rich in dark volcanic grains. Deep-water sandstone is therefore compatible with continental sediment delivered by a sudden current.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Well-sorted sand with large cross-bed sets → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dune deposit</mark>.</li><li>Discrete sand layers among floodplain fines → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Stream deposit</mark>.</li><li>Sand, local shells and wave-reworked gravel → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Beach deposit</mark>.</li><li>Sand advances over earlier marine mud → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Delta progradation</mark>.</li><li>Graded sand beds among deep-water mud → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Turbidity-current deposits</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.9, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.9 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.9, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.9 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.9, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.9 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p8-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.9-p8-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.9, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.9 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-10a":{"source":"<p>Siltstone, mudstone, and shale preserve particles finer than sand. Silt commonly includes quartz and can feel gritty. Clay-sized particles are finer; clay-rich shale commonly splits into thin sheets because its minerals are aligned. Mud includes silt and clay.</p><p>Fine sediment settles where energetic transport subsides: floodplains, quiet lake centers, sheltered coastal water, and offshore settings. Mud can also remain suspended and be transported far from a river mouth. Deposits may include thin sand layers from stronger events.</p><p>Color, fossils, and structures add environmental information. Dark organic-rich material can support low-oxygen preservation; mudcracks indicate drying and exposure. Fine-grained rocks often weather into gentler slopes, but lithification and local conditions affect their resistance.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Silt, clay, mudstone, and shale</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Siltstone, mudstone, and shale preserve particles finer than sand.</li><li>Silt commonly includes quartz and can feel gritty.</li><li>Clay-sized particles are finer.</li><li>Clay-rich shale commonly splits into thin sheets because its minerals are aligned.</li><li>Mud includes silt and clay.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Where fine sediment settles</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fine sediment settles where energetic transport subsides<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Floodplains, quiet lake centers, sheltered coastal water, and offshore settings.</li></ul></li><li>Mud can also remain suspended and be transported far from a river mouth.</li><li>Deposits may include thin sand layers from stronger events.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Color, fossils, and structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Color, fossils, and structures add environmental information.</li><li>Dark organic-rich material can support low-oxygen preservation.</li><li>Mudcracks indicate drying and exposure.</li><li>Fine-grained rocks often weather into gentler slopes, but lithification and local conditions affect their resistance.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-fine-abundance":{"source":"<h4>Transport and settling</h4><ul><li>Fine-grained rocks are the most abundant clastic sedimentary rocks in the supplied textbook. Small particles travel readily in wind and water and settle in quieter settings.</li><li>Clay covers more of the seafloor than any other sediment type. Shale is common even though its weak, easily eroded outcrops can look less conspicuous than sandstone cliffs.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transport and settling</h4><ul><li>Fine-grained rocks are the most abundant clastic sedimentary rocks in the supplied textbook. Small particles travel readily in wind and water and settle in quieter settings.</li><li>Clay covers more of the seafloor than any other sediment type. Shale is common even though its weak, easily eroded outcrops can look less conspicuous than sandstone cliffs.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-10-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Classify the fine-grained rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Siltstone is dominated by silt. Mud includes both silt and clay, and its rock can be called mudstone or mudrock.</li><li>Shale characteristically splits into thin chips or flakes. Fine grain size and splitting are separate observations. Interbedded sand or limestone and varying colors add information about a particular sequence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deposit fines on land</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Floodwaters spread fine sediment beyond a river channel, and slow water lets silt settle. Lakes receive mud from streams, wind and nearby slopes.</li><li>Wind can also deposit extensive silt layers, especially from sediment ground by glaciers. Chemical weathering supplies clay-rich soils that may later become fine-grained rock.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Trap mud along the coast</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Tidal mud flats are repeatedly flooded and exposed, sometimes leaving evaporite minerals after drying. Lagoons trap clay and mud delivered from land.</li><li>Shallow continental seas and shelves also accumulate fines. Small grain size therefore supports quiet deposition, but does not by itself mean the water was very deep.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Let deep-water mud accumulate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fine particles carried by currents and wind can reach ocean basins. Organic-rich mud in relatively low-oxygen conditions can produce dark shale.</li><li>The interpretation depends on composition and fossils as well as color. A dark fine-grained rock is evidence to investigate, not a direct measurement of an exact ancient water depth.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Erode slopes and badlands</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shale, mudstone and siltstone commonly weather into soft slopes and loose chips. Debris from stronger cliffs can cover these weak units.</li><li>Streams preferentially erode some weak layers, while intricately drained badlands develop in easily eroded sediment. Modern slope shape helps identify resistance; depositional environment still requires the rock evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rock dominated by silt-sized particles → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Siltstone</mark>.</li><li>Fine-grained rock splits into thin chips → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Shale</mark>.</li><li>General rock formed from silt and clay → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Mudstone or mudrock</mark>.</li><li>Powdery silt deposited across land by wind → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Windblown silt</mark>.</li><li>Intricately drained soft sedimentary terrain → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Badlands</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.10, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.10 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p2-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p2-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.10, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.10 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.10, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.10 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.10, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.10 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Classify the fine-grained rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Siltstone is dominated by silt. Mud includes both silt and clay, and its rock can be called mudstone or mudrock.</li><li>Shale characteristically splits into thin chips or flakes. Fine grain size and splitting are separate observations. Interbedded sand or limestone and varying colors add information about a particular sequence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deposit fines on land</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Floodwaters spread fine sediment beyond a river channel, and slow water lets silt settle. Lakes receive mud from streams, wind and nearby slopes.</li><li>Wind can also deposit extensive silt layers, especially from sediment ground by glaciers. Chemical weathering supplies clay-rich soils that may later become fine-grained rock.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Trap mud along the coast</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Tidal mud flats are repeatedly flooded and exposed, sometimes leaving evaporite minerals after drying. Lagoons trap clay and mud delivered from land.</li><li>Shallow continental seas and shelves also accumulate fines. Small grain size therefore supports quiet deposition, but does not by itself mean the water was very deep.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Let deep-water mud accumulate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fine particles carried by currents and wind can reach ocean basins. Organic-rich mud in relatively low-oxygen conditions can produce dark shale.</li><li>The interpretation depends on composition and fossils as well as color. A dark fine-grained rock is evidence to investigate, not a direct measurement of an exact ancient water depth.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Erode slopes and badlands</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shale, mudstone and siltstone commonly weather into soft slopes and loose chips. Debris from stronger cliffs can cover these weak units.</li><li>Streams preferentially erode some weak layers, while intricately drained badlands develop in easily eroded sediment.</li><li>Modern slope shape helps identify resistance.</li><li>Depositional environment still requires the rock evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rock dominated by silt-sized particles → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Siltstone</mark>.</li><li>Fine-grained rock splits into thin chips → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Shale</mark>.</li><li>General rock formed from silt and clay → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Mudstone or mudrock</mark>.</li><li>Powdery silt deposited across land by wind → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Windblown silt</mark>.</li><li>Intricately drained soft sedimentary terrain → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Badlands</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.10, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.10 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p2-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p2-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.10, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.10 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.10, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.10 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.10-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.10, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.10 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-11a":{"source":"<p>Limestone consists mainly of calcite; dolostone consists mainly of dolomite. Carbonate rocks may form through chemical precipitation, biological accumulation, or later chemical replacement. Shells, reef frameworks, carbonate mud, and rounded coated grains can contribute to limestone.</p><p>Warm, shallow marine water is an important carbonate setting, especially where little land-derived mud obscures the water. Reefs build skeletal material. Lakes and springs can also deposit carbonate, so limestone alone is not proof of a tropical ocean. Dissolution can give exposed limestone pits or irregular surfaces; dilute acid helps identify calcite.</p><p>Read fossils, grain types, bedding, and associated deposits to reconstruct conditions. Carbonate cliffs versus rounded slopes depend partly on climate and erosion. Do not confuse a carbonate sedimentary rock with marble: marble records later metamorphism of carbonate rock.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Composition and carbonate components</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Limestone consists mainly of calcite.</li><li>dolostone consists mainly of dolomite.</li><li>Carbonate rocks may form through chemical precipitation, biological accumulation, or later chemical replacement.</li><li>Shells, reef frameworks, carbonate mud, and rounded coated grains can contribute to limestone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Carbonate settings and identification</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Warm, shallow marine water is an important carbonate setting, especially where little land-derived mud obscures the water.</li><li>Reefs build skeletal material.</li><li>Lakes and springs can also deposit carbonate, so limestone alone is not proof of a tropical ocean.</li><li>Dissolution can give exposed limestone pits or irregular surfaces.</li><li>Dilute acid helps identify calcite.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct the environment</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Read fossils, grain types, bedding, and associated deposits to reconstruct conditions.</li><li>Carbonate cliffs versus rounded slopes depend partly on climate and erosion.</li><li>Do not confuse a carbonate sedimentary rock with marble<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Marble records later metamorphism of carbonate rock.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g7-11-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify limestone and dolostone</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Limestone consists mainly of calcite and commonly contains fossils. It can dissolve and reacts with hydrochloric acid.</li><li>Dolostone consists mainly of dolomite and generally resists dissolution better. Carbonate can be mixed with clay or quartz, so color alone does not distinguish the minerals or establish where the rock formed.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Precipitate carbonate outside the sea</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Limestone can form in evaporative lakes and as chemical deposits around springs. Porous layered travertine can accumulate in hot or cold springs, lakes and caves.</li><li>Each new layer coats material already present. The lake pillars and spring deposits are carbonate examples without requiring a marine reef at the site.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build marine carbonate and carbonate sand</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Reef organisms extract calcium carbonate to construct shells and skeletons. Their remains and lime mud accumulate on shelves.</li><li>Waves can break reef material into calcite sand that later becomes clastic limestone. Carbonate composition and clastic texture can therefore occur together. In the deepest sea, carbonate may dissolve instead of being preserved.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Replace calcite with dolomite</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Magnesium-bearing fluids can interact with limestone and replace calcite with dolomite. Replacement may remain incomplete, leaving limestone and dolostone together.</li><li>This is a chemical modification of the rock, distinct from moving sand grains or simply growing a new reef. Keep original sediment formation and later mineral replacement separate.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Dissolve openings and shape the landscape</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In dry settings, carbonate rocks can form resistant cliffs. In wetter conditions, dissolution exploits fractures and bedding, leaving openings, pillars and caves.</li><li>Groundwater carries dissolved carbonate away. If a cave roof collapses, a sinkhole can form. Erosion resistance depends on climate and structure, so limestone does not always produce the same landform.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rock made mainly of calcite → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Limestone</mark>.</li><li>Rock made mainly of dolomite → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dolostone</mark>.</li><li>Waves break carbonate shells into sand → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Clastic carbonate sediment</mark>.</li><li>Groundwater removes soluble carbonate → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cave development</mark>.</li><li>A cave roof fails and lowers the surface → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sinkhole formation</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.11, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.11 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.11, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.11 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.11, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.11 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.11, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.11 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify limestone and dolostone</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Limestone consists mainly of calcite and commonly contains fossils. It can dissolve and reacts with hydrochloric acid.</li><li>Dolostone consists mainly of dolomite and generally resists dissolution better. Carbonate can be mixed with clay or quartz, so color alone does not distinguish the minerals or establish where the rock formed.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Precipitate carbonate outside the sea</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Limestone can form in evaporative lakes and as chemical deposits around springs. Porous layered travertine can accumulate in hot or cold springs, lakes and caves.</li><li>Each new layer coats material already present. The lake pillars and spring deposits are carbonate examples without requiring a marine reef at the site.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build marine carbonate and carbonate sand</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Reef organisms extract calcium carbonate to construct shells and skeletons. Their remains and lime mud accumulate on shelves.</li><li>Waves can break reef material into calcite sand that later becomes clastic limestone. Carbonate composition and clastic texture can therefore occur together. In the deepest sea, carbonate may dissolve instead of being preserved.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Replace calcite with dolomite</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Magnesium-bearing fluids can interact with limestone and replace calcite with dolomite. Replacement may remain incomplete, leaving limestone and dolostone together.</li><li>This is a chemical modification of the rock, distinct from moving sand grains or simply growing a new reef. Keep original sediment formation and later mineral replacement separate.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Dissolve openings and shape the landscape</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In dry settings, carbonate rocks can form resistant cliffs. In wetter conditions, dissolution exploits fractures and bedding, leaving openings, pillars and caves.</li><li>Groundwater carries dissolved carbonate away. If a cave roof collapses, a sinkhole can form. Erosion resistance depends on climate and structure, so limestone does not always produce the same landform.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rock made mainly of calcite → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Limestone</mark>.</li><li>Rock made mainly of dolomite → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dolostone</mark>.</li><li>Waves break carbonate shells into sand → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Clastic carbonate sediment</mark>.</li><li>Groundwater removes soluble carbonate → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cave development</mark>.</li><li>A cave roof fails and lowers the surface → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sinkhole formation</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.11, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.11 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.11, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.11 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.11, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.11 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.11-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.11, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.11 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-12a":{"source":"<p>A <b>facies</b> is a body of sediment or rock with characteristics representing an environment. Moving shorelines shift facies laterally, producing a vertical succession at a fixed location.</p><p>In a <b>transgression</b>, the sea advances over land because sea level rises relative to the land. Beach sand may be overlain by offshore mud and then carbonate material: the site becomes more marine through time. In a <b>regression</b>, the sea retreats and shallower-water or land deposits overlie deeper-water deposits. Relative sea-level change can result from changing water level or vertical land movement.</p><p>Follow both the shoreline and one fixed observation point in the source sequence. Lateral shifts and vertical stacking are different views of the same change. The exact rock sequence depends on which environments and sediment supplies exist; do not memorize sandstone–shale–limestone as a universal rule.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Facies and shifting environments</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <b>facies</b> is a body of sediment or rock with characteristics representing an environment.</li><li>Moving shorelines shift facies laterally, producing a vertical succession at a fixed location.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transgression versus regression</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In a <b>transgression</b>, the sea advances over land because sea level rises relative to the land.</li><li>Beach sand may be overlain by offshore mud and then carbonate material<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The site becomes more marine through time.</li></ul></li><li>In a <b>regression</b>, the sea retreats and shallower-water or land deposits overlie deeper-water deposits.</li><li>Relative sea-level change can result from changing water level or vertical land movement.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read shoreline movement and vertical stacking</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Follow both the shoreline and one fixed observation point in the source sequence.</li><li>Lateral shifts and vertical stacking are different views of the same change.</li><li>The exact rock sequence depends on which environments and sediment supplies exist.</li><li>Do not memorize sandstone–shale–limestone as a universal rule.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-connect-sequences":{"source":"<h3>The separate assessment column</h3><ul><li>Its base is red, well-sorted sandstone with large cross beds, supporting sand dunes.</li><li>Shell-bearing tan sand and marine shale/limestone above it record a marine advance; return to sand and upper breccia records later shallowing and land deposition.</li><li>This column has a different base from the numbered plateau investigation. Read each figure independently.</li></ul><h3>Denver-area section</h3><ul><li>Locate the old red rocks, Dakota Sandstone, Pierre Shale, and Precambrian igneous/metamorphic basement in the original figure.</li><li>No basaltic unit is shown. An absence claim applies to the figure, not every rock in the region.</li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T6\">Practice sequence interpretation</button></p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The separate assessment column</h4><ul><li>Its base is red, well-sorted sandstone with large cross beds, supporting sand dunes.</li><li>Shell-bearing tan sand and marine shale/limestone above it record a marine advance.</li><li>Return to sand and upper breccia records later shallowing and land deposition.</li><li>This column has a different base from the numbered plateau investigation. Read each figure independently.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Denver-area section</h4><ul><li>Locate the old red rocks, Dakota Sandstone, Pierre Shale, and Precambrian igneous/metamorphic basement in the original figure.</li><li>No basaltic unit is shown. An absence claim applies to the figure, not every rock in the region.</li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T6\">Practice sequence interpretation</button></p></section>"},"g7-12-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Advance the sea across land</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied model, the sea is on the left and land is on the right. A transgression moves the shoreline landward as relative sea level rises, whether the water rises or the land subsides.</li><li>Beach sand covers an earlier erosion surface. Follow one fixed location while neighboring environments move across it.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Stack sand, mud and reef carbonate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At the fixed location, an early beach is followed by a lagoon and then an offshore reef. The preserved order is beach sandstone, lagoon mudstone and reef limestone, from oldest at the bottom to youngest at the top.</li><li>Different environments also coexist laterally. Their sedimentary expressions are called facies.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Retreat the sea and build outward</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>During regression, the shoreline and neighboring facies shift seaward, toward the left in this source. Lagoon mud spreads over earlier reef limestone, and beach or dune sand advances over mud.</li><li>Previously deposited material can become exposed and reworked. New layers accumulate above the older transgressive package rather than replacing its entire history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare two vertical successions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The example transgression records sandstone below mudstone below limestone. Its regressive succession reverses the environmental trend: limestone, then mudstone, then sandstone.</li><li>Those particular rocks belong to this illustrated setting, not every coastline. Direction of shoreline migration and independent environmental clues carry the interpretation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Let a layer end naturally</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A layer need not continue forever. A facies can thin out where its depositional environment ends, and a channel deposit may be restricted to the channel itself.</li><li>Erosion can also remove material. Before correlating two exposures, consider both lateral facies differences and later removal rather than assuming a missing bed was never deposited anywhere.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shoreline and facies migrate landward → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Transgression</mark>.</li><li>Shoreline and facies migrate seaward → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Regression</mark>.</li><li>Different sedimentary expressions of neighboring environments → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Facies</mark>.</li><li>Environment ends and its deposit thins away → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Facies pinchout</mark>.</li><li>Gravel stays within a channel → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Restricted channel deposit</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Advance the sea across land</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied model, the sea is on the left and land is on the right. A transgression moves the shoreline landward as relative sea level rises, whether the water rises or the land subsides.</li><li>Beach sand covers an earlier erosion surface. Follow one fixed location while neighboring environments move across it.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Stack sand, mud and reef carbonate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At the fixed location, an early beach is followed by a lagoon and then an offshore reef. The preserved order is beach sandstone, lagoon mudstone and reef limestone, from oldest at the bottom to youngest at the top.</li><li>Different environments also coexist laterally. Their sedimentary expressions are called facies.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Retreat the sea and build outward</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>During regression, the shoreline and neighboring facies shift seaward, toward the left in this source. Lagoon mud spreads over earlier reef limestone, and beach or dune sand advances over mud.</li><li>Previously deposited material can become exposed and reworked. New layers accumulate above the older transgressive package rather than replacing its entire history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare two vertical successions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The example transgression records sandstone below mudstone below limestone.</li><li>Its regressive succession reverses the environmental trend<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Limestone, then mudstone, then sandstone.</li></ul></li><li>Those particular rocks belong to this illustrated setting, not every coastline. Direction of shoreline migration and independent environmental clues carry the interpretation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Let a layer end naturally</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A layer need not continue forever. A facies can thin out where its depositional environment ends, and a channel deposit may be restricted to the channel itself.</li><li>Erosion can also remove material. Before correlating two exposures, consider both lateral facies differences and later removal rather than assuming a missing bed was never deposited anywhere.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shoreline and facies migrate landward → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Transgression</mark>.</li><li>Shoreline and facies migrate seaward → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Regression</mark>.</li><li>Different sedimentary expressions of neighboring environments → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Facies</mark>.</li><li>Environment ends and its deposit thins away → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Facies pinchout</mark>.</li><li>Gravel stays within a channel → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Restricted channel deposit</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p6 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.12-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.12, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.12 · PDF p9 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-13a":{"source":"<p>Begin with observations: grain size and sorting, composition, fossils, color, bedding, cross beds, and contacts. Then infer an environment. Keep an observation such as “large cross beds” distinct from an interpretation such as “wind dunes.”</p><p>Read an undisturbed sequence from older at the bottom toward younger at the top. Use changes between units to reconstruct changes in environment. Red beds can reflect oxidation; dark organic-rich deposits may reflect limited oxygen. Neither color alone proves a unique setting. Several clues should agree.</p><p>Interpret erosional contacts and missing intervals explicitly. A marine unit above land deposits can support transgression; land deposits above marine material can support regression. Explain both the environment of each unit and the transition between them.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe before interpreting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Begin with observations<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Grain size and sorting, composition, fossils, color, bedding, cross beds, and contacts.</li></ul></li><li>Then infer an environment.</li><li>Keep an observation such as “large cross beds” distinct from an interpretation such as “wind dunes.”</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the sequence upward</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Read an undisturbed sequence from older at the bottom toward younger at the top.</li><li>Use changes between units to reconstruct changes in environment.</li><li>Red beds can reflect oxidation.</li><li>Dark organic-rich deposits may reflect limited oxygen.</li><li>Neither color alone proves a unique setting.</li><li>Several clues should agree.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Explain contacts and transitions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Interpret erosional contacts and missing intervals explicitly.</li><li>A marine unit above land deposits can support transgression.</li><li>Land deposits above marine material can support regression.</li><li>Explain both the environment of each unit and the transition between them.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-13-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Describe before interpreting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Begin with observations: color, clast size, roundness, sorting, bed thickness and contacts. Red colors commonly reflect oxidation, while dark gray may accompany organic material and low oxygen.</li><li>These clues support interpretations when combined. Color alone does not uniquely identify land, water depth or one depositional environment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read processes in the structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Grading can record weakening flow or settling. Large cross beds in well-sorted sand support wind-built dunes.</li><li>Mudcracks form when wet mud contracts during drying and may later fill with sand. A mudcracked bed records exposure to drying, but identifying the exact coastal or inland setting still needs context.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use fossils and flow indicators</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Marine fossils support seawater deposition, while plant remains help interpret land and climate context. In the supplied examples, cross beds slope down-current, while imbricated river clasts lean against the current.</li><li>Asymmetric ripples can record one flow direction; symmetric ripples commonly reflect back-and-forth waves. Keep the indicators and their directions distinct.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the example from the bottom</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The example begins with well-sorted, cross-bedded dune sandstone, then shell-bearing beach sand, then marine shale and reef limestone. This upward passage records a transgression.</li><li>Younger sand with land plants and coal records a later retreat of the sea. The interpretation follows the changing evidence at one location.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Finish with a new source of debris</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Angular granite boulders in muddy breccia cap that example. Limited rounding and nearby granite terrain support debris flows from steep mountains.</li><li>This is a later tectonic and sediment-supply episode. Preserve the distinction between the observed fragments and the inferred setting, and do not transfer this five-unit example onto the separate four-unit plateau investigation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wet sediment contracts during drying → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Mudcracks</mark>.</li><li>Inclined deposits slope down-current → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cross beds</mark>.</li><li>Flat clasts lean against flow → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Imbrication</mark>.</li><li>Back-and-forth waves make balanced ridges → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Symmetric ripples</mark>.</li><li>Identify actual grains, structures and fossils first → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Observation before interpretation</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im3.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Describe before interpreting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Begin with observations<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Color, clast size, roundness, sorting, bed thickness and contacts.</li></ul></li><li>Red colors commonly reflect oxidation, while dark gray may accompany organic material and low oxygen.</li><li>These clues support interpretations when combined. Color alone does not uniquely identify land, water depth or one depositional environment.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read processes in the structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Grading can record weakening flow or settling. Large cross beds in well-sorted sand support wind-built dunes.</li><li>Mudcracks form when wet mud contracts during drying and may later fill with sand. A mudcracked bed records exposure to drying, but identifying the exact coastal or inland setting still needs context.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use fossils and flow indicators</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Marine fossils support seawater deposition, while plant remains help interpret land and climate context. In the supplied examples, cross beds slope down-current, while imbricated river clasts lean against the current.</li><li>Asymmetric ripples can record one flow direction.</li><li>Symmetric ripples commonly reflect back-and-forth waves.</li><li>Keep the indicators and their directions distinct.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the example from the bottom</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The example begins with well-sorted, cross-bedded dune sandstone, then shell-bearing beach sand, then marine shale and reef limestone. This upward passage records a transgression.</li><li>Younger sand with land plants and coal records a later retreat of the sea. The interpretation follows the changing evidence at one location.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Finish with a new source of debris</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Angular granite boulders in muddy breccia cap that example. Limited rounding and nearby granite terrain support debris flows from steep mountains.</li><li>This is a later tectonic and sediment-supply episode. Preserve the distinction between the observed fragments and the inferred setting, and do not transfer this five-unit example onto the separate four-unit plateau investigation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wet sediment contracts during drying → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Mudcracks</mark>.</li><li>Inclined deposits slope down-current → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cross beds</mark>.</li><li>Flat clasts lean against flow → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Imbrication</mark>.</li><li>Back-and-forth waves make balanced ridges → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Symmetric ripples</mark>.</li><li>Identify actual grains, structures and fossils first → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Observation before interpretation</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im3.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.13-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.13, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.13 · PDF p8 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-14a":{"source":"<p>Sediment and sedimentary rocks store groundwater in pores and fractures. Connected pore spaces allow water to move; pore volume and connectivity are different properties. Sand and gravel are useful construction materials, and limestone supplies carbonate for cement and other uses.</p><p>Sedimentary basins can preserve coal, petroleum, and natural gas. Organic material supplies the starting carbon; burial and chemical change contribute to fuel formation. Reservoir rock stores fluids, while less permeable layers can help trap them. Resources occur through a sequence of processes, not merely because a rock has one name.</p><p>Layers also record past environments, biological remains, and events. Fossils, sedimentary structures, and sequences help reconstruct Earth history. The supplied lecture ending introduces this societal importance but does not establish a new exam weighting.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Groundwater and construction materials</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sediment and sedimentary rocks store groundwater in pores and fractures.</li><li>Connected pore spaces allow water to move.</li><li>Pore volume and connectivity are different properties.</li><li>Sand and gravel are useful construction materials, and limestone supplies carbonate for cement and other uses.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Coal, petroleum, and natural gas</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sedimentary basins can preserve coal, petroleum, and natural gas.</li><li>Organic material supplies the starting carbon.</li><li>Burial and chemical change contribute to fuel formation.</li><li>Reservoir rock stores fluids, while less permeable layers can help trap them.</li><li>Resources occur through a sequence of processes, not merely because a rock has one name.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The record preserved in layers</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Layers also record past environments, biological remains, and events.</li><li>Fossils, sedimentary structures, and sequences help reconstruct Earth history.</li><li>The supplied lecture ending introduces this societal importance but does not establish a new exam weighting.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-resources":{"source":"<h4>Sedimentary resources</h4><ul><li>Coal forms from accumulated plant remains. Rock salt can form by evaporation; limestone supplies cement manufacture. Petroleum is associated with organic-rich sedimentary rocks.</li><li>A useful building stone is not automatically sedimentary. Granite crystallizes from magma, so a quarried granite block contrasts with coal, sedimentary salt, and limestone.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Sedimentary resources</h4><ul><li>Coal forms from accumulated plant remains.</li><li>Rock salt can form by evaporation.</li><li>Limestone supplies cement manufacture.</li><li>Petroleum is associated with organic-rich sedimentary rocks.</li><li>A useful building stone is not automatically sedimentary. Granite crystallizes from magma, so a quarried granite block contrasts with coal, sedimentary salt, and limestone.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-14-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Store groundwater between grains</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Much groundwater occupies pores between sediment grains and fractures in rock. Connected openings allow water to move; an isolated opening can store water without transmitting it well.</li><li>A sedimentary rock can therefore act as both a record of deposition and a host for a later fluid history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Form, move and trap petroleum</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Oil and natural gas form in organic-rich sedimentary rocks and can migrate upward. Some escape, while some collect where rock geometry and a seal trap them.</li><li>Distinguish the source that generates petroleum from the reservoir that stores it and the trapping conditions that keep it underground. A porous bed alone does not prove an oil field.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Obtain solid sedimentary resources</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Coal comes from buried plant material. Suitable limestone supplies material for manufactured cement.</li><li>Salt can be mined from old deposits or harvested by evaporating brine. Migrating groundwater can concentrate uranium in sedimentary rocks. These resources have different origins even when all occur within a sedimentary sequence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read climate, life and past events</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Modern sedimentary processes help interpret ancient deposits. Layers preserve changing environments, fossils, past climate and evidence of events such as landslides, storms and earthquakes.</li><li>Reconstruct the event from its deposit and context. The record supports understanding of past change; it does not specify the exact date of a future hazard.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Sort aggregate for construction</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sand and gravel are widely used construction resources. Material is excavated, screened into sizes and sometimes crushed.</li><li>Selected sizes can become concrete aggregate, tile ingredients or fill beneath roads and buildings. Aggregate is the granular material; manufactured cement binds a concrete mixture. Natural cement between rock grains is another use of the same word.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Pores and fractures store water → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Groundwater</mark>.</li><li>Organic-rich rock generates oil and gas → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Petroleum source rock</mark>.</li><li>Granular material is screened for construction → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Aggregate</mark>.</li><li>Suitable limestone is processed for concrete binder → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Manufactured cement</mark>.</li><li>Groundwater transports and concentrates a metal → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sedimentary uranium deposit</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.14, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.14 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.14, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.14 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.14, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.14 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.14, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.14 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Store groundwater between grains</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Much groundwater occupies pores between sediment grains and fractures in rock.</li><li>Connected openings allow water to move.</li><li>An isolated opening can store water without transmitting it well.</li><li>A sedimentary rock can therefore act as both a record of deposition and a host for a later fluid history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Form, move and trap petroleum</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Oil and natural gas form in organic-rich sedimentary rocks and can migrate upward. Some escape, while some collect where rock geometry and a seal trap them.</li><li>Distinguish the source that generates petroleum from the reservoir that stores it and the trapping conditions that keep it underground. A porous bed alone does not prove an oil field.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Obtain solid sedimentary resources</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Coal comes from buried plant material. Suitable limestone supplies material for manufactured cement.</li><li>Salt can be mined from old deposits or harvested by evaporating brine. Migrating groundwater can concentrate uranium in sedimentary rocks. These resources have different origins even when all occur within a sedimentary sequence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read climate, life and past events</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Modern sedimentary processes help interpret ancient deposits. Layers preserve changing environments, fossils, past climate and evidence of events such as landslides, storms and earthquakes.</li><li>Reconstruct the event from its deposit and context.</li><li>The record supports understanding of past change.</li><li>It does not specify the exact date of a future hazard.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Sort aggregate for construction</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sand and gravel are widely used construction resources. Material is excavated, screened into sizes and sometimes crushed.</li><li>Selected sizes can become concrete aggregate, tile ingredients or fill beneath roads and buildings.</li><li>Aggregate is the granular material.</li><li>Manufactured cement binds a concrete mixture.</li><li>Natural cement between rock grains is another use of the same word.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Pores and fractures store water → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Groundwater</mark>.</li><li>Organic-rich rock generates oil and gas → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Petroleum source rock</mark>.</li><li>Granular material is screened for construction → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Aggregate</mark>.</li><li>Suitable limestone is processed for concrete binder → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Manufactured cement</mark>.</li><li>Groundwater transports and concentrates a metal → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sedimentary uranium deposit</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.14, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.14 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.14, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.14 · PDF p3 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.14, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.14 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.14-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.14, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.14 · PDF p7 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-15a":{"source":"<p>The Front Range exposes older crystalline basement. Younger sedimentary layers record sediment shed from mountains, wind-blown sand, shoreline deposits, and marine environments. The supplied map, cross section, and photographs show the relations used to reconstruct that changing history.</p><p>The lowest coarse reddish sediment includes sandstone, conglomerate, and breccia deposited by streams and debris flows from the Ancestral Rockies. Overlying units record later environments; interpret each with its grains, structures, and fossils. The present steep dips also record deformation after deposition.</p><p>Separate deposition from later tilting and erosion. A coarse unit can indicate nearby relief during deposition, whereas its present inclination need not be the slope on which it originally formed. Use the source sequence rather than assigning an age from color alone.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the Front Range evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Front Range exposes older crystalline basement.</li><li>Younger sedimentary layers record sediment shed from mountains, wind-blown sand, shoreline deposits, and marine environments.</li><li>The supplied map, cross section, and photographs show the relations used to reconstruct that changing history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct the depositional sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The lowest coarse reddish sediment includes sandstone, conglomerate, and breccia deposited by streams and debris flows from the Ancestral Rockies.</li><li>Overlying units record later environments.</li><li>Interpret each with its grains, structures, and fossils.</li><li>The present steep dips also record deformation after deposition.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate deposition, tilting, and erosion</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Separate deposition from later tilting and erosion.</li><li>A coarse unit can indicate nearby relief during deposition, whereas its present inclination need not be the slope on which it originally formed.</li><li>Use the source sequence rather than assigning an age from color alone.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-15-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Expose and tilt a regional sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Front Range exposes old Precambrian granite and metamorphic basement. Sedimentary units rest above it and now dip eastward away from the mountains.</li><li>Resistant Dakota sandstone forms a hogback, while weaker fine-grained layers erode into lower areas. Map colors identify units and ages; blue-colored old red rocks are not actually blue sandstone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Erode the Ancestral Rockies</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The lowest sedimentary package contains reddish coarse conglomerate, breccia and sandstone. Streams and debris flows carried sediment from the Ancestral Rockies onto older basement.</li><li>Younger red units include coastal mudflat deposits and well-sorted dune sandstone. The source records changing environments rather than one continuous kind of red deposit.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Pass through wetlands to a shoreline</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Jurassic Morrison Formation includes stream, wetland and lake deposits with dinosaur evidence. Above it, the Dakota Formation contains shoreline and beach features, including ripple marks, plant fossils and tracks.</li><li>These units were deposited before their later tilting. A fossil track on a tilted bed records the old depositional surface, not a dinosaur walking up the modern slope.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Advance and then retreat the inland sea</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Marine shale above the Dakota contains shells, shark teeth and fish bones. Sandstone and limestone interbeds record smaller shifts.</li><li>Later sandstone, shale and coal mark a return to shoreline and land environments. The sea advanced across the region and was later overrun by deltas; compare the whole sequence to the paleogeographic maps.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read younger mountains and the subsurface</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The youngest Green Mountain deposits include crystalline and volcanic clasts derived from erosion of the rising Front Range. Deformation folded and faulted the buried sequence, and geological interpretation helped locate petroleum at depth.</li><li>Separate deposition, later tilting, erosion and fluid trapping. Present elevation alone is not a complete history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Old crystalline rocks below the sedimentary cover → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Precambrian basement</mark>.</li><li>Coarse red debris shed from ancient mountains → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Ancestral Rockies deposits</mark>.</li><li>Jurassic stream, wetland and lake sequence → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Morrison Formation</mark>.</li><li>Resistant shoreline sandstone forms a hogback → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dakota Formation</mark>.</li><li>Young sediment derived from rising Front Range rocks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Green Mountain deposits</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.15, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.15 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.15, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.15 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.15, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.15 · PDF p5 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Expose and tilt a regional sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Front Range exposes old Precambrian granite and metamorphic basement. Sedimentary units rest above it and now dip eastward away from the mountains.</li><li>Resistant Dakota sandstone forms a hogback, while weaker fine-grained layers erode into lower areas.</li><li>Map colors identify units and ages.</li><li>Blue-colored old red rocks are not actually blue sandstone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Erode the Ancestral Rockies</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The lowest sedimentary package contains reddish coarse conglomerate, breccia and sandstone. Streams and debris flows carried sediment from the Ancestral Rockies onto older basement.</li><li>Younger red units include coastal mudflat deposits and well-sorted dune sandstone. The source records changing environments rather than one continuous kind of red deposit.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Pass through wetlands to a shoreline</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Jurassic Morrison Formation includes stream, wetland and lake deposits with dinosaur evidence. Above it, the Dakota Formation contains shoreline and beach features, including ripple marks, plant fossils and tracks.</li><li>These units were deposited before their later tilting. A fossil track on a tilted bed records the old depositional surface, not a dinosaur walking up the modern slope.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Advance and then retreat the inland sea</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Marine shale above the Dakota contains shells, shark teeth and fish bones. Sandstone and limestone interbeds record smaller shifts.</li><li>Later sandstone, shale and coal mark a return to shoreline and land environments.</li><li>The sea advanced across the region and was later overrun by deltas.</li><li>Compare the whole sequence to the paleogeographic maps.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read younger mountains and the subsurface</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The youngest Green Mountain deposits include crystalline and volcanic clasts derived from erosion of the rising Front Range. Deformation folded and faulted the buried sequence, and geological interpretation helped locate petroleum at depth.</li><li>Separate deposition, later tilting, erosion and fluid trapping. Present elevation alone is not a complete history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Old crystalline rocks below the sedimentary cover → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Precambrian basement</mark>.</li><li>Coarse red debris shed from ancient mountains → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Ancestral Rockies deposits</mark>.</li><li>Jurassic stream, wetland and lake sequence → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Morrison Formation</mark>.</li><li>Resistant shoreline sandstone forms a hogback → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dakota Formation</mark>.</li><li>Young sediment derived from rising Front Range rocks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Green Mountain deposits</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.15, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.15 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.15, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.15 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.15-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.15, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.15 · PDF p5 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g7-16a":{"source":"<p>The supplied investigation combines four units, their grain types, fossils, and structures. Identify at least two useful attributes of each unit before assigning an environment. Coal and plant remains support vegetation-rich land settings; mudcracks support exposure and drying; marine fossils support marine deposition.</p><p>Large cross beds in well-sorted sand support dunes, while smaller cross beds can occur in channels or shoreline deposits. Distinguish sandstone from carbonate rock by composition and fossils, not merely cliff color. Read the succession upward to infer environmental change.</p><p>This lab uses original practice scenarios based on those relationships. It does not treat a missing or unreadable worksheet label as a verified answer. For the full original investigation, inspect the source pages and make your own observation-to-interpretation table.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify each unit from its clues</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied investigation combines four units, their grain types, fossils, and structures.</li><li>Identify at least two useful attributes of each unit before assigning an environment.</li><li>Coal and plant remains support vegetation-rich land settings.</li><li>Mudcracks support exposure and drying.</li><li>marine fossils support marine deposition.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Interpret structures and succession</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Large cross beds in well-sorted sand support dunes, while smaller cross beds can occur in channels or shoreline deposits.</li><li>Distinguish sandstone from carbonate rock by composition and fossils, not merely cliff color.</li><li>Read the succession upward to infer environmental change.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the original investigation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>This lab uses original practice scenarios based on those relationships.</li><li>It does not treat a missing or unreadable worksheet label as a verified answer.</li><li>For the full original investigation, inspect the source pages and make your own observation-to-interpretation table.</li></ul></section>"},"g7-connect-plateau":{"source":"<h3>Begin with observations</h3><ul><li>Trace the layers before assigning rock names. Resistant units form cliffs and ledges; softer units form slopes. Different landforms show that the units do not resist erosion equally.</li><li>The photograph shows modern exposure. Fossils, grain sizes, and sedimentary structures supply the evidence for ancient environments.</li></ul><h3>Read the numbered column from bottom to top</h3><ul><li><b>4 — River system and floodplain.</b> Rounded gravel and coarse sand record transport; fossil wood, plant-bearing mudstone, and dinosaur tracks support land conditions.</li><li><b>3 — Beach and river deposits.</b> Shell-bearing quartz sand supports shoreline deposition. Basal conglomerate, erosion, wood, and leaves preserve river influence.</li><li><b>2 — Muddy marine water.</b> Fine sediment indicates low energy; marine fossils and local fossil-bearing limestone establish the marine interpretation.</li><li><b>1 — Shoreline or delta, then coastal land.</b> Basal sand contains marine shells. Higher sandstone and mudstone include plants, mudcracks, and coal, consistent with coastal land and wetlands.</li></ul><h3>Reconstruct both transitions</h3><ul><li><b>4 → 3 → 2: transgression.</b> The sea advances across land at this fixed location.</li><li><b>2 → 1: regression.</b> Shoreline and coastal deposits replace marine mud as the sea retreats.</li><li>The complete history is <b>transgression followed by regression</b>. Red color alone does not mean dunes; this basal unit has independent river and land evidence.</li></ul><p><a class=\"btn pri\" href=\"#exam2/cases/e2-connect-plateau-investigation\">Start the plateau investigation</a> <button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T6\">Practice sedimentary history</button> <button class=\"btn\" data-e2-label=\"E2T6\">Practice these figures</button></p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Begin with observations</h4><ul><li>Trace the layers before assigning rock names.</li><li>Resistant units form cliffs and ledges.</li><li>Softer units form slopes.</li><li>Different landforms show that the units do not resist erosion equally.</li><li>The photograph shows modern exposure. Fossils, grain sizes, and sedimentary structures supply the evidence for ancient environments.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the numbered column from bottom to top</h4><ul><li><b>4 — River system and floodplain.</b></li><li>Rounded gravel and coarse sand record transport.</li><li>Fossil wood, plant-bearing mudstone, and dinosaur tracks support land conditions.</li><li><b>3 — Beach and river deposits.</b> Shell-bearing quartz sand supports shoreline deposition. Basal conglomerate, erosion, wood, and leaves preserve river influence.</li><li><b>2 — Muddy marine water.</b></li><li>Fine sediment indicates low energy.</li><li>Marine fossils and local fossil-bearing limestone establish the marine interpretation.</li><li><b>1 — Shoreline or delta, then coastal land.</b> Basal sand contains marine shells. Higher sandstone and mudstone include plants, mudcracks, and coal, consistent with coastal land and wetlands.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct both transitions</h4><ul><li><b>4 → 3 → 2</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Transgression.</b></li></ul></li><li>The sea advances across land at this fixed location.</li><li><b>2 → 1</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Regression.</b></li></ul></li><li>Shoreline and coastal deposits replace marine mud as the sea retreats.</li><li>The complete history is <b>transgression followed by regression</b>.</li><li>Red color alone does not mean dunes.</li><li>This basal unit has independent river and land evidence.</li></ul><p><a class=\"btn pri\" href=\"#exam2/cases/e2-connect-plateau-investigation\">Start the plateau investigation</a> <button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T6\">Practice sedimentary history</button> <button class=\"btn\" data-e2-label=\"E2T6\">Practice these figures</button></p></section>"},"g7-16-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate landscape observation from history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The plateau photograph shows cliffs, ledges and softer slopes. These are modern expressions of differing rock resistance.</li><li>The gray slope and tan cliffs help locate units, but exact depositional settings require the supplied column, textures and fossils. The investigation has four numbered units, with unit four oldest at the bottom and unit one youngest at the top.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Start with the oldest river deposits</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Unit four includes rounded pebbles, coarse sand, wood, plant-bearing mudstone and dinosaur tracks. Together they support a river system and floodplain.</li><li>This is not the dune sandstone at the base of a different Connect sequence. Always identify which source column is being interpreted before transferring an answer.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Advance from shoreline sand into marine shale</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Unit three has yellow-tan quartz sandstone with marine shell fragments. Its basal conglomerate cuts an erosion surface and locally includes wood and leaves, supporting beach and river influence.</li><li>Overlying unit two is organic-rich gray shale with marine fossils and local limestone. This change from land through shoreline toward marine mud supports a transgression.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Return toward shore and coastal land</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Upper unit one starts with shell-bearing tan sandstone and passes into sandstone, mudstone, plants, mudcracks and coal. Shoreline or delta deposition gave way to coastal land and wetlands.</li><li>That upward change above marine shale supports regression. The upper package is sandstone and mudstone with coal, not a reef limestone cap.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">State the complete history and its evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>From oldest to youngest, the sequence records river and floodplain, shoreline sand, marine mud, then shoreline and coastal land. The whole record is a transgression followed by a regression.</li><li>The lower yellow-tan sandstone is compared with the Dakota in the Denver example. Give observations for each interpretation rather than treating a matching name as independent proof.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Unit 4: rounded gravel, wood, plants and dinosaur tracks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">River system and floodplain</mark>.</li><li>Unit 3: shell-bearing sand with basal river evidence → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Beach and river influence</mark>.</li><li>Unit 2: organic gray shale with marine fossils → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Muddy marine water</mark>.</li><li>Unit 1: sand, mud, plants, mudcracks and coal → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Shoreline to coastal land</mark>.</li><li>Land → marine mud → coastal land → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Transgression followed by regression</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.16, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.16 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.16, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.16 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate landscape observation from history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The plateau photograph shows cliffs, ledges and softer slopes. These are modern expressions of differing rock resistance.</li><li>The gray slope and tan cliffs help locate units, but exact depositional settings require the supplied column, textures and fossils. The investigation has four numbered units, with unit four oldest at the bottom and unit one youngest at the top.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Start with the oldest river deposits</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Unit four includes rounded pebbles, coarse sand, wood, plant-bearing mudstone and dinosaur tracks. Together they support a river system and floodplain.</li><li>This is not the dune sandstone at the base of a different Connect sequence. Always identify which source column is being interpreted before transferring an answer.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Advance from shoreline sand into marine shale</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Unit three has yellow-tan quartz sandstone with marine shell fragments. Its basal conglomerate cuts an erosion surface and locally includes wood and leaves, supporting beach and river influence.</li><li>Overlying unit two is organic-rich gray shale with marine fossils and local limestone. This change from land through shoreline toward marine mud supports a transgression.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Return toward shore and coastal land</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Upper unit one starts with shell-bearing tan sandstone and passes into sandstone, mudstone, plants, mudcracks and coal. Shoreline or delta deposition gave way to coastal land and wetlands.</li><li>That upward change above marine shale supports regression. The upper package is sandstone and mudstone with coal, not a reef limestone cap.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">State the complete history and its evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>From oldest to youngest, the sequence records river and floodplain, shoreline sand, marine mud, then shoreline and coastal land. The whole record is a transgression followed by a regression.</li><li>The lower yellow-tan sandstone is compared with the Dakota in the Denver example. Give observations for each interpretation rather than treating a matching name as independent proof.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Unit 4<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Rounded gravel, wood, plants and dinosaur tracks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">River system and floodplain</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>Unit 3<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Shell-bearing sand with basal river evidence → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Beach and river influence</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>Unit 2<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Organic gray shale with marine fossils → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Muddy marine water</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>Unit 1<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Sand, mud, plants, mudcracks and coal → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Shoreline to coastal land</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>Land → marine mud → coastal land → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Transgression followed by regression</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.16, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.16 · PDF p2 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter7-standard/7.16-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original course illustration for section 7.16, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 7.16 · PDF p4 · original figure; geometry retained</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-1a":{"source":"<p><b>Stress</b> is force applied over an area. Confining pressure acts approximately equally in all directions; differential stress varies with direction. Compression shortens, tension stretches, and shear moves neighboring material past itself. <b>Strain</b> is the resulting change in shape or size. Displacement and rotation can also change a rock’s position or orientation.</p><p>The classroom diagram shows rock strength against depth. In shallow crust, increasing confinement resists fracture and slip, so strength generally increases downward. At greater depth, temperature permits ductile deformation and strength decreases. The curve reaches a maximum near the brittle–ductile transition; it does not imply rock melts there.</p><p>Read the axes first: depth increases downward and strength increases rightward. A rock can remain intact below its strength threshold and deform or fracture when imposed stress exceeds it. Depth is a proxy for changing conditions, not a universal fixed boundary.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Stress and strain</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Stress</b> is force applied over an area.</li><li>Confining pressure acts approximately equally in all directions.</li><li>differential stress varies with direction.</li><li>Compression shortens, tension stretches, and shear moves neighboring material past itself.</li><li><b>Strain</b> is the resulting change in shape or size.</li><li>Displacement and rotation can also change a rock’s position or orientation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">How strength changes with depth</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The classroom diagram shows rock strength against depth.</li><li>In shallow crust, increasing confinement resists fracture and slip, so strength generally increases downward.</li><li>At greater depth, temperature permits ductile deformation and strength decreases.</li><li>The curve reaches a maximum near the brittle–ductile transition.</li><li>It does not imply rock melts there.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the axes and threshold</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Read the axes first<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Depth increases downward and strength increases rightward.</li></ul></li><li>A rock can remain intact below its strength threshold and deform or fracture when imposed stress exceeds it.</li><li>Depth is a proxy for changing conditions, not a universal fixed boundary.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-rotation":{"source":"<h4>Read two different observations</h4><ul><li>Photograph A shows formerly horizontal layers tilted: their orientation changed, demonstrating <b>rotation</b>. This observation alone does not measure an internal change in each grain’s shape.</li><li>Photograph B contains pebbles that were originally round and became flattened or elongated: the change of shape demonstrates <b>strain</b>.</li></ul><h4>They can occur together</h4><ul><li>Rotation changes orientation; strain changes size or shape. Translation changes position. A deforming rock can undergo more than one of these.</li><li>These are authentic textbook photographs. The original Chapter 8 Connect screenshots were not supplied, so these are supported visual practice rather than a reconstruction of their unseen geometry.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read two different observations</h4><ul><li>Photograph A shows formerly horizontal layers tilted<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Their orientation changed, demonstrating <b>rotation</b>.</li></ul></li><li>This observation alone does not measure an internal change in each grain’s shape.</li><li>Photograph B contains pebbles that were originally round and became flattened or elongated<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The change of shape demonstrates <b>strain</b>.</li></ul></li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">They can occur together</h4><ul><li>Rotation changes orientation.</li><li>Strain changes size or shape.</li><li>Translation changes position.</li><li>A deforming rock can undergo more than one of these.</li><li>These are authentic textbook photographs. The original Chapter 8 Connect screenshots were not supplied, so these are supported visual practice rather than a reconstruction of their unseen geometry.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-1-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Force, stress and pressure</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A force is a push or pull. Stress is force divided by the area over which it acts, so the same force on a smaller area produces greater stress. Confining pressure acts equally from every direction; differential stress differs with direction.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deform without confusing the response</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rock may translate to a new position, rotate to a new orientation, or change its shape and size. That internal change is strain. A tilted bed records rotation; flattened pebbles record internal strain. More than one response can occur together.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow strength into the crust</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On the supplied strength graph, depth increases downward and strength increases to the right. In shallow brittle rock, confinement generally increases resistance to breaking. Deeper, hotter rock can deform by solid-state flow and strength decreases. The curve peaks near the transition.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the limits visible</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The brittle–ductile transition is a change in dominant behavior, not a universal fixed depth or a melting line. Temperature, composition, fluids, pressure, and strain rate affect how a rock responds. The graph is a model, not a single depth that applies everywhere.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Equal loading from all directions → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Confining pressure</mark>.</li><li>Unequal stresses in different directions → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Differential stress</mark>.</li><li>Rock moved to a new position → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Displacement</mark>.</li><li>Originally horizontal bed tilted → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Rotation</mark>.</li><li>Pebble changed shape internally → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Strain</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Force, stress and pressure · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Deform without confusing the response · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Follow strength into the crust · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p11-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p11-Im1.png\" alt=\"Keep the limits visible · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 11\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 11 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Force, stress and pressure</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A force is a push or pull.</li><li>Stress is force divided by the area over which it acts, so the same force on a smaller area produces greater stress.</li><li>Confining pressure acts equally from every direction.</li><li>Differential stress differs with direction.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deform without confusing the response</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rock may translate to a new position, rotate to a new orientation, or change its shape and size.</li><li>That internal change is strain.</li><li>A tilted bed records rotation.</li><li>Flattened pebbles record internal strain.</li><li>More than one response can occur together.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow strength into the crust</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On the supplied strength graph, depth increases downward and strength increases to the right. In shallow brittle rock, confinement generally increases resistance to breaking. Deeper, hotter rock can deform by solid-state flow and strength decreases. The curve peaks near the transition.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the limits visible</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The brittle–ductile transition is a change in dominant behavior, not a universal fixed depth or a melting line. Temperature, composition, fluids, pressure, and strain rate affect how a rock responds. The graph is a model, not a single depth that applies everywhere.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Equal loading from all directions → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Confining pressure</mark>.</li><li>Unequal stresses in different directions → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Differential stress</mark>.</li><li>Rock moved to a new position → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Displacement</mark>.</li><li>Originally horizontal bed tilted → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Rotation</mark>.</li><li>Pebble changed shape internally → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Strain</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Force, stress and pressure · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Deform without confusing the response · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Follow strength into the crust · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p11-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.1-p11-Im1.png\" alt=\"Keep the limits visible · textbook section 8.1, PDF page 11\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.1 · PDF page 11 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-2a":{"source":"<p>The same differential stress can produce different structures under different conditions. At cool shallow levels, compression, tension, or shear can produce brittle failure and faults. At warmer depths, material can fold, stretch, or shear through distributed deformation while remaining solid.</p><p>Temperature, pressure, rock type, fluids, and the conditions of deformation affect behavior. Hotter rock generally deforms more easily. Fluids can alter minerals, move dissolved material, and form veins by precipitating in openings. A shear zone distributes deformation through a broader volume; a brittle fault concentrates movement along a fracture.</p><p>Compare one changing condition at a time. “Deep” and “shallow” describe the course’s general model rather than exact universal depths. Do not infer a unique stress type solely from the fact that a rock is brittle or ductile.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Brittle versus ductile response</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The same differential stress can produce different structures under different conditions.</li><li>At cool shallow levels, compression, tension, or shear can produce brittle failure and faults.</li><li>At warmer depths, material can fold, stretch, or shear through distributed deformation while remaining solid.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Conditions that control behavior</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Temperature, pressure, rock type, fluids, and the conditions of deformation affect behavior.</li><li>Hotter rock generally deforms more easily.</li><li>Fluids can alter minerals, move dissolved material, and form veins by precipitating in openings.</li><li>A shear zone distributes deformation through a broader volume.</li><li>A brittle fault concentrates movement along a fracture.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare one condition at a time</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compare one changing condition at a time.</li><li>“Deep” and “shallow” describe the course’s general model rather than exact universal depths.</li><li>Do not infer a unique stress type solely from the fact that a rock is brittle or ductile.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-2-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compression, tension, and shear</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression squeezes and shortens rock, tension stretches it, and shear makes adjacent material move past itself. The same stress category can produce different structures under different conditions. In the shallow crust, compression can form faults; deeper, hot rocks can fold or flow.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From brittle fracture to solid flow</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A brittle rock breaks or slips when stressed. At depth, higher confinement and temperature commonly favor ductile behavior: minerals and rock masses change shape without a throughgoing fracture. Ductile does not mean liquid, and the transition depends on the material and conditions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow minerals and fluids</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At increasing depth, minerals may deform, grow, or react to form new minerals stable under changed conditions. Water in pore spaces opposes part of the confining pressure and can assist fracture or reactions. Fluid effects depend on location and conditions rather than one universal outcome.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use experiments and observations</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Geologists investigate mineral stability and rock behavior using controlled temperature and pressure experiments, field observations, and measurements of natural rocks. Each line of evidence has a defined range of conditions. A single specimen cannot establish the strength of every rock at a given depth.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Squeezing that shortens rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Compression</mark>.</li><li>Pulling that stretches rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Tension</mark>.</li><li>Adjacent material moves past itself → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Shear</mark>.</li><li>Fracture and slip near the surface → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Brittle behavior</mark>.</li><li>Solid rock changes shape without a throughgoing break → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Ductile behavior</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Compression, tension, and shear · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"From brittle fracture to solid flow · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Follow minerals and fluids · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Use experiments and observations · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p4-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p4-Im3.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p5-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p5-Im3.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compression, tension, and shear</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression squeezes and shortens rock, tension stretches it, and shear makes adjacent material move past itself.</li><li>The same stress category can produce different structures under different conditions.</li><li>In the shallow crust, compression can form faults.</li><li>Deeper, hot rocks can fold or flow.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From brittle fracture to solid flow</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A brittle rock breaks or slips when stressed.</li><li>At depth, higher confinement and temperature commonly favor ductile behavior<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Minerals and rock masses change shape without a throughgoing fracture.</li></ul></li><li>Ductile does not mean liquid, and the transition depends on the material and conditions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow minerals and fluids</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At increasing depth, minerals may deform, grow, or react to form new minerals stable under changed conditions. Water in pore spaces opposes part of the confining pressure and can assist fracture or reactions. Fluid effects depend on location and conditions rather than one universal outcome.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use experiments and observations</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Geologists investigate mineral stability and rock behavior using controlled temperature and pressure experiments, field observations, and measurements of natural rocks. Each line of evidence has a defined range of conditions. A single specimen cannot establish the strength of every rock at a given depth.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Squeezing that shortens rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Compression</mark>.</li><li>Pulling that stretches rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Tension</mark>.</li><li>Adjacent material moves past itself → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Shear</mark>.</li><li>Fracture and slip near the surface → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Brittle behavior</mark>.</li><li>Solid rock changes shape without a throughgoing break → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Ductile behavior</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Compression, tension, and shear · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"From brittle fracture to solid flow · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p7-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p7-Im1.png\" alt=\"Follow minerals and fluids · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Use experiments and observations · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p4-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p4-Im3.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p5-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.2-p5-Im3.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.2, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.2 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-3a":{"source":"<p>A <b>joint</b> is a fracture with little or no shear displacement. A fault records relative displacement across a fracture. Joint sets have similar orientations and may divide rock into blocks. Tectonic stress can produce joints; cooling and contraction can produce columnar joints; removal of overlying weight can produce curved unloading joints.</p><p>Columnar joints develop as solidified igneous rock cools and contracts. Their polygonal geometry records contraction rather than sedimentary layering. Unloading changes stress near the surface and can form sheetlike fractures roughly parallel to a curved exposed surface.</p><p><b>Strike</b> is the compass orientation of a horizontal line on a plane. <b>Dip</b> is the inclination of the plane, measured downward from horizontal and perpendicular to strike. Read orientation from the source diagram; the supplied lecture explicitly does not require detailed compass operation.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Joints versus faults</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <b>joint</b> is a fracture with little or no shear displacement.</li><li>A fault records relative displacement across a fracture.</li><li>Joint sets have similar orientations and may divide rock into blocks.</li><li>Tectonic stress can produce joints.</li><li>Cooling and contraction can produce columnar joints.</li><li>removal of overlying weight can produce curved unloading joints.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Cooling and unloading joints</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Columnar joints develop as solidified igneous rock cools and contracts.</li><li>Their polygonal geometry records contraction rather than sedimentary layering.</li><li>Unloading changes stress near the surface and can form sheetlike fractures roughly parallel to a curved exposed surface.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Strike and dip</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Strike</b> is the compass orientation of a horizontal line on a plane.</li><li><b>Dip</b> is the inclination of the plane, measured downward from horizontal and perpendicular to strike.</li><li>Read orientation from the source diagram.</li><li>The supplied lecture explicitly does not require detailed compass operation.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-joints":{"source":"<h4>Three source examples</h4><ul><li>Photograph A is the textbook example of tectonic joints. Forces associated with burial and tectonics can create joints.</li><li>Photograph B shows columnar joints caused by cooling and contraction. Photograph C shows unloading joints: reduced pressure during uplift permits fracturing parallel to the surface.</li></ul><h4>Infer orientation carefully</h4><ul><li>A joint opens perpendicular to the direction in which rock is pulled apart. In the textbook example, north–south horizontal pulling produces vertical joints trending east–west.</li><li>A joint has no appreciable displacement parallel to its surface. A fracture with such displacement is a fault. A photograph’s appearance alone need not establish its cause without context.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Three source examples</h4><ul><li>Photograph A is the textbook example of tectonic joints. Forces associated with burial and tectonics can create joints.</li><li>Photograph B shows columnar joints caused by cooling and contraction.</li><li>Photograph C shows unloading joints<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Reduced pressure during uplift permits fracturing parallel to the surface.</li></ul></li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Infer orientation carefully</h4><ul><li>A joint opens perpendicular to the direction in which rock is pulled apart. In the textbook example, north–south horizontal pulling produces vertical joints trending east–west.</li><li>A joint has no appreciable displacement parallel to its surface. A fracture with such displacement is a fault. A photograph’s appearance alone need not establish its cause without context.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-3-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish two fractures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A joint is a crack with little or no shear displacement along its surface. A fault is a fracture across which rocks have slipped. Follow a matching bed or other marker across the break before deciding whether movement occurred.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Open joints under different causes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Joints can open as rocks cool and contract, as overlying rock is removed and pressure decreases, or under tectonic stresses. Columnar joints form during cooling. Sheet-like unloading joints follow reduced confinement as deeply formed rock approaches the surface.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Relate joint orientation to stress</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An opening-mode joint is oriented approximately perpendicular to the direction in which the rock is pulled apart. Joint sets can divide an outcrop into regular blocks. Identify the opening direction and the visible planes before deciding which stresses acted.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Faults cut and offset</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Differential stress can produce a fracture along which blocks slip. The fault orientation depends on the full stress field, including stresses in the other directions. An observed fracture alone does not establish the amount or sense of motion without a displaced marker or other evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Open fracture with little shear offset → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Joint</mark>.</li><li>Fracture with relative slip → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault</mark>.</li><li>Contraction during cooling → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Columnar joints</mark>.</li><li>Reduced pressure during erosion and uplift → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Unloading joints</mark>.</li><li>Open crack roughly perpendicular to pull → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Tension joint</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Distinguish two fractures · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im3.png\" alt=\"Open joints under different causes · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p6-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p6-Im2.png\" alt=\"Relate joint orientation to stress · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p11-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p11-Im1.png\" alt=\"Faults cut and offset · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 11\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 11 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish two fractures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A joint is a crack with little or no shear displacement along its surface. A fault is a fracture across which rocks have slipped. Follow a matching bed or other marker across the break before deciding whether movement occurred.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Open joints under different causes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Joints can open as rocks cool and contract, as overlying rock is removed and pressure decreases, or under tectonic stresses. Columnar joints form during cooling. Sheet-like unloading joints follow reduced confinement as deeply formed rock approaches the surface.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Relate joint orientation to stress</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An opening-mode joint is oriented approximately perpendicular to the direction in which the rock is pulled apart. Joint sets can divide an outcrop into regular blocks. Identify the opening direction and the visible planes before deciding which stresses acted.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Faults cut and offset</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Differential stress can produce a fracture along which blocks slip. The fault orientation depends on the full stress field, including stresses in the other directions. An observed fracture alone does not establish the amount or sense of motion without a displaced marker or other evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Open fracture with little shear offset → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Joint</mark>.</li><li>Fracture with relative slip → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault</mark>.</li><li>Contraction during cooling → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Columnar joints</mark>.</li><li>Reduced pressure during erosion and uplift → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Unloading joints</mark>.</li><li>Open crack roughly perpendicular to pull → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Tension joint</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Distinguish two fractures · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im3.png\" alt=\"Open joints under different causes · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p6-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p6-Im2.png\" alt=\"Relate joint orientation to stress · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p11-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p11-Im1.png\" alt=\"Faults cut and offset · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 11\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 11 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.3-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.3, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.3 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-4a":{"source":"<p>A fault is a fracture with relative movement. On an inclined fault, the <b>hanging wall</b> is above the plane and the <b>footwall</b> is below it. Name the blocks geometrically before describing their motion.</p><p>On a <b>normal fault</b>, the hanging wall moves down relative to the footwall; extension commonly produces these faults. On a <b>reverse fault</b>, the hanging wall moves up; shortening commonly produces them. A low-angle reverse fault is a thrust. On a <b>strike-slip fault</b>, relative movement is predominantly horizontal. Oblique slip combines components.</p><p>Several normal faults can bound uplifted horsts and downdropped grabens. Infer relative movement from matching offset layers, arrows, or other markers. A photograph of a steep cliff by itself does not establish the movement direction.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the fault blocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A fault is a fracture with relative movement.</li><li>On an inclined fault, the <b>hanging wall</b> is above the plane and the <b>footwall</b> is below it.</li><li>Name the blocks geometrically before describing their motion.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Normal, reverse, thrust, and strike-slip faults</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On a <b>normal fault</b>, the hanging wall moves down relative to the footwall.</li><li>Extension commonly produces these faults.</li><li>On a <b>reverse fault</b>, the hanging wall moves up.</li><li>Shortening commonly produces them.</li><li>A low-angle reverse fault is a thrust.</li><li>On a <b>strike-slip fault</b>, relative movement is predominantly horizontal.</li><li>Oblique slip combines components.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use markers to establish movement</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Several normal faults can bound uplifted horsts and downdropped grabens.</li><li>Infer relative movement from matching offset layers, arrows, or other markers.</li><li>A photograph of a steep cliff by itself does not establish the movement direction.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-4-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Orient a planar surface</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Strike is the direction of a horizontal line on an inclined plane. Dip is the direction and angle of steepest descent of that plane. Slip may be along dip, along strike, or oblique. Plane orientation and movement direction answer different questions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the two walls</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>For an inclined fault, the hanging wall is the block above the plane and the footwall lies below it. The labels follow the fault plane; they are not assigned by which block appears higher on the modern landscape.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare fault movements</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On a normal fault the hanging wall moves down relative to the footwall, commonly during extension. On a reverse fault it moves up, commonly during shortening; a thrust is a low-angle reverse fault. Strike-slip faults have predominantly horizontal movement.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build horsts and grabens</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A graben is a block that has dropped relative to blocks on either side along normal faults. A horst is relatively raised. These are relative displacements within a fault system, so present elevation alone does not define every block’s complete history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Block above an inclined fault plane → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hanging wall</mark>.</li><li>Block below an inclined fault plane → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Footwall</mark>.</li><li>Hanging wall moves down → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Normal fault</mark>.</li><li>Hanging wall moves up → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Reverse fault</mark>.</li><li>Predominantly horizontal slip → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Strike-slip fault</mark>.</li><li>Dropped block between normal faults → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Graben</mark>.</li><li>Relatively raised block → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Horst</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Orient a planar surface · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p6-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p6-Im2.png\" alt=\"Identify the two walls · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Compare fault movements · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Build horsts and grabens · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p8-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p8-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 8 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Orient a planar surface</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Strike is the direction of a horizontal line on an inclined plane. Dip is the direction and angle of steepest descent of that plane. Slip may be along dip, along strike, or oblique. Plane orientation and movement direction answer different questions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the two walls</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>For an inclined fault, the hanging wall is the block above the plane and the footwall lies below it.</li><li>The labels follow the fault plane.</li><li>They are not assigned by which block appears higher on the modern landscape.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare fault movements</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On a normal fault the hanging wall moves down relative to the footwall, commonly during extension.</li><li>On a reverse fault it moves up, commonly during shortening.</li><li>A thrust is a low-angle reverse fault.</li><li>Strike-slip faults have predominantly horizontal movement.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build horsts and grabens</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A graben is a block that has dropped relative to blocks on either side along normal faults. A horst is relatively raised. These are relative displacements within a fault system, so present elevation alone does not define every block’s complete history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Block above an inclined fault plane → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hanging wall</mark>.</li><li>Block below an inclined fault plane → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Footwall</mark>.</li><li>Hanging wall moves down → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Normal fault</mark>.</li><li>Hanging wall moves up → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Reverse fault</mark>.</li><li>Predominantly horizontal slip → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Strike-slip fault</mark>.</li><li>Dropped block between normal faults → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Graben</mark>.</li><li>Relatively raised block → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Horst</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Orient a planar surface · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p6-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p6-Im2.png\" alt=\"Identify the two walls · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Compare fault movements · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Build horsts and grabens · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p8-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p8-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 8 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.4-p9-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.4, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.4 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-5a":{"source":"<p>A <b>fold</b> bends layers. In a simple upright sequence, an anticline arches upward and a syncline forms a trough. A monocline has one main dipping segment between flatter portions. A dome dips outward in several directions; a basin dips inward. Use rock ages where needed to distinguish overturned anticlines and synclines reliably.</p><p>The <b>hinge</b> is the region of greatest curvature, the limbs are the sides, and the axial surface connects hinges through successive layers. Symmetric and asymmetric folds differ in limb geometry. An inclined or overturned axial surface is different from a plunging fold axis; one does not automatically imply the other.</p><p>Compression commonly shortens layered rock through folding. Infer shortening from geometric evidence, and state the viewing direction before translating a cross-section direction into compass directions. Inspect the original examples to distinguish upright, inclined, overturned, and plunging configurations.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Fold types</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <b>fold</b> bends layers.</li><li>In a simple upright sequence, an anticline arches upward and a syncline forms a trough.</li><li>A monocline has one main dipping segment between flatter portions.</li><li>A dome dips outward in several directions.</li><li>a basin dips inward.</li><li>Use rock ages where needed to distinguish overturned anticlines and synclines reliably.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Hinges, limbs, axial surfaces, and plunge</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The <b>hinge</b> is the region of greatest curvature, the limbs are the sides, and the axial surface connects hinges through successive layers.</li><li>Symmetric and asymmetric folds differ in limb geometry.</li><li>An inclined or overturned axial surface is different from a plunging fold axis.</li><li>One does not automatically imply the other.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Interpret shortening and viewpoint</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression commonly shortens layered rock through folding.</li><li>Infer shortening from geometric evidence, and state the viewing direction before translating a cross-section direction into compass directions.</li><li>Inspect the original examples to distinguish upright, inclined, overturned, and plunging configurations.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-fold-distinction":{"source":"<h4>Asymmetry: inspect the cross section</h4><ul><li>Block A has an inclined axial surface and limbs dipping by different amounts. That is the textbook’s asymmetric fold.</li><li>An upright fold has a vertical axial surface. Axial-surface orientation and hinge orientation are separate measurements.</li></ul><h4>Plunge: follow the hinge</h4><ul><li>Block B illustrates a hinge inclined away from horizontal: a plunging fold. Follow the hinge along the block rather than judging only the front face.</li><li>A fold can be both asymmetric and plunging. Unequal limb dips alone do not establish plunge, and plunge alone does not establish an inclined axial surface.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Asymmetry: inspect the cross section</h4><ul><li>Block A has an inclined axial surface and limbs dipping by different amounts. That is the textbook’s asymmetric fold.</li><li>An upright fold has a vertical axial surface. Axial-surface orientation and hinge orientation are separate measurements.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Plunge: follow the hinge</h4><ul><li>Block B illustrates a hinge inclined away from horizontal<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A plunging fold.</li></ul></li><li>Follow the hinge along the block rather than judging only the front face.</li><li>A fold can be both asymmetric and plunging. Unequal limb dips alone do not establish plunge, and plunge alone does not establish an inclined axial surface.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-5-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Shorten layered rock</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression can shorten and bend layered rocks into folds while the layers remain traceable. The curved region of maximum bending is the hinge, and the sides are limbs. Trace the same layer through the whole structure before naming a fold.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare anticlines and synclines</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An anticline has older rocks in its core and a syncline has younger rocks in its core. In an upright fold, anticlines commonly arch upward and synclines trough downward. Overturning complicates simple shape rules, so use age relations where they are known.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Domes, basins, and monoclines</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A dome is a roughly circular structure with older rocks in its center; a basin has younger rocks in its center. A monocline is a step-like bend connecting more gently dipping segments. Modern topography can differ because rocks erode at different rates.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Describe axial orientation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An axial surface joins hinges through successive layers. It can be upright or inclined. A fold is overturned where a limb has rotated beyond vertical. A plunging hinge is inclined rather than horizontal. These descriptions address different aspects of geometry.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Older rocks in the core → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Anticline</mark>.</li><li>Younger rocks in the core → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Syncline</mark>.</li><li>Step-like bend in otherwise gently dipping layers → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Monocline</mark>.</li><li>Circular structure with older central rocks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dome</mark>.</li><li>Circular structure with younger central rocks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Basin</mark>.</li><li>Surface connecting successive hinges → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Axial surface</mark>.</li><li>Two limbs dip by different amounts → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Asymmetric fold</mark>.</li><li>A hinge is inclined rather than horizontal → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Plunging fold</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Shorten layered rock · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Compare anticlines and synclines · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Domes, basins, and monoclines · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Describe axial orientation · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 8 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Shorten layered rock</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression can shorten and bend layered rocks into folds while the layers remain traceable. The curved region of maximum bending is the hinge, and the sides are limbs. Trace the same layer through the whole structure before naming a fold.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare anticlines and synclines</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An anticline has older rocks in its core and a syncline has younger rocks in its core. In an upright fold, anticlines commonly arch upward and synclines trough downward. Overturning complicates simple shape rules, so use age relations where they are known.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Domes, basins, and monoclines</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A dome is a roughly circular structure with older rocks in its center.</li><li>A basin has younger rocks in its center.</li><li>A monocline is a step-like bend connecting more gently dipping segments.</li><li>Modern topography can differ because rocks erode at different rates.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Describe axial orientation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An axial surface joins hinges through successive layers. It can be upright or inclined. A fold is overturned where a limb has rotated beyond vertical. A plunging hinge is inclined rather than horizontal. These descriptions address different aspects of geometry.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Older rocks in the core → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Anticline</mark>.</li><li>Younger rocks in the core → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Syncline</mark>.</li><li>Step-like bend in otherwise gently dipping layers → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Monocline</mark>.</li><li>Circular structure with older central rocks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dome</mark>.</li><li>Circular structure with younger central rocks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Basin</mark>.</li><li>Surface connecting successive hinges → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Axial surface</mark>.</li><li>Two limbs dip by different amounts → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Asymmetric fold</mark>.</li><li>A hinge is inclined rather than horizontal → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Plunging fold</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Shorten layered rock · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Compare anticlines and synclines · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Domes, basins, and monoclines · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Describe axial orientation · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 8 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.5-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.5, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.5 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-6a":{"source":"<p><b>Foliation</b> is a planar metamorphic fabric. It can result from aligned platy minerals, mineral bands, flattened objects, or distributed shearing. Slaty cleavage allows fine-grained rock to split along closely spaced planes. Schistosity reflects aligned visible platy minerals. Gneissic foliation is expressed as alternating mineral-rich bands.</p><p><b>Lineation</b> is a linear feature, such as aligned elongated crystals or stretched objects. A rock can contain both foliation and lineation. Bedding is an inherited sedimentary layer; cleavage can cut across it, so the two must be traced separately.</p><p>Relict features are preserved from the parent rock, including bedding, clasts, or other textures. Shearing can deform grains into lenses and rotate minerals. Diagnose the feature from its geometry before assigning a rock name, and avoid treating every planar surface as original bedding.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Planar fabrics: foliation and cleavage</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Foliation</b> is a planar metamorphic fabric.</li><li>It can result from aligned platy minerals, mineral bands, flattened objects, or distributed shearing.</li><li>Slaty cleavage allows fine-grained rock to split along closely spaced planes.</li><li>Schistosity reflects aligned visible platy minerals.</li><li>Gneissic foliation is expressed as alternating mineral-rich bands.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Lineation versus bedding</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Lineation</b> is a linear feature, such as aligned elongated crystals or stretched objects.</li><li>A rock can contain both foliation and lineation.</li><li>Bedding is an inherited sedimentary layer.</li><li>cleavage can cut across it, so the two must be traced separately.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Relict features and deformation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Relict features are preserved from the parent rock, including bedding, clasts, or other textures.</li><li>Shearing can deform grains into lenses and rotate minerals.</li><li>Diagnose the feature from its geometry before assigning a rock name, and avoid treating every planar surface as original bedding.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-6-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize cleavage</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rock cleavage is a tendency to split along closely spaced surfaces produced by deformation and mineral alignment. It may cut across the original bedding. Slate is a fine-grained rock that commonly has well-developed cleavage.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize foliation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Foliation is a planar fabric expressed by aligned minerals or compositional layers. Some rocks split into thin sheets, while others show coarse light and dark bands. Distinguish this metamorphic fabric from depositional bedding that may still be visible.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize lineation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lineation is a linear fabric: elongated minerals, stretched objects, or intersections of planar features align along a direction. It is not the same geometric feature as a planar foliation. Different lineations reflect different processes and should be described before interpretation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read inherited structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Relict bedding, clasts, and other features can survive metamorphism and record the parent rock. Minerals can rotate, grow, and become elongated during deformation. Compare these younger fabrics with preserved older structures rather than assuming all lines are bedding.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Closely spaced splitting planes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cleavage</mark>.</li><li>Planar alignment or mineral banding → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Foliation</mark>.</li><li>Aligned elongated objects → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Lineation</mark>.</li><li>Sedimentary layer preserved through metamorphism → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Relict bedding</mark>.</li><li>A metamorphic plane cuts an older sedimentary layer → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cleavage across bedding</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Recognize cleavage · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Recognize foliation · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Recognize lineation · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Read inherited structures · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 8 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize cleavage</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rock cleavage is a tendency to split along closely spaced surfaces produced by deformation and mineral alignment. It may cut across the original bedding. Slate is a fine-grained rock that commonly has well-developed cleavage.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize foliation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Foliation is a planar fabric expressed by aligned minerals or compositional layers. Some rocks split into thin sheets, while others show coarse light and dark bands. Distinguish this metamorphic fabric from depositional bedding that may still be visible.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize lineation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lineation is a linear fabric<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Elongated minerals, stretched objects, or intersections of planar features align along a direction.</li></ul></li><li>It is not the same geometric feature as a planar foliation.</li><li>Different lineations reflect different processes and should be described before interpretation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read inherited structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Relict bedding, clasts, and other features can survive metamorphism and record the parent rock. Minerals can rotate, grow, and become elongated during deformation. Compare these younger fabrics with preserved older structures rather than assuming all lines are bedding.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Closely spaced splitting planes → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cleavage</mark>.</li><li>Planar alignment or mineral banding → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Foliation</mark>.</li><li>Aligned elongated objects → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Lineation</mark>.</li><li>Sedimentary layer preserved through metamorphism → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Relict bedding</mark>.</li><li>A metamorphic plane cuts an older sedimentary layer → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cleavage across bedding</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Recognize cleavage · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Recognize foliation · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Recognize lineation · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p8-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.6-p8-Im1.png\" alt=\"Read inherited structures · textbook section 8.6, PDF page 8\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.6 · PDF page 8 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-7a":{"source":"<p>In the supplied progressive shale sequence, <b>slate → phyllite → schist → gneiss</b> records increasing metamorphic conditions. Slate is fine grained and dull with cleavage; phyllite has a sheen; schist has visible aligned minerals; gneiss has mineral banding. This is a course model for suitable parent composition, not a universal path followed by every rock.</p><p>Quartz sandstone can become <b>quartzite</b>, whose interlocking grains make it strong and can cause fractures to cross grains. Limestone can become <b>marble</b>, dominated by recrystallized carbonate minerals. Basalt and other suitable volcanic rocks can form greenstone; hotter conditions can form amphibolite. Contact metamorphism can produce hornfels; partial melting can form migmatite.</p><p>Identify grain size, fabric, minerals, and preserved parent features together. Schist is named partly by texture, so the mineral assemblage can vary. Marble and quartzite often lack strong foliation, even though both are metamorphic.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The progressive shale sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied progressive shale sequence, <b>slate → phyllite → schist → gneiss</b> records increasing metamorphic conditions.</li><li>Slate is fine grained and dull with cleavage.</li><li>Phyllite has a sheen.</li><li>Schist has visible aligned minerals.</li><li>gneiss has mineral banding.</li><li>This is a course model for suitable parent composition, not a universal path followed by every rock.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Parent rocks and other metamorphic products</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Quartz sandstone can become <b>quartzite</b>, whose interlocking grains make it strong and can cause fractures to cross grains.</li><li>Limestone can become <b>marble</b>, dominated by recrystallized carbonate minerals.</li><li>Basalt and other suitable volcanic rocks can form greenstone.</li><li>Hotter conditions can form amphibolite.</li><li>Contact metamorphism can produce hornfels.</li><li>Partial melting can form migmatite.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use texture and minerals together</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Identify grain size, fabric, minerals, and preserved parent features together.</li><li>Schist is named partly by texture, so the mineral assemblage can vary.</li><li>Marble and quartzite often lack strong foliation, even though both are metamorphic.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-grade-photos":{"source":"<h4>Compare the four original photographs</h4><ul><li>The textbook progression for metamorphosed shale is <b>slate → phyllite → schist → gneiss</b>, from lower to higher grade.</li><li>B: slate is dull with fine cleavage. D: phyllite has a mica sheen. A: schist has visible mineral crystals and schistosity. C: gneiss separates light and dark minerals into bands.</li></ul><h4>What the exercise tests</h4><ul><li>The textbook names remain printed on these unaltered photographs. The ordering task tests grade relationships while you inspect the textures; it does not claim blind specimen identification.</li><li>This is the supplied shale-derived sequence. Different starting compositions follow different metamorphic paths.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the four original photographs</h4><ul><li>The textbook progression for metamorphosed shale is <b>slate → phyllite → schist → gneiss</b>, from lower to higher grade.</li><li>B<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Slate is dull with fine cleavage.</li></ul></li><li>D<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Phyllite has a mica sheen.</li></ul></li><li>A<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Schist has visible mineral crystals and schistosity.</li></ul></li><li>C<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Gneiss separates light and dark minerals into bands.</li></ul></li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What the exercise tests</h4><ul><li>The textbook names remain printed on these unaltered photographs.</li><li>The ordering task tests grade relationships while you inspect the textures.</li><li>It does not claim blind specimen identification.</li><li>This is the supplied shale-derived sequence. Different starting compositions follow different metamorphic paths.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-7-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow a shale-derived sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>For suitable shale-rich parent material, increasing metamorphic grade can produce slate, then phyllite, schist, and gneiss. Slate is dull and fine grained, phyllite has a sheen, schist has visible aligned minerals, and gneiss commonly displays compositional bands.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transform sandstone and limestone</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Quartz-rich sandstone can become quartzite as quartz grains recrystallize and interlock; fractures may cut across grains. Limestone can become marble, a rock of recrystallized carbonate minerals. Both can have weak foliation, so use composition and texture together.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transform igneous parent rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Mafic volcanic rocks can produce greenstone or other metamorphic rocks as mineral assemblages change. Felsic volcanic and plutonic rocks have different compositions and can form different products. A metamorphic name depends on the rock and its observable features, not grade alone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use multiple clues</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Hornfels commonly forms when rock is heated near an intrusion. Gneissic banding can form in several parent compositions, and the parent of a particular gneiss may remain uncertain. Mineralogy, grain size, fabric, and field context jointly support identification.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fine dull rock with cleavage → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Slate</mark>.</li><li>Fine rock with a silky sheen → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Phyllite</mark>.</li><li>Visible aligned mica-rich minerals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Schist</mark>.</li><li>Coarse light and dark mineral bands → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Gneiss</mark>.</li><li>Interlocking quartz grains derived from sandstone → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Quartzite</mark>.</li><li>Recrystallized carbonate derived from limestone → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Marble</mark>.</li><li>Contact-heated, fine-grained rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hornfels</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Follow a shale-derived sequence · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Transform sandstone and limestone · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Transform igneous parent rocks · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Use multiple clues · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p6-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p6-Im3.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow a shale-derived sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>For suitable shale-rich parent material, increasing metamorphic grade can produce slate, then phyllite, schist, and gneiss. Slate is dull and fine grained, phyllite has a sheen, schist has visible aligned minerals, and gneiss commonly displays compositional bands.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transform sandstone and limestone</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Quartz-rich sandstone can become quartzite as quartz grains recrystallize and interlock.</li><li>Fractures may cut across grains.</li><li>Limestone can become marble, a rock of recrystallized carbonate minerals.</li><li>Both can have weak foliation, so use composition and texture together.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transform igneous parent rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Mafic volcanic rocks can produce greenstone or other metamorphic rocks as mineral assemblages change. Felsic volcanic and plutonic rocks have different compositions and can form different products. A metamorphic name depends on the rock and its observable features, not grade alone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use multiple clues</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Hornfels commonly forms when rock is heated near an intrusion. Gneissic banding can form in several parent compositions, and the parent of a particular gneiss may remain uncertain. Mineralogy, grain size, fabric, and field context jointly support identification.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fine dull rock with cleavage → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Slate</mark>.</li><li>Fine rock with a silky sheen → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Phyllite</mark>.</li><li>Visible aligned mica-rich minerals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Schist</mark>.</li><li>Coarse light and dark mineral bands → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Gneiss</mark>.</li><li>Interlocking quartz grains derived from sandstone → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Quartzite</mark>.</li><li>Recrystallized carbonate derived from limestone → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Marble</mark>.</li><li>Contact-heated, fine-grained rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hornfels</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Follow a shale-derived sequence · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Transform sandstone and limestone · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Transform igneous parent rocks · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p10-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p10-Im2.png\" alt=\"Use multiple clues · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 10\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 10 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p6-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.7-p6-Im3.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.7, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.7 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-8a":{"source":"<p>Metamorphism changes minerals, textures, or both through heat, pressure, stress, and fluids while the rock remains predominantly solid. Burial and tectonic thickening raise pressure and temperature. Magma heats neighboring rocks; circulating hot fluids can alter minerals. Shearing can deform and heat rock locally.</p><p><b>Recrystallization</b> changes crystal size and texture. <b>Neocrystallization</b> grows new minerals as old minerals become unstable. Pressure solution dissolves material where stress is greatest and can move it elsewhere. Chemical remobilization concentrates different minerals into bands. Rotation, flattening, and stretching change geometry.</p><p>Foliation develops through alignment, growth, dissolution, and deformation. Metamorphism and deformation can occur together or independently. Crossing into melting is a separate transition; do not describe solid-state recrystallization as simply melting and freezing again.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Causes of metamorphism</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Metamorphism changes minerals, textures, or both through heat, pressure, stress, and fluids while the rock remains predominantly solid.</li><li>Burial and tectonic thickening raise pressure and temperature.</li><li>Magma heats neighboring rocks.</li><li>Circulating hot fluids can alter minerals.</li><li>Shearing can deform and heat rock locally.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Processes that change the rock</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Recrystallization</b> changes crystal size and texture.</li><li><b>Neocrystallization</b> grows new minerals as old minerals become unstable.</li><li>Pressure solution dissolves material where stress is greatest and can move it elsewhere.</li><li>Chemical remobilization concentrates different minerals into bands.</li><li>Rotation, flattening, and stretching change geometry.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Foliation and the limit at melting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Foliation develops through alignment, growth, dissolution, and deformation.</li><li>Metamorphism and deformation can occur together or independently.</li><li>Crossing into melting is a separate transition.</li><li>do not describe solid-state recrystallization as simply melting and freezing again.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-process-set":{"source":"<h4>Chemical and physical changes</h4><ul><li>Recrystallization and growth of new minerals can change grain size and arrangement. Remobilization redistributes constituents; pressure solution dissolves highly stressed grain edges and deposits material elsewhere or removes it in fluids.</li><li>Physical deformation can flatten grains, rotate platy minerals into alignment, or shear and stretch crystals. These processes can accompany metamorphism; no single specimen must record every process.</li></ul><h4>Connect the setting to the texture</h4><ul><li>Regional metamorphism commonly combines heating with deformation, producing foliation and/or lineation. Foliation is planar; lineation is linear.</li><li>Contact heating can produce new or larger minerals without strong differential stress. Such rocks commonly lack foliation and lineation. Metamorphism does not require complete melting.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Chemical and physical changes</h4><ul><li>Recrystallization and growth of new minerals can change grain size and arrangement.</li><li>Remobilization redistributes constituents.</li><li>Pressure solution dissolves highly stressed grain edges and deposits material elsewhere or removes it in fluids.</li><li>Physical deformation can flatten grains, rotate platy minerals into alignment, or shear and stretch crystals.</li><li>These processes can accompany metamorphism.</li><li>No single specimen must record every process.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the setting to the texture</h4><ul><li>Regional metamorphism commonly combines heating with deformation, producing foliation and/or lineation.</li><li>Foliation is planar.</li><li>Lineation is linear.</li><li>Contact heating can produce new or larger minerals without strong differential stress. Such rocks commonly lack foliation and lineation. Metamorphism does not require complete melting.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-metamorphic-causes":{"source":"<h4>Change the rock’s physical and chemical conditions</h4><ul><li>Increasing temperature and pressure can make existing minerals unstable and favor new minerals. Pressure increases with burial; temperature increases with depth and near magma.</li><li>Hot fluids can carry dissolved constituents into, through, or out of rock, changing its chemistry and mineral assemblage.</li></ul><h4>Do not require one setting or one fabric</h4><ul><li>Heating beside magma can cause contact metamorphism; broader heating with deformation can cause regional metamorphism.</li><li>Strong tectonic deformation and complete melting are not required in every metamorphic setting. The chemical and temperature–pressure conditions matter.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Change the rock’s physical and chemical conditions</h4><ul><li>Increasing temperature and pressure can make existing minerals unstable and favor new minerals.</li><li>Pressure increases with burial.</li><li>Temperature increases with depth and near magma.</li><li>Hot fluids can carry dissolved constituents into, through, or out of rock, changing its chemistry and mineral assemblage.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Do not require one setting or one fabric</h4><ul><li>Heating beside magma can cause contact metamorphism.</li><li>Broader heating with deformation can cause regional metamorphism.</li><li>Strong tectonic deformation and complete melting are not required in every metamorphic setting. The chemical and temperature–pressure conditions matter.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-8-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Conditions drive solid-state change</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Heat, pressure, differential stress, and chemically active fluids can change rock minerals or texture. Burial, tectonic activity, nearby magma, and fluid circulation provide different combinations of those drivers. Metamorphism and deformation may occur together or independently.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Change grain size or mineral identity</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Recrystallization can enlarge existing mineral crystals. Neocrystallization forms new minerals as reactions make a different assemblage stable. These terms describe different changes; neither means the whole rock must melt and freeze again.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Dissolve, transport, and rearrange</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Material can dissolve at stressed grain contacts and move in fluids. Chemical components can be redistributed, producing different mineral-rich layers. Whether the bulk composition changes depends on movement into or out of the rock, which must be supported by evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deform mineral fabrics</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>During deformation, minerals and objects can rotate, flatten, stretch, or form aligned fabrics. Metamorphism can preserve older features as relicts while adding new minerals and foliation. Read the sequence of overprinting rather than assuming all features formed simultaneously.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Existing grains grow into larger crystals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Recrystallization</mark>.</li><li>New mineral species grow → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Neocrystallization</mark>.</li><li>Material dissolves at stressed contacts → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Pressure solution</mark>.</li><li>Minerals align into a planar fabric → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Foliation</mark>.</li><li>Minerals move into different bands → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Chemical remobilization</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Conditions drive solid-state change · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Change grain size or mineral identity · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Dissolve, transport, and rearrange · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p9-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p9-Im1.png\" alt=\"Deform mineral fabrics · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Conditions drive solid-state change</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Heat, pressure, differential stress, and chemically active fluids can change rock minerals or texture. Burial, tectonic activity, nearby magma, and fluid circulation provide different combinations of those drivers. Metamorphism and deformation may occur together or independently.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Change grain size or mineral identity</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Recrystallization can enlarge existing mineral crystals.</li><li>Neocrystallization forms new minerals as reactions make a different assemblage stable.</li><li>These terms describe different changes.</li><li>Neither means the whole rock must melt and freeze again.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Dissolve, transport, and rearrange</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Material can dissolve at stressed grain contacts and move in fluids. Chemical components can be redistributed, producing different mineral-rich layers. Whether the bulk composition changes depends on movement into or out of the rock, which must be supported by evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deform mineral fabrics</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>During deformation, minerals and objects can rotate, flatten, stretch, or form aligned fabrics. Metamorphism can preserve older features as relicts while adding new minerals and foliation. Read the sequence of overprinting rather than assuming all features formed simultaneously.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Existing grains grow into larger crystals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Recrystallization</mark>.</li><li>New mineral species grow → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Neocrystallization</mark>.</li><li>Material dissolves at stressed contacts → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Pressure solution</mark>.</li><li>Minerals align into a planar fabric → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Foliation</mark>.</li><li>Minerals move into different bands → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Chemical remobilization</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Conditions drive solid-state change · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Change grain size or mineral identity · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Dissolve, transport, and rearrange · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p9-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.8-p9-Im1.png\" alt=\"Deform mineral fabrics · textbook section 8.8, PDF page 9\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.8 · PDF page 9 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-9a":{"source":"<p>Metamorphic grade reflects the conditions a rock experienced and can be constrained by mineral assemblages. In the course pressure–temperature model, normal regional conditions increase pressure and temperature together. <b>High-temperature/low-pressure</b> conditions occur near shallow magma bodies; <b>high-pressure/low-temperature</b> conditions occur where relatively cold material is subducted.</p><p>At an ocean–continent convergent margin, a subducting slab, the overriding mountain belt, and a shallow intrusion experience different conditions. Contact metamorphism surrounds heat sources; regional metamorphism affects broader buried or thickened regions. Continental collision can thicken crust and produce extensive metamorphism.</p><p>Read the axes and fields on the supplied graph. A location on a pressure–temperature diagram is not automatically a geographic depth with a single universal temperature. The October 6 lecture reaches subduction and contact settings; its collision discussion is explicitly left for later.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Grade and pressure–temperature conditions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Metamorphic grade reflects the conditions a rock experienced and can be constrained by mineral assemblages.</li><li>In the course pressure–temperature model, normal regional conditions increase pressure and temperature together.</li><li><b>High-temperature/low-pressure</b> conditions occur near shallow magma bodies.</li><li><b>high-pressure/low-temperature</b> conditions occur where relatively cold material is subducted.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Contact, regional, and collision settings</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At an ocean–continent convergent margin, a subducting slab, the overriding mountain belt, and a shallow intrusion experience different conditions.</li><li>Contact metamorphism surrounds heat sources.</li><li>Regional metamorphism affects broader buried or thickened regions.</li><li>Continental collision can thicken crust and produce extensive metamorphism.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the graph and coverage limits</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Read the axes and fields on the supplied graph.</li><li>A location on a pressure–temperature diagram is not automatically a geographic depth with a single universal temperature.</li><li>The October 6 lecture reaches subduction and contact settings.</li><li>Its collision discussion is explicitly left for later.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-9-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read pressure and temperature separately</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied diagram distinguishes high-temperature, low-pressure conditions; high-pressure, relatively low-temperature conditions; and typical regional conditions. Pressure increases with burial, but temperature depends on the thermal history. High pressure does not always mean the hottest rock.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Heat beside magma</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A shallow intrusion heats adjacent country rock and creates a contact aureole. That setting can reach high temperature at relatively low pressure. The aureole’s extent and mineral assemblages depend on heat, host composition, and fluids.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Subduct a relatively cold slab</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At an ocean–continent convergent boundary, a relatively cold slab descends and experiences increasing pressure before it fully warms. High-pressure, low-temperature metamorphism can occur along the subduction zone; a shallow magma body has a contrasting pressure–temperature history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bury rocks during collision</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>During continent–continent collision, thrusting and crustal thickening bury and heat rocks over broad regions. Regional metamorphism and deformation can accompany one another. Map the location and conditions separately rather than assigning one condition to an entire plate boundary.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shallow rock heated near an intrusion → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">High temperature, low pressure</mark>.</li><li>Relatively cold material carried deep in subduction → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">High pressure, low temperature</mark>.</li><li>Broad burial and heating in a mountain belt → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Regional metamorphism</mark>.</li><li>Zone heated around an intrusion → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Contact aureole</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p2-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p2-Im2.png\" alt=\"Read pressure and temperature separately · textbook section 8.9, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.9 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Subduct a relatively cold slab · textbook section 8.9, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.9 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p5-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p5-Im3.png\" alt=\"Bury rocks during collision · textbook section 8.9, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.9 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read pressure and temperature separately</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied diagram distinguishes high-temperature, low-pressure conditions.</li><li>High-pressure, relatively low-temperature conditions.</li><li>And typical regional conditions.</li><li>Pressure increases with burial, but temperature depends on the thermal history.</li><li>High pressure does not always mean the hottest rock.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Heat beside magma</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A shallow intrusion heats adjacent country rock and creates a contact aureole. That setting can reach high temperature at relatively low pressure. The aureole’s extent and mineral assemblages depend on heat, host composition, and fluids.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Subduct a relatively cold slab</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At an ocean–continent convergent boundary, a relatively cold slab descends and experiences increasing pressure before it fully warms.</li><li>High-pressure, low-temperature metamorphism can occur along the subduction zone.</li><li>A shallow magma body has a contrasting pressure–temperature history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bury rocks during collision</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>During continent–continent collision, thrusting and crustal thickening bury and heat rocks over broad regions. Regional metamorphism and deformation can accompany one another. Map the location and conditions separately rather than assigning one condition to an entire plate boundary.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shallow rock heated near an intrusion → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">High temperature, low pressure</mark>.</li><li>Relatively cold material carried deep in subduction → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">High pressure, low temperature</mark>.</li><li>Broad burial and heating in a mountain belt → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Regional metamorphism</mark>.</li><li>Zone heated around an intrusion → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Contact aureole</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p2-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p2-Im2.png\" alt=\"Read pressure and temperature separately · textbook section 8.9, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.9 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Subduct a relatively cold slab · textbook section 8.9, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.9 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p5-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.9-p5-Im3.png\" alt=\"Bury rocks during collision · textbook section 8.9, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.9 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-10a":{"source":"<p>Extension can form continental rifts, normal faults, tilted blocks, and grabens. Crust becomes thinner as it stretches. Magma and hot fluids can alter rock during rifting and ocean opening. Hydrothermal metamorphism is especially important where seawater circulates through hot rock at mid-ocean ridges.</p><p>Strike-slip faults accommodate predominantly horizontal motion and can occur at transform boundaries or within continents. Shearing changes structures at depth. A bend can make neighboring material converge or separate, creating local uplift or subsidence even though the main motion is lateral.</p><p>Interpret the entire geometry: arrows, fault trace, matching displaced features, and the bend direction. Straight horizontal motion should not be assigned the same vertical effect as every bend. Keep plate-scale setting separate from the smaller local stress field.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Extension and rifting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Extension can form continental rifts, normal faults, tilted blocks, and grabens.</li><li>Crust becomes thinner as it stretches.</li><li>Magma and hot fluids can alter rock during rifting and ocean opening.</li><li>Hydrothermal metamorphism is especially important where seawater circulates through hot rock at mid-ocean ridges.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Strike-slip motion and bends</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Strike-slip faults accommodate predominantly horizontal motion and can occur at transform boundaries or within continents.</li><li>Shearing changes structures at depth.</li><li>A bend can make neighboring material converge or separate, creating local uplift or subsidence even though the main motion is lateral.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Interpret the full fault geometry</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Interpret the entire geometry<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Arrows, fault trace, matching displaced features, and the bend direction.</li></ul></li><li>Straight horizontal motion should not be assigned the same vertical effect as every bend.</li><li>Keep plate-scale setting separate from the smaller local stress field.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-fault-blocks":{"source":"<h4>Compare with both neighboring blocks</h4><ul><li>A <b>graben</b> is a block down relative to the blocks on either side, bounded by normal faults. A <b>horst</b> is relatively high between neighboring blocks.</li><li>Normal-fault movement lowers the hanging wall relative to its footwall. Continental extension stretches and thins the crust and can produce these fault-bounded blocks.</li></ul><h4>Do not reverse the tectonic interpretation</h4><ul><li>A high horst does not require regional compression. Its height is relative to adjacent blocks in the normal-fault system.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare with both neighboring blocks</h4><ul><li>A <b>graben</b> is a block down relative to the blocks on either side, bounded by normal faults. A <b>horst</b> is relatively high between neighboring blocks.</li><li>Normal-fault movement lowers the hanging wall relative to its footwall. Continental extension stretches and thins the crust and can produce these fault-bounded blocks.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Do not reverse the tectonic interpretation</h4><ul><li>A high horst does not require regional compression. Its height is relative to adjacent blocks in the normal-fault system.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-10-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Rift a continent</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continental extension stretches and thins crust, commonly forming normal faults, tilted blocks, and grabens. Magma and warm fluids may accompany rifting. Extension and the pressure–temperature response vary from place to place during the evolution of a rift.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Open an ocean basin</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>As continental rifting progresses to seafloor spreading, new oceanic crust forms at a ridge. Seawater can circulate through hot fractured rock and cause hydrothermal metamorphism. The hot-water pathway is especially important at mid-ocean ridges.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Move horizontally on strike-slip faults</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A strike-slip fault accommodates primarily horizontal relative motion. Looking across the fault, the opposite side moves right in a right-lateral fault and left in a left-lateral fault. Offset streams and roads preserve the displacement of originally continuous markers.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bends add local complexity</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A bend or step in a strike-slip system can make adjacent rocks converge or pull apart. Local shortening can build folds and ridges, while local extension can produce a basin. The main lateral motion alone does not determine the sign of every bend.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Downdropped block during crustal extension → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Graben</mark>.</li><li>Circulation through hot oceanic rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hydrothermal metamorphism</mark>.</li><li>Opposite side appears displaced right → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Right-lateral strike slip</mark>.</li><li>Opposite side appears displaced left → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Left-lateral strike slip</mark>.</li><li>Strike-slip step where blocks separate → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Releasing bend</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Rift a continent · textbook section 8.10, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.10 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Open an ocean basin · textbook section 8.10, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.10 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p4-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p4-Im3.png\" alt=\"Move horizontally on strike-slip faults · textbook section 8.10, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.10 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p5-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p5-Im3.png\" alt=\"Bends add local complexity · textbook section 8.10, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.10 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Rift a continent</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continental extension stretches and thins crust, commonly forming normal faults, tilted blocks, and grabens. Magma and warm fluids may accompany rifting. Extension and the pressure–temperature response vary from place to place during the evolution of a rift.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Open an ocean basin</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>As continental rifting progresses to seafloor spreading, new oceanic crust forms at a ridge. Seawater can circulate through hot fractured rock and cause hydrothermal metamorphism. The hot-water pathway is especially important at mid-ocean ridges.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Move horizontally on strike-slip faults</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A strike-slip fault accommodates primarily horizontal relative motion. Looking across the fault, the opposite side moves right in a right-lateral fault and left in a left-lateral fault. Offset streams and roads preserve the displacement of originally continuous markers.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bends add local complexity</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A bend or step in a strike-slip system can make adjacent rocks converge or pull apart. Local shortening can build folds and ridges, while local extension can produce a basin. The main lateral motion alone does not determine the sign of every bend.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Downdropped block during crustal extension → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Graben</mark>.</li><li>Circulation through hot oceanic rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hydrothermal metamorphism</mark>.</li><li>Opposite side appears displaced right → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Right-lateral strike slip</mark>.</li><li>Opposite side appears displaced left → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Left-lateral strike slip</mark>.</li><li>Strike-slip step where blocks separate → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Releasing bend</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Rift a continent · textbook section 8.10, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.10 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Open an ocean basin · textbook section 8.10, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.10 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p4-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p4-Im3.png\" alt=\"Move horizontally on strike-slip faults · textbook section 8.10, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.10 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p5-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.10-p5-Im3.png\" alt=\"Bends add local complexity · textbook section 8.10, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.10 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-11a":{"source":"<p>Faults, folds, joints, and cleavage can form during the same deformation episode. A fault propagating upward can bend overlying beds into a monocline. Beds moving over a bend in a thrust can fold into anticlines and synclines. Thrust belts combine shortening, stacked sheets, and related fabrics.</p><p>Cleavage commonly lies approximately parallel to the axial surfaces of folds formed in the same event, and it can cut original bedding. This relation helps locate fold geometry where only parts of the rock are exposed. The relation is a structural clue, not proof that all fabrics everywhere formed simultaneously.</p><p>Use cross-cutting and overprinting to distinguish separate events. Older thrusts can later be folded or faulted. Observations must support a shared history; visual proximity alone does not establish age or cause.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Related faults, folds, and fabrics</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Faults, folds, joints, and cleavage can form during the same deformation episode.</li><li>A fault propagating upward can bend overlying beds into a monocline.</li><li>Beds moving over a bend in a thrust can fold into anticlines and synclines.</li><li>Thrust belts combine shortening, stacked sheets, and related fabrics.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Cleavage and axial surfaces</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Cleavage commonly lies approximately parallel to the axial surfaces of folds formed in the same event, and it can cut original bedding.</li><li>This relation helps locate fold geometry where only parts of the rock are exposed.</li><li>The relation is a structural clue, not proof that all fabrics everywhere formed simultaneously.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish separate events</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Use cross-cutting and overprinting to distinguish separate events.</li><li>Older thrusts can later be folded or faulted.</li><li>Observations must support a shared history.</li><li>Visual proximity alone does not establish age or cause.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-structure-associations":{"source":"<h4>Match the structure to the mechanism</h4><ul><li>Stresses from shearing and bending near faults commonly produce joints and small folds. They can suggest a nearby fault, but are not unique proof of one.</li><li>Movement on normal faults can rotate fault-bounded blocks like books on a shelf, tilting their layers.</li></ul><h4>Follow a changing fault surface</h4><ul><li>Layers may bend into a monocline above an upward-propagating fault before the fault cuts them.</li><li>As layers move over a bend in a thrust, they can form an anticline near the front of the thrust sheet and a syncline farther back. Joints and cleavage can accompany these folds.</li><li>These relationships describe possible associations across different settings. They do not mean every fault produces every structure.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Match the structure to the mechanism</h4><ul><li>Stresses from shearing and bending near faults commonly produce joints and small folds. They can suggest a nearby fault, but are not unique proof of one.</li><li>Movement on normal faults can rotate fault-bounded blocks like books on a shelf, tilting their layers.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow a changing fault surface</h4><ul><li>Layers may bend into a monocline above an upward-propagating fault before the fault cuts them.</li><li>As layers move over a bend in a thrust, they can form an anticline near the front of the thrust sheet and a syncline farther back. Joints and cleavage can accompany these folds.</li><li>These relationships describe possible associations across different settings. They do not mean every fault produces every structure.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-11-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Link deformation structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Faults, joints, folds, tilted layers, and shear zones can form in related systems. A fault propagating upward can bend layers above it, and slip over a bend in a thrust fault can fold the overlying rocks. Structural associations must be supported by geometry.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build a fold-and-thrust belt</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression can move thrust sheets over one another and shorten a region. Beds in and above the sheets may form anticlines and synclines. Related joints, smaller faults, and metamorphic fabrics can occur, but not every nearby structure must share the same age.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare bedding with cleavage</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Cleavage in a fold commonly lies roughly parallel to the axial surface. It cuts bedding at different angles on the limbs and may be nearly perpendicular to bedding near a hinge in the supplied example. Trace both surfaces across the fold.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate shared from later events</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A younger fault can cut an older fold, and an older fault can itself be folded. Use cross-cutting, overprinting, and the geometry of displaced markers to order events. Proximity alone does not prove two structures formed together.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fault bends overlying layers → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault-related fold</mark>.</li><li>Low-angle reverse faults stack sheets → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fold-and-thrust belt</mark>.</li><li>Metamorphic splitting crosses beds → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cleavage</mark>.</li><li>Surface through successive hinges → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Axial surface</mark>.</li><li>A later structure cuts an earlier one → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cross-cutting relation</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Link deformation structures · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p5-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p5-Im3.png\" alt=\"Build a fold-and-thrust belt · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p6-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p6-Im3.png\" alt=\"Compare bedding with cleavage · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Separate shared from later events · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Link deformation structures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Faults, joints, folds, tilted layers, and shear zones can form in related systems. A fault propagating upward can bend layers above it, and slip over a bend in a thrust fault can fold the overlying rocks. Structural associations must be supported by geometry.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build a fold-and-thrust belt</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression can move thrust sheets over one another and shorten a region. Beds in and above the sheets may form anticlines and synclines. Related joints, smaller faults, and metamorphic fabrics can occur, but not every nearby structure must share the same age.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare bedding with cleavage</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Cleavage in a fold commonly lies roughly parallel to the axial surface. It cuts bedding at different angles on the limbs and may be nearly perpendicular to bedding near a hinge in the supplied example. Trace both surfaces across the fold.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate shared from later events</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A younger fault can cut an older fold, and an older fault can itself be folded. Use cross-cutting, overprinting, and the geometry of displaced markers to order events. Proximity alone does not prove two structures formed together.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fault bends overlying layers → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault-related fold</mark>.</li><li>Low-angle reverse faults stack sheets → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fold-and-thrust belt</mark>.</li><li>Metamorphic splitting crosses beds → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cleavage</mark>.</li><li>Surface through successive hinges → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Axial surface</mark>.</li><li>A later structure cuts an earlier one → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cross-cutting relation</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Link deformation structures · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p5-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p5-Im3.png\" alt=\"Build a fold-and-thrust belt · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p6-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p6-Im3.png\" alt=\"Compare bedding with cleavage · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.11-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Separate shared from later events · textbook section 8.11, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.11 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-12a":{"source":"<p>Weathering and erosion exploit joints, faults, and cleavage. Joint sets divide rock into blocks and influence cliffs. Fault scarps offset the land surface; fault breccia, crushed zones, and aligned notches can expose traces. Striations on a fault surface record slip direction.</p><p>Erosion of inclined resistant layers can form dip slopes and hogbacks. A ridge is not automatically an anticline: landforms depend on resistance and erosion as well as structure. Metamorphic foliation can create slabs, shiny outcrops, or complex ridges and folds.</p><p>Infer geometry by tracing beds and fabrics, then comparing their orientation with the landscape. Distinguish the modern erosional surface from the original rock structure. Multiple observations are needed before locating a fault or determining its motion.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Fractures and fault traces</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Weathering and erosion exploit joints, faults, and cleavage.</li><li>Joint sets divide rock into blocks and influence cliffs.</li><li>Fault scarps offset the land surface.</li><li>Fault breccia, crushed zones, and aligned notches can expose traces.</li><li>Striations on a fault surface record slip direction.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Erosion of beds and metamorphic fabrics</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Erosion of inclined resistant layers can form dip slopes and hogbacks.</li><li>A ridge is not automatically an anticline<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Landforms depend on resistance and erosion as well as structure.</li></ul></li><li>Metamorphic foliation can create slabs, shiny outcrops, or complex ridges and folds.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare rock structure with the landscape</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Infer geometry by tracing beds and fabrics, then comparing their orientation with the landscape.</li><li>Distinguish the modern erosional surface from the original rock structure.</li><li>Multiple observations are needed before locating a fault or determining its motion.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-intro":{"source":"<p>The Chapter 8 introduction shows the <b>Valley and Ridge Province</b> in the Appalachians. Trace the curved ridges on the map, then compare them with the cross section. Folded sedimentary layers and thrust faults record shortening. Cleavage may cut the older bedding because it developed during later deformation.</p><p>The source describes several Paleozoic deformation episodes, culminating in collision between Africa and eastern North America around 300 million years ago. Shortening stacked and folded rocks while burial and heating promoted metamorphism. Subsequent erosion removed weaker layers more rapidly, leaving resistant units as ridges.</p><p>Use this sequence to separate processes: deposition first creates layers; deformation changes their geometry; mineral reactions and fabric development can occur during metamorphism; erosion exposes and reshapes the structure. A ridge is not automatically an anticline. Read bedding, rock resistance, and map geometry together.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the Valley and Ridge pattern</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Chapter 8 introduction shows the <b>Valley and Ridge Province</b> in the Appalachians.</li><li>Trace the curved ridges on the map, then compare them with the cross section.</li><li>Folded sedimentary layers and thrust faults record shortening.</li><li>Cleavage may cut the older bedding because it developed during later deformation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Collision, metamorphism, and erosion</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The source describes several Paleozoic deformation episodes, culminating in collision between Africa and eastern North America around 300 million years ago.</li><li>Shortening stacked and folded rocks while burial and heating promoted metamorphism.</li><li>Subsequent erosion removed weaker layers more rapidly, leaving resistant units as ridges.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate the geological processes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Use this sequence to separate processes<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Deposition first creates layers.</li><li>Deformation changes their geometry.</li><li>Mineral reactions and fabric development can occur during metamorphism.</li><li>Erosion exposes and reshapes the structure.</li></ul></li><li>A ridge is not automatically an anticline.</li><li>Read bedding, rock resistance, and map geometry together.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-12-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Joints guide erosion</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Joint sets divide rock into blocks and provide pathways along which weathering can act. Their orientation can influence cliffs, pillars, notches, and drainage. Recognize the fracture patterns in an outcrop before attributing them to one particular stress history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Find a fault in the terrain</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Faults can produce aligned notches, scarps, crushed angular rock, displaced layers, and striations. Several observations are more persuasive than a straight valley alone. A scarp shows relative topographic offset but does not by itself identify the complete fault history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Erode tilted and folded layers</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Resistant tilted beds can support ridges and dip slopes while weaker units erode into lower ground. A ridge does not automatically mark an anticline, and a valley does not automatically mark a syncline. Follow rock layers and ages through the landscape.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read metamorphic outcrops</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Foliation, cleavage, and resistant folded layers can influence the shapes of metamorphic outcrops. Modern topography is a product of both structure and erosion. Distinguish a visible rock fabric from a hypothesis about when it formed.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Repeated fractures divide rock into blocks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Joint sets</mark>.</li><li>Crushed rock and offset marker beds → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault-zone evidence</mark>.</li><li>Slope approximately follows an inclined bed → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dip slope</mark>.</li><li>Resistant tilted bed forms a narrow ridge → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hogback</mark>.</li><li>Planar mineral alignment in outcrop → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Foliation</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Joints guide erosion · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Find a fault in the terrain · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Erode tilted and folded layers · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Read metamorphic outcrops · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Joints guide erosion</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Joint sets divide rock into blocks and provide pathways along which weathering can act. Their orientation can influence cliffs, pillars, notches, and drainage. Recognize the fracture patterns in an outcrop before attributing them to one particular stress history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Find a fault in the terrain</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Faults can produce aligned notches, scarps, crushed angular rock, displaced layers, and striations. Several observations are more persuasive than a straight valley alone. A scarp shows relative topographic offset but does not by itself identify the complete fault history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Erode tilted and folded layers</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Resistant tilted beds can support ridges and dip slopes while weaker units erode into lower ground. A ridge does not automatically mark an anticline, and a valley does not automatically mark a syncline. Follow rock layers and ages through the landscape.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read metamorphic outcrops</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Foliation, cleavage, and resistant folded layers can influence the shapes of metamorphic outcrops. Modern topography is a product of both structure and erosion. Distinguish a visible rock fabric from a hypothesis about when it formed.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Repeated fractures divide rock into blocks → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Joint sets</mark>.</li><li>Crushed rock and offset marker beds → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault-zone evidence</mark>.</li><li>Slope approximately follows an inclined bed → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dip slope</mark>.</li><li>Resistant tilted bed forms a narrow ridge → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Hogback</mark>.</li><li>Planar mineral alignment in outcrop → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Foliation</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Joints guide erosion · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p3-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p3-Im2.png\" alt=\"Find a fault in the terrain · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Erode tilted and folded layers · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.12-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Read metamorphic outcrops · textbook section 8.12, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.12 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-13a":{"source":"<p>Structural fieldwork records observations, sketches, orientations, maps, and samples. Measurements of strike and dip help project structures below the surface. Laboratory work can identify minerals, textures, and chemical conditions.</p><p>A fault is younger than units it cuts. An unfaulted intrusion or deposit that crosses and seals a fault constrains the fault to be older than that feature. Sediment accumulating during fault movement can record changing relief and tilting. The available evidence may constrain an interval rather than one exact date.</p><p>Minerals can grow before, during, or after deformation. Their relationship to fabrics helps distinguish these possibilities. Preserve uncertainty where an exposure or age is missing. Reconstruct the event sequence from several relations, not from a rock name alone.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Collect structural evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Structural fieldwork records observations, sketches, orientations, maps, and samples.</li><li>Measurements of strike and dip help project structures below the surface.</li><li>Laboratory work can identify minerals, textures, and chemical conditions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Faults and sealing relationships</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A fault is younger than units it cuts.</li><li>An unfaulted intrusion or deposit that crosses and seals a fault constrains the fault to be older than that feature.</li><li>Sediment accumulating during fault movement can record changing relief and tilting.</li><li>The available evidence may constrain an interval rather than one exact date.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Mineral growth and event sequences</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Minerals can grow before, during, or after deformation.</li><li>Their relationship to fabrics helps distinguish these possibilities.</li><li>Preserve uncertainty where an exposure or age is missing.</li><li>Reconstruct the event sequence from several relations, not from a rock name alone.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-13-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe, measure, and record</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Geologists record field sketches, photographs, rock samples, strike and dip measurements, and the positions of contacts and structures. Measurements let them project features below the surface and connect separated outcrops. Record the observation before the interpretation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bracket fault activity</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A fault is younger than a unit it cuts. An unfaulted layer or intrusion that seals the fault is younger than the movement it seals at that exposure. These relations bracket an interval; they do not prove that a fault never moved elsewhere or at another time.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Inspect minerals and overprints</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Minerals may grow before, during, or after deformation. Their shapes and positions relative to cleavage and veins can help establish a sequence. Microscopic textures and mineral chemistry add evidence about metamorphic conditions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Integrate an event sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Folded veins, later cleavage, and cross-cutting features can record multiple events. Distinguish an observed overprint from the proposed explanation. Missing exposures and uncertain ages can limit the sequence to relative order rather than a precise date.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Direction of a horizontal line on a bed → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Strike</mark>.</li><li>Direction and angle of steepest descent → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dip</mark>.</li><li>Fault cuts an older unit → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault movement is younger than that unit</mark>.</li><li>Unfaulted deposit seals a fault → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sealing deposit is younger than the observed movement</mark>.</li><li>Mineral texture viewed under a microscope → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Metamorphic evidence</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Observe, measure, and record · textbook section 8.13, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.13 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Bracket fault activity · textbook section 8.13, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.13 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Inspect minerals and overprints · textbook section 8.13, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.13 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Integrate an event sequence · textbook section 8.13, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.13 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe, measure, and record</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Geologists record field sketches, photographs, rock samples, strike and dip measurements, and the positions of contacts and structures. Measurements let them project features below the surface and connect separated outcrops. Record the observation before the interpretation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bracket fault activity</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A fault is younger than a unit it cuts.</li><li>An unfaulted layer or intrusion that seals the fault is younger than the movement it seals at that exposure.</li><li>These relations bracket an interval.</li><li>They do not prove that a fault never moved elsewhere or at another time.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Inspect minerals and overprints</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Minerals may grow before, during, or after deformation. Their shapes and positions relative to cleavage and veins can help establish a sequence. Microscopic textures and mineral chemistry add evidence about metamorphic conditions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Integrate an event sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Folded veins, later cleavage, and cross-cutting features can record multiple events. Distinguish an observed overprint from the proposed explanation. Missing exposures and uncertain ages can limit the sequence to relative order rather than a precise date.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Direction of a horizontal line on a bed → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Strike</mark>.</li><li>Direction and angle of steepest descent → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Dip</mark>.</li><li>Fault cuts an older unit → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault movement is younger than that unit</mark>.</li><li>Unfaulted deposit seals a fault → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sealing deposit is younger than the observed movement</mark>.</li><li>Mineral texture viewed under a microscope → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Metamorphic evidence</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Observe, measure, and record · textbook section 8.13, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.13 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Bracket fault activity · textbook section 8.13, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.13 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Inspect minerals and overprints · textbook section 8.13, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.13 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p7-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.13-p7-Im2.png\" alt=\"Integrate an event sequence · textbook section 8.13, PDF page 7\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.13 · PDF page 7 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-14a":{"source":"<p>The New England maps and cross section show belts of rock, faults, folds, and metamorphic variation. Older crystalline rocks and Paleozoic units record deformation and metamorphism; younger Mesozoic rocks occur in rift-related fault blocks. Recent deposits record still later glacial and shoreline processes.</p><p>Distinguish the collision history associated with assembly of the Appalachians from later rifting between North America and Africa. Metamorphic mineral patterns and pressure–temperature paths help reconstruct burial, heating, and later change. Different locations have different histories, so read the map legend and cross-section direction.</p><p>Do not equate every fault on the map with the same episode. The sequence includes older deformation, metamorphism, younger extension, and erosion/deposition. Exact ages and geographic labels must follow the supplied figures.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the maps and cross section</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The New England maps and cross section show belts of rock, faults, folds, and metamorphic variation.</li><li>Older crystalline rocks and Paleozoic units record deformation and metamorphism.</li><li>Younger Mesozoic rocks occur in rift-related fault blocks.</li><li>Recent deposits record still later glacial and shoreline processes.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Collision versus later rifting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Distinguish the collision history associated with assembly of the Appalachians from later rifting between North America and Africa.</li><li>Metamorphic mineral patterns and pressure–temperature paths help reconstruct burial, heating, and later change.</li><li>Different locations have different histories, so read the map legend and cross-section direction.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep events and ages separate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Do not equate every fault on the map with the same episode.</li><li>The sequence includes older deformation, metamorphism, younger extension, and erosion/deposition.</li><li>Exact ages and geographic labels must follow the supplied figures.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-appalachians":{"source":"<h4>Keep the episodes separate</h4><ul><li>The Chapter 8 introduction identifies a collision between <b>Africa and eastern North America</b> about 300 million years ago, near the culmination of Appalachian deformation.</li><li>Later continental rifting separated North America from Africa. Section 8.14 places younger Mesozoic rocks in rift-related tilted and down-dropped blocks.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the episodes separate</h4><ul><li>The Chapter 8 introduction identifies a collision between <b>Africa and eastern North America</b> about 300 million years ago, near the culmination of Appalachian deformation.</li><li>Later continental rifting separated North America from Africa. Section 8.14 places younger Mesozoic rocks in rift-related tilted and down-dropped blocks.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-14-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the regional map</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>New England contains different rock belts, faults, folds, and metamorphic rocks. The supplied map and cross section show structures at the surface and interpreted relations below it. Use the legend and orientation before comparing locations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate collision from later rifting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Paleozoic collisions contributed to Appalachian deformation and metamorphism. Later Mesozoic extension opened rift basins as North America separated from Africa. These are separate events; a modern map can display structures inherited from both.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare metamorphic grade</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The source map shows the highest metamorphic conditions in the central part of its illustrated area, decreasing toward both east and west. Grade is constrained using mineral assemblages and textures, not topographic elevation alone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct different pressure–temperature paths</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied regional interpretation, rocks in eastern Vermont were buried and then heated, while rocks in New Hampshire were initially heated and then buried. These paths are evidence-based reconstructions for specific locations, not a universal order for all mountain belts.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Paleozoic assembly of Appalachian mountains → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Continental collision</mark>.</li><li>Younger Mesozoic extension → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Rifting</mark>.</li><li>Highest grade in the supplied regional map → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Central part of the area</mark>.</li><li>Eastern Vermont path in the supplied figure → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Burial followed by heating</mark>.</li><li>New Hampshire path in the supplied figure → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Heating followed by burial</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p2-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p2-Im2.png\" alt=\"Read the regional map · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Separate collision from later rifting · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im3.png\" alt=\"Compare metamorphic grade · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Reconstruct different pressure–temperature paths · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the regional map</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>New England contains different rock belts, faults, folds, and metamorphic rocks. The supplied map and cross section show structures at the surface and interpreted relations below it. Use the legend and orientation before comparing locations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate collision from later rifting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Paleozoic collisions contributed to Appalachian deformation and metamorphism.</li><li>Later Mesozoic extension opened rift basins as North America separated from Africa.</li><li>These are separate events.</li><li>A modern map can display structures inherited from both.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare metamorphic grade</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The source map shows the highest metamorphic conditions in the central part of its illustrated area, decreasing toward both east and west. Grade is constrained using mineral assemblages and textures, not topographic elevation alone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct different pressure–temperature paths</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied regional interpretation, rocks in eastern Vermont were buried and then heated, while rocks in New Hampshire were initially heated and then buried. These paths are evidence-based reconstructions for specific locations, not a universal order for all mountain belts.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Paleozoic assembly of Appalachian mountains → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Continental collision</mark>.</li><li>Younger Mesozoic extension → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Rifting</mark>.</li><li>Highest grade in the supplied regional map → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Central part of the area</mark>.</li><li>Eastern Vermont path in the supplied figure → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Burial followed by heating</mark>.</li><li>New Hampshire path in the supplied figure → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Heating followed by burial</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p2-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p2-Im2.png\" alt=\"Read the regional map · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Separate collision from later rifting · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im3.png\" alt=\"Compare metamorphic grade · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.14-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Reconstruct different pressure–temperature paths · textbook section 8.14, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.14 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g8-15a":{"source":"<p>The supplied investigation combines bedding, cleavage, rock samples, and a linear fractured zone. First identify the rocks from mineral and texture evidence: fine dull cleavage supports slate; quartz-rich interlocking sand grains support quartzite; recrystallized calcite supports marble; coarse mineral bands support gneiss.</p><p>Trace bedding to interpret the fold, then compare cleavage and the viewing direction to infer shortening. A highly fractured boundary may be a fault, but fault type requires movement or an independently verified geometric relation. Differences in metamorphic grade across a boundary help constrain the structural history.</p><p>Keep each reasoning step explicit: observation → rock or structure → parent material or process → integrated sequence. Written Connect summaries support style calibration; missing original screenshots are not a basis for grading their unseen numbered locations.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the rock samples</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied investigation combines bedding, cleavage, rock samples, and a linear fractured zone.</li><li>First identify the rocks from mineral and texture evidence<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Fine dull cleavage supports slate.</li></ul></li><li>Quartz-rich interlocking sand grains support quartzite.</li><li>Recrystallized calcite supports marble.</li><li>coarse mineral bands support gneiss.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Trace structures and constrain their history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Trace bedding to interpret the fold, then compare cleavage and the viewing direction to infer shortening.</li><li>A highly fractured boundary may be a fault, but fault type requires movement or an independently verified geometric relation.</li><li>Differences in metamorphic grade across a boundary help constrain the structural history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Make each reasoning step explicit</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Keep each reasoning step explicit<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Observation → rock or structure → parent material or process → integrated sequence.</li></ul></li><li>Written Connect summaries support style calibration.</li><li>Missing original screenshots are not a basis for grading their unseen numbered locations.</li></ul></section>"},"g-connect-compass":{"source":"<h4>Read the viewing direction first</h4><ul><li>The original field sketch says <b>Looking North</b>. Left is west and right is east. The bedding is folded and cleavage is near vertical.</li><li>For the folding-and-cleavage interpretation requested in section 8.15, horizontal shortening is across the sketch, approximately <b>east–west</b>, perpendicular to the near-vertical cleavage.</li></ul><h4>State the inference and its limit</h4><ul><li>This uses the supplied textbook’s joint interpretation of fold orientation and cleavage; it is not an exact stress magnitude or a reconstruction of a missing Connect screenshot.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the viewing direction first</h4><ul><li>The original field sketch says <b>Looking North</b>. Left is west and right is east. The bedding is folded and cleavage is near vertical.</li><li>For the folding-and-cleavage interpretation requested in section 8.15, horizontal shortening is across the sketch, approximately <b>east–west</b>, perpendicular to the near-vertical cleavage.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">State the inference and its limit</h4><ul><li>This uses the supplied textbook’s joint interpretation of fold orientation and cleavage.</li><li>It is not an exact stress magnitude or a reconstruction of a missing Connect screenshot.</li></ul></section>"},"g8-15-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe the terrain and fold</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied north-looking perspective, blocky hills have bedding dip slopes facing toward the center. The cross section shows a down-curving fold, and near-vertical cleavage cuts bedding. The observations support an upright or steeply inclined syncline.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the rock samples</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The eastern coarse black-and-white banded sample is gneiss; the dull fine-grained sample with cleavage is slate. Interlocking quartz grains support quartzite, while fine calcite and deformed fossils support marble. Quartzite commonly began as sandstone and marble as limestone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the two sides of the stream</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A straight stream follows an intensely fractured and shattered zone between the eastern banded rocks and lower-grade rocks to the west. The observations suggest a fault zone; the checked Connect summary calls the boundary a reverse fault. Identify that key as supplied assessment evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Infer shortening and history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The source perspective looks north, and the fold and cleavage indicate east–west shortening in the supplied checked summary. The eastern banded rocks are higher grade than the rocks in the grassy area and southern hill. Separate those source-specific relations from rules for every syncline.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Coarse black-and-white bands with visible minerals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Gneiss</mark>.</li><li>Dull fine-grained rock with cleavage → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Slate</mark>.</li><li>Hard interlocking quartz grains → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Quartzite</mark>.</li><li>Fine calcite with deformed fossils → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Marble</mark>.</li><li>Down-curving layers with inward-dipping limbs → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Syncline</mark>.</li><li>Straight stream through intensely shattered rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault-zone evidence</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Observe the terrain and fold · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Identify the rock samples · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Infer shortening and history · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe the terrain and fold</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied north-looking perspective, blocky hills have bedding dip slopes facing toward the center. The cross section shows a down-curving fold, and near-vertical cleavage cuts bedding. The observations support an upright or steeply inclined syncline.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the rock samples</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The eastern coarse black-and-white banded sample is gneiss.</li><li>The dull fine-grained sample with cleavage is slate.</li><li>Interlocking quartz grains support quartzite, while fine calcite and deformed fossils support marble.</li><li>Quartzite commonly began as sandstone and marble as limestone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the two sides of the stream</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A straight stream follows an intensely fractured and shattered zone between the eastern banded rocks and lower-grade rocks to the west.</li><li>The observations suggest a fault zone.</li><li>The checked Connect summary calls the boundary a reverse fault.</li><li>Identify that key as supplied assessment evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Infer shortening and history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The source perspective looks north, and the fold and cleavage indicate east–west shortening in the supplied checked summary. The eastern banded rocks are higher grade than the rocks in the grassy area and southern hill. Separate those source-specific relations from rules for every syncline.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Key distinctions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Coarse black-and-white bands with visible minerals → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Gneiss</mark>.</li><li>Dull fine-grained rock with cleavage → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Slate</mark>.</li><li>Hard interlocking quartz grains → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Quartzite</mark>.</li><li>Fine calcite with deformed fossils → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Marble</mark>.</li><li>Down-curving layers with inward-dipping limbs → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Syncline</mark>.</li><li>Straight stream through intensely shattered rock → <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fault-zone evidence</mark>.</li></ul></section><details><summary>Original figures for this section</summary><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Observe the terrain and fold · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 2\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 2 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Identify the rock samples · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 4\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 4 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Infer shortening and history · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 3\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 3 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 5\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 5 · original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"assets/figures/chapter8-standard/8.15-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional original source illustration · textbook section 8.15, PDF page 6\"></a><figcaption>Exploring Geology · 8.15 · PDF page 6 · original figure</figcaption></figure></details>"},"g9-1-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Five relative-age tools</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Most sedimentary beds and many lava layers begin approximately horizontal: <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">original horizontality</mark>. Dune foresets and delta slopes are exceptions; tilting or folding generally records later deformation.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Superposition</mark> places younger beds above older beds in an undisturbed sequence. First check whether the beds have been overturned.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Inclusions</mark> are older than the rock that contains them. A granite fragment inside conglomerate existed before the conglomerate formed.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Crosscutting relationships</mark> make the cutting fault, vein, or dike younger than the material it cuts. A feature that stops at a contact need not cut the next unit.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Baked contacts</mark> identify preexisting rock heated by younger magma. A sill can bake rock above and below; a lava flow can bake underlying rock while later sediment deposited above remains unbaked.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deep time from ordinary processes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Siccar Point combines steep older beds, erosion, and gently dipping younger beds. Deposition, lithification, deformation, and erosion require a sequence; the present arrangement was not deposited all at once.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Uniformitarianism</mark> uses processes observable today to interpret earlier events. It does not require every process to operate at an unchanging rate.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Five relative-age tools</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Most sedimentary beds and many lava layers begin approximately horizontal<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Original horizontality</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>Dune foresets and delta slopes are exceptions.</li><li>Tilting or folding generally records later deformation.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Superposition</mark> places younger beds above older beds in an undisturbed sequence. First check whether the beds have been overturned.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Inclusions</mark> are older than the rock that contains them. A granite fragment inside conglomerate existed before the conglomerate formed.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Crosscutting relationships</mark> make the cutting fault, vein, or dike younger than the material it cuts. A feature that stops at a contact need not cut the next unit.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Baked contacts</mark> identify preexisting rock heated by younger magma.</li><li>A sill can bake rock above and below.</li><li>A lava flow can bake underlying rock while later sediment deposited above remains unbaked.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deep time from ordinary processes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Siccar Point combines steep older beds, erosion, and gently dipping younger beds.</li><li>Deposition, lithification, deformation, and erosion require a sequence.</li><li>The present arrangement was not deposited all at once.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Uniformitarianism</mark> uses processes observable today to interpret earlier events. It does not require every process to operate at an unchanging rate.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g-c9q-principles":{"source":"<h4>Orientation and order</h4><ul><li>Original horizontality says most sediments begin in approximately horizontal layers. Tilted or folded beds normally record later deformation; exceptions include dune faces and delta slopes.</li><li>Superposition compares positions in an undisturbed depositional stack: younger layers form above older layers. Uplift does not necessarily tilt a bed.</li></ul><h4>Intrusions and contacts</h4><ul><li>A dike that cuts a host rock is younger by cross-cutting relations. Baking or alteration of the host beside magma independently gives a contact-effects relation.</li><li>Inclusions concern pieces of older material enclosed within a younger rock. Do not use superposition as a substitute for cross-cutting simply because an intrusive body is visible.</li></ul><h4>A supplied feedback conflict</h4><ul><li>Assessment Q3 marks “contact effects; superposition” correct for a dike-intrusion stem and marks “cross-cutting relations; contact effects” incorrect. Its figure did not render. The textbook explicitly supports cross-cutting and contact effects for an intrusion cutting and baking its host. Practice here states those observations directly; the original feedback conflict remains unresolved.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Orientation and order</h4><ul><li>Original horizontality says most sediments begin in approximately horizontal layers.</li><li>Tilted or folded beds normally record later deformation.</li><li>Exceptions include dune faces and delta slopes.</li><li>Superposition compares positions in an undisturbed depositional stack<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Younger layers form above older layers.</li></ul></li><li>Uplift does not necessarily tilt a bed.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Intrusions and contacts</h4><ul><li>A dike that cuts a host rock is younger by cross-cutting relations. Baking or alteration of the host beside magma independently gives a contact-effects relation.</li><li>Inclusions concern pieces of older material enclosed within a younger rock. Do not use superposition as a substitute for cross-cutting simply because an intrusive body is visible.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">A supplied feedback conflict</h4><ul><li>Assessment Q3 marks “contact effects; superposition” correct for a dike-intrusion stem and marks “cross-cutting relations; contact effects” incorrect.</li><li>Its figure did not render.</li><li>The textbook explicitly supports cross-cutting and contact effects for an intrusion cutting and baking its host.</li><li>Practice here states those observations directly.</li><li>The original feedback conflict remains unresolved.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-1-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Check orientation before using up and down as an age order.</li><li>An inclusion predates the rock that contains it.</li><li>Date the visible contact, not an imagined extension.</li><li>A sill and a surface lava flow produce different contact patterns.</li><li>A sequence of events is not yet a numerical clock.</li></ul></section><p>A relationship dates an event relative to something it affects.</p><h3>More original course figures</h3><p>Compare these additional examples with the relationships explained above. Open an image to inspect the original.</p><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.1-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.1-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Dipping sedimentary beds beside a river\"></a><figcaption>Dipping sedimentary beds beside a river · Exploring Geology9.1 · PDFp4 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.1-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.1-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Nearly horizontal layers exposed by a river canyon\"></a><figcaption>Nearly horizontal layers exposed by a river canyon · Exploring Geology9.1 · PDFp4 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Check orientation before using up and down as an age order.</li><li>An inclusion predates the rock that contains it.</li><li>Date the visible contact, not an imagined extension.</li><li>A sill and a surface lava flow produce different contact patterns.</li><li>A sequence of events is not yet a numerical clock.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A relationship dates an event relative to something it affects.</li></ul><h3>More original course figures</h3><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compare these additional examples with the relationships explained above.</li><li>Open an image to inspect the original.</li></ul><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.1-p4-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.1-p4-Im1.png\" alt=\"Dipping sedimentary beds beside a river\"></a><figcaption>Dipping sedimentary beds beside a river · Exploring Geology9.1 · PDFp4 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.1-p4-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.1-p4-Im2.png\" alt=\"Nearly horizontal layers exposed by a river canyon\"></a><figcaption>Nearly horizontal layers exposed by a river canyon · Exploring Geology9.1 · PDFp4 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure>"},"g9-2-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build, expose, and cut</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sediment accumulates in successive layers, becomes rock, and may be uplifted as the sea withdraws. Streams then cut down; tributaries and slope failure widen a canyon.</li><li>A canyon or erosion surface is <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">younger than the rocks it cuts</mark>. A dated lava that covers the surface is younger than that surface, giving a second age limit.</li><li>A river terrace records a former channel or floodplain level. An elevated abandoned terrace predates the river cutting to its present lower level.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Surface clues and their limits</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Soil development can help compare surfaces under similar environmental conditions. Different climate, material, and disturbance histories complicate comparison.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Desert varnish</mark> is a dark coating containing iron and manganese oxides; it can accumulate over long exposure but can also be stripped or reset.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Desert pavement</mark> concentrates surface clasts as fine material is removed or moves downward. Well-developed pavement and varnish support prolonged exposure, not a universal stopwatch.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cosmogenic isotopes</mark> accumulate through cosmic-ray interactions at exposed surfaces and can constrain exposure duration under an appropriate model.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build, expose, and cut</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sediment accumulates in successive layers, becomes rock, and may be uplifted as the sea withdraws.</li><li>Streams then cut down.</li><li>Tributaries and slope failure widen a canyon.</li><li>A canyon or erosion surface is <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">younger than the rocks it cuts</mark>. A dated lava that covers the surface is younger than that surface, giving a second age limit.</li><li>A river terrace records a former channel or floodplain level. An elevated abandoned terrace predates the river cutting to its present lower level.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Surface clues and their limits</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Soil development can help compare surfaces under similar environmental conditions. Different climate, material, and disturbance histories complicate comparison.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Desert varnish</mark> is a dark coating containing iron and manganese oxides.</li><li>It can accumulate over long exposure but can also be stripped or reset.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Desert pavement</mark> concentrates surface clasts as fine material is removed or moves downward. Well-developed pavement and varnish support prolonged exposure, not a universal stopwatch.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cosmogenic isotopes</mark> accumulate through cosmic-ray interactions at exposed surfaces and can constrain exposure duration under an appropriate model.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g-c9q-surface":{"source":"<h4>Lava and the landscape</h4><ul><li>The depicted lava lies on an existing landscape surface. The covered surface therefore existed before that eruption.</li><li>A dated lava can constrain the age of the surface beneath it, but it does not date every older episode that shaped that landscape.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Lava and the landscape</h4><ul><li>The depicted lava lies on an existing landscape surface. The covered surface therefore existed before that eruption.</li><li>A dated lava can constrain the age of the surface beneath it, but it does not date every older episode that shaped that landscape.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-2-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The canyon is younger than every rock it cuts.</li><li>Older substrate → erosion surface → younger cover.</li><li>A raised terrace preserves an earlier channel position.</li><li>Surface maturity records exposure under suitable conditions.</li><li>State which event each age actually measures.</li></ul></section><p>Carving and burial bracket a surface independently of bedrock formation.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The canyon is younger than every rock it cuts.</li><li>Older substrate → erosion surface → younger cover.</li><li>A raised terrace preserves an earlier channel position.</li><li>Surface maturity records exposure under suitable conditions.</li><li>State which event each age actually measures.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Carving and burial bracket a surface independently of bedrock formation.</li></ul>"},"g9-3-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the contact</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">unconformity</mark> is a surface representing missing geologic record through erosion, nondeposition, or both. The gap is time, not necessarily a visibly open space.</li><li>An <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">angular unconformity</mark> separates older tilted or folded strata from younger beds at a different orientation. Sequence: deposition → deformation → erosion → renewed deposition.</li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">nonconformity</mark> places sedimentary rock on an eroded surface of nonlayered crystalline rock, such as granite. The granite formed at depth before uplift and erosion exposed it.</li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">disconformity</mark> separates essentially parallel sedimentary beds. Fossils, weathering, or erosional relief may reveal a gap that bedding orientation alone hides.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Do not skip the missing events</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Erosion can truncate layers and remove a long record. Nondeposition can leave missing time even where little material was removed.</li><li>The supplied chapter identifies angular contacts over steeply layered metamorphic rocks as well as tilted sedimentary beds. Inspect the geometry and rock character; do not classify from the word “metamorphic” alone.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the contact</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">unconformity</mark> is a surface representing missing geologic record through erosion, nondeposition, or both. The gap is time, not necessarily a visibly open space.</li><li>An <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">angular unconformity</mark> separates older tilted or folded strata from younger beds at a different orientation.</li><li>Sequence<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Deposition → deformation → erosion → renewed deposition.</li></ul></li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">nonconformity</mark> places sedimentary rock on an eroded surface of nonlayered crystalline rock, such as granite. The granite formed at depth before uplift and erosion exposed it.</li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">disconformity</mark> separates essentially parallel sedimentary beds. Fossils, weathering, or erosional relief may reveal a gap that bedding orientation alone hides.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Do not skip the missing events</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Erosion can truncate layers and remove a long record. Nondeposition can leave missing time even where little material was removed.</li><li>The supplied chapter identifies angular contacts over steeply layered metamorphic rocks as well as tilted sedimentary beds.</li><li>Inspect the geometry and rock character.</li><li>Do not classify from the word “metamorphic” alone.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g9-c9a-unconformities":{"source":"<h3>What crosses the time gap?</h3><ul><li><b>Angular unconformity:</b> younger deposits overlie eroded older beds with a different orientation. In the left block, the older layers are folded before erosion and burial.</li><li><b>Nonconformity:</b> younger sediment overlies eroded igneous or metamorphic basement. The middle block shows sediment above nonlayered crystalline rock.</li><li><b>Disconformity:</b> a time gap separates essentially parallel sedimentary layers. In the right block, parallel beds do not mean continuous deposition.</li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T13\">Practice relative ages and contacts</button></p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What crosses the time gap?</h4><ul><li><b>Angular unconformity</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Younger deposits overlie eroded older beds with a different orientation.</li></ul></li><li>In the left block, the older layers are folded before erosion and burial.</li><li><b>Nonconformity</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Younger sediment overlies eroded igneous or metamorphic basement.</li></ul></li><li>The middle block shows sediment above nonlayered crystalline rock.</li><li><b>Disconformity</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A time gap separates essentially parallel sedimentary layers.</li></ul></li><li>In the right block, parallel beds do not mean continuous deposition.</li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T13\">Practice relative ages and contacts</button></p></section>"},"g-c9q-siccar":{"source":"<h4>Read the contact</h4><ul><li>Gray sandstone and shale below the contact were deposited, tilted toward vertical, and eroded before reddish sandstone and conglomerate accumulated above them.</li><li>The younger deposits include fragments of the older gray rocks. Different orientations and the truncated lower beds record more than one episode.</li></ul><h4>Use the right visual evidence</h4><ul><li>The image here is the original textbook close-up. The supplied assessment captures show a broken image placeholder, so their unseen figure cannot be checked for exact visual equivalence.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the contact</h4><ul><li>Gray sandstone and shale below the contact were deposited, tilted toward vertical, and eroded before reddish sandstone and conglomerate accumulated above them.</li><li>The younger deposits include fragments of the older gray rocks. Different orientations and the truncated lower beds record more than one episode.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the right visual evidence</h4><ul><li>The image here is the original textbook close-up. The supplied assessment captures show a broken image placeholder, so their unseen figure cannot be checked for exact visual equivalence.</li></ul></section>"},"g-c9q-gaps":{"source":"<h4>Granite below sandstone</h4><ul><li>Granite must crystallize at depth, then become exposed through uplift and removal of overlying rock before sediment is deposited directly on its eroded surface.</li><li>The original labeled photograph is useful for reconstructing events. Its printed “nonconformity” label is a teaching reference, not a hidden identification answer.</li></ul><h4>Similar limestone can conceal missing time</h4><ul><li>A disconformity separates essentially parallel sedimentary layers and records an interval of nondeposition and/or erosion. The rocks above and below can look very similar.</li><li>Compare their fossil assemblages and established fossil ranges to test for an age gap. Similar mineral content, porosity, or fracture orientation alone does not establish continuous deposition.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Granite below sandstone</h4><ul><li>Granite must crystallize at depth, then become exposed through uplift and removal of overlying rock before sediment is deposited directly on its eroded surface.</li><li>The original labeled photograph is useful for reconstructing events. Its printed “nonconformity” label is a teaching reference, not a hidden identification answer.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Similar limestone can conceal missing time</h4><ul><li>A disconformity separates essentially parallel sedimentary layers and records an interval of nondeposition and/or erosion. The rocks above and below can look very similar.</li><li>Compare their fossil assemblages and established fossil ranges to test for an age gap. Similar mineral content, porosity, or fracture orientation alone does not establish continuous deposition.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-3-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Older tilted beds are truncated beneath younger cover.</li><li>Crystalline basement must be exposed before sediment buries it.</li><li>Parallel layers can still contain a major time gap.</li><li>Read rock type, orientation and contact together.</li><li>A missing record is not evidence that nothing happened.</li></ul></section><p>The contact preserves a gap, while the rocks define its type.</p><h3>More original course figures</h3><p>Compare these additional examples with the relationships explained above. Open an image to inspect the original.</p><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.3-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.3-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original labeled contact between sandstone and granite\"></a><figcaption>Original labeled contact between sandstone and granite · Exploring Geology9.3 · PDFp5 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Older tilted beds are truncated beneath younger cover.</li><li>Crystalline basement must be exposed before sediment buries it.</li><li>Parallel layers can still contain a major time gap.</li><li>Read rock type, orientation and contact together.</li><li>A missing record is not evidence that nothing happened.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The contact preserves a gap, while the rocks define its type.</li></ul><h3>More original course figures</h3><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compare these additional examples with the relationships explained above.</li><li>Open an image to inspect the original.</li></ul><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.3-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.3-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Original labeled contact between sandstone and granite\"></a><figcaption>Original labeled contact between sandstone and granite · Exploring Geology9.3 · PDFp5 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure>"},"g9-4-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Parent, daughter, and half-life</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Isotopes</mark> of an element have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons. Unstable parent isotopes decay into daughter products.</li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">half-life</mark> removes half of the remaining parent: 100% → 50% → 25% → 12.5%. Equal time intervals remove successively smaller amounts, not a fixed number of atoms.</li><li>Parent/daughter ratios can give an age when the initial daughter contribution and later gain or loss are addressed. A mass spectrometer measures isotope proportions; each result has analytical uncertainty.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Choose the material and the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>K–Ar and Ar–Ar commonly date volcanic eruption or cooling; U–Pb in zircon commonly dates crystallization. Rb–Sr and Sm–Nd are useful for suitable older rocks and mineral systems.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Carbon-14</mark> is useful for comparatively recent carbon-bearing material such as charcoal or bone, not for dating a billion-year-old granite.</li><li>A slowly cooled granite may record zircon crystallization and later biotite cooling through about 300°C at different times. Different mineral ages need not be contradictory.</li><li>Detrital mineral grains can date their source rocks rather than sediment deposition. New metamorphic minerals or reset systems may date metamorphism. Always name the event being measured.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Parent, daughter, and half-life</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Isotopes</mark> of an element have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons. Unstable parent isotopes decay into daughter products.</li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">half-life</mark> removes half of the remaining parent<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>100% → 50% → 25% → 12.5%.</li></ul></li><li>Equal time intervals remove successively smaller amounts, not a fixed number of atoms.</li><li>Parent/daughter ratios can give an age when the initial daughter contribution and later gain or loss are addressed.</li><li>A mass spectrometer measures isotope proportions.</li><li>Each result has analytical uncertainty.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Choose the material and the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>K–Ar and Ar–Ar commonly date volcanic eruption or cooling.</li><li>U–Pb in zircon commonly dates crystallization.</li><li>Rb–Sr and Sm–Nd are useful for suitable older rocks and mineral systems.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Carbon-14</mark> is useful for comparatively recent carbon-bearing material such as charcoal or bone, not for dating a billion-year-old granite.</li><li>A slowly cooled granite may record zircon crystallization and later biotite cooling through about 300°C at different times. Different mineral ages need not be contradictory.</li><li>Detrital mineral grains can date their source rocks rather than sediment deposition. New metamorphic minerals or reset systems may date metamorphism. Always name the event being measured.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g9-c9a-isotopes":{"source":"<h3>Find the fraction remaining</h3><ul><li>For this simplified closed-system exercise with no initial daughters, original parent total=parent now+daughter now.</li><li>Halving sequence: 1→1/2→1/4→1/8. Count elapsed half-lives, then multiply by that sample’s isotope half-life.</li><li><b>Granite G:</b>125/(125+875)=1/8; three half-lives × 500 million years=1500 million years.</li><li><b>Dike D:</b>500/(500+500)=1/2; one half-life × 40 million years=40 million years.</li></ul><h3>Keep the interpretation constrained</h3><ul><li>The two samples use different isotope half-lives. Daughter abundance alone does not compare their ages.</li><li>A numerical age depends on what event started or reset the isotope clock; the exercise dates these igneous samples under its stated assumptions.</li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T14\">Practice numerical ages</button></p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Find the fraction remaining</h4><ul><li>For this simplified closed-system exercise with no initial daughters, original parent total=parent now+daughter now.</li><li>Halving sequence<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>1→1/2→1/4→1/8. Count elapsed half-lives, then multiply by that sample’s isotope half-life.</li></ul></li><li><b>Granite G:</b>125/(125+875)=1/8.</li><li>Three half-lives × 500 million years=1500 million years.</li><li><b>Dike D:</b>500/(500+500)=1/2.</li><li>One half-life × 40 million years=40 million years.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the interpretation constrained</h4><ul><li>The two samples use different isotope half-lives. Daughter abundance alone does not compare their ages.</li><li>A numerical age depends on what event started or reset the isotope clock.</li><li>The exercise dates these igneous samples under its stated assumptions.</li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T14\">Practice numerical ages</button></p></section>"},"g9-4-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>One half-life leaves 50%; two leave 25%.</li><li>A laboratory ratio needs a geological interpretation.</li><li>Different materials and timescales require different isotope systems.</li><li>Different mineral clocks can date different events in one rock.</li><li>A detrital grain can be much older than its host deposit.</li></ul></section><p>Choose both the isotope clock and the geological event being dated.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>One half-life leaves 50%.</li><li>Two leave 25%.</li><li>A laboratory ratio needs a geological interpretation.</li><li>Different materials and timescales require different isotope systems.</li><li>Different mineral clocks can date different events in one rock.</li><li>A detrital grain can be much older than its host deposit.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Choose both the isotope clock and the geological event being dated.</li></ul>"},"g9-5-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Ways the record is preserved</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Hard shells, bones, and teeth commonly survive better than soft tissues. Mineral replacement can preserve form; silica replacement produces petrified wood.</li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">mold</mark> is the cavity or impression left after an organism dissolves or disappears. A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">cast</mark> forms when that mold is filled.</li><li>Carbon films preserve carbon-rich outlines; impressions preserve shapes. Amber can entomb small organisms. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Stromatolites</mark> are layered structures built through microbial activity.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Trace fossils</mark>, including footprints, trackways, and burrows, record activity rather than a preserved body. Trackways can reveal movement and behavior.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Preservation is selective</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rapid burial and protection from scavenging, weathering, and oxygen favor preservation. Transport, compaction, and dissolution can destroy or alter remains; fossil absence is not proof that no life existed.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Pseudofossils</mark> resemble organisms but form without them. Branching manganese-oxide dendrites are mineral patterns. Concretions are localized cemented bodies and are not automatically fossils, although they may enclose one.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Ways the record is preserved</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Hard shells, bones, and teeth commonly survive better than soft tissues.</li><li>Mineral replacement can preserve form.</li><li>Silica replacement produces petrified wood.</li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">mold</mark> is the cavity or impression left after an organism dissolves or disappears. A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">cast</mark> forms when that mold is filled.</li><li>Carbon films preserve carbon-rich outlines.</li><li>Impressions preserve shapes.</li><li>Amber can entomb small organisms.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Stromatolites</mark> are layered structures built through microbial activity.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Trace fossils</mark>, including footprints, trackways, and burrows, record activity rather than a preserved body. Trackways can reveal movement and behavior.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Preservation is selective</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rapid burial and protection from scavenging, weathering, and oxygen favor preservation.</li><li>Transport, compaction, and dissolution can destroy or alter remains.</li><li>Fossil absence is not proof that no life existed.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Pseudofossils</mark> resemble organisms but form without them. Branching manganese-oxide dendrites are mineral patterns. Concretions are localized cemented bodies and are not automatically fossils, although they may enclose one.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g-c9q-fossils":{"source":"<h4>Body and trace evidence</h4><ul><li>Preserved leaves and other body remains are fossils. Footprints and sediment-filled burrows record biological activity and are trace fossils.</li><li>Dendrites are branching mineral growths, commonly manganese oxides along fractures or bedding. Their plant-like shape does not make them fossils.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Body and trace evidence</h4><ul><li>Preserved leaves and other body remains are fossils. Footprints and sediment-filled burrows record biological activity and are trace fossils.</li><li>Dendrites are branching mineral growths, commonly manganese oxides along fractures or bedding. Their plant-like shape does not make them fossils.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-5-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Hard parts and rapid burial favor preservation.</li><li>Mold = cavity; cast = later infill.</li><li>Replacement, films, impressions and entombment preserve different evidence.</li><li>Tracks and burrows record behavior rather than preserved bodies.</li><li>An organic-looking shape does not prove a biological origin.</li></ul></section><p>Burial and preservation select which evidence of life survives.</p><h3>More original course figures</h3><p>Compare these additional examples with the relationships explained above. Open an image to inspect the original.</p><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.5-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.5-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Body-fossil preservation\"></a><figcaption>Body-fossil preservation · Exploring Geology9.5 · PDFp3 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.5-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.5-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Preserved traces in sediment\"></a><figcaption>Preserved traces in sediment · Exploring Geology9.5 · PDFp6 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Hard parts and rapid burial favor preservation.</li><li>Mold = cavity.</li><li>Cast = later infill.</li><li>Replacement, films, impressions and entombment preserve different evidence.</li><li>Tracks and burrows record behavior rather than preserved bodies.</li><li>An organic-looking shape does not prove a biological origin.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Burial and preservation select which evidence of life survives.</li></ul><h3>More original course figures</h3><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compare these additional examples with the relationships explained above.</li><li>Open an image to inspect the original.</li></ul><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.5-p3-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.5-p3-Im1.png\" alt=\"Body-fossil preservation\"></a><figcaption>Body-fossil preservation · Exploring Geology9.5 · PDFp3 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.5-p6-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.5-p6-Im1.png\" alt=\"Preserved traces in sediment\"></a><figcaption>Preserved traces in sediment · Exploring Geology9.5 · PDFp6 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure>"},"g9-6-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The broad sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Faunal succession</mark> is the systematic change of fossil assemblages through rock sequences. This relative pattern was recognized before isotope dating supplied numerical ages.</li><li>Precambrian time comprises roughly 90% of Earth history; much of its record is dominated by simple life and microbial structures.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Paleozoic</mark> records major marine diversification and the expansion of plants and animals onto land. The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Mesozoic</mark> includes dinosaur-dominated terrestrial ecosystems; the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cenozoic</mark> records extensive mammal diversification.</li><li>Course boundary values are about 541, 252, and 66 million years ago. Broad labels summarize change; they do not mean that all members of a group originated or vanished simultaneously.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why populations change</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Environmental setting, climate, resources, reproduction, adaptability, competition, and predator–prey relationships influence survival and reproduction.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Natural selection</mark> explains changes in populations through differences in survival and reproductive success. Evolutionary change is an observation; mechanisms explain how it occurs.</li><li>Gradual change and geologically rapid episodes separated by relative stability can both occur. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Punctuated equilibrium</mark> does not mean an individual suddenly transforms into a new species.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The broad sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Faunal succession</mark> is the systematic change of fossil assemblages through rock sequences. This relative pattern was recognized before isotope dating supplied numerical ages.</li><li>Precambrian time comprises roughly 90% of Earth history.</li><li>Much of its record is dominated by simple life and microbial structures.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Paleozoic</mark> records major marine diversification and the expansion of plants and animals onto land.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Mesozoic</mark> includes dinosaur-dominated terrestrial ecosystems.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cenozoic</mark> records extensive mammal diversification.</li><li>Course boundary values are about 541, 252, and 66 million years ago.</li><li>Broad labels summarize change.</li><li>They do not mean that all members of a group originated or vanished simultaneously.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why populations change</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Environmental setting, climate, resources, reproduction, adaptability, competition, and predator–prey relationships influence survival and reproduction.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Natural selection</mark> explains changes in populations through differences in survival and reproductive success.</li><li>Evolutionary change is an observation.</li><li>Mechanisms explain how it occurs.</li><li>Gradual change and geologically rapid episodes separated by relative stability can both occur. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Punctuated equilibrium</mark> does not mean an individual suddenly transforms into a new species.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g9-6-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Separate the regional time pattern from local environmental effects.</li><li>Dominance during an era does not necessarily mark first origin.</li><li>Survival depends on needs, conditions and inherited variation.</li><li>A changing resource can affect an entire ecological relationship.</li><li>An observed succession and its explanation are different claims.</li></ul></section><p>Faunal succession is an observed pattern; mechanisms explain that pattern.</p><h3>More original course figures</h3><p>Compare these additional examples with the relationships explained above. Open an image to inspect the original.</p><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Skeletal evidence in the fossil record\"></a><figcaption>Skeletal evidence in the fossil record · Exploring Geology9.6 · PDFp5 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Associated fossil skeletons\"></a><figcaption>Associated fossil skeletons · Exploring Geology9.6 · PDFp5 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p8-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p8-Im3.png\" alt=\"Living organisms in the section’s biological comparison\"></a><figcaption>Living organisms in the section’s biological comparison · Exploring Geology9.6 · PDFp8 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Separate the regional time pattern from local environmental effects.</li><li>Dominance during an era does not necessarily mark first origin.</li><li>Survival depends on needs, conditions and inherited variation.</li><li>A changing resource can affect an entire ecological relationship.</li><li>An observed succession and its explanation are different claims.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Faunal succession is an observed pattern.</li><li>Mechanisms explain that pattern.</li></ul><h3>More original course figures</h3><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compare these additional examples with the relationships explained above.</li><li>Open an image to inspect the original.</li></ul><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p5-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p5-Im1.png\" alt=\"Skeletal evidence in the fossil record\"></a><figcaption>Skeletal evidence in the fossil record · Exploring Geology9.6 · PDFp5 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p5-Im2.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p5-Im2.png\" alt=\"Associated fossil skeletons\"></a><figcaption>Associated fossil skeletons · Exploring Geology9.6 · PDFp5 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p8-Im3.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.6-p8-Im3.png\" alt=\"Living organisms in the section’s biological comparison\"></a><figcaption>Living organisms in the section’s biological comparison · Exploring Geology9.6 · PDFp8 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure>"},"g9-7-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Constrain an interval</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">index fossil</mark> is especially useful when it is widespread, abundant, and restricted to a short time interval. A long-ranging fossil usually gives a weaker age limit.</li><li>An assemblage narrows an age to the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">overlap of fossil ranges</mark>. Do not take the union of every interval or average unrelated dates.</li><li>Microfossils can provide useful age markers. Abrupt changes in assemblages can help locate boundaries.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Match rocks without forcing a match</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Correlate fossil assemblages alongside lithology and depositional environment. A missing species can reflect an unsuitable habitat rather than missing time.</li><li>An eroded unit may instead create a genuine gap. Distinguish environmental exclusion from erosion using contacts and the surrounding sequence.</li><li>Overlapping partial sections can build a longer composite record. Equivalent units need not have identical thickness. William Smith used repeated fossil successions to correlate strata and map geology.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Constrain an interval</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">index fossil</mark> is especially useful when it is widespread, abundant, and restricted to a short time interval. A long-ranging fossil usually gives a weaker age limit.</li><li>An assemblage narrows an age to the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">overlap of fossil ranges</mark>. Do not take the union of every interval or average unrelated dates.</li><li>Microfossils can provide useful age markers. Abrupt changes in assemblages can help locate boundaries.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Match rocks without forcing a match</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Correlate fossil assemblages alongside lithology and depositional environment. A missing species can reflect an unsuitable habitat rather than missing time.</li><li>An eroded unit may instead create a genuine gap. Distinguish environmental exclusion from erosion using contacts and the surrounding sequence.</li><li>Overlapping partial sections can build a longer composite record. Equivalent units need not have identical thickness. William Smith used repeated fossil successions to correlate strata and map geology.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g-c9q-overlap":{"source":"<h4>Use the intersection of ranges</h4><ul><li>A layer containing several contemporaneous fossil species must fit the interval during which their ranges overlap. A combination can constrain age more narrowly than any one long-ranging species.</li><li>This interpretation assumes the fossils belong to the deposit rather than having been eroded from older rocks and redeposited. The full ranges and geologic context matter.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the intersection of ranges</h4><ul><li>A layer containing several contemporaneous fossil species must fit the interval during which their ranges overlap. A combination can constrain age more narrowly than any one long-ranging species.</li><li>This interpretation assumes the fossils belong to the deposit rather than having been eroded from older rocks and redeposited. The full ranges and geologic context matter.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-7-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Short duration, abundance and wide distribution make a useful index.</li><li>Use the intersection, not the sum, of fossil ranges.</li><li>A gap in one column does not erase the record at another site.</li><li>Overlapping sections can reconstruct a longer regional record.</li><li>Correlate evidence across sites, then account for what differs.</li></ul></section><p>Age constraints tighten where independent fossil ranges overlap.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Short duration, abundance and wide distribution make a useful index.</li><li>Use the intersection, not the sum, of fossil ranges.</li><li>A gap in one column does not erase the record at another site.</li><li>Overlapping sections can reconstruct a longer regional record.</li><li>Correlate evidence across sites, then account for what differs.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Age constraints tighten where independent fossil ranges overlap.</li></ul>"},"g9-8-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Relative framework, then numerical calibration</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The timescale developed from superposition, fossil succession, and correlation before numerical ages were available. Isotope dates later calibrated that framework.</li><li>The three Phanerozoic eras are <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Paleozoic → Mesozoic → Cenozoic</mark>. “Precambrian” is a broad earlier span, not a fourth era equivalent to those three.</li><li>Paleozoic periods in the supplied course sequence: <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cambrian, Ordovician, Silurian, Devonian, Mississippian, Pennsylvanian, Permian</mark>. Mississippian and Pennsylvanian together correspond to the Carboniferous used elsewhere.</li><li>Mesozoic periods: <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous</mark>. Cenozoic periods: <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Paleogene, Neogene, Quaternary</mark>. Major extinction events mark some boundaries.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bracket events with dated rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An interbedded ash can closely date a depositional horizon. Dated volcanic layers below and above a fossil bed bracket its age.</li><li>A dike is younger than beds it cuts; clasts eroded from that dike are older than a later deposit containing them. Neither relationship makes the sediment exactly the dike age.</li><li>An unconformity is younger than the underlying record and older than overlying deposition. Use source boundary values as course approximations rather than exact natural switches.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Relative framework, then numerical calibration</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The timescale developed from superposition, fossil succession, and correlation before numerical ages were available. Isotope dates later calibrated that framework.</li><li>The three Phanerozoic eras are <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Paleozoic → Mesozoic → Cenozoic</mark>. “Precambrian” is a broad earlier span, not a fourth era equivalent to those three.</li><li>Paleozoic periods in the supplied course sequence<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cambrian, Ordovician, Silurian, Devonian, Mississippian, Pennsylvanian, Permian</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>Mississippian and Pennsylvanian together correspond to the Carboniferous used elsewhere.</li><li>Mesozoic periods<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>Cenozoic periods<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Paleogene, Neogene, Quaternary</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>Major extinction events mark some boundaries.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bracket events with dated rocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An interbedded ash can closely date a depositional horizon. Dated volcanic layers below and above a fossil bed bracket its age.</li><li>A dike is younger than beds it cuts.</li><li>Clasts eroded from that dike are older than a later deposit containing them.</li><li>Neither relationship makes the sediment exactly the dike age.</li><li>An unconformity is younger than the underlying record and older than overlying deposition. Use source boundary values as course approximations rather than exact natural switches.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g9-c9a-periods":{"source":"<h3>Oldest to youngest</h3><ul><li><b>Paleozoic:</b> Cambrian → Ordovician → Silurian → Devonian → Mississippian → Pennsylvanian → Permian.</li><li><b>Mesozoic:</b> Triassic → Jurassic → Cretaceous.</li><li><b>Cenozoic:</b> Paleogene → Neogene → Quaternary. Quaternary is not a blank in this 12-label source task.</li></ul><h3>How to read this source figure</h3><ul><li>Trace each callout into the period column. Five boxes are on the left and seven on the right; read each side from top to bottom.</li><li>The supplied Connect graphic retains older numeric boundaries, including 65, 251, and 542 Ma. The current supplied textbook9.8 instead states 66, 252, and 541 Ma. This exercise checks period placement; use the textbook values for numeric course recall.</li><li>Mississippian and Pennsylvanian are combined as the Carboniferous in many other timescales. Keep the full supplied bank here.</li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T16\">Practice time and Earth history</button></p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Oldest to youngest</h4><ul><li><b>Paleozoic</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Cambrian → Ordovician → Silurian → Devonian → Mississippian → Pennsylvanian → Permian.</li></ul></li><li><b>Mesozoic</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Triassic → Jurassic → Cretaceous.</li></ul></li><li><b>Cenozoic</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Paleogene → Neogene → Quaternary.</li></ul></li><li>Quaternary is not a blank in this 12-label source task.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">How to read this source figure</h4><ul><li>Trace each callout into the period column.</li><li>Five boxes are on the left and seven on the right.</li><li>Read each side from top to bottom.</li><li>The supplied Connect graphic retains older numeric boundaries, including 65, 251, and 542 Ma.</li><li>The current supplied textbook9.8 instead states 66, 252, and 541 Ma.</li><li>This exercise checks period placement.</li><li>Use the textbook values for numeric course recall.</li><li>Mississippian and Pennsylvanian are combined as the Carboniferous in many other timescales. Keep the full supplied bank here.</li></ul><p><button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T16\">Practice time and Earth history</button></p></section>"},"g-c9q-time":{"source":"<h4>Oldest to youngest</h4><ul><li>Precambrian time precedes the Cambrian and the abundant hard-part fossil record emphasized in the course. It does not mean there was no earlier life.</li><li>The large sequence is <b>Precambrian → Paleozoic → Mesozoic → Cenozoic</b>. Paleozoic, Mesozoic, and Cenozoic are eras; Precambrian is a broader informal interval containing several eons.</li></ul><h4>Connect chart limitation</h4><ul><li>The assessment’s A–D chart did not render. Its checked D response cannot establish where D was drawn. Learn the temporal relation here without inventing the missing geometry.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Oldest to youngest</h4><ul><li>Precambrian time precedes the Cambrian and the abundant hard-part fossil record emphasized in the course. It does not mean there was no earlier life.</li><li>The large sequence is <b>Precambrian → Paleozoic → Mesozoic → Cenozoic</b>.</li><li>Paleozoic, Mesozoic, and Cenozoic are eras.</li><li>Precambrian is a broader informal interval containing several eons.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect chart limitation</h4><ul><li>The assessment’s A–D chart did not render. Its checked D response cannot establish where D was drawn. Learn the temporal relation here without inventing the missing geometry.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-8-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Time units were recognized before their numerical ages were calibrated.</li><li>Keep periods nested within their correct eras.</li><li>Dated volcanic units can bracket fossil-bearing sediment.</li><li>Cutting and containment place the dike on opposite sides of later events.</li><li>Preserve the historical figure without mixing its dates into current course teaching.</li></ul></section><p>Fossil succession supplies the sequence; dated relationships calibrate it.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Time units were recognized before their numerical ages were calibrated.</li><li>Keep periods nested within their correct eras.</li><li>Dated volcanic units can bracket fossil-bearing sediment.</li><li>Cutting and containment place the dike on opposite sides of later events.</li><li>Preserve the historical figure without mixing its dates into current course teaching.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fossil succession supplies the sequence.</li><li>Dated relationships calibrate it.</li></ul>"},"g9-9-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why early estimates were incomplete</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An early ocean-salt estimate assumed salt only accumulated; it neglected removal into deposits. An early cooling estimate omitted internal radioactive heat. Both illustrate how a missing process biases a model.</li><li>Erosion, annual ice layers, seasonal sediment <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">varves</mark>, and slow plate motion independently demonstrate long durations, but no single one must preserve all of Earth history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Old material and converging clocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied chapter uses an Earth age of about <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">4.55 billion years</mark>. Meteorites around 4.53–4.58 billion years and ancient lunar material support early solar-system formation ages.</li><li>About 4.0-billion-year-old Acasta gneiss preserves an older igneous history altered by metamorphism. Some detrital zircons exceed 4.4 billion years; their host sediment is younger.</li><li>Earth recycles crust through erosion, melting, metamorphism, and plate processes. Its oldest surviving rock need not equal its formation age.</li><li>Agreement among suitable independent isotope systems and coherent regional age patterns strengthens the interpretation. An individual mineral age still requires an event and material context.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why early estimates were incomplete</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An early ocean-salt estimate assumed salt only accumulated.</li><li>It neglected removal into deposits.</li><li>An early cooling estimate omitted internal radioactive heat.</li><li>Both illustrate how a missing process biases a model.</li><li>Erosion, annual ice layers, seasonal sediment <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">varves</mark>, and slow plate motion independently demonstrate long durations, but no single one must preserve all of Earth history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Old material and converging clocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied chapter uses an Earth age of about <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">4.55 billion years</mark>. Meteorites around 4.53–4.58 billion years and ancient lunar material support early solar-system formation ages.</li><li>About 4.0-billion-year-old Acasta gneiss preserves an older igneous history altered by metamorphism.</li><li>Some detrital zircons exceed 4.4 billion years.</li><li>Their host sediment is younger.</li><li>Earth recycles crust through erosion, melting, metamorphism, and plate processes. Its oldest surviving rock need not equal its formation age.</li><li>Agreement among suitable independent isotope systems and coherent regional age patterns strengthens the interpretation. An individual mineral age still requires an event and material context.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g-c9q-records":{"source":"<h4>Count repeated seasons</h4><ul><li>Tree growth rings, annual ice layers, and lake varves can preserve repeated yearly cycles. A varve includes the alternating seasonal sediment layers representing one year.</li><li>Count complete annual cycles, not every light or dark stripe as a separate year. Identify gaps or disturbed records before assuming an uninterrupted count.</li></ul><h4>The oldest material is Precambrian</h4><ul><li>The textbook describes approximately 4.0-billion-year-old Acasta Gneiss in Canada and still older individual zircon grains, including Australian material, up to about 4.4 billion years.</li><li>A grain’s crystallization age, the age of its present host rock, and the age of Earth are different targets. These ancient dates fall in Precambrian time.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Count repeated seasons</h4><ul><li>Tree growth rings, annual ice layers, and lake varves can preserve repeated yearly cycles. A varve includes the alternating seasonal sediment layers representing one year.</li><li>Count complete annual cycles, not every light or dark stripe as a separate year. Identify gaps or disturbed records before assuming an uninterrupted count.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The oldest material is Precambrian</h4><ul><li>The textbook describes approximately 4.0-billion-year-old Acasta Gneiss in Canada and still older individual zircon grains, including Australian material, up to about 4.4 billion years.</li><li>A grain’s crystallization age, the age of its present host rock, and the age of Earth are different targets. These ancient dates fall in Precambrian time.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-9-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Evaluate all important inputs, outputs and heat sources.</li><li>One complete seasonal cycle is the relevant counting unit.</li><li>Rates constrain duration when the assumptions fit the event.</li><li>A surviving crystal, its host and Earth can have different ages.</li><li>Converging independent evidence supports the age of Earth.</li></ul></section><p>Independent records support duration at different scales.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Evaluate all important inputs, outputs and heat sources.</li><li>One complete seasonal cycle is the relevant counting unit.</li><li>Rates constrain duration when the assumptions fit the event.</li><li>A surviving crystal, its host and Earth can have different ages.</li><li>Converging independent evidence supports the age of Earth.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Independent records support duration at different scales.</li></ul>"},"g9-10-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Impacts and a hot planet</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An impact converts kinetic energy into shock, fracture, heating, and sometimes melting. Excavation ejects material; a smaller crater may remain simple, whereas larger complex craters can develop central rebound peaks.</li><li>Early Earth retained formation heat, experienced more impacts, and contained more heat-producing radioactive isotopes. Archean ultramafic lavas and greenstone belts record distinctive early conditions.</li><li>The onset and character of early plate tectonics remain uncertain in the supplied discussion; do not infer a precise start date from a single rock.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Atmosphere, water, and free oxygen</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Light early gases escaped; volcanic outgassing supplied water vapor, carbon dioxide, and sulfur-bearing gases. Cooling and water accumulation helped establish oceans.</li><li>Photosynthetic microbes produced <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">free oxygen</mark>. Oxygen combined with dissolved iron to form iron-rich deposits; banded iron formations alternate iron-rich layers with silica-rich material.</li><li>The course places the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Great Oxygenation Event</mark> around 2.3–2.4 billion years ago, followed by further changes. Oxygen in minerals is not the same as free oxygen gas in the atmosphere.</li><li>Preserved oxygen-sensitive minerals and later oxidized red beds help reconstruct atmospheric change. The detailed oxygenation history remains an active scientific problem.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Impacts and a hot planet</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An impact converts kinetic energy into shock, fracture, heating, and sometimes melting.</li><li>Excavation ejects material.</li><li>A smaller crater may remain simple, whereas larger complex craters can develop central rebound peaks.</li><li>Early Earth retained formation heat, experienced more impacts, and contained more heat-producing radioactive isotopes. Archean ultramafic lavas and greenstone belts record distinctive early conditions.</li><li>The onset and character of early plate tectonics remain uncertain in the supplied discussion.</li><li>Do not infer a precise start date from a single rock.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Atmosphere, water, and free oxygen</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Light early gases escaped.</li><li>Volcanic outgassing supplied water vapor, carbon dioxide, and sulfur-bearing gases.</li><li>Cooling and water accumulation helped establish oceans.</li><li>Photosynthetic microbes produced <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">free oxygen</mark>.</li><li>Oxygen combined with dissolved iron to form iron-rich deposits.</li><li>Banded iron formations alternate iron-rich layers with silica-rich material.</li><li>The course places the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Great Oxygenation Event</mark> around 2.3–2.4 billion years ago, followed by further changes. Oxygen in minerals is not the same as free oxygen gas in the atmosphere.</li><li>Preserved oxygen-sensitive minerals and later oxidized red beds help reconstruct atmospheric change. The detailed oxygenation history remains an active scientific problem.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g-c9q-early-earth":{"source":"<h4>Simple and complex craters</h4><ul><li>In the textbook model, a relatively small impact makes a simple crater. A larger impact can make a complex crater with a central peak.</li><li>Both kinds occur on the Moon. Shape is not a rule that every simple crater is younger, or that moons have only simple craters.</li></ul><h4>From photosynthesis to iron formation</h4><ul><li>Cyanobacteria release oxygen through photosynthesis. Oxygen entering the atmosphere and oceans changed which minerals could form and persist.</li><li>The pictured iron formation has red and gray bands, largely iron oxides and quartz. Dissolved iron oxidized and precipitated in ocean water. This differs from lava cooling or iron oxidation in a soil on land.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Simple and complex craters</h4><ul><li>In the textbook model, a relatively small impact makes a simple crater. A larger impact can make a complex crater with a central peak.</li><li>Both kinds occur on the Moon. Shape is not a rule that every simple crater is younger, or that moons have only simple craters.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From photosynthesis to iron formation</h4><ul><li>Cyanobacteria release oxygen through photosynthesis. Oxygen entering the atmosphere and oceans changed which minerals could form and persist.</li><li>The pictured iron formation has red and gray bands, largely iron oxides and quartz. Dissolved iron oxidized and precipitated in ocean water. This differs from lava cooling or iron oxidation in a soil on land.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-10-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Impact excavates material and redistributes it around the crater.</li><li>Similar processes can have very different early-Earth intensities.</li><li>Free oxygen is chemically different from oxygen bound in rock.</li><li>Oxygen production can drive iron precipitation before abundant atmospheric oxygen accumulates.</li><li>Treat the oxygen curve as an interpretation supported by geological evidence.</li></ul></section><p>Early Earth changed physically, chemically and biologically.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Impact excavates material and redistributes it around the crater.</li><li>Similar processes can have very different early-Earth intensities.</li><li>Free oxygen is chemically different from oxygen bound in rock.</li><li>Oxygen production can drive iron precipitation before abundant atmospheric oxygen accumulates.</li><li>Treat the oxygen curve as an interpretation supported by geological evidence.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Early Earth changed physically, chemically and biologically.</li></ul>"},"g9-11-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From microbial structures to marine diversity</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Ancient stromatolites record microbial communities by about 3.5 billion years ago in the supplied examples. The oldest preserved evidence is not necessarily the exact origin of life.</li><li>Photosynthesis increased oxygen; ozone protection and changing cell organization helped create opportunities for later life. The source places sexual reproduction after about 1.5 billion years ago.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cambrian explosion</mark>, beginning around the course value of 541 Ma, was a geologically rapid diversification over millions of years, including many shelled organisms. It was not a single instantaneous event.</li><li>Early Paleozoic seas included trilobites, corals, crinoids, and mollusks. Fish and reefs expanded, followed by plants and insects on land and later amphibian and reptile diversification.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The end-Permian crisis</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Great Dying</mark> at about 252 Ma eliminated a large fraction of marine and terrestrial species. Its scale distinguishes it from ordinary background turnover.</li><li>The source discusses Siberian volcanism, environmental effects of Pangaea, ocean anoxia or circulation changes, and a disputed impact proposal. These are causal hypotheses with different support, not equally established facts.</li><li>When reconstructing a crisis, separate dated coincidence, a plausible mechanism, and direct evidence. Do not turn a listed possibility into a confirmed sole cause.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From microbial structures to marine diversity</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Ancient stromatolites record microbial communities by about 3.5 billion years ago in the supplied examples. The oldest preserved evidence is not necessarily the exact origin of life.</li><li>Photosynthesis increased oxygen.</li><li>Ozone protection and changing cell organization helped create opportunities for later life.</li><li>The source places sexual reproduction after about 1.5 billion years ago.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cambrian explosion</mark>, beginning around the course value of 541 Ma, was a geologically rapid diversification over millions of years, including many shelled organisms. It was not a single instantaneous event.</li><li>Early Paleozoic seas included trilobites, corals, crinoids, and mollusks. Fish and reefs expanded, followed by plants and insects on land and later amphibian and reptile diversification.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">The end-Permian crisis</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Great Dying</mark> at about 252 Ma eliminated a large fraction of marine and terrestrial species. Its scale distinguishes it from ordinary background turnover.</li><li>The source discusses Siberian volcanism, environmental effects of Pangaea, ocean anoxia or circulation changes, and a disputed impact proposal. These are causal hypotheses with different support, not equally established facts.</li><li>When reconstructing a crisis, separate dated coincidence, a plausible mechanism, and direct evidence. Do not turn a listed possibility into a confirmed sole cause.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g-c9q-cambrian":{"source":"<h4>What changed</h4><ul><li>The course describes a geologically rapid diversification and widespread appearance of organisms with shells or other hard protective parts at the beginning of the Cambrian.</li><li>The textbook places this at about 541 Ma and describes changes over roughly 20–30 million years. “Explosion” is not a volcanic blast, and it was not the origin of all life.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What changed</h4><ul><li>The course describes a geologically rapid diversification and widespread appearance of organisms with shells or other hard protective parts at the beginning of the Cambrian.</li><li>The textbook places this at about 541 Ma and describes changes over roughly 20–30 million years. “Explosion” is not a volcanic blast, and it was not the origin of all life.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-11-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A microbial construction can preserve biological evidence.</li><li>Diversification and improved preservation both shape the fossil record.</li><li>Reconstruct communities from multiple kinds of preserved evidence.</li><li>A broad extinction requires an explanation extending beyond one species or site.</li><li>Coincident timing, a proposed mechanism and a demonstrated cause are distinct.</li></ul></section><p>Life diversified through changing environments, leaving a selective record.</p><h3>More original course figures</h3><p>Compare these additional examples with the relationships explained above. Open an image to inspect the original.</p><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.11-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.11-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional field evidence in the early-life section\"></a><figcaption>Additional field evidence in the early-life section · Exploring Geology9.11 · PDFp2 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A microbial construction can preserve biological evidence.</li><li>Diversification and improved preservation both shape the fossil record.</li><li>Reconstruct communities from multiple kinds of preserved evidence.</li><li>A broad extinction requires an explanation extending beyond one species or site.</li><li>Coincident timing, a proposed mechanism and a demonstrated cause are distinct.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Life diversified through changing environments, leaving a selective record.</li></ul><h3>More original course figures</h3><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compare these additional examples with the relationships explained above.</li><li>Open an image to inspect the original.</li></ul><figure><a href=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.11-p2-Im1.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><img loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto\" src=\"animations/chapter9/source/9.11-p2-Im1.png\" alt=\"Additional field evidence in the early-life section\"></a><figcaption>Additional field evidence in the early-life section · Exploring Geology9.11 · PDFp2 · unchanged original figure</figcaption></figure>"},"g9-12-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Life before the end-Cretaceous boundary</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Triassic ecosystems included early mammals, dinosaur relatives, conifers, and marine ammonites. Mammals did not originate only after dinosaurs disappeared.</li><li>Jurassic ecosystems included diverse dinosaurs and early birds such as Archaeopteryx. Cretaceous ecosystems included flowering plants, birds, mammals, marine reptiles, fish, and plankton.</li><li>Dinosaur hip categories describe anatomy; a group name alone does not establish bird ancestry. Read the relationship described by the source instead of treating a label as a family tree.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Extinction and later diversification</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At about 66 Ma the end-Cretaceous crisis eliminated nonavian dinosaurs and many other groups. The Chicxulub impact in Mexico supplied dust and gases capable of disrupting sunlight and food webs.</li><li>The chapter also discusses Deccan volcanism as a possible contributing influence. Distinguish an impact mechanism from an unsupported claim that every extinction had one identical cause.</li><li>Mammals diversified extensively in the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cenozoic</mark>. Later ice-age ecosystems included large mammals; climate and other changing conditions are relevant to their losses.</li><li>Origin, diversification, and extinction are separate milestones. A group can exist long before it becomes ecologically prominent.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Life before the end-Cretaceous boundary</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Triassic ecosystems included early mammals, dinosaur relatives, conifers, and marine ammonites. Mammals did not originate only after dinosaurs disappeared.</li><li>Jurassic ecosystems included diverse dinosaurs and early birds such as Archaeopteryx. Cretaceous ecosystems included flowering plants, birds, mammals, marine reptiles, fish, and plankton.</li><li>Dinosaur hip categories describe anatomy.</li><li>A group name alone does not establish bird ancestry.</li><li>Read the relationship described by the source instead of treating a label as a family tree.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Extinction and later diversification</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At about 66 Ma the end-Cretaceous crisis eliminated nonavian dinosaurs and many other groups. The Chicxulub impact in Mexico supplied dust and gases capable of disrupting sunlight and food webs.</li><li>The chapter also discusses Deccan volcanism as a possible contributing influence. Distinguish an impact mechanism from an unsupported claim that every extinction had one identical cause.</li><li>Mammals diversified extensively in the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cenozoic</mark>.</li><li>Later ice-age ecosystems included large mammals.</li><li>Climate and other changing conditions are relevant to their losses.</li><li>Origin, diversification, and extinction are separate milestones. A group can exist long before it becomes ecologically prominent.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g-c9q-mesozoic":{"source":"<h4>Mesozoic context</h4><ul><li>The Mesozoic is the course’s “age of dinosaurs.” Its periods, oldest to youngest, are Triassic, Jurassic, and Cretaceous.</li><li>The following Cenozoic is associated with the expansion of mammals. Do not confuse an era with one period within it.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Mesozoic context</h4><ul><li>The Mesozoic is the course’s “age of dinosaurs.” Its periods, oldest to youngest, are Triassic, Jurassic, and Cretaceous.</li><li>The following Cenozoic is associated with the expansion of mammals. Do not confuse an era with one period within it.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-12-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Period, habitat and organism group are different classifications.</li><li>Do not replace a diverse era with one representative animal.</li><li>A boundary event can alter conditions across many ecosystems.</li><li>Lineages change across generations; individuals do not become new species on command.</li><li>New discoveries can refine a reconstruction without erasing its evidence.</li></ul></section><p>Changing communities cross boundaries without every lineage beginning there.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Period, habitat and organism group are different classifications.</li><li>Do not replace a diverse era with one representative animal.</li><li>A boundary event can alter conditions across many ecosystems.</li><li>Lineages change across generations.</li><li>Individuals do not become new species on command.</li><li>New discoveries can refine a reconstruction without erasing its evidence.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Changing communities cross boundaries without every lineage beginning there.</li></ul>"},"g9-13-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Correlate with independent evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Traceable lateral continuity is strong evidence that separated exposures belong to one unit. Distinctive beds, repeated sequences, shared events, and regional unconformities also support correlation.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Facies</mark> can change sideways: a sandy beach and offshore mud may form at the same time. Equal age does not require identical lithology or thickness.</li><li>Fossil assemblages, suitable isotope ages, and paleomagnetic reversal patterns add constraints. A matching pattern suggests correlation but must be evaluated with the rest of the evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build a sequence, not a guess</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Missing units may result from nondeposition, environmental restriction, thickness changes, or erosion. Inspect contacts to distinguish these alternatives.</li><li>The supplied block combines old metamorphic basement, granite, unconformities, sedimentary beds, a dike and lava, fault motion, canyon cutting, and river gravel. Apply each visible relationship to a pair of events first.</li><li>A fault can move more than once. A preexisting scarp and later displacement of lava can represent different episodes; “the fault” need not be one instant.</li><li>Keep an event uncertain if no observed relation orders it. A complete-looking list is less useful than an honest set of supported constraints.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Correlate with independent evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Traceable lateral continuity is strong evidence that separated exposures belong to one unit. Distinctive beds, repeated sequences, shared events, and regional unconformities also support correlation.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Facies</mark> can change sideways<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A sandy beach and offshore mud may form at the same time.</li></ul></li><li>Equal age does not require identical lithology or thickness.</li><li>Fossil assemblages, suitable isotope ages, and paleomagnetic reversal patterns add constraints. A matching pattern suggests correlation but must be evaluated with the rest of the evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build a sequence, not a guess</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Missing units may result from nondeposition, environmental restriction, thickness changes, or erosion. Inspect contacts to distinguish these alternatives.</li><li>The supplied block combines old metamorphic basement, granite, unconformities, sedimentary beds, a dike and lava, fault motion, canyon cutting, and river gravel. Apply each visible relationship to a pair of events first.</li><li>A fault can move more than once.</li><li>A preexisting scarp and later displacement of lava can represent different episodes.</li><li>“The fault” need not be one instant.</li><li>Keep an event uncertain if no observed relation orders it. A complete-looking list is less useful than an honest set of supported constraints.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g-c9q-correlation":{"source":"<h4>Physical continuity first</h4><ul><li>Where exposure permits, physically tracing a bed continuously between two sections is the surest evidence that it is the same unit.</li><li>Matching distinctive rock types, fossils, and position within a sequence supports correlation when continuity cannot be followed. Similar color or equal height alone can be misleading.</li></ul><h4>Read the original columns</h4><ul><li>The dashed connections in these textbook columns are interpreted correlations, not exposed rock connecting the columns. Corresponding units can have different thicknesses.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Physical continuity first</h4><ul><li>Where exposure permits, physically tracing a bed continuously between two sections is the surest evidence that it is the same unit.</li><li>Matching distinctive rock types, fossils, and position within a sequence supports correlation when continuity cannot be followed. Similar color or equal height alone can be misleading.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the original columns</h4><ul><li>The dashed connections in these textbook columns are interpreted correlations, not exposed rock connecting the columns. Corresponding units can have different thicknesses.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-13-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Explain the matches and the units that fail to match.</li><li>Independent evidence can constrain the same interpretation in different ways.</li><li>Same age need not mean same lithology or thickness.</li><li>Each contact supplies a specific earlier-than or later-than relationship.</li><li>A fault’s latest movement can be younger than its first movement.</li></ul></section><p>A strong history explains both matching units and lateral differences.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Explain the matches and the units that fail to match.</li><li>Independent evidence can constrain the same interpretation in different ways.</li><li>Same age need not mean same lithology or thickness.</li><li>Each contact supplies a specific earlier-than or later-than relationship.</li><li>A fault’s latest movement can be younger than its first movement.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A strong history explains both matching units and lateral differences.</li></ul>"},"g9-14-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Hazards from a longer record</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lava superposition, weathering, and suitable isotopic dates reconstruct eruption history. This constrains hazards without predicting the exact date of the next eruption.</li><li>Offset dated rocks constrain fault activity; a lava covering a scarp can limit older movement. Scarp preservation also depends on erosion.</li><li>Flood gauges often cover a much shorter interval than deposits, terraces, soils, and datable charcoal. Geological evidence extends the history beyond human measurements.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Resources and human history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Intrusions and crosscutting hydrothermal veins help reconstruct mineralization. The timing of folding, burial, migration, and erosion matters to oil and gas traps.</li><li>Basin stratigraphy controls groundwater pathways. An isotope-based water residence time is a different measurement from the age of the surrounding sediment.</li><li>Archaeology combines layer order, construction relationships, tree rings, and appropriate radiocarbon dates. Reused building stones can predate the wall containing them.</li><li>Ancient hominid deposits can be bracketed by volcanic ash; carbon-14 is not a suitable clock for material millions of years old.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Hazards from a longer record</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lava superposition, weathering, and suitable isotopic dates reconstruct eruption history. This constrains hazards without predicting the exact date of the next eruption.</li><li>Offset dated rocks constrain fault activity.</li><li>A lava covering a scarp can limit older movement.</li><li>Scarp preservation also depends on erosion.</li><li>Flood gauges often cover a much shorter interval than deposits, terraces, soils, and datable charcoal. Geological evidence extends the history beyond human measurements.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Resources and human history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Intrusions and crosscutting hydrothermal veins help reconstruct mineralization. The timing of folding, burial, migration, and erosion matters to oil and gas traps.</li><li>Basin stratigraphy controls groundwater pathways. An isotope-based water residence time is a different measurement from the age of the surrounding sediment.</li><li>Archaeology combines layer order, construction relationships, tree rings, and appropriate radiocarbon dates. Reused building stones can predate the wall containing them.</li><li>Ancient hominid deposits can be bracketed by volcanic ash.</li><li>Carbon-14 is not a suitable clock for material millions of years old.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g-c9q-origins":{"source":"<h4>Relative and numerical evidence</h4><ul><li>Superposition and inclusions apply to archaeological deposits as well as older rocks. A reused fragment predates the younger wall or deposit containing it.</li><li>Appropriately young organic remains can be dated with carbon-14. Older human-associated sequences can be constrained by interbedded volcanic rocks and fossil evidence.</li></ul><h4>Match method to material and timescale</h4><ul><li>The textbook places useful carbon-14 applications here in the hundreds to tens of thousands of years, not millions of years. Bones, wood, and charcoal preserve organic carbon; volcanic minerals supply other isotope systems.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Relative and numerical evidence</h4><ul><li>Superposition and inclusions apply to archaeological deposits as well as older rocks. A reused fragment predates the younger wall or deposit containing it.</li><li>Appropriately young organic remains can be dated with carbon-14. Older human-associated sequences can be constrained by interbedded volcanic rocks and fossil evidence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Match method to material and timescale</h4><ul><li>The textbook places useful carbon-14 applications here in the hundreds to tens of thousands of years, not millions of years.</li><li>Bones, wood, and charcoal preserve organic carbon.</li><li>Volcanic minerals supply other isotope systems.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-14-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Past-event ages inform hazard assessment without giving an exact forecast.</li><li>Use deposits before and after an event to constrain its timing.</li><li>A resource depends on the sequence of processes that formed and preserved it.</li><li>Water age and aquifer-rock age describe different events.</li><li>A useful age must fit both the sample and its archaeological context.</li></ul></section><p>Choose an event and a method before treating an age as useful.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Past-event ages inform hazard assessment without giving an exact forecast.</li><li>Use deposits before and after an event to constrain its timing.</li><li>A resource depends on the sequence of processes that formed and preserved it.</li><li>Water age and aquifer-rock age describe different events.</li><li>A useful age must fit both the sample and its archaeological context.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Choose an event and a method before treating an age as useful.</li></ul>"},"g9-15-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Old rocks, younger gaps, much younger canyon</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Basement metamorphic rocks formed about 1.7 Ga, with somewhat younger granite. Uplift and erosion exposed this deep crust before later sediment accumulated.</li><li>Late Precambrian strata around 1.1 Ga were deposited, tilted, and eroded. Younger Paleozoic strata lie across the resulting angular unconformity.</li><li>Contacts above crystalline basement may be nonconformities; contacts above tilted layered rocks may be angular unconformities. Parallel sedimentary intervals also contain disconformities.</li><li>Paleozoic deposits record changing marine, river, and dune environments. Missing Ordovician and Silurian record and other gaps prevent treating the exposed column as continuous time.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bracket the canyon separately</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The chapter describes much later deformation and uplift, followed by major canyon excavation chiefly during the last roughly 5 million years. This is its reconstruction, not the age of the wall rocks.</li><li>An approximately 8 Ma plateau lava predates the main canyon; lava entering an existing canyon around 4–5 Ma shows the canyon already existed then. Faults also offset much younger basalt.</li><li>The canyon preserves only parts of Earth history: the earliest billions of years and long later intervals are absent. Rock thickness is not a uniform clock.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Old rocks, younger gaps, much younger canyon</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Basement metamorphic rocks formed about 1.7 Ga, with somewhat younger granite. Uplift and erosion exposed this deep crust before later sediment accumulated.</li><li>Late Precambrian strata around 1.1 Ga were deposited, tilted, and eroded. Younger Paleozoic strata lie across the resulting angular unconformity.</li><li>Contacts above crystalline basement may be nonconformities.</li><li>Contacts above tilted layered rocks may be angular unconformities.</li><li>Parallel sedimentary intervals also contain disconformities.</li><li>Paleozoic deposits record changing marine, river, and dune environments. Missing Ordovician and Silurian record and other gaps prevent treating the exposed column as continuous time.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bracket the canyon separately</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The chapter describes much later deformation and uplift, followed by major canyon excavation chiefly during the last roughly 5 million years. This is its reconstruction, not the age of the wall rocks.</li><li>An approximately 8 Ma plateau lava predates the main canyon.</li><li>Lava entering an existing canyon around 4–5 Ma shows the canyon already existed then.</li><li>Faults also offset much younger basalt.</li><li>The canyon preserves only parts of Earth history<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The earliest billions of years and long later intervals are absent.</li></ul></li><li>Rock thickness is not a uniform clock.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g-c9q-canyon":{"source":"<h4>Lower gap in the textbook history</h4><ul><li>Basement rocks are approximately 1.7 billion years old; the lower late-Precambrian deposits are about 1.1 billion years old. The lower unconformity therefore represents about <b>600 million years</b> missing from this local rock record.</li><li>The younger upper unconformity is a different surface. Where surfaces merge westward, the combined Great Unconformity represents an even larger gap.</li></ul><h4>Keep the quantities straight</h4><ul><li>1.7 − 1.1 = 0.6 billion years = 600 million years. This gap is not the age of the modern canyon incision or the age of Earth.</li></ul>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Lower gap in the textbook history</h4><ul><li>Basement rocks are approximately 1.7 billion years old.</li><li>The lower late-Precambrian deposits are about 1.1 billion years old.</li><li>The lower unconformity therefore represents about <b>600 million years</b> missing from this local rock record.</li><li>The younger upper unconformity is a different surface. Where surfaces merge westward, the combined Great Unconformity represents an even larger gap.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the quantities straight</h4><ul><li>1.7 − 1.1 = 0.6 billion years = 600 million years. This gap is not the age of the modern canyon incision or the age of Earth.</li></ul></section>"},"g9-15-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The basement, erosion surface and cover represent separate events.</li><li>Unconformity type depends on local relationships along the surface.</li><li>Thickness and continuity of recorded time are different properties.</li><li>Date deformation and incision using their own crosscutting evidence.</li><li>The canyon is an exposure of a long, interrupted history.</li></ul></section><p>Ancient wall rocks and recent incision must be dated separately.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The basement, erosion surface and cover represent separate events.</li><li>Unconformity type depends on local relationships along the surface.</li><li>Thickness and continuity of recorded time are different properties.</li><li>Date deformation and incision using their own crosscutting evidence.</li><li>The canyon is an exposure of a long, interrupted history.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Ancient wall rocks and recent incision must be dated separately.</li></ul>"},"g9-16-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the field notes before ordering labels</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied investigation, C is Cambrian beach sandstone/conglomerate, B is Ordovician marine shale, A is Permian terrestrial sandstone, S is Jurassic river/lake sandstone, and K is Cretaceous marine limestone. Fossils establish their relative sequence.</li><li>Granite G is weathered beneath C, and C contains G clasts: G is older and the depositional contact is a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">nonconformity</mark>. The eroded parallel B–A contact is a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">disconformity</mark>.</li><li>Basalt dike D bakes and cuts A, B, C, and G, so it is younger than those units. Fault F cuts several sedimentary units locally and in nearby exposures; include the field notes beyond the block view.</li><li>River gravel R has a developed soil and middle-Cenozoic mammals. Its former channel is truncated by canyon N, and lava L enters that canyon: R deposition precedes canyon cutting, which precedes that lava.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compute ages under stated assumptions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>For G, 125 parent atoms and 875 daughter atoms give a parent fraction of 1/8. Under the simple closed-system, zero-initial-daughter model, that is three 500-million-year half-lives, or <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">1,500 Ma</mark>.</li><li>For D, equal parent and daughter amounts give one half-life under the same model. With a 40-million-year half-life, the calculated age is <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">40 Ma</mark>.</li><li>These are instructional calculations, not measured analytical certainty. Separate numerical constraints, fossil ordering, and visible crosscutting evidence; do not infer an unsupported order merely from alphabetical labels.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the field notes before ordering labels</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the supplied investigation, C is Cambrian beach sandstone/conglomerate, B is Ordovician marine shale, A is Permian terrestrial sandstone, S is Jurassic river/lake sandstone, and K is Cretaceous marine limestone. Fossils establish their relative sequence.</li><li>Granite G is weathered beneath C, and C contains G clasts<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>G is older and the depositional contact is a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">nonconformity</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>The eroded parallel B–A contact is a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">disconformity</mark>.</li><li>Basalt dike D bakes and cuts A, B, C, and G, so it is younger than those units.</li><li>Fault F cuts several sedimentary units locally and in nearby exposures.</li><li>Include the field notes beyond the block view.</li><li>River gravel R has a developed soil and middle-Cenozoic mammals.</li><li>Its former channel is truncated by canyon N, and lava L enters that canyon<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>R deposition precedes canyon cutting, which precedes that lava.</li></ul></li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compute ages under stated assumptions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>For G, 125 parent atoms and 875 daughter atoms give a parent fraction of 1/8. Under the simple closed-system, zero-initial-daughter model, that is three 500-million-year half-lives, or <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">1,500 Ma</mark>.</li><li>For D, equal parent and daughter amounts give one half-life under the same model. With a 40-million-year half-life, the calculated age is <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">40 Ma</mark>.</li><li>These are instructional calculations, not measured analytical certainty.</li><li>Separate numerical constraints, fossil ordering, and visible crosscutting evidence.</li><li>Do not infer an unsupported order merely from alphabetical labels.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g9-c9a-terrain":{"source":"<h3>Rock units: identify the oldest foundation</h3><ul><li>G is granite basement. C lies above it and contains granite clasts, so G existed before C.</li><li>The lower sedimentary sequence is C then B then A. The higher sequence includes S and then K. Letters are names, not alphabetical age order.</li></ul><h3>Features: trace the physical contacts</h3><ul><li>F cuts older rocks; D crosses F, so the fault preceded the dike.</li><li>Old channels R interrupt the dike; canyon N truncates an old channel. Those relations place R after D and N after R.</li><li>Lava L poured into the already formed canyon and has little weathering or soil. It is younger than the canyon and youngest among the units requested in the source exercise.</li><li>Feature sequence: F→D→R→N→L. Granite G is oldest among the specified rock units; fault F is oldest among the specified later features. These are different comparison sets.</li></ul><h3>Add the isotope evidence</h3><ul><li>The table dates G at 1500 million years and D at 40 million years under the exercise assumptions.</li><li>Relative order does not supply an exact numerical date for every remaining event.</li></ul><p><a class=\"btn pri\" href=\"#exam2/cases/e2-c9a-terrain-investigation\">Start the terrain investigation</a> <button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T17\">Practice geologic history</button></p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Rock units: identify the oldest foundation</h4><ul><li>G is granite basement. C lies above it and contains granite clasts, so G existed before C.</li><li>The lower sedimentary sequence is C then B then A. The higher sequence includes S and then K. Letters are names, not alphabetical age order.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Features: trace the physical contacts</h4><ul><li>F cuts older rocks.</li><li>D crosses F, so the fault preceded the dike.</li><li>Old channels R interrupt the dike.</li><li>Canyon N truncates an old channel.</li><li>Those relations place R after D and N after R.</li><li>Lava L poured into the already formed canyon and has little weathering or soil. It is younger than the canyon and youngest among the units requested in the source exercise.</li><li>Feature sequence<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>F→D→R→N→L.</li></ul></li><li>Granite G is oldest among the specified rock units.</li><li>Fault F is oldest among the specified later features.</li><li>These are different comparison sets.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Add the isotope evidence</h4><ul><li>The table dates G at 1500 million years and D at 40 million years under the exercise assumptions.</li><li>Relative order does not supply an exact numerical date for every remaining event.</li></ul><p><a class=\"btn pri\" href=\"#exam2/cases/e2-c9a-terrain-investigation\">Start the terrain investigation</a> <button class=\"btn\" data-e2-practice=\"E2T17\">Practice geologic history</button></p></section>"},"g9-16-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>G → C → B → A → S → K follows the supplied evidence.</li><li>A local contact determines which feature interrupts the other.</li><li>125 / (125 + 875) = 1/8 → 3 half-lives → 1,500 Ma.</li><li>500 / (500 + 500) = 1/2 → 1 half-life → 40 Ma.</li><li>Old gravels precede canyon cutting; lava then enters the canyon.</li></ul></section><p>Build the event order from contacts, fossils and the supplied isotope data.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the evidence to the event</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>G → C → B → A → S → K follows the supplied evidence.</li><li>A local contact determines which feature interrupts the other.</li><li>125 / (125 + 875) = 1/8 → 3 half-lives → 1,500 Ma.</li><li>500 / (500 + 500) = 1/2 → 1 half-life → 40 Ma.</li><li>Old gravels precede canyon cutting.</li><li>Lava then enters the canyon.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Build the event order from contacts, fossils and the supplied isotope data.</li></ul>"},"g10-1-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Map the surface</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sonar</mark> uses sound travel time and a known sound speed to infer depth; a ship can map a swath rather than only the point directly below it.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Submersibles and remotely operated vehicles</mark> observe and sample restricted areas directly. A photograph supplies local detail; it does not map an entire basin.</li><li>Satellite measurements of <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">sea-surface height and gravity</mark> help infer broad seafloor relief. The satellite is not seeing through deep seawater to photograph the bottom.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Sample and image below it</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Ocean drilling retrieves rock and sediment cores. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fossils, sediment properties, and basalt isotopes</mark> address different questions about environment, relative ages, and numerical ages.</li><li>Oxygen and carbon isotope measurements in appropriate marine records help investigate climate and ocean changes. An isotope ratio needs its geological context.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Seismic reflection</mark> uses reflected waves to image buried layers and structures; it complements cores rather than directly collecting rock.</li></ul></section></div><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Additional sample evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Manganese-rich nodules are another seafloor resource discussed in the source. Their composition and occurrence provide a different investigation target from mapping depth or collecting a core.</li></ul></section>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Map the surface</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sonar</mark> uses sound travel time and a known sound speed to infer depth.</li><li>A ship can map a swath rather than only the point directly below it.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Submersibles and remotely operated vehicles</mark> observe and sample restricted areas directly.</li><li>A photograph supplies local detail.</li><li>It does not map an entire basin.</li><li>Satellite measurements of <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">sea-surface height and gravity</mark> help infer broad seafloor relief. The satellite is not seeing through deep seawater to photograph the bottom.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Sample and image below it</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Ocean drilling retrieves rock and sediment cores. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Fossils, sediment properties, and basalt isotopes</mark> address different questions about environment, relative ages, and numerical ages.</li><li>Oxygen and carbon isotope measurements in appropriate marine records help investigate climate and ocean changes. An isotope ratio needs its geological context.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Seismic reflection</mark> uses reflected waves to image buried layers and structures.</li><li>It complements cores rather than directly collecting rock.</li></ul></section></div><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Additional sample evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Manganese-rich nodules are another seafloor resource discussed in the source. Their composition and occurrence provide a different investigation target from mapping depth or collecting a core.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-1-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What a recovered core adds</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A core preserves the order and thickness of actual sediment and volcanic layers. Rock type and structures constrain depositional conditions; fossils support environmental interpretation and correlation.</li><li>Appropriate volcanic minerals can supply isotopic ages. Comparing layer thickness with elapsed time estimates accumulation rate; neither a fossil photograph nor a depth sounding supplies all those measurements.</li><li>Oxygen and carbon isotope patterns can help reconstruct chemistry and climate. Interpret them with sample age and geological context.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Nodules and indirect measurements</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Manganese precipitating from seawater</mark> can form rounded, metal-rich nodules on the deep seafloor. Sampling composition, mapping distribution and evaluating environmental effects are different tasks.</li><li>A satellite measures sea-surface height related to gravity; it does not optically photograph a deep abyssal plain. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Seismic reflection</mark> maps buried geometry, whereas drilling recovers material.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">What a recovered core adds</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A core preserves the order and thickness of actual sediment and volcanic layers.</li><li>Rock type and structures constrain depositional conditions.</li><li>Fossils support environmental interpretation and correlation.</li><li>Appropriate volcanic minerals can supply isotopic ages.</li><li>Comparing layer thickness with elapsed time estimates accumulation rate.</li><li>Neither a fossil photograph nor a depth sounding supplies all those measurements.</li><li>Oxygen and carbon isotope patterns can help reconstruct chemistry and climate. Interpret them with sample age and geological context.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Nodules and indirect measurements</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Manganese precipitating from seawater</mark> can form rounded, metal-rich nodules on the deep seafloor. Sampling composition, mapping distribution and evaluating environmental effects are different tasks.</li><li>A satellite measures sea-surface height related to gravity.</li><li>It does not optically photograph a deep abyssal plain.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Seismic reflection</mark> maps buried geometry, whereas drilling recovers material.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-2-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build oceanic crust</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">mid-ocean ridge</mark>, plates diverge; mantle rises and magma adds new oceanic crust. Submarine eruptions make pillow basalt, fissures solidify as dikes, and deeper magma crystallizes within the crust.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Normal faults</mark> accommodate extension. Slow-spreading ridges commonly have a pronounced axial rift; fast-spreading ridges commonly have a broader, smoother crest.</li><li>Young hot crust is relatively buoyant. With distance and age it cools, becomes denser, and subsides; sediment can smooth its rough surface. Carbonate preservation also depends on water chemistry and dissolution depth.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Circulate seawater and build vents</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cold seawater descends through fractures; heated water rises</mark> above a magma chamber, reacting with rock and carrying dissolved chemicals.</li><li>When vent fluid mixes with cold seawater, sulfide particles precipitate. The dark plume and mineral chimney form through <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">precipitation</mark>, not combustion.</li><li>Lecture 15 slide 9 connects <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\">black smokers with ore deposits and unique ecology</mark>. Chemical energy can support bacteria and the associated food web without sunlight.</li></ul></section></div><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Source end members and preservation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied fast-ridge example spreads at about 10 cm per year; the slow example spreads at about 1–2 cm per year and has a 1–3 km axial rift. These are textbook end members, not universal thresholds.</li><li>Below the local <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">carbonate compensation depth (CCD)</mark>, carbonate dissolution can match or exceed accumulation. The source gives about 4,500–5,000 m and explicitly says the actual depth varies geographically. Clay and silica-rich sediment can dominate below it.</li><li>Vent water commonly exceeds 350°C in the source description. Pressure in deep water prevents boiling under those conditions; hot water is still different from the magma that heats it.</li></ul></section>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build oceanic crust</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">mid-ocean ridge</mark>, plates diverge.</li><li>Mantle rises and magma adds new oceanic crust.</li><li>Submarine eruptions make pillow basalt, fissures solidify as dikes, and deeper magma crystallizes within the crust.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Normal faults</mark> accommodate extension.</li><li>Slow-spreading ridges commonly have a pronounced axial rift.</li><li>Fast-spreading ridges commonly have a broader, smoother crest.</li><li>Young hot crust is relatively buoyant.</li><li>With distance and age it cools, becomes denser, and subsides.</li><li>Sediment can smooth its rough surface.</li><li>Carbonate preservation also depends on water chemistry and dissolution depth.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Circulate seawater and build vents</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cold seawater descends through fractures</mark>.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Heated water rises</mark> above a magma chamber, reacting with rock and carrying dissolved chemicals.</li><li>When vent fluid mixes with cold seawater, sulfide particles precipitate. The dark plume and mineral chimney form through <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">precipitation</mark>, not combustion.</li><li>Lecture 15 slide 9 connects <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\">black smokers with ore deposits and unique ecology</mark>. Chemical energy can support bacteria and the associated food web without sunlight.</li></ul></section></div><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Source end members and preservation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied fast-ridge example spreads at about 10 cm per year.</li><li>The slow example spreads at about 1–2 cm per year and has a 1–3 km axial rift.</li><li>These are textbook end members, not universal thresholds.</li><li>Below the local <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">carbonate compensation depth (CCD)</mark>, carbonate dissolution can match or exceed accumulation. The source gives about 4,500–5,000 m and explicitly says the actual depth varies geographically. Clay and silica-rich sediment can dominate below it.</li><li>Vent water commonly exceeds 350°C in the source description.</li><li>Pressure in deep water prevents boiling under those conditions.</li><li>Hot water is still different from the magma that heats it.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-2-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate crust construction from hydrothermal circulation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Magma freezes as pillow basalt, dikes and deeper igneous rock; seawater circulates through fractures above hot rock. Hot vent water is not the same material as the magma heating it.</li><li>Dissolved metals and sulfur precipitate when hot vent fluid cools and mixes with seawater. This produces the dark mineral plume and chimney rather than combustion.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Chemical energy</mark> supports bacteria and the associated vent food web. Tube worms, clams, crabs and other organisms illustrate an ecosystem that does not require sunlight at the deep seafloor.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the source end members conditionally</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The fast and slow ridge profiles compare magmatic supply, cooling and faulting. A deep axial rift is characteristic of the slow end member, not every spreading center.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">carbonate compensation depth</mark> is a local preservation boundary, not the depth of the crust–mantle contact. Clay and silica-rich sediment can accumulate where carbonate dissolves.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate crust construction from hydrothermal circulation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Magma freezes as pillow basalt, dikes and deeper igneous rock.</li><li>Seawater circulates through fractures above hot rock.</li><li>Hot vent water is not the same material as the magma heating it.</li><li>Dissolved metals and sulfur precipitate when hot vent fluid cools and mixes with seawater. This produces the dark mineral plume and chimney rather than combustion.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Chemical energy</mark> supports bacteria and the associated vent food web. Tube worms, clams, crabs and other organisms illustrate an ecosystem that does not require sunlight at the deep seafloor.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the source end members conditionally</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The fast and slow ridge profiles compare magmatic supply, cooling and faulting. A deep axial rift is characteristic of the slow end member, not every spreading center.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">carbonate compensation depth</mark> is a local preservation boundary, not the depth of the crust–mantle contact. Clay and silica-rich sediment can accumulate where carbonate dissolves.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-3-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Relief and sediment patterns</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Continental shelves are shallow; trenches are deepest</mark>. Ridges stand above surrounding abyssal seafloor but usually remain submerged.</li><li>Old oceanic lithosphere cools and subsides. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Abyssal plains</mark> can become smooth where sediment buries rough volcanic relief.</li><li>Sediment tends to be thicker on older crust and near major continental sediment sources. The Gulf offshore Texas and Louisiana combines large sediment supply with long accumulation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the age pattern</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">The youngest seafloor lies along spreading centers</mark>; age generally increases away from them. Use the color legend before interpreting a numbered map.</li><li>The Atlantic's paired age bands record spreading on both sides of the ridge. Pacific subduction has removed substantial older oceanic crust, so its surviving pattern differs.</li><li>The western Pacific is generally older and deeper than the region near the eastern spreading system. These broad relationships do not make every local depth depend only on age.</li></ul></section></div><div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Geographic map patterns</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied sediment maps emphasize thick accumulation offshore Texas and Louisiana. Ridges and isolated young crust generally have less sediment.</li><li>Both Atlantic basin sides can be deeper than the ridge crest; compare ridge elevation with older, cooled basin crust.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Relief and sediment patterns</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Continental shelves are shallow</mark>.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Trenches are deepest</mark>.</li><li>Ridges stand above surrounding abyssal seafloor but usually remain submerged.</li><li>Old oceanic lithosphere cools and subsides. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Abyssal plains</mark> can become smooth where sediment buries rough volcanic relief.</li><li>Sediment tends to be thicker on older crust and near major continental sediment sources. The Gulf offshore Texas and Louisiana combines large sediment supply with long accumulation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the age pattern</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">The youngest seafloor lies along spreading centers</mark>.</li><li>Age generally increases away from them.</li><li>Use the color legend before interpreting a numbered map.</li><li>The Atlantic's paired age bands record spreading on both sides of the ridge. Pacific subduction has removed substantial older oceanic crust, so its surviving pattern differs.</li><li>The western Pacific is generally older and deeper than the region near the eastern spreading system. These broad relationships do not make every local depth depend only on age.</li></ul></section></div><div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Geographic map patterns</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied sediment maps emphasize thick accumulation offshore Texas and Louisiana. Ridges and isolated young crust generally have less sediment.</li><li>Both Atlantic basin sides can be deeper than the ridge crest.</li><li>Compare ridge elevation with older, cooled basin crust.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g10-3-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the three map quantities separate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Depth colors show relief; sediment-thickness colors show accumulated cover; age colors show when the oceanic crust formed. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Read each legend anew</mark>.</li><li>An older plate has had time to cool and collect sediment, but a nearby major river can strongly increase sediment supply. Thin cover far from land does not uniquely identify young crust.</li><li>A blank or no-data patch represents unavailable information, not automatically shallow water, zero sediment or young crust.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Explain what survives on each plate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Spreading creates age bands on both sides of a ridge. Subduction can consume one side of an oceanic record, producing asymmetry.</li><li>The age limit printed on a supplied map describes that map and its source. Avoid turning an old legend into an unconditional claim that no older oceanic material exists anywhere.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the three map quantities separate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Depth colors show relief.</li><li>Sediment-thickness colors show accumulated cover.</li><li>Age colors show when the oceanic crust formed.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Read each legend anew</mark>.</li><li>An older plate has had time to cool and collect sediment, but a nearby major river can strongly increase sediment supply. Thin cover far from land does not uniquely identify young crust.</li><li>A blank or no-data patch represents unavailable information, not automatically shallow water, zero sediment or young crust.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Explain what survives on each plate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Spreading creates age bands on both sides of a ridge. Subduction can consume one side of an oceanic record, producing asymmetry.</li><li>The age limit printed on a supplied map describes that map and its source. Avoid turning an old legend into an unconditional claim that no older oceanic material exists anywhere.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-4-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read island and seamount history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A volcanic edifice may grow above sea level, then become inactive as the plate carries it away from the hotspot. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cooling, subsidence, erosion, and wave planation</mark> can leave a submerged or flat-topped seamount.</li><li>In a simple fixed-hotspot model, the youngest active volcano marks one end of an age-progressive chain. Older islands record the plate's earlier locations.</li><li>Hawaii's young southeastern end and older northwestern islands indicate <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">northwest plate motion relative to the hotspot</mark>. A chain's orientation alone is insufficient without ages or an active end.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build a broad basalt plateau</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lecture 15 slide 12 shows <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\">rising mantle plume → submarine flood basalts → oceanic plateau</mark>. Its silhouette is broad and elevated above the adjacent deep seafloor.</li><li>The plume rises through the mantle as <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">hot solid rock</mark>. Melting near the lithosphere supplies liquid magma; the plume is not a pipe of liquid magma extending through the whole mantle.</li><li>Repeated widespread basalt eruptions build a plateau over millions of years. Kerguelen and Ontong Java are source examples. Hotspot settings occur in oceans and on continents.</li></ul></section></div><div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the supplied hotspot maps</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The exercise includes hotspots west and south of Africa and on the African continent. Hotspots are not limited to ocean basins.</li><li>Among the American regions in the supplied exercise, western North America has the largest listed concentration. This is a result for that source comparison, not a numerical world inventory.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read island and seamount history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A volcanic edifice may grow above sea level, then become inactive as the plate carries it away from the hotspot. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Cooling, subsidence, erosion, and wave planation</mark> can leave a submerged or flat-topped seamount.</li><li>In a simple fixed-hotspot model, the youngest active volcano marks one end of an age-progressive chain. Older islands record the plate's earlier locations.</li><li>Hawaii's young southeastern end and older northwestern islands indicate <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">northwest plate motion relative to the hotspot</mark>. A chain's orientation alone is insufficient without ages or an active end.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build a broad basalt plateau</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lecture 15 slide 12 shows <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\">rising mantle plume → submarine flood basalts → oceanic plateau</mark>. Its silhouette is broad and elevated above the adjacent deep seafloor.</li><li>The plume rises through the mantle as <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">hot solid rock</mark>.<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Melting near the lithosphere supplies liquid magma.</li><li>The plume is not a pipe of liquid magma extending through the whole mantle.</li></ul></li><li>Repeated widespread basalt eruptions build a plateau over millions of years.<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Kerguelen and Ontong Java are source examples.</li><li>Hotspot settings occur in oceans and on continents.</li></ul></li></ul></section></div><div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the supplied hotspot maps</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The exercise includes hotspots west and south of Africa and on the African continent. Hotspots are not limited to ocean basins.</li><li>Among the American regions in the supplied exercise, western North America has the largest listed concentration. This is a result for that source comparison, not a numerical world inventory.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g10-4-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From a volcanic peak to a submerged flat top</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Eruptions construct a volcano; wave erosion can bevel its summit near sea level; cooling and subsidence lower the foundation after magmatism wanes. A flat-topped submerged seamount records a sequence of processes.</li><li>A hot-spot age progression is interpreted relative to the magma source. Locate the active end and compare dated volcanoes before inferring the plate-motion direction.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the scale of basalt construction</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An oceanic plateau is a broad elevated basaltic region, often mostly submerged. Kerguelen and Ontong Java are examples in the source.</li><li>The rising plume in the model is <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">hot solid mantle</mark>. Melting and magma supply occur nearer the lithosphere; repeated flood-basalt eruptions leave a persistent thick accumulation.</li><li>Lecture 15 slide 12 emphasizes <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\">plume-related flood basalts and plateau construction</mark> over millions of years. The schematic does not imply that this growth happens on a human timescale.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From a volcanic peak to a submerged flat top</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Eruptions construct a volcano.</li><li>Wave erosion can bevel its summit near sea level.</li><li>Cooling and subsidence lower the foundation after magmatism wanes.</li><li>A flat-topped submerged seamount records a sequence of processes.</li><li>A hot-spot age progression is interpreted relative to the magma source. Locate the active end and compare dated volcanoes before inferring the plate-motion direction.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the scale of basalt construction</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An oceanic plateau is a broad elevated basaltic region, often mostly submerged. Kerguelen and Ontong Java are examples in the source.</li><li>The rising plume in the model is <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">hot solid mantle</mark>.<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Melting and magma supply occur nearer the lithosphere.</li><li>Repeated flood-basalt eruptions leave a persistent thick accumulation.</li></ul></li><li>Lecture 15 slide 12 emphasizes <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\">plume-related flood basalts and plateau construction</mark> over millions of years. The schematic does not imply that this growth happens on a human timescale.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-5-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Generate arc magma</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">ocean–ocean convergent boundary</mark> can form a trench and volcanic island arc. The descending slab lies beneath the overriding plate.</li><li>Water released during metamorphic reactions in the slab enters the mantle wedge and helps generate melt. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Water-assisted mantle melting</mark> is not wholesale melting of the descending plate.</li><li>Arc volcanic rocks commonly include andesite and composite volcanoes. Lesser Antilles, Aleutian, and Mariana systems are course examples.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Explain the adjacent basin</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>As the subduction system retreats, <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">slab rollback</mark> can move the trench and arc. Extension behind the arc can open a backarc basin.</li><li>Arc volcanism and backarc extension can overlap in time. Do not force the arc to cease before every backarc basin begins.</li><li>The curved geometry of an arc is related to a descending plate intersecting a curved Earth. A map-view curve by itself is not a plate-motion arrow.</li></ul></section></div><div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Locate the arc systems</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Many course arc examples flank Asia in the western Pacific and adjoining Indian Ocean. Read the boundary geometry as well as the geographic position.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Generate arc magma</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">ocean–ocean convergent boundary</mark> can form a trench and volcanic island arc. The descending slab lies beneath the overriding plate.</li><li>Water released during metamorphic reactions in the slab enters the mantle wedge and helps generate melt. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Water-assisted mantle melting</mark> is not wholesale melting of the descending plate.</li><li>Arc volcanic rocks commonly include andesite and composite volcanoes. Lesser Antilles, Aleutian, and Mariana systems are course examples.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Explain the adjacent basin</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>As the subduction system retreats, <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">slab rollback</mark> can move the trench and arc. Extension behind the arc can open a backarc basin.</li><li>Arc volcanism and backarc extension can overlap in time. Do not force the arc to cease before every backarc basin begins.</li><li>The curved geometry of an arc is related to a descending plate intersecting a curved Earth. A map-view curve by itself is not a plate-motion arrow.</li></ul></section></div><div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Locate the arc systems</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Many course arc examples flank Asia in the western Pacific and adjoining Indian Ocean. Read the boundary geometry as well as the geographic position.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g10-5-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect slab, mantle wedge and arc crust</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Heating and metamorphic reactions in the descending plate release water; water promotes mantle melting above the slab. The volcano is not simply the surface end of a liquid descending plate.</li><li>Repeated magma additions thicken the arc crust. Interactions with that growing crust can yield intermediate magmas and composite volcanoes.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Combine map view with cross section</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The trench and arc can curve because subduction occurs on a sphere and the slab fits a smaller width at depth. A straight cross section cannot show the whole map-view shape.</li><li>Rollback migrates the trench and arc; extension within or behind the arc can make a back-arc basin while convergence continues at the trench.</li><li>Use examples such as the Aleutians, Marianas, Tonga and Sumatra–Java to locate the trench, arc and incoming plate. A hot-spot chain does not require that geometry.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect slab, mantle wedge and arc crust</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Heating and metamorphic reactions in the descending plate release water.</li><li>Water promotes mantle melting above the slab.</li><li>The volcano is not simply the surface end of a liquid descending plate.</li><li>Repeated magma additions thicken the arc crust. Interactions with that growing crust can yield intermediate magmas and composite volcanoes.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Combine map view with cross section</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The trench and arc can curve because subduction occurs on a sphere and the slab fits a smaller width at depth. A straight cross section cannot show the whole map-view shape.</li><li>Rollback migrates the trench and arc.</li><li>Extension within or behind the arc can make a back-arc basin while convergence continues at the trench.</li><li>Use examples such as the Aleutians, Marianas, Tonga and Sumatra–Java to locate the trench, arc and incoming plate. A hot-spot chain does not require that geometry.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-6-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the tectonic histories</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sea of Japan</mark> opened as the arc separated from Asia through backarc extension in the source reconstruction. The assessment's present-tense wording is not evidence that spreading continues today.</li><li>Parts of the Philippine Sea contain basins and separated arc remnants. A modern backarc basin lies behind the Mariana arc.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">western Bering Sea</mark> trapped oceanic crust when subduction shifted offshore. Its shallow eastern shelf belongs to the continental platform.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate flooded continents from new ocean crust</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shallow seas near China and between Australia and New Guinea occupy continental platforms. Being underwater does not make their crust oceanic.</li><li>The Gulf of California combines short spreading segments and transform faults after the plate boundary shifted inland. Baja moved north relative to mainland North America.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Backarc opening, trapped ocean, flooded continental platform, and transform-linked rifting</mark> explain different seas; the word sea does not specify one origin.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the tectonic histories</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Sea of Japan</mark> opened as the arc separated from Asia through backarc extension in the source reconstruction. The assessment's present-tense wording is not evidence that spreading continues today.</li><li>Parts of the Philippine Sea contain basins and separated arc remnants. A modern backarc basin lies behind the Mariana arc.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">western Bering Sea</mark> trapped oceanic crust when subduction shifted offshore. Its shallow eastern shelf belongs to the continental platform.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate flooded continents from new ocean crust</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shallow seas near China and between Australia and New Guinea occupy continental platforms. Being underwater does not make their crust oceanic.</li><li>The Gulf of California combines short spreading segments and transform faults after the plate boundary shifted inland. Baja moved north relative to mainland North America.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Backarc opening, trapped ocean, flooded continental platform, and transform-linked rifting</mark> explain different seas.</li><li>The word sea does not specify one origin.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g10-6-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Several histories can put water behind an arc</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Sea of Japan records <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">historical rifting and back-arc spreading</mark>. Historical opening does not establish present-day spreading activity.</li><li>The Philippine Sea’s basins and remnant arc ridges record repeated episodes of back-arc opening. The source identifies active opening close to the Mariana arc.</li><li>The western Bering basin contains oceanic crust trapped behind an offshore-shifted subduction system; its eastern shallow region overlies continental crust.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Do not classify all small seas as oceanic spreading basins</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The China Sea, Java Sea and shallow Australia–Papua New Guinea region include continental platforms. Flooded continental crust can remain shallow without a new plate boundary at every coastline.</li><li>The Gulf of California combines <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">transform faults</mark> with short spreading segments as Baja moves north relative to North America. It is a different boundary history from Japan or the Bering Sea.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Several histories can put water behind an arc</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Sea of Japan records <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">historical rifting and back-arc spreading</mark>. Historical opening does not establish present-day spreading activity.</li><li>The Philippine Sea’s basins and remnant arc ridges record repeated episodes of back-arc opening. The source identifies active opening close to the Mariana arc.</li><li>The western Bering basin contains oceanic crust trapped behind an offshore-shifted subduction system.</li><li>Its eastern shallow region overlies continental crust.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Do not classify all small seas as oceanic spreading basins</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The China Sea, Java Sea and shallow Australia–Papua New Guinea region include continental platforms. Flooded continental crust can remain shallow without a new plate boundary at every coastline.</li><li>The Gulf of California combines <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">transform faults</mark> with short spreading segments as Baja moves north relative to North America. It is a different boundary history from Japan or the Bering Sea.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-7-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Match a basin with its mechanism</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">North Sea</mark> includes extensional basins where normal faults thinned continental crust; buried sedimentary systems include petroleum resources.</li><li>The Baltic occupies flooded, glacially and river-modified continental topography. Flooding is different from producing new oceanic crust.</li><li>The Mediterranean combines distinct histories: western rifting and eastern remnants of an older ocean modified by convergence.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use specific examples</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Black Sea includes a trapped remnant basin associated with the former Tethyan region and later flooding history.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Persian Gulf</mark> lies beside the Zagros thrust belt; loading and flexure create a foreland basin.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Red Sea</mark> records continental rifting and divergence. North Sea extension, Persian Gulf loading, and Red Sea separation should not be interchanged.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Match a basin with its mechanism</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">North Sea</mark> includes extensional basins where normal faults thinned continental crust.</li><li>Buried sedimentary systems include petroleum resources.</li><li>The Baltic occupies flooded, glacially and river-modified continental topography. Flooding is different from producing new oceanic crust.</li><li>The Mediterranean combines distinct histories<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Western rifting and eastern remnants of an older ocean modified by convergence.</li></ul></li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use specific examples</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Black Sea includes a trapped remnant basin associated with the former Tethyan region and later flooding history.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Persian Gulf</mark> lies beside the Zagros thrust belt.</li><li>Loading and flexure create a foreland basin.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Red Sea</mark> records continental rifting and divergence. North Sea extension, Persian Gulf loading, and Red Sea separation should not be interchanged.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g10-7-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the sea, then explain its basin</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Baltic: a continental valley reshaped by glaciers and flooded by rising sea level. North Sea: continental crust thinned by extension and normal faulting.</li><li>Black Sea: a remnant basin associated with Tethys and later enclosure. The proposed catastrophic postglacial flooding is an interpretation discussed by the source, not an uncontested conclusion.</li><li>The Mediterranean includes western Cenozoic rifting and eastern remnant oceanic crust plus convergence; its regions do not share one simple origin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare extension with loading</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Persian Gulf: Zagros thrust loading flexes Arabia downward into a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">foreland trough</mark>. Red Sea: Arabia separates from Africa, with continental rifting and southern seafloor spreading.</li><li>Do not infer a basin mechanism from water depth or a geographic name alone. Use crust, faults, adjoining mountains and the reconstruction.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the sea, then explain its basin</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Baltic<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A continental valley reshaped by glaciers and flooded by rising sea level.</li></ul></li><li>North Sea<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Continental crust thinned by extension and normal faulting.</li></ul></li><li>Black Sea<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A remnant basin associated with Tethys and later enclosure.</li></ul></li><li>The proposed catastrophic postglacial flooding is an interpretation discussed by the source, not an uncontested conclusion.</li><li>The Mediterranean includes western Cenozoic rifting and eastern remnant oceanic crust plus convergence.</li><li>Its regions do not share one simple origin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare extension with loading</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Persian Gulf<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Zagros thrust loading flexes Arabia downward into a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">foreland trough</mark>.</li></ul></li><li>Red Sea<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Arabia separates from Africa, with continental rifting and southern seafloor spreading.</li></ul></li><li>Do not infer a basin mechanism from water depth or a geographic name alone. Use crust, faults, adjoining mountains and the reconstruction.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-8-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the environmental conditions together</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Reef-building corals favor <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">warm, clear, shallow water</mark>. Light supports photosynthetic partners; suspended sediment can block light or bury the reef.</li><li>The chapter and lecture emphasize tropical distributions, broadly near the equator. Clear shallow water near Iceland fails the warmth condition; tropical conditions alone fail if sediment obscures the light.</li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">fringing reef</mark> borders the shore. A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">barrier reef</mark> is separated from land by a lagoon. A carbonate platform such as the Bahamas is broader than an individual volcanic-island reef.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow the Darwin subsidence model</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A volcanic island initially supports a fringing reef. As the island cools, subsides, and erodes, coral can continue growing upward if suitable conditions persist.</li><li>The reef becomes separated from the shrinking island by a lagoon, then can remain as an <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">atoll surrounding a lagoon</mark> after the island is submerged.</li><li>Reef growth must keep pace with relative submergence. The source also discusses alternative carbonate-platform histories; the sequence is a model, not the history of every carbonate rock.</li></ul></section></div><div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Locate reefs and test all conditions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Caribbean, Bahamas, Philippines, and Polynesia are warm-water reef examples in the sources. Aleutian and Iceland examples fail the warmth condition.</li><li>A clear shallow site still needs suitable temperature; a warm site still needs enough light and sufficiently low sediment interference.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the environmental conditions together</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Reef-building corals favor <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">warm, clear, shallow water</mark>.</li><li>Light supports photosynthetic partners.</li><li>Suspended sediment can block light or bury the reef.</li><li>The chapter and lecture emphasize tropical distributions, broadly near the equator.</li><li>Clear shallow water near Iceland fails the warmth condition.</li><li>Tropical conditions alone fail if sediment obscures the light.</li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">fringing reef</mark> borders the shore. A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">barrier reef</mark> is separated from land by a lagoon. A carbonate platform such as the Bahamas is broader than an individual volcanic-island reef.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow the Darwin subsidence model</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A volcanic island initially supports a fringing reef. As the island cools, subsides, and erodes, coral can continue growing upward if suitable conditions persist.</li><li>The reef becomes separated from the shrinking island by a lagoon, then can remain as an <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">atoll surrounding a lagoon</mark> after the island is submerged.</li><li>Reef growth must keep pace with relative submergence.</li><li>The source also discusses alternative carbonate-platform histories.</li><li>The sequence is a model, not the history of every carbonate rock.</li></ul></section></div><div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Locate reefs and test all conditions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Caribbean, Bahamas, Philippines, and Polynesia are warm-water reef examples in the sources. Aleutian and Iceland examples fail the warmth condition.</li><li>A clear shallow site still needs suitable temperature.</li><li>A warm site still needs enough light and sufficiently low sediment interference.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g10-8-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the carbonate landform</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fringing reefs adjoin a shoreline; barrier reefs enclose a landward lagoon; atolls form rings around lagoons. Broad carbonate platforms include more extensive shallow accumulations.</li><li>Wave erosion and reworking supply carbonate sand from reef and shell material. Clear, shallow, warm conditions support the reef communities emphasized by this section.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Track two vertical motions in Darwin’s model</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The volcanic foundation sinks while the reef builds upward. A barrier reef and lagoon develop as the island shrinks; continued growth can preserve an atoll after the volcano becomes submerged.</li><li>Keeping pace with subsidence or sea-level rise is a condition, not an automatic result. The source also mentions another model involving carbonate-platform erosion.</li><li>The Great Barrier Reef, Caribbean, Bahamas, Pacific islands, East Africa, Maldives and Red Sea connect geographic examples to environmental conditions.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify the carbonate landform</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Fringing reefs adjoin a shoreline.</li><li>Barrier reefs enclose a landward lagoon.</li><li>Atolls form rings around lagoons.</li><li>Broad carbonate platforms include more extensive shallow accumulations.</li><li>Wave erosion and reworking supply carbonate sand from reef and shell material. Clear, shallow, warm conditions support the reef communities emphasized by this section.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Track two vertical motions in Darwin’s model</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The volcanic foundation sinks while the reef builds upward.</li><li>A barrier reef and lagoon develop as the island shrinks.</li><li>Continued growth can preserve an atoll after the volcano becomes submerged.</li><li>Keeping pace with subsidence or sea-level rise is a condition, not an automatic result. The source also mentions another model involving carbonate-platform erosion.</li><li>The Great Barrier Reef, Caribbean, Bahamas, Pacific islands, East Africa, Maldives and Red Sea connect geographic examples to environmental conditions.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-9-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the margin profile</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A typical profile progresses from the gently sloping <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">shelf</mark> to steeper <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">slope</mark>, then a sediment-rich <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">rise</mark> and abyssal seafloor where those features are present.</li><li>Active margins coincide with active plate boundaries; passive margins do not. Passive shelves are commonly wider, but shelf width alone is not the definition.</li><li>Rifting thins continental crust by normal faulting. A passive margin can preserve fault blocks buried beneath a thick sediment wedge, transitioning seaward to oceanic crust.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transport and deposit sediment</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lecture 15 slides 2–6 and October 20 develop <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\">turbidity currents, submarine canyons, and submarine fans</mark>. A dense mixture of water and sediment flows downslope under gravity.</li><li>Repeated currents can incise canyons and carry sediment into deep water. Upper parts of some canyons also reflect river erosion during lower sea level.</li><li>As a flow spreads and slows near the slope's foot, it deposits a submarine fan. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Coarse grains settling before fine grains</mark> leave a bed that fines upward; separate events can stack multiple turbidites.</li><li>The lecture's roughly 60 mph cable-break example illustrates an individual powerful event; it is not the speed of every turbidity current. Coherent submarine landslide blocks and mixed sediment-water currents are distinct end-member descriptions.</li></ul></section></div><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the rocks as well as the relief</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The passive-margin section changes from thinned continental crust of granitic composition to thinner oceanic crust composed of basalt and gabbro. Sedimentary cover lies above the crust and varies in thickness.</li></ul></section>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the margin profile</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A typical profile progresses from the gently sloping <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">shelf</mark> to steeper <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">slope</mark>, then a sediment-rich <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">rise</mark> and abyssal seafloor where those features are present.</li><li>Active margins coincide with active plate boundaries.</li><li>Passive margins do not.</li><li>Passive shelves are commonly wider, but shelf width alone is not the definition.</li><li>Rifting thins continental crust by normal faulting. A passive margin can preserve fault blocks buried beneath a thick sediment wedge, transitioning seaward to oceanic crust.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Transport and deposit sediment</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Lecture 15 slides 2–6 and October 20 develop <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\">turbidity currents, submarine canyons, and submarine fans</mark>. A dense mixture of water and sediment flows downslope under gravity.</li><li>Repeated currents can incise canyons and carry sediment into deep water. Upper parts of some canyons also reflect river erosion during lower sea level.</li><li>As a flow spreads and slows near the slope's foot, it deposits a submarine fan.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Coarse grains settling before fine grains</mark> leave a bed that fines upward.</li><li>Separate events can stack multiple turbidites.</li><li>The lecture's roughly 60 mph cable-break example illustrates an individual powerful event.</li><li>It is not the speed of every turbidity current.</li><li>Coherent submarine landslide blocks and mixed sediment-water currents are distinct end-member descriptions.</li></ul></section></div><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the rocks as well as the relief</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The passive-margin section changes from thinned continental crust of granitic composition to thinner oceanic crust composed of basalt and gabbro. Sedimentary cover lies above the crust and varies in thickness.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-9-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize connected features without conflating them</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A shelf is a gently sloping submerged continental edge; a slope is the steeper descent; a rise is a sediment apron. A canyon cuts an erosional pathway through part of this terrain.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Turbidity currents</mark> mix sediment and water into a dense moving flow. Submarine landslides can retain more coherent blocks and leave irregular scars and deposits.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Explain the deposit left by the flow</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>As a current slows, larger grains settle before smaller grains, making a bed that fines upward. Repeated events stack separate graded beds.</li><li>A submarine fan is the spreading deposit near the slope foot and rise, rather than the canyon itself or the within-bed grain-size trend. Lecture 15 slides 4 and 6 emphasize these source-to-pathway-to-deposit connections.</li><li>An inherited narrow shelf does not, by itself, prove an active margin. Check the boundary, earthquake and volcanism evidence as in the section 10.13 investigation.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize connected features without conflating them</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A shelf is a gently sloping submerged continental edge.</li><li>A slope is the steeper descent.</li><li>A rise is a sediment apron.</li><li>A canyon cuts an erosional pathway through part of this terrain.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Turbidity currents</mark> mix sediment and water into a dense moving flow. Submarine landslides can retain more coherent blocks and leave irregular scars and deposits.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Explain the deposit left by the flow</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>As a current slows, larger grains settle before smaller grains, making a bed that fines upward. Repeated events stack separate graded beds.</li><li>A submarine fan is the spreading deposit near the slope foot and rise, rather than the canyon itself or the within-bed grain-size trend. Lecture 15 slides 4 and 6 emphasize these source-to-pathway-to-deposit connections.</li><li>An inherited narrow shelf does not, by itself, prove an active margin. Check the boundary, earthquake and volcanism evidence as in the section 10.13 investigation.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-10-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Accumulate evaporites</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Evaporation of restricted seawater</mark> precipitates salts such as halite, gypsum, and sylvite. A barrier can restrict exchange while repeated seawater supply sustains thick accumulation.</li><li>Warm, dry conditions favor evaporation, but a thick deposit requires enough dissolved material and an appropriate basin history.</li><li>Halite is NaCl. Mineral chemistry identifies the material; it does not by itself explain the basin geometry or prove a recalled exam formula.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deform after burial</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Buried salt is <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">weak and comparatively low density</mark>. Under suitable loading it flows in the solid state and can rise into diapirs or domes.</li><li>Salt can also provide a weak detachment, allowing overlying sediment to fold or slide. Salt glaciers represent another flow geometry.</li><li>Gulf Coast salt formed early in the basin's restricted marine history, was buried, and later moved upward and sideways. Salt structures can deform sediment and help create petroleum traps.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Accumulate evaporites</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Evaporation of restricted seawater</mark> precipitates salts such as halite, gypsum, and sylvite. A barrier can restrict exchange while repeated seawater supply sustains thick accumulation.</li><li>Warm, dry conditions favor evaporation, but a thick deposit requires enough dissolved material and an appropriate basin history.</li><li>Halite is NaCl.</li><li>Mineral chemistry identifies the material.</li><li>It does not by itself explain the basin geometry or prove a recalled exam formula.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deform after burial</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Buried salt is <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">weak and comparatively low density</mark>. Under suitable loading it flows in the solid state and can rise into diapirs or domes.</li><li>Salt can also provide a weak detachment, allowing overlying sediment to fold or slide. Salt glaciers represent another flow geometry.</li><li>Gulf Coast salt formed early in the basin's restricted marine history, was buried, and later moved upward and sideways. Salt structures can deform sediment and help create petroleum traps.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g10-10-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Accumulate soluble minerals in a restricted basin</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Evaporation concentrates brine; restricted exchange prevents the concentrated solution from immediately mixing into the open ocean. Halite, gypsum and other soluble salts can precipitate.</li><li>Repeated inflow can replenish water and dissolved components, allowing extended deposition. Tidal flats, coastal basins and narrow young seas provide source examples.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deform the deposit after burial</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Weak, relatively low-density salt can <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">flow as a solid</mark>, bend or pierce overlying strata and form domes. Weak salt layers can also aid folding and faulting; exposed salt can spread downslope as a salt glacier.</li><li>In the Gulf Coast model, buried Mesozoic salt moved upward and sideways after sediment loading. Domes, folds, faults and some seafloor bumps reflect that movement.</li><li>The Sigsbee Escarpment is tied to the front of a large subsurface salt mass in the source model. Petroleum can be trapped by associated structures; salt rising is not petroleum generation.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Accumulate soluble minerals in a restricted basin</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Evaporation concentrates brine.</li><li>Restricted exchange prevents the concentrated solution from immediately mixing into the open ocean.</li><li>Halite, gypsum and other soluble salts can precipitate.</li><li>Repeated inflow can replenish water and dissolved components, allowing extended deposition. Tidal flats, coastal basins and narrow young seas provide source examples.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deform the deposit after burial</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Weak, relatively low-density salt can <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">flow as a solid</mark>, bend or pierce overlying strata and form domes.</li><li>Weak salt layers can also aid folding and faulting.</li><li>Exposed salt can spread downslope as a salt glacier.</li><li>In the Gulf Coast model, buried Mesozoic salt moved upward and sideways after sediment loading. Domes, folds, faults and some seafloor bumps reflect that movement.</li><li>The Sigsbee Escarpment is tied to the front of a large subsurface salt mass in the source model.</li><li>Petroleum can be trapped by associated structures.</li><li>Salt rising is not petroleum generation.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-11-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow the map sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Before breakup, Pangaea joined the major continents. Rift systems and new oceanic crust then separated parts of the supercontinent.</li><li>In the supplied reconstruction, <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">the Central Atlantic opened before the South Atlantic, and the North Atlantic opened later</mark>. Ages and positions should be read from the actual maps.</li><li>Around 150 Ma the central Atlantic and Gulf were developing; later Cretaceous configurations show wider southern Atlantic separation and continued northern reorganization.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate reconstruction from forecast</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Indian Ocean developed as fragments separated; later India–Asia convergence and Tethyan closure changed the arrangement.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Spreading creates oceanic lithosphere; subduction consumes it</mark>. Their distribution explains Atlantic expansion and broad Pacific contraction in the course reconstruction.</li><li>Forward projections assume plate motions and boundaries persist. A future map is a conditional scenario, not an assured event; the October 15 garbled transcript does not override the checked map sequence.</li></ul></section></div><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Timeline in the supplied reconstruction</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Before about 200 Ma: Pangaea joins the major continental masses; subsequent rifting begins breakup.</li><li>By about 150 Ma: the Central Atlantic and Gulf are developing. Around 120 Ma: continued central and southern Atlantic opening and Gondwana breakup reorganize the Indian Ocean region.</li><li>At about 90 Ma: the South Atlantic separates South America and Africa; the northern Atlantic sector in the source has not yet opened fully.</li><li>The source places northern Atlantic opening near 80 Ma. By about 30 Ma, India–Asia collision and convergence near southern Europe nearly close the Tethys.</li><li>These dated maps are reconstruction snapshots. They support the central → south → north opening order without establishing the exact future arrangement.</li></ul></section>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow the map sequence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Before breakup, Pangaea joined the major continents. Rift systems and new oceanic crust then separated parts of the supercontinent.</li><li>In the supplied reconstruction, <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">the Central Atlantic opened before the South Atlantic, and the North Atlantic opened later</mark>. Ages and positions should be read from the actual maps.</li><li>Around 150 Ma the central Atlantic and Gulf were developing.</li><li>Later Cretaceous configurations show wider southern Atlantic separation and continued northern reorganization.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate reconstruction from forecast</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Indian Ocean developed as fragments separated.</li><li>Later India–Asia convergence and Tethyan closure changed the arrangement.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Spreading creates oceanic lithosphere</mark>.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Subduction consumes it</mark>.</li><li>Their distribution explains Atlantic expansion and broad Pacific contraction in the course reconstruction.</li><li>Forward projections assume plate motions and boundaries persist.</li><li>A future map is a conditional scenario, not an assured event.</li><li>The October 15 garbled transcript does not override the checked map sequence.</li></ul></section></div><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Timeline in the supplied reconstruction</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Before about 200 Ma<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Pangaea joins the major continental masses.</li><li>Subsequent rifting begins breakup.</li></ul></li><li>By about 150 Ma<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The Central Atlantic and Gulf are developing.</li></ul></li><li>Around 120 Ma<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Continued central and southern Atlantic opening and Gondwana breakup reorganize the Indian Ocean region.</li></ul></li><li>At about 90 Ma<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The South Atlantic separates South America and Africa.</li><li>The northern Atlantic sector in the source has not yet opened fully.</li></ul></li><li>The source places northern Atlantic opening near 80 Ma. By about 30 Ma, India–Asia collision and convergence near southern Europe nearly close the Tethys.</li><li>These dated maps are reconstruction snapshots. They support the central → south → north opening order without establishing the exact future arrangement.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-11-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep opening stages and time labels separate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The source opens the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Central Atlantic</mark> before the South Atlantic and later northern Atlantic sector. Continental rifting can precede full seafloor spreading.</li><li>The reconstruction also shows India separating from Gondwana and later approaching Asia, with much of Tethys consumed during convergence. Each panel is a world reconstruction, not half a globe.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Account for creation and destruction</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Spreading creates lithosphere; subduction consumes it. An expanding ocean does not imply that Earth as a whole expands.</li><li>The source’s Atlantic–Pacific contrast is a basin-scale trend. Do not turn it into a claim that no subduction exists anywhere in or near the Atlantic.</li><li>A future map depends on continuing velocities and assumptions about collisions and boundary changes. Approximate historical reconstruction landmarks and conditional forecasts have different evidential status.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep opening stages and time labels separate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The source opens the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Central Atlantic</mark> before the South Atlantic and later northern Atlantic sector. Continental rifting can precede full seafloor spreading.</li><li>The reconstruction also shows India separating from Gondwana and later approaching Asia, with much of Tethys consumed during convergence. Each panel is a world reconstruction, not half a globe.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Account for creation and destruction</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Spreading creates lithosphere.</li><li>Subduction consumes it.</li><li>An expanding ocean does not imply that Earth as a whole expands.</li><li>The source’s Atlantic–Pacific contrast is a basin-scale trend. Do not turn it into a claim that no subduction exists anywhere in or near the Atlantic.</li><li>A future map depends on continuing velocities and assumptions about collisions and boundary changes. Approximate historical reconstruction landmarks and conditional forecasts have different evidential status.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-12-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe the present boundaries</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Gulf includes broad shelves, deep central areas, and thick sediment. Large river input helps build the sediment cover along the northern margin.</li><li>The Lesser Antilles arc lies along the Caribbean's eastern side, where Atlantic lithosphere descends <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">westward beneath the Caribbean</mark> in the supplied map interpretation.</li><li>Pacific subduction occurs along Central America; transforms occur elsewhere, including the Cayman region. Reefs and thick basaltic crust add further distinct features.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Jurassic rifting and separation involving Yucatán opened the Gulf. Spreading later ceased there while sediment accumulation continued.</li><li>The course discusses a thick basaltic Caribbean plateau and a model involving entry from the Pacific, while noting that its origin is debated.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Rifting, sedimentation, plateau volcanism, subduction, and transform motion</mark> belong to different stages or locations. The text's later east-dipping phrase conflicts with its earlier map account; practice follows the mapped westward descent.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe the present boundaries</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Gulf includes broad shelves, deep central areas, and thick sediment. Large river input helps build the sediment cover along the northern margin.</li><li>The Lesser Antilles arc lies along the Caribbean's eastern side, where Atlantic lithosphere descends <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">westward beneath the Caribbean</mark> in the supplied map interpretation.</li><li>Pacific subduction occurs along Central America.</li><li>Transforms occur elsewhere, including the Cayman region.</li><li>Reefs and thick basaltic crust add further distinct features.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Jurassic rifting and separation involving Yucatán opened the Gulf. Spreading later ceased there while sediment accumulation continued.</li><li>The course discusses a thick basaltic Caribbean plateau and a model involving entry from the Pacific, while noting that its origin is debated.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Rifting, sedimentation, plateau volcanism, subduction, and transform motion</mark> belong to different stages or locations.</li><li>The text's later east-dipping phrase conflicts with its earlier map account.</li><li>Practice follows the mapped westward descent.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g10-12-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate inherited Gulf structure from active Caribbean boundaries</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Jurassic extension and spreading opened the Gulf, while restricted basins accumulated salt and later sediment buried the older structures. Spreading in the Gulf then ceased in the source reconstruction.</li><li>The Caribbean combines thick basaltic crust, transforms, trenches and arcs. The Cayman Trough and Lesser Antilles are different kinds of boundary feature.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Retain the source’s unresolved statements</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The present-setting text describes Atlantic lithosphere descending westward beneath the Caribbean. A later line calls the Lesser Antilles zone east-dipping. This is an <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">internal source conflict</mark>; practice uses the present map and westward relationship rather than keying contradictory directions.</li><li>The source also discusses debate over a Pacific origin versus local formation of the Caribbean basaltic plateau. A reconstructed path is a model, not a directly observed journey.</li><li>On-land maps and ages, seafloor surveys, seismic sections, drilling and paleomagnetic/spreading reconstructions provide complementary tests.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate inherited Gulf structure from active Caribbean boundaries</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Jurassic extension and spreading opened the Gulf, while restricted basins accumulated salt and later sediment buried the older structures. Spreading in the Gulf then ceased in the source reconstruction.</li><li>The Caribbean combines thick basaltic crust, transforms, trenches and arcs. The Cayman Trough and Lesser Antilles are different kinds of boundary feature.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Retain the source’s unresolved statements</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The present-setting text describes Atlantic lithosphere descending westward beneath the Caribbean.</li><li>A later line calls the Lesser Antilles zone east-dipping.</li><li>This is an <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">internal source conflict</mark>.</li><li>Practice uses the present map and westward relationship rather than keying contradictory directions.</li><li>The source also discusses debate over a Pacific origin versus local formation of the Caribbean basaltic plateau. A reconstructed path is a model, not a directly observed journey.</li><li>On-land maps and ages, seafloor surveys, seismic sections, drilling and paleomagnetic/spreading reconstructions provide complementary tests.</li></ul></section>"},"g10-13-a":{"source":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Infer processes from several observations</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The investigation combines an old abyssal floor, a broad basalt plateau, an age-progressive island chain, an arc/trench system, and a narrow young spreading ocean.</li><li>The plateau is about 40 Ma. In the island chain, <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">younger ages toward the active southeastern end</mark> support northwest plate motion relative to the hotspot.</li><li>The central margin's seismic section shows <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">normal faults and thinned continental crust buried by Cenozoic sediment</mark>. Broad shelves, canyons, and fans add surface evidence of its passive-margin history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Make explicitly conditional predictions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A narrow shelf on the western continent does not alone establish an active boundary. Check whether a trench, active boundary, or other diagnostic evidence is present.</li><li>In the narrow central/eastern ocean, youngest crust occurs at the middle ridge, with older crust toward its margins. In the constant-motion scenario, it was narrower in the past and widens in the future.</li><li>At 20 Ma before the depicted present, the plateau had traveled for about half its present 40-million-year interval and the island chain was shorter. Continued movement could carry the plateau toward the trench; collision is a possible model outcome.</li></ul></section></div><div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct the assigned scenario</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At 20 Ma before the depicted present, the island chain was shorter and the plateau lay about half its present distance from its hotspot under the exercise’s steady-motion assumption.</li><li>Both the western narrow sea and the narrow central–eastern ocean were narrower in the source scenario. Continued divergence makes the central–eastern ocean nearly twice its depicted width after another 20 Ma.</li><li>Continued motion may carry the plateau into the trench. The final checked cross-section choice was D; its original four alternatives are too small to grade reliably here. Use the readable textbook cross section to practice the matching extensional geometry.</li></ul></section></div>","html":"<div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Infer processes from several observations</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The investigation combines an old abyssal floor, a broad basalt plateau, an age-progressive island chain, an arc/trench system, and a narrow young spreading ocean.</li><li>The plateau is about 40 Ma. In the island chain, <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">younger ages toward the active southeastern end</mark> support northwest plate motion relative to the hotspot.</li><li>The central margin's seismic section shows <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">normal faults and thinned continental crust buried by Cenozoic sediment</mark>. Broad shelves, canyons, and fans add surface evidence of its passive-margin history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Make explicitly conditional predictions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A narrow shelf on the western continent does not alone establish an active boundary. Check whether a trench, active boundary, or other diagnostic evidence is present.</li><li>In the narrow central/eastern ocean, youngest crust occurs at the middle ridge, with older crust toward its margins. In the constant-motion scenario, it was narrower in the past and widens in the future.</li><li>At 20 Ma before the depicted present, the plateau had traveled for about half its present 40-million-year interval and the island chain was shorter.</li><li>Continued movement could carry the plateau toward the trench.</li><li>Collision is a possible model outcome.</li></ul></section></div><div class=\"e2-reading-prose\"><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct the assigned scenario</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At 20 Ma before the depicted present, the island chain was shorter and the plateau lay about half its present distance from its hotspot under the exercise’s steady-motion assumption.</li><li>Both the western narrow sea and the narrow central–eastern ocean were narrower in the source scenario. Continued divergence makes the central–eastern ocean nearly twice its depicted width after another 20 Ma.</li><li>Continued motion may carry the plateau into the trench.</li><li>The final checked cross-section choice was D.</li><li>Its original four alternatives are too small to grade reliably here.</li><li>Use the readable textbook cross section to practice the matching extensional geometry.</li></ul></section></div>"},"g10-13-standard":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use every numbered location as evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Number 1 is the broad abyssal region; number 2 identifies the basaltic plateau and hot-spot track; number 3 is the volcanic arc beside the trench; number 4 is the back-arc spreading system.</li><li>Number 5 is the quiet western continental edge, even though its shelf is narrow. Number 6 is the broad central margin with canyons and sediment delivery. Number 8 identifies the eastern spreading ridge and number 9 the eastern continental shelf.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">A–A′ section</mark> is a separate cross-sectional line; the source does not include a location marker 7 on this terrain image. Preserve the actual labels instead of inventing a missing number.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct the past and project the future conditionally</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The plateau existed 40 million years ago, but a 19 Ma volcano did not yet exist 20 million years ago. The active southeast end and older northwest chain constrain relative motion.</li><li>Oldest oceanic crust near the eastern margins, around 20–25 Ma, constrains early basin opening. A precise speed additionally needs a distance and an explicit motion model.</li><li>For a future sketch, state that motion continues. Buoyant plateau material approaching subduction may accrete; a future collision is a conditional scenario, not an observed event.</li><li>Assignment 17’s tiny four-alternative image remains inadequate for exact-image grading. The readable textbook <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">A–A′ section</mark> supports the normal-faulted relation without recreating unavailable answer geometry.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use every numbered location as evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Number 1 is the broad abyssal region.</li><li>Number 2 identifies the basaltic plateau and hot-spot track.</li><li>Number 3 is the volcanic arc beside the trench.</li><li>Number 4 is the back-arc spreading system.</li><li>Number 5 is the quiet western continental edge, even though its shelf is narrow. Number 6 is the broad central margin with canyons and sediment delivery. Number 8 identifies the eastern spreading ridge and number 9 the eastern continental shelf.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">A–A′ section</mark> is a separate cross-sectional line.</li><li>The source does not include a location marker 7 on this terrain image.</li><li>Preserve the actual labels instead of inventing a missing number.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct the past and project the future conditionally</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The plateau existed 40 million years ago, but a 19 Ma volcano did not yet exist 20 million years ago. The active southeast end and older northwest chain constrain relative motion.</li><li>Oldest oceanic crust near the eastern margins, around 20–25 Ma, constrains early basin opening. A precise speed additionally needs a distance and an explicit motion model.</li><li>For a future sketch, state that motion continues.</li><li>Buoyant plateau material approaching subduction may accrete.</li><li>A future collision is a conditional scenario, not an observed event.</li><li>Assignment 17’s tiny four-alternative image remains inadequate for exact-image grading. The readable textbook <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">A–A′ section</mark> supports the normal-faulted relation without recreating unavailable answer geometry.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-1-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Three controls on regional elevation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Crustal thickness, density, and temperature</mark> together control regional elevation. Thick crust generally stands higher; equally thick but less dense crust also stands higher.</li><li>Heating expands rock and lowers its density. Cooling contracts rock and promotes subsidence. Mantle upwelling can support high regions even where the crust is not exceptionally thick.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"October 22 lecture; source checked against textbook 11.1–11.2\">Moho separates crust from mantle</mark>; its depth measures crustal thickness, not the whole thickness of the lithosphere. Typical oceanic crust is about 7 km thick; continental crust is commonly 30–50 km.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Add, remove, or deform material</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression stacks and folds crust; thrust faults produce shortening and thickening. Extension stretches crust and normal faults move upper blocks down, thinning the regional crust.</li><li>Erosion removes crustal material. Deposition and volcanic eruptions add material at the surface; intrusions add material at depth. Adding a surface load also depresses its support, so surface buildup and isostatic response must both be considered.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Lithosphere includes crust and strong uppermost mantle</mark>. Its dense mantle part can grow as a plate cools; old oceanic lithosphere becomes thicker, denser, and lower than ridge lithosphere without making the crust thicker.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why the surveying observation mattered</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Himalaya attracted a plumb-line weight less strongly than a model using the visible mountain mass predicted. A <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"October 22 lecture; source checked against textbook 11.1–11.2\">low-density crustal root displaces denser mantle</mark>, reducing the expected attraction.</li><li>Isostasy describes adjustment to the types and thicknesses of material at depth. The iceberg analogy illustrates buoyancy; it does not require the mantle to be a liquid ocean.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Evidence for uplift</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Repeated GPS measurements can detect modern elevation change. Marine fossils in rocks high in the Himalaya show that material deposited in a marine setting now stands at great elevation.</li><li>Exposed deep-formed rocks and appropriate isotopic constraints can support exhumation history. A rock’s formation age alone is not automatically an uplift date.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Three controls on regional elevation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Crustal thickness, density, and temperature</mark> together control regional elevation.</li><li>Thick crust generally stands higher.</li><li>Equally thick but less dense crust also stands higher.</li><li>Heating expands rock and lowers its density. Cooling contracts rock and promotes subsidence. Mantle upwelling can support high regions even where the crust is not exceptionally thick.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"October 22 lecture; source checked against textbook 11.1–11.2\">Moho separates crust from mantle</mark>.</li><li>Its depth measures crustal thickness, not the whole thickness of the lithosphere.</li><li>Typical oceanic crust is about 7 km thick.</li><li>Continental crust is commonly 30–50 km.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Add, remove, or deform material</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Compression stacks and folds crust.</li><li>Thrust faults produce shortening and thickening.</li><li>Extension stretches crust and normal faults move upper blocks down, thinning the regional crust.</li><li>Erosion removes crustal material.</li><li>Deposition and volcanic eruptions add material at the surface.</li><li>Intrusions add material at depth.</li><li>Adding a surface load also depresses its support, so surface buildup and isostatic response must both be considered.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Lithosphere includes crust and strong uppermost mantle</mark>.</li><li>Its dense mantle part can grow as a plate cools.</li><li>Old oceanic lithosphere becomes thicker, denser, and lower than ridge lithosphere without making the crust thicker.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why the surveying observation mattered</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Himalaya attracted a plumb-line weight less strongly than a model using the visible mountain mass predicted. A <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"October 22 lecture; source checked against textbook 11.1–11.2\">low-density crustal root displaces denser mantle</mark>, reducing the expected attraction.</li><li>Isostasy describes adjustment to the types and thicknesses of material at depth.</li><li>The iceberg analogy illustrates buoyancy.</li><li>It does not require the mantle to be a liquid ocean.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Evidence for uplift</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Repeated GPS measurements can detect modern elevation change. Marine fossils in rocks high in the Himalaya show that material deposited in a marine setting now stands at great elevation.</li><li>Exposed deep-formed rocks and appropriate isotopic constraints can support exhumation history. A rock’s formation age alone is not automatically an uplift date.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-2-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Where high regions form</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Subduction, continental collision, and mantle upwelling</mark> are three major settings for regional high ground. The same elevation does not imply the same mechanism.</li><li>The Andes combine crustal shortening, magmatic additions, and heating above subduction. Himalaya–Tibet reflects collision between India and Asia, with one continent partly underthrust beneath the other.</li><li>East Africa illustrates high ground supported by heating, thinned lithosphere, and mantle upwelling. The Basin and Range can have moderately high regional elevation despite extended crust because its mantle lithosphere is thin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Erosion and rebound act together</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A growing range becomes higher when uplift outpaces erosion. Rivers and glaciers carry eroded material to nearby basins.</li><li>Removing mass permits <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">isostatic rebound</mark> of the buoyant root. Rebound offsets some erosion, but the range and root can still shrink through prolonged erosion.</li><li>Deep metamorphic and plutonic rocks become exposed as uplift and erosion work together. Sediment loading depresses adjacent basins and creates more room for fill.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the North American profile</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Broad elevation generally decreases from the western high regions toward the Mississippi basin; local ranges and plateaus complicate that pattern.</li><li>The supplied <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"October 22 lecture; source checked against textbook 11.1–11.2\">rule of thumb is 6 km of extra crust for roughly 1 km of extra elevation</mark>. The Phoenix–Flagstaff example compares about 28 and 40 km of crust; it is an approximate isostatic comparison, not a universal equation.</li><li>Appalachian elevation has declined as erosion reduced its once-thick crustal root. An old mountain belt need not mark a modern plate boundary.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Where high regions form</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Subduction, continental collision, and mantle upwelling</mark> are three major settings for regional high ground. The same elevation does not imply the same mechanism.</li><li>The Andes combine crustal shortening, magmatic additions, and heating above subduction. Himalaya–Tibet reflects collision between India and Asia, with one continent partly underthrust beneath the other.</li><li>East Africa illustrates high ground supported by heating, thinned lithosphere, and mantle upwelling. The Basin and Range can have moderately high regional elevation despite extended crust because its mantle lithosphere is thin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Erosion and rebound act together</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A growing range becomes higher when uplift outpaces erosion. Rivers and glaciers carry eroded material to nearby basins.</li><li>Removing mass permits <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">isostatic rebound</mark> of the buoyant root. Rebound offsets some erosion, but the range and root can still shrink through prolonged erosion.</li><li>Deep metamorphic and plutonic rocks become exposed as uplift and erosion work together. Sediment loading depresses adjacent basins and creates more room for fill.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the North American profile</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Broad elevation generally decreases from the western high regions toward the Mississippi basin.</li><li>Local ranges and plateaus complicate that pattern.</li><li>The supplied <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"October 22 lecture; source checked against textbook 11.1–11.2\">rule of thumb is 6 km of extra crust for roughly 1 km of extra elevation</mark>.</li><li>The Phoenix–Flagstaff example compares about 28 and 40 km of crust.</li><li>It is an approximate isostatic comparison, not a universal equation.</li><li>Appalachian elevation has declined as erosion reduced its once-thick crustal root. An old mountain belt need not mark a modern plate boundary.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-3-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build relief without a regional root</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Local relief differs from regional elevation</mark>. A local mountain may rest on ordinary crust; it does not require an entire thickened regional root.</li><li>Volcanoes pile lava, ash, and scoria onto the surface. A scoria cone or composite volcano is constructed relief; an eroded volcanic remnant may remain high because it resists erosion.</li><li>Reverse faults raise one block over another. Along normal faults, a relatively high block can form a mountain next to a downdropped basin even as the region extends and thins.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Fold geometry and resistant layers</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Folding can raise the surface directly. Buried thrust faults may bend overlying rocks and damage well pipes, as in the Los Angeles example.</li><li>A folded resistant sandstone may remain as a mountain long after folding stops. Modern relief can reflect erosion of an older structure.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Differential erosion</mark> removes weaker rock faster than resistant rock. A granite pluton can be exposed as an erosional remnant, as at Stone Mountain, Georgia.</li><li>A hard, nearly horizontal cap can protect a flat-topped mesa. Tilted resistant beds can form ridges whose shape follows the layers.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build relief without a regional root</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Local relief differs from regional elevation</mark>.</li><li>A local mountain may rest on ordinary crust.</li><li>It does not require an entire thickened regional root.</li><li>Volcanoes pile lava, ash, and scoria onto the surface.</li><li>A scoria cone or composite volcano is constructed relief.</li><li>An eroded volcanic remnant may remain high because it resists erosion.</li><li>Reverse faults raise one block over another. Along normal faults, a relatively high block can form a mountain next to a downdropped basin even as the region extends and thins.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Fold geometry and resistant layers</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Folding can raise the surface directly. Buried thrust faults may bend overlying rocks and damage well pipes, as in the Los Angeles example.</li><li>A folded resistant sandstone may remain as a mountain long after folding stops. Modern relief can reflect erosion of an older structure.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Differential erosion</mark> removes weaker rock faster than resistant rock. A granite pluton can be exposed as an erosional remnant, as at Stone Mountain, Georgia.</li><li>A hard, nearly horizontal cap can protect a flat-topped mesa. Tilted resistant beds can form ridges whose shape follows the layers.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-4-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the cause of accommodation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">basin is low relative to its surroundings</mark> and can trap sediment and water. The tectonic cause must be identified separately from the fill.</li><li>Passive-margin basins overlie stretched continental edges that cooled and subsided after rifting. Continental-rift and normal-fault basins form when fault blocks drop during extension.</li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">foreland basin</mark> forms where thrust-sheet weight flexes the plate downward in front of a thrust belt. The Persian Gulf is a course example.</li><li>Strike-slip motion can create a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">pull-apart basin</mark> at an appropriate releasing bend or step. Not every bend or strike-slip fault produces a basin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use sediment and regional geometry</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Coarse debris accumulates near steep faulted basin margins; finer lake sediments and evaporites can collect farther inward. The fill depends on water supply, relief, transport, and climate.</li><li>A forearc basin lies between the trench/accretionary region and the arc. Forearc and foreland identify different structural positions.</li><li>Broad regional subsidence can follow cooling, deep movement, or density changes. A basin can form inside a continent and can occur at high elevation relative to sea level.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Michigan: observations and uncertainty</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The bedrock map has younger strata toward the center, and the cross section has layers that thicken inward. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Thicker basinward strata record subsidence during deposition</mark>.</li><li>The Michigan Basin’s origin remains incompletely resolved. Rifting and flow, thinning, or cooling of lower crust are discussed possibilities; the textbook does not establish one exclusive cause.</li><li>Gulf Coast basins record rifting, later passive-margin subsidence, and prolonged sedimentation. A place may preserve several stages of basin history.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the cause of accommodation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">basin is low relative to its surroundings</mark> and can trap sediment and water. The tectonic cause must be identified separately from the fill.</li><li>Passive-margin basins overlie stretched continental edges that cooled and subsided after rifting. Continental-rift and normal-fault basins form when fault blocks drop during extension.</li><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">foreland basin</mark> forms where thrust-sheet weight flexes the plate downward in front of a thrust belt. The Persian Gulf is a course example.</li><li>Strike-slip motion can create a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">pull-apart basin</mark> at an appropriate releasing bend or step. Not every bend or strike-slip fault produces a basin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use sediment and regional geometry</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Coarse debris accumulates near steep faulted basin margins.</li><li>Finer lake sediments and evaporites can collect farther inward.</li><li>The fill depends on water supply, relief, transport, and climate.</li><li>A forearc basin lies between the trench/accretionary region and the arc. Forearc and foreland identify different structural positions.</li><li>Broad regional subsidence can follow cooling, deep movement, or density changes. A basin can form inside a continent and can occur at high elevation relative to sea level.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Michigan: observations and uncertainty</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The bedrock map has younger strata toward the center, and the cross section has layers that thicken inward. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Thicker basinward strata record subsidence during deposition</mark>.</li><li>The Michigan Basin’s origin remains incompletely resolved.</li><li>Rifting and flow, thinning, or cooling of lower crust are discussed possibilities.</li><li>The textbook does not establish one exclusive cause.</li><li>Gulf Coast basins record rifting, later passive-margin subsidence, and prolonged sedimentation. A place may preserve several stages of basin history.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-5-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow an ocean–continent cross section</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The oceanic plate bends into a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">trench</mark>. Sediment scraped from it and supplied from land forms a faulted, folded <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">accretionary prism</mark>.</li><li>A forearc basin sits between the offshore subduction region and the main magmatic belt. Magma rising into the continent adds crust at depth and at the surface.</li><li>Compression forms thrusts and folds that shorten and thicken continental crust. The weight of inland thrust sheets flexes adjacent continental crust down into a foreland basin.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Forearc refers to position relative to the arc; foreland refers to the thrust-loaded continental side</mark>. Their names and causes must not be exchanged.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Convergence does not force one upper-plate response</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An overriding plate pushing toward the slab relative to the asthenosphere favors shortening. A retreating slab and an overriding plate moving away can permit extension above subduction.</li><li>A thick or hot collision belt can spread under its own weight, producing normal faults and basins. Regional convergence and local extension can coexist.</li><li>Continental collision thickens crust by underthrusting and stacked slices. Stresses may reach far into the interior.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Catskill basin evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Devonian thrust loading depressed a basin west of the Appalachian source. Sediment supplied from the east became generally coarser and thicker toward the mountain belt.</li><li>The Catskill Delta illustrates how a basin’s sedimentary record connects deposition, source direction, and active thrusting. Its delta deposits are not a modern coastline map.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow an ocean–continent cross section</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The oceanic plate bends into a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">trench</mark>. Sediment scraped from it and supplied from land forms a faulted, folded <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">accretionary prism</mark>.</li><li>A forearc basin sits between the offshore subduction region and the main magmatic belt. Magma rising into the continent adds crust at depth and at the surface.</li><li>Compression forms thrusts and folds that shorten and thicken continental crust. The weight of inland thrust sheets flexes adjacent continental crust down into a foreland basin.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Forearc refers to position relative to the arc</mark>.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Foreland refers to the thrust-loaded continental side</mark>.</li><li>Their names and causes must not be exchanged.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Convergence does not force one upper-plate response</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An overriding plate pushing toward the slab relative to the asthenosphere favors shortening. A retreating slab and an overriding plate moving away can permit extension above subduction.</li><li>A thick or hot collision belt can spread under its own weight, producing normal faults and basins. Regional convergence and local extension can coexist.</li><li>Continental collision thickens crust by underthrusting and stacked slices. Stresses may reach far into the interior.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Catskill basin evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Devonian thrust loading depressed a basin west of the Appalachian source. Sediment supplied from the east became generally coarser and thicker toward the mountain belt.</li><li>The Catskill Delta illustrates how a basin’s sedimentary record connects deposition, source direction, and active thrusting. Its delta deposits are not a modern coastline map.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-6-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Two ways fault blocks accommodate extension</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Nonrotating normal-fault blocks can form a high <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">horst</mark> and low <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">graben</mark>. Sediment eroded from the ranges fills adjacent basins.</li><li>In rotating-block systems, blocks and faults rotate together, like books sliding on a shelf. A down-rotated corner becomes a basin; an up-rotated corner becomes a ridge.</li><li>Continued rotation can leave older strata dipping more steeply than younger syn-extensional strata. Rotated low-angle faults can accommodate large displacement.</li><li>Death Valley combines normal-fault relief, basin sediments, and evaporites. A closed basin below sea level is not necessarily connected to the ocean.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read units before assigning an age</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Units deposited <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">before basin formation</mark> may start with similar thickness across the area, then be tilted and faulted.</li><li>Units deposited <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">during basin formation</mark> can thicken into the basin and contain coarse debris from steep basin margins. Later basin-fill units may show less deformation.</li><li>A younger flat unit that overlaps and seals the basin faults shows that the mapped faulting ended before that unit was deposited.</li><li>The source accumulation rate is thickness divided by depositional time. In the supplied example, rapid accumulation after about 15 Ma helps locate basin initiation; this is an example-specific age.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Extension beside an arc</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Extension may occur in a forearc or behind an arc even though a slab is subducting nearby. Strong backarc extension can make a new small ocean basin.</li><li>Classify the local deformation and the regional plate setting separately. A nearby trench does not automatically make every basin compressional.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Two ways fault blocks accommodate extension</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Nonrotating normal-fault blocks can form a high <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">horst</mark> and low <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">graben</mark>. Sediment eroded from the ranges fills adjacent basins.</li><li>In rotating-block systems, blocks and faults rotate together, like books sliding on a shelf.</li><li>A down-rotated corner becomes a basin.</li><li>An up-rotated corner becomes a ridge.</li><li>Continued rotation can leave older strata dipping more steeply than younger syn-extensional strata. Rotated low-angle faults can accommodate large displacement.</li><li>Death Valley combines normal-fault relief, basin sediments, and evaporites. A closed basin below sea level is not necessarily connected to the ocean.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read units before assigning an age</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Units deposited <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">before basin formation</mark> may start with similar thickness across the area, then be tilted and faulted.</li><li>Units deposited <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">during basin formation</mark> can thicken into the basin and contain coarse debris from steep basin margins. Later basin-fill units may show less deformation.</li><li>A younger flat unit that overlaps and seals the basin faults shows that the mapped faulting ended before that unit was deposited.</li><li>The source accumulation rate is thickness divided by depositional time.</li><li>In the supplied example, rapid accumulation after about 15 Ma helps locate basin initiation.</li><li>This is an example-specific age.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Extension beside an arc</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Extension may occur in a forearc or behind an arc even though a slab is subducting nearby. Strong backarc extension can make a new small ocean basin.</li><li>Classify the local deformation and the regional plate setting separately. A nearby trench does not automatically make every basin compressional.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-7-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe the hotspot setting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continental hot spots commonly combine <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">regional uplift, volcanism, and rifting</mark>. A rising, mostly solid mantle plume can supply heat and decompression melting.</li><li>At Afar, the Red Sea, Gulf of Aden, and East African Rift meet. The first two contain spreading systems; the continental East African Rift need not inevitably become a new ocean.</li><li>Yellowstone records the present young end of a volcanic track. The older Snake River Plain extends southwest, consistent with southwestward North American motion relative to the hot spot.</li><li>Geysers and hot springs reflect heat from hot rock and magma. Their presence does not mean an eruption is happening now.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From doming to a passive margin</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Heating and doming commonly accompany three radiating rift arms. Normal faults lower blocks; lakes, sediment, and volcanic rocks fill the basins.</li><li>Basalt can come from mantle-derived magma; more felsic magma can form where the continental crust melts. Not all magma in this setting has one composition.</li><li>If two arms continue to spreading, they separate pieces of the continent. A third arm may become a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">failed rift</mark>, remain low, and guide a river.</li><li>After spreading moves offshore, the rifted edge cools and subsides into a passive margin. Rivers following a failed arm can build a delta at the bend in the continental outline.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe the hotspot setting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continental hot spots commonly combine <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">regional uplift, volcanism, and rifting</mark>. A rising, mostly solid mantle plume can supply heat and decompression melting.</li><li>At Afar, the Red Sea, Gulf of Aden, and East African Rift meet.</li><li>The first two contain spreading systems.</li><li>The continental East African Rift need not inevitably become a new ocean.</li><li>Yellowstone records the present young end of a volcanic track. The older Snake River Plain extends southwest, consistent with southwestward North American motion relative to the hot spot.</li><li>Geysers and hot springs reflect heat from hot rock and magma. Their presence does not mean an eruption is happening now.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From doming to a passive margin</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Heating and doming commonly accompany three radiating rift arms.</li><li>Normal faults lower blocks.</li><li>Lakes, sediment, and volcanic rocks fill the basins.</li><li>Basalt can come from mantle-derived magma.</li><li>More felsic magma can form where the continental crust melts.</li><li>Not all magma in this setting has one composition.</li><li>If two arms continue to spreading, they separate pieces of the continent. A third arm may become a <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">failed rift</mark>, remain low, and guide a river.</li><li>After spreading moves offshore, the rifted edge cools and subsides into a passive margin. Rivers following a failed arm can build a delta at the bend in the continental outline.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-8-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Basement and its cover</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">continental shield</mark> exposes old crystalline igneous and metamorphic basement, commonly Precambrian. A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">continental platform</mark> has younger, broadly flat sedimentary cover above that basement.</li><li>The basement–cover boundary is a major unconformity; sediment over eroded crystalline rock is a nonconformity. Basement can be present beneath a platform without being exposed.</li><li>The Ohio section shows gently dipping Paleozoic cover above buried Precambrian basement. Its vertical exaggeration makes layers and dips appear larger than their true scale.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Stable is relative</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continental interiors are often less deformed than plate margins, but distant stresses can reactivate old basement faults and fold overlying cover.</li><li>Thrust and sediment loading near the margin can flex the plate, making broad interior basins and uplifts. Plate boundaries can affect locations far inland.</li><li>Low relief allows a modest sea-level rise to flood far into a continent. Sedimentary cover records changes in climate, sea level, drainage, and latitude.</li><li>The Urals lie within a modern continental interior because an older collision joined landmasses around them. Modern position alone does not reveal the original tectonic setting.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Basement and its cover</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">continental shield</mark> exposes old crystalline igneous and metamorphic basement, commonly Precambrian. A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">continental platform</mark> has younger, broadly flat sedimentary cover above that basement.</li><li>The basement–cover boundary is a major unconformity.</li><li>Sediment over eroded crystalline rock is a nonconformity.</li><li>Basement can be present beneath a platform without being exposed.</li><li>The Ohio section shows gently dipping Paleozoic cover above buried Precambrian basement. Its vertical exaggeration makes layers and dips appear larger than their true scale.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Stable is relative</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continental interiors are often less deformed than plate margins, but distant stresses can reactivate old basement faults and fold overlying cover.</li><li>Thrust and sediment loading near the margin can flex the plate, making broad interior basins and uplifts. Plate boundaries can affect locations far inland.</li><li>Low relief allows a modest sea-level rise to flood far into a continent. Sedimentary cover records changes in climate, sea level, drainage, and latitude.</li><li>The Urals lie within a modern continental interior because an older collision joined landmasses around them. Modern position alone does not reveal the original tectonic setting.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-9-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify a terrane from more than scenery</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">tectonic terrane is fault bounded and has a distinct geologic history</mark>. Compare rock sequences, ages, structures, fossils, chemistry, and magnetic directions across the boundary.</li><li>Terranes may originate as oceanic crust, island arcs, continental fragments, oceanic islands, or plateaus. Pillow basalt supports a submarine origin but does not alone identify one exact original latitude.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Accretion</mark> attaches a terrane to another landmass, commonly when buoyant crust is scraped from a descending plate at a subduction zone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bracket when pieces joined</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An intrusion crossing both terranes and their boundary shows they were together before that intrusion formed. An overlapping sedimentary unit similarly gives a younger limit on joining.</li><li>Cobbles derived from one terrane and deposited on the other show proximity by the time of deposition. Fossils and paleomagnetism help test different earlier origins.</li><li>Equal rock ages do not prove two terranes formed together. Same-age pillow basalt and continental metamorphic rocks can record very different settings.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Alaska as an example</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Alaska is a mosaic of continental pieces, arcs, oceanic material, and later overlap deposits. Some younger units cover boundaries after accretion.</li><li>Wrangellia is interpreted to have originated far south of its present position and later moved north. Similar rock sequences and ages connect fragments now dispersed along the western margin.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify a terrane from more than scenery</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">tectonic terrane is fault bounded and has a distinct geologic history</mark>. Compare rock sequences, ages, structures, fossils, chemistry, and magnetic directions across the boundary.</li><li>Terranes may originate as oceanic crust, island arcs, continental fragments, oceanic islands, or plateaus. Pillow basalt supports a submarine origin but does not alone identify one exact original latitude.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Accretion</mark> attaches a terrane to another landmass, commonly when buoyant crust is scraped from a descending plate at a subduction zone.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bracket when pieces joined</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An intrusion crossing both terranes and their boundary shows they were together before that intrusion formed. An overlapping sedimentary unit similarly gives a younger limit on joining.</li><li>Cobbles derived from one terrane and deposited on the other show proximity by the time of deposition. Fossils and paleomagnetism help test different earlier origins.</li><li>Equal rock ages do not prove two terranes formed together. Same-age pillow basalt and continental metamorphic rocks can record very different settings.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Alaska as an example</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Alaska is a mosaic of continental pieces, arcs, oceanic material, and later overlap deposits. Some younger units cover boundaries after accretion.</li><li>Wrangellia is interpreted to have originated far south of its present position and later moved north. Similar rock sequences and ages connect fragments now dispersed along the western margin.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-10-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Several ages answer different questions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continents grow by <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">accretion of crustal pieces</mark> and are reshaped by rifting, shortening, extension, and strike-slip movement.</li><li>A map of exposed rock ages shows the ages of surface units. It does not automatically give the age of buried basement or the time terranes were assembled.</li><li>The Acasta Gneiss is about 4.0 billion years old in the source. Much of the Canadian Shield is younger Precambrian crystalline rock; other provinces were added later.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read North American provinces</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Grenville Province was added along the southeastern shield margin around 1.1 billion years ago. Avalon and other eastern terranes joined during Paleozoic events.</li><li>The continental platform has Paleozoic and Mesozoic cover over Precambrian basement. The Coastal Plain has younger cover along a rifted margin; some underlying crust has a far older history.</li><li>Alaska and western North America contain many accreted terranes. Mexico includes Paleozoic and Mesozoic arc terranes. A single continent therefore contains multiple formation and assembly ages.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare other continents</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Other continents also combine ancient shields, younger cover, and added terranes. Western Australia is older than much of its eastern accreted region; Asia includes many assembled pieces.</li><li>California includes oceanic material, island arcs, and accretionary-prism rocks. Some granites and volcanic cover formed after pieces were assembled and should not be mistaken for transported terranes.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Several ages answer different questions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continents grow by <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">accretion of crustal pieces</mark> and are reshaped by rifting, shortening, extension, and strike-slip movement.</li><li>A map of exposed rock ages shows the ages of surface units. It does not automatically give the age of buried basement or the time terranes were assembled.</li><li>The Acasta Gneiss is about 4.0 billion years old in the source.</li><li>Much of the Canadian Shield is younger Precambrian crystalline rock.</li><li>Other provinces were added later.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read North American provinces</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Grenville Province was added along the southeastern shield margin around 1.1 billion years ago. Avalon and other eastern terranes joined during Paleozoic events.</li><li>The continental platform has Paleozoic and Mesozoic cover over Precambrian basement.</li><li>The Coastal Plain has younger cover along a rifted margin.</li><li>Some underlying crust has a far older history.</li><li>Alaska and western North America contain many accreted terranes. Mexico includes Paleozoic and Mesozoic arc terranes. A single continent therefore contains multiple formation and assembly ages.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare other continents</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Other continents also combine ancient shields, younger cover, and added terranes.</li><li>Western Australia is older than much of its eastern accreted region.</li><li>Asia includes many assembled pieces.</li><li>California includes oceanic material, island arcs, and accretionary-prism rocks. Some granites and volcanic cover formed after pieces were assembled and should not be mistaken for transported terranes.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-11-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow the supplied reconstructions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The sequence is a source reconstruction, with uncertainty in some older continental positions. At its 600 Ma starting frame, Rodinia is breaking apart; Gondwana groups the major southern landmasses.</li><li>Around 370 Ma, North America had joined some European and Avalonian pieces while approaching Africa and South America. Different collisions happened at different times.</li><li>Late Paleozoic collisions assembled <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Pangaea</mark>; the source’s 280 Ma frame shows the joined continents. The Appalachian and Ouachita belts are part of that assembly history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Names, regions, and oceans</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Laurasia</mark> groups North America, Europe, and Asia in the source’s Jurassic frame. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Gondwana</mark> includes Africa, South America, Antarctica, Australia, India, and associated fragments.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Tethys is an ocean/sea, not a supercontinent</mark>. Do not mix continental group names with ocean names.</li><li>By the 150 Ma frame, the central Atlantic separated North America from Africa, while South America remained attached to Africa. Atlantic opening occurred in stages, not all at once.</li><li>Present plate motions permit conditional future predictions. Changing boundaries and motions prevent treating a projection as a guaranteed future map.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow the supplied reconstructions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The sequence is a source reconstruction, with uncertainty in some older continental positions.</li><li>At its 600 Ma starting frame, Rodinia is breaking apart.</li><li>Gondwana groups the major southern landmasses.</li><li>Around 370 Ma, North America had joined some European and Avalonian pieces while approaching Africa and South America. Different collisions happened at different times.</li><li>Late Paleozoic collisions assembled <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Pangaea</mark>.</li><li>The source’s 280 Ma frame shows the joined continents.</li><li>The Appalachian and Ouachita belts are part of that assembly history.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Names, regions, and oceans</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Laurasia</mark> groups North America, Europe, and Asia in the source’s Jurassic frame. <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Gondwana</mark> includes Africa, South America, Antarctica, Australia, India, and associated fragments.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Tethys is an ocean/sea, not a supercontinent</mark>. Do not mix continental group names with ocean names.</li><li>By the 150 Ma frame, the central Atlantic separated North America from Africa, while South America remained attached to Africa. Atlantic opening occurred in stages, not all at once.</li><li>Present plate motions permit conditional future predictions. Changing boundaries and motions prevent treating a projection as a guaranteed future map.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-12-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Three Paleozoic collisions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">orogeny is a mountain-building event</mark>. The Appalachian record includes several events rather than one sudden uplift.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Taconic orogeny</mark>, around 450 Ma in the source, followed collision of an offshore island arc with eastern North America.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Acadian orogeny</mark>, around 400 Ma, involved Avalonian landmasses. Loading of thrust sheets created the Catskill foreland basin.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Alleghenian orogeny</mark>, about 330–300 Ma, involved Gondwana, including Africa and South America, and formed the major Appalachian–Ouachita collision belt.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">An opening–closing–opening cycle</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Earlier rifting of Rodinia created a passive eastern margin. Offshore subduction and arc approach later changed that margin into a convergent system.</li><li>After major collisions, erosion reduced the high belt and supplied sediment to distant basins. Datable grains can identify far-traveled source material.</li><li>By about 220 Ma, renewed rifting formed normal-fault basins with red sediment and basalt. Jurassic spreading around 180 Ma opened the Atlantic and left a passive margin.</li><li>The modern eastern coast is not an active subduction boundary. Old folds and thrusts preserve a former plate setting even where the present margin is passive.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">A checked stage label is disputed</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied assessment’s northern-Appalachian cross section shows an oceanic slab descending east beneath an offshore arc, matching the pre-Taconic arc-subduction panel in textbook11.12.</li><li>Its checked capture instead marks collision with Gondwana. The lab preserves that discrepancy and asks you to identify the visible subduction relationship rather than memorize the conflicting stage key.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Three Paleozoic collisions</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">orogeny is a mountain-building event</mark>. The Appalachian record includes several events rather than one sudden uplift.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Taconic orogeny</mark>, around 450 Ma in the source, followed collision of an offshore island arc with eastern North America.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Acadian orogeny</mark>, around 400 Ma, involved Avalonian landmasses. Loading of thrust sheets created the Catskill foreland basin.</li><li>The <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Alleghenian orogeny</mark>, about 330–300 Ma, involved Gondwana, including Africa and South America, and formed the major Appalachian–Ouachita collision belt.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">An opening–closing–opening cycle</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Earlier rifting of Rodinia created a passive eastern margin. Offshore subduction and arc approach later changed that margin into a convergent system.</li><li>After major collisions, erosion reduced the high belt and supplied sediment to distant basins. Datable grains can identify far-traveled source material.</li><li>By about 220 Ma, renewed rifting formed normal-fault basins with red sediment and basalt. Jurassic spreading around 180 Ma opened the Atlantic and left a passive margin.</li><li>The modern eastern coast is not an active subduction boundary. Old folds and thrusts preserve a former plate setting even where the present margin is passive.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">A checked stage label is disputed</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The supplied assessment’s northern-Appalachian cross section shows an oceanic slab descending east beneath an offshore arc, matching the pre-Taconic arc-subduction panel in textbook11.12.</li><li>Its checked capture instead marks collision with Gondwana. The lab preserves that discrepancy and asks you to identify the visible subduction relationship rather than memorize the conflicting stage key.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-13-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From rifting to convergence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Late Precambrian rifting thinned western North America; its former continental neighbor is uncertain. An Early Paleozoic passive margin accumulated shallow-water cover on the shelf and deep-water sediment farther west.</li><li>Later Paleozoic arc collisions transported deep-water rocks over shallower shelf rocks and formed a foreland basin. Terranes preserve this assembly record.</li><li>Mesozoic subduction generated volcanic arcs and granitic batholiths, including the Sierra Nevada. Thrust loading farther inland helped create a foreland basin flooded by a seaway.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Shallow subduction and later extension</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>During the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Laramide orogeny</mark>, approximately 80–40 Ma in the source, shallow-angle subduction transmitted compression far inland and shifted magmatism eastward.</li><li>Rocky Mountain uplifts and adjacent basins record that inland compression. The earlier Ancestral Rockies are a different event related to distant eastern collisions.</li><li>As the plate setting changed, widespread Cenozoic extension formed Basin and Range normal faults and thinned crust and lithosphere. Upwelling helped support parts of the Rio Grande Rift.</li><li>Overrunning a spreading center helped replace part of the convergent margin with the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">San Andreas transform</mark>. The modern West contains different boundary types, not one uniform setting.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Resolve the supplied assessment conflict</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The checked Connect capture for its final western-history question marks an Early Paleozoic passive-margin answer on a figure showing an arc terrane, thrusting, and a foreland basin.</li><li>The textbook’s corresponding cross section belongs to later Paleozoic collision. Practice here grades the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">visible evidence of shortening and accretion</mark>; that conflicting stage-name key is not treated as settled.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">From rifting to convergence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Late Precambrian rifting thinned western North America.</li><li>Its former continental neighbor is uncertain.</li><li>An Early Paleozoic passive margin accumulated shallow-water cover on the shelf and deep-water sediment farther west.</li><li>Later Paleozoic arc collisions transported deep-water rocks over shallower shelf rocks and formed a foreland basin. Terranes preserve this assembly record.</li><li>Mesozoic subduction generated volcanic arcs and granitic batholiths, including the Sierra Nevada. Thrust loading farther inland helped create a foreland basin flooded by a seaway.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Shallow subduction and later extension</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>During the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Laramide orogeny</mark>, approximately 80–40 Ma in the source, shallow-angle subduction transmitted compression far inland and shifted magmatism eastward.</li><li>Rocky Mountain uplifts and adjacent basins record that inland compression. The earlier Ancestral Rockies are a different event related to distant eastern collisions.</li><li>As the plate setting changed, widespread Cenozoic extension formed Basin and Range normal faults and thinned crust and lithosphere. Upwelling helped support parts of the Rio Grande Rift.</li><li>Overrunning a spreading center helped replace part of the convergent margin with the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">San Andreas transform</mark>. The modern West contains different boundary types, not one uniform setting.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Resolve the supplied assessment conflict</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The checked Connect capture for its final western-history question marks an Early Paleozoic passive-margin answer on a figure showing an arc terrane, thrusting, and a foreland basin.</li><li>The textbook’s corresponding cross section belongs to later Paleozoic collision.</li><li>Practice here grades the <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">visible evidence of shortening and accretion</mark>.</li><li>That conflicting stage-name key is not treated as settled.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-14-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the stated future changes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At Time 1, the western coast is passive. The exercise explicitly introduces <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">eastward subduction beneath the western continent</mark> and rifting in the east; these are scenario givens, not inferred from coast shape alone.</li><li>The offshore continental fragment moves toward the main continent and can be accreted as a terrane, modifying the western coastline.</li><li>A trench and accretionary prism develop offshore; an inland magmatic belt and a farther-inland thrust belt develop above the slab. Thrust loading creates a foreland basin beside the belt.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the original A–D sites</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Site A</mark> lies at the western coastal forearc/accretionary region. Distinguish the coastal basin from the volcanic belt farther inland.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Site B</mark> lies at the future magmatic belt, where intrusion, eruption, and associated heating can thicken and raise the crust.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Site C</mark> lies within the fold-and-thrust belt, where shortening stacks and thickens the crust. A nearby low foreland basin forms from loading, not the same mechanism as the mountain.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Site D</mark> is the eastern rifted edge. By Time 2, spreading has moved away and the coast is a cooling, subsiding passive margin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Predict with a cause</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The former hotspot leaves an indentation and a failed-rift corridor. A low corridor, a rifted coast, a magmatic belt, and a thrust-loaded basin can coexist in one reconstructed region.</li><li>For every prediction, state the site, expected rise or subsidence, and a mechanism: magmatic addition, shortening, thinning, cooling, or flexural loading. Use the full O/A/M/V/B/P feature bank when sketching the map.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the stated future changes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At Time 1, the western coast is passive.</li><li>The exercise explicitly introduces <mark class=\"e2-study-term\">eastward subduction beneath the western continent</mark> and rifting in the east.</li><li>These are scenario givens, not inferred from coast shape alone.</li><li>The offshore continental fragment moves toward the main continent and can be accreted as a terrane, modifying the western coastline.</li><li>A trench and accretionary prism develop offshore.</li><li>An inland magmatic belt and a farther-inland thrust belt develop above the slab.</li><li>Thrust loading creates a foreland basin beside the belt.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the original A–D sites</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Site A</mark> lies at the western coastal forearc/accretionary region. Distinguish the coastal basin from the volcanic belt farther inland.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Site B</mark> lies at the future magmatic belt, where intrusion, eruption, and associated heating can thicken and raise the crust.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Site C</mark> lies within the fold-and-thrust belt, where shortening stacks and thickens the crust. A nearby low foreland basin forms from loading, not the same mechanism as the mountain.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-term\">Site D</mark> is the eastern rifted edge. By Time 2, spreading has moved away and the coast is a cooling, subsiding passive margin.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Predict with a cause</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The former hotspot leaves an indentation and a failed-rift corridor. A low corridor, a rifted coast, a magmatic belt, and a thrust-loaded basin can coexist in one reconstructed region.</li><li>For every prediction, state the site, expected rise or subsidence, and a mechanism<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Magmatic addition, shortening, thinning, cooling, or flexural loading.</li></ul></li><li>Use the full O/A/M/V/B/P feature bank when sketching the map.</li></ul></section>"},"g11-14-numbered":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read each marker where it actually appears</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Region1 is the broad ocean, underlain by oceanic crust and thin sediment. It is not the future trench location at the coast.</li><li>Region2 is the offshore continental fragment that can become an accreted terrane.</li><li>Region3 is the western coastal accretionary region; region4 is the magmatic belt farther inland.</li><li>Region5 marks the eastern rifted edge, which becomes a passive margin as spreading moves away.</li><li>The source’s A–D site letters belong to its later simplified map. Do not transfer a number onto a different feature just because a nearby site uses a letter.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read each marker where it actually appears</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Region1 is the broad ocean, underlain by oceanic crust and thin sediment. It is not the future trench location at the coast.</li><li>Region2 is the offshore continental fragment that can become an accreted terrane.</li><li>Region3 is the western coastal accretionary region.</li><li>Region4 is the magmatic belt farther inland.</li><li>Region5 marks the eastern rifted edge, which becomes a passive margin as spreading moves away.</li><li>The source’s A–D site letters belong to its later simplified map. Do not transfer a number onto a different feature just because a nearby site uses a letter.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-1-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Locate the release of energy</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An earthquake is a sudden release of energy stored in rock. Seismic waves carry part of that energy outward through Earth and along its surface; the disturbance can be detected far from its source.</li><li>The hypocenter, also called the focus, is the place where rupture begins. The epicenter is the point on the surface directly above it. A rupture can spread far beyond its starting point.</li><li>A seismic station contains instruments that record ground motion. For comparable paths, a nearby station receives a given wave before a more distant station. Read the actual station positions rather than memorizing a station number.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">All three fault motions can produce earthquakes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A normal fault lowers the hanging wall relative to the footwall and commonly accommodates extension. A reverse fault raises the hanging wall; a gently dipping reverse fault is a thrust fault.</li><li>A strike-slip fault displaces its two sides mainly horizontally. Normal, reverse, thrust, and strike-slip faults can all generate earthquakes when they slip abruptly.</li><li>The fault type describes relative movement. It does not, by itself, supply the earthquake magnitude, hypocentral depth, or expected damage.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Other sources of seismic energy</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Explosive eruptions, magma pushing into rock, faulting beneath a heavy volcano, and collapse of unstable volcanic slopes can produce seismic signals. Distinctive volcanic tremors can accompany magma movement.</li><li>Large landslides shake the ground as rock breaks and moves. Underground explosions compress surrounding material and generate waves that seismic networks can detect.</li><li>Reservoir filling adds a load to the crust; deep wastewater injection can alter conditions on existing faults. These are human-induced mechanisms, not evidence that every earthquake near a well or reservoir has the same cause.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Locate the release of energy</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An earthquake is a sudden release of energy stored in rock.</li><li>Seismic waves carry part of that energy outward through Earth and along its surface.</li><li>The disturbance can be detected far from its source.</li><li>The hypocenter, also called the focus, is the place where rupture begins. The epicenter is the point on the surface directly above it. A rupture can spread far beyond its starting point.</li><li>A seismic station contains instruments that record ground motion. For comparable paths, a nearby station receives a given wave before a more distant station. Read the actual station positions rather than memorizing a station number.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">All three fault motions can produce earthquakes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A normal fault lowers the hanging wall relative to the footwall and commonly accommodates extension.</li><li>A reverse fault raises the hanging wall.</li><li>A gently dipping reverse fault is a thrust fault.</li><li>A strike-slip fault displaces its two sides mainly horizontally. Normal, reverse, thrust, and strike-slip faults can all generate earthquakes when they slip abruptly.</li><li>The fault type describes relative movement. It does not, by itself, supply the earthquake magnitude, hypocentral depth, or expected damage.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Other sources of seismic energy</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Explosive eruptions, magma pushing into rock, faulting beneath a heavy volcano, and collapse of unstable volcanic slopes can produce seismic signals. Distinctive volcanic tremors can accompany magma movement.</li><li>Large landslides shake the ground as rock breaks and moves. Underground explosions compress surrounding material and generate waves that seismic networks can detect.</li><li>Reservoir filling adds a load to the crust.</li><li>Deep wastewater injection can alter conditions on existing faults.</li><li>These are human-induced mechanisms, not evidence that every earthquake near a well or reservoir has the same cause.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-2-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Store strain before slip</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Stress is force per area; strain is the resulting change in shape. A locked fault can resist motion while nearby rock bends and stores elastic strain energy.</li><li>Elastic behavior means the material can return toward its former shape when the load is removed. Friction and rock strength help resist slip until the applied stress exceeds that resistance.</li><li>The earthquake-cycle graph rises gradually during loading and drops abruptly during slip. The graph is an explanatory model, not a calendar for predicting the next earthquake.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Rupture spreads across a fault</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rupture starts in a small area at the hypocenter and expands along a fault surface. The entire fault plane does not begin moving simultaneously.</li><li>If rupture reaches the ground surface, it can create a fault scarp or other surface break. A rupture may expand farther in one direction than the other.</li><li>Rupture stops when the remaining stress cannot overcome resistance farther along the fault. An earthquake does not have to break every part of the fault.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate rebound from permanent offset</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>After slip, some elastic deformation is recovered, but the displacement across the fault is permanent. An offset stream or displaced road does not return to its old alignment simply because the surrounding rock rebounds.</li><li>Repeated locking and slipping is stick-slip behavior. The recurrence interval is the average time between comparable repeated events, not a guaranteed waiting period.</li><li>Fresh scarps are sharp; erosion rounds their upper edges and deposits sediment at their bases. Scarp shape and dated deposits can help reconstruct earlier events.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Store strain before slip</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Stress is force per area.</li><li>Strain is the resulting change in shape.</li><li>A locked fault can resist motion while nearby rock bends and stores elastic strain energy.</li><li>Elastic behavior means the material can return toward its former shape when the load is removed. Friction and rock strength help resist slip until the applied stress exceeds that resistance.</li><li>The earthquake-cycle graph rises gradually during loading and drops abruptly during slip. The graph is an explanatory model, not a calendar for predicting the next earthquake.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Rupture spreads across a fault</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rupture starts in a small area at the hypocenter and expands along a fault surface. The entire fault plane does not begin moving simultaneously.</li><li>If rupture reaches the ground surface, it can create a fault scarp or other surface break. A rupture may expand farther in one direction than the other.</li><li>Rupture stops when the remaining stress cannot overcome resistance farther along the fault. An earthquake does not have to break every part of the fault.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate rebound from permanent offset</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>After slip, some elastic deformation is recovered, but the displacement across the fault is permanent. An offset stream or displaced road does not return to its old alignment simply because the surrounding rock rebounds.</li><li>Repeated locking and slipping is stick-slip behavior. The recurrence interval is the average time between comparable repeated events, not a guaranteed waiting period.</li><li>Fresh scarps are sharp.</li><li>Erosion rounds their upper edges and deposits sediment at their bases.</li><li>Scarp shape and dated deposits can help reconstruct earlier events.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-3-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use depth and tectonic position together</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Most earthquakes cluster along plate boundaries, but some occur within plates. A continental coast is not automatically a plate boundary: Atlantic passive margins differ from the active western margins of the Americas.</li><li>The supplied maps use yellow for 0–70 km, green for 70–300 km, and red for depths greater than 300 km. These colors are the source map legend, not the lab’s reading-emphasis categories.</li><li>Mid-ocean ridges have shallow earthquakes. Subduction zones combine shallow, intermediate, and deep earthquakes along a descending slab.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Infer the direction of subduction</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Start near the trench and follow the sequence from shallow to deeper earthquakes. That depth progression traces the inclined slab; the deepest dots do not mark the trench.</li><li>Near Japan the supplied pattern deepens westward, so the Pacific slab descends toward the west. Beneath western South America the pattern deepens eastward, so the slab descends beneath the continent.</li><li>A map plots epicenters at the surface and uses color to encode hypocentral depth. A cross section makes the inclined earthquake zone easier to see.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish the major regional settings</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Mediterranean-to-Asia belt includes continental collision and other convergence. Eastern Africa has a diffuse rift-related seismic zone. Low-seismicity interiors separate many active belts.</li><li>The Aleutians mark subduction south of Alaska; the San Andreas marks continental transform motion. Hawaii illustrates volcanic and associated seismic activity within a plate.</li><li>Shallow seismicity follows the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. Broad ocean interiors and passive margins generally have less seismicity, which does not mean zero possible earthquakes.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use depth and tectonic position together</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Most earthquakes cluster along plate boundaries, but some occur within plates.</li><li>A continental coast is not automatically a plate boundary<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Atlantic passive margins differ from the active western margins of the Americas.</li></ul></li><li>The supplied maps use yellow for 0–70 km, green for 70–300 km, and red for depths greater than 300 km. These colors are the source map legend, not the lab’s reading-emphasis categories.</li><li>Mid-ocean ridges have shallow earthquakes. Subduction zones combine shallow, intermediate, and deep earthquakes along a descending slab.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Infer the direction of subduction</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Start near the trench and follow the sequence from shallow to deeper earthquakes.</li><li>That depth progression traces the inclined slab.</li><li>The deepest dots do not mark the trench.</li><li>Near Japan the supplied pattern deepens westward, so the Pacific slab descends toward the west. Beneath western South America the pattern deepens eastward, so the slab descends beneath the continent.</li><li>A map plots epicenters at the surface and uses color to encode hypocentral depth. A cross section makes the inclined earthquake zone easier to see.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish the major regional settings</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Mediterranean-to-Asia belt includes continental collision and other convergence. Eastern Africa has a diffuse rift-related seismic zone. Low-seismicity interiors separate many active belts.</li><li>The Aleutians mark subduction south of Alaska.</li><li>The San Andreas marks continental transform motion.</li><li>Hawaii illustrates volcanic and associated seismic activity within a plate.</li><li>Shallow seismicity follows the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. Broad ocean interiors and passive margins generally have less seismicity, which does not mean zero possible earthquakes.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-4-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Spreading ridges and oceanic transforms</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>New oceanic lithosphere is hot and thin. Only its relatively shallow, cool part behaves brittlely, so ridge earthquakes are generally shallow and small to moderate.</li><li>Normal faulting and magma entering fissures generate earthquakes along the ridge axis. Strike-slip motion occurs on transform faults connecting adjacent ridge segments.</li><li>As a plate moves away from the ridge, cooling, subsidence, and bending can activate faults away from the axis. Not every ridge-system earthquake lies on the spreading axis.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Subduction produces several earthquake sources</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Bending before the trench can fracture the approaching plate. Thrust faults in the accretionary prism accommodate scraped-off material.</li><li>The megathrust is the plate-contact thrust fault. Large slip and vertical seafloor displacement can produce destructive tsunamis; not every subduction earthquake produces one.</li><li>The cold descending slab supports intermediate and deep earthquakes, approaching 700 km in the supplied chapter. This deep slab seismicity is distinct from a shallow megathrust rupture. The overriding plate can also have thrusting, magma movement, and volcanic seismicity.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Collisions and continental interiors</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continental collision spreads deformation across a broad zone. Thrusting near the boundary and reactivation of faults inside either plate can produce earthquakes; strike-slip and normal motion can also accommodate regional deformation.</li><li>Continental rifts commonly generate normal-fault earthquakes. Continental transform faults generate mostly shallow strike-slip earthquakes, sometimes large ones.</li><li>Magma intrusion and renewed stress on old faults provide intraplate mechanisms. New Madrid illustrates that distance from a modern boundary does not make a continent’s interior immune.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Spreading ridges and oceanic transforms</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>New oceanic lithosphere is hot and thin. Only its relatively shallow, cool part behaves brittlely, so ridge earthquakes are generally shallow and small to moderate.</li><li>Normal faulting and magma entering fissures generate earthquakes along the ridge axis. Strike-slip motion occurs on transform faults connecting adjacent ridge segments.</li><li>As a plate moves away from the ridge, cooling, subsidence, and bending can activate faults away from the axis. Not every ridge-system earthquake lies on the spreading axis.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Subduction produces several earthquake sources</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Bending before the trench can fracture the approaching plate. Thrust faults in the accretionary prism accommodate scraped-off material.</li><li>The megathrust is the plate-contact thrust fault.</li><li>Large slip and vertical seafloor displacement can produce destructive tsunamis.</li><li>Not every subduction earthquake produces one.</li><li>The cold descending slab supports intermediate and deep earthquakes, approaching 700 km in the supplied chapter. This deep slab seismicity is distinct from a shallow megathrust rupture. The overriding plate can also have thrusting, magma movement, and volcanic seismicity.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Collisions and continental interiors</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Continental collision spreads deformation across a broad zone.</li><li>Thrusting near the boundary and reactivation of faults inside either plate can produce earthquakes.</li><li>Strike-slip and normal motion can also accommodate regional deformation.</li><li>Continental rifts commonly generate normal-fault earthquakes. Continental transform faults generate mostly shallow strike-slip earthquakes, sometimes large ones.</li><li>Magma intrusion and renewed stress on old faults provide intraplate mechanisms. New Madrid illustrates that distance from a modern boundary does not make a continent’s interior immune.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-5-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate wave travel from particle motion</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A seismic wave transfers energy through material. Individual particles oscillate near their positions; they do not travel all the way from the earthquake to a recording station.</li><li>P-waves are compressional body waves. Particle motion is parallel to wave propagation, and these waves travel through solids and liquids. They arrive before S-waves along comparable paths.</li><li>S-waves are shear body waves. Particle motion is perpendicular to propagation. They travel through solids but cannot propagate as shear waves through liquid.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the two surface waves</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Love waves produce horizontal side-to-side motion perpendicular to their travel direction. Rayleigh waves produce rolling, elliptical motion with vertical and horizontal components.</li><li>Both are surface-wave types, distinct from P and S body waves. Surface waves can cause destructive building motion; structural response also depends on wave period and building properties.</li><li>Amplitude is the size of the displacement. Period is the time for one full cycle. Resonance can intensify shaking when the forcing period matches a structure’s natural response.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Record arrivals without confusing instrument and record</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A seismometer senses ground motion. In the mechanical illustration the frame moves with the ground while a suspended mass resists motion through inertia; their relative movement makes a record.</li><li>Three perpendicular components record north–south, east–west, and vertical motion. A seismogram is the resulting plot of ground motion against time, not the instrument itself.</li><li>In the supplied record, background vibrations precede P-wave arrival, then S-wave arrival, then larger surface-wave motion. Increasing source distance generally increases the P–S interval.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate wave travel from particle motion</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A seismic wave transfers energy through material.</li><li>Individual particles oscillate near their positions.</li><li>They do not travel all the way from the earthquake to a recording station.</li><li>P-waves are compressional body waves. Particle motion is parallel to wave propagation, and these waves travel through solids and liquids. They arrive before S-waves along comparable paths.</li><li>S-waves are shear body waves. Particle motion is perpendicular to propagation. They travel through solids but cannot propagate as shear waves through liquid.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the two surface waves</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Love waves produce horizontal side-to-side motion perpendicular to their travel direction. Rayleigh waves produce rolling, elliptical motion with vertical and horizontal components.</li><li>Both are surface-wave types, distinct from P and S body waves.</li><li>Surface waves can cause destructive building motion.</li><li>Structural response also depends on wave period and building properties.</li><li>Amplitude is the size of the displacement. Period is the time for one full cycle. Resonance can intensify shaking when the forcing period matches a structure’s natural response.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Record arrivals without confusing instrument and record</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A seismometer senses ground motion.</li><li>In the mechanical illustration the frame moves with the ground while a suspended mass resists motion through inertia.</li><li>Their relative movement makes a record.</li><li>Three perpendicular components record north–south, east–west, and vertical motion. A seismogram is the resulting plot of ground motion against time, not the instrument itself.</li><li>In the supplied record, background vibrations precede P-wave arrival, then S-wave arrival, then larger surface-wave motion. Increasing source distance generally increases the P–S interval.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-6-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Turn arrival differences into distance</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Measure the interval between the first P and S arrivals on each seismogram. A larger P–S interval generally indicates a more distant source; use the supplied travel-time relation rather than inventing one universal speed.</li><li>In the example, ISCO has the shortest interval, DUG is intermediate, and WUAZ has the longest. The plotted distance is a radius around a station, not a direction toward the source.</li><li>Draw distance circles around at least three stations. Their shared intersection locates the epicenter in the simplified exercise. Real networks use many records and more detailed models.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the local-magnitude nomograph</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Measure the maximum S-wave amplitude in the chapter’s local-magnitude exercise and use the distance inferred from the arrival interval. Connect distance and amplitude on the outer scales.</li><li>The line crosses the middle magnitude scale at the estimated local magnitude. Separate station measurements should converge on one event magnitude after accounting for distance.</li><li>A one-unit magnitude increase corresponds to ten times the ground-motion amplitude in this scale and <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"October 27 lecture: energy increases about 32 times per magnitude unit; checked against textbook 12.6\">approximately 32 times the energy</mark>. Two magnitude units correspond to roughly 1,000 times the energy, not 64.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep three measurements distinct</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Local magnitude uses an amplitude-and-distance relationship. Moment magnitude uses seismic moment, related to the area that slipped, amount of slip, and rigidity; it is useful for large earthquakes.</li><li>Modified Mercalli intensity describes observed shaking and damage at a place, using Roman numerals I–XII. Different sites can have different intensities during one earthquake.</li><li>Local ground conditions and building vulnerability influence damage. The site with the greatest damage is useful evidence, but damage alone does not identify the precise hypocenter or replace instrument data.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Turn arrival differences into distance</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Measure the interval between the first P and S arrivals on each seismogram.</li><li>A larger P–S interval generally indicates a more distant source.</li><li>Use the supplied travel-time relation rather than inventing one universal speed.</li><li>In the example, ISCO has the shortest interval, DUG is intermediate, and WUAZ has the longest. The plotted distance is a radius around a station, not a direction toward the source.</li><li>Draw distance circles around at least three stations. Their shared intersection locates the epicenter in the simplified exercise. Real networks use many records and more detailed models.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the local-magnitude nomograph</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Measure the maximum S-wave amplitude in the chapter’s local-magnitude exercise and use the distance inferred from the arrival interval. Connect distance and amplitude on the outer scales.</li><li>The line crosses the middle magnitude scale at the estimated local magnitude. Separate station measurements should converge on one event magnitude after accounting for distance.</li><li>A one-unit magnitude increase corresponds to ten times the ground-motion amplitude in this scale and <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"October 27 lecture: energy increases about 32 times per magnitude unit; checked against textbook 12.6\">approximately 32 times the energy</mark>. Two magnitude units correspond to roughly 1,000 times the energy, not 64.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep three measurements distinct</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Local magnitude uses an amplitude-and-distance relationship.</li><li>Moment magnitude uses seismic moment, related to the area that slipped, amount of slip, and rigidity.</li><li>It is useful for large earthquakes.</li><li>Modified Mercalli intensity describes observed shaking and damage at a place, using Roman numerals I–XII. Different sites can have different intensities during one earthquake.</li><li>Local ground conditions and building vulnerability influence damage. The site with the greatest damage is useful evidence, but damage alone does not identify the precise hypocenter or replace instrument data.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-7-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why the same earthquake causes different damage</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Ground motion can bend, shake, or collapse structures. Performance depends on design, connections, materials, and the local shaking, not simply whether a building is made of one named material.</li><li>Loose sediment can amplify motion relative to firm bedrock. A structure’s natural period can also interact with the shaking, so proximity to a fault alone does not explain every damaged building.</li><li>Ground rupture can offset roads and foundations. On steep slopes, shaking can trigger landslides and rock falls that threaten sites beyond the immediate fault trace.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Liquefaction and aftershocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Liquefaction occurs when shaking causes loose, water-saturated sediment to lose much of its strength. Buildings can settle or tilt as their support fails; the sediment has not melted into magma.</li><li>Aftershocks follow stress changes caused by the main earthquake. They can damage structures already weakened by the first shock, even when their magnitudes are smaller.</li><li>Both foundation conditions and structural condition matter after the main shock. A building that remains standing is not automatically undamaged or safe.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Damage can continue after shaking</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Broken gas lines can feed fires; broken water mains can hinder firefighting. Dam failures and land subsidence can cause flooding. Underwater displacement can generate a tsunami.</li><li>Hazard maps, stronger construction, base isolation, secured utilities, and emergency planning can reduce losses. They cannot make an area completely free of earthquake risk.</li><li>Current safety guidance: Drop, Cover, and Hold On under sturdy nearby furniture when possible. The supplied course material includes doorway advice; current USGS guidance rejects the doorway as the preferred refuge. Avoid open flames when gas lines may be damaged.</li></ul></section><p class=\"small\"><a href=\"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/earthquake-hazards/earthquake-facts-earthquake-fantasy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Current USGS guidance: Drop, Cover, and Hold On</a></p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Why the same earthquake causes different damage</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Ground motion can bend, shake, or collapse structures. Performance depends on design, connections, materials, and the local shaking, not simply whether a building is made of one named material.</li><li>Loose sediment can amplify motion relative to firm bedrock. A structure’s natural period can also interact with the shaking, so proximity to a fault alone does not explain every damaged building.</li><li>Ground rupture can offset roads and foundations. On steep slopes, shaking can trigger landslides and rock falls that threaten sites beyond the immediate fault trace.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Liquefaction and aftershocks</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Liquefaction occurs when shaking causes loose, water-saturated sediment to lose much of its strength.</li><li>Buildings can settle or tilt as their support fails.</li><li>The sediment has not melted into magma.</li><li>Aftershocks follow stress changes caused by the main earthquake. They can damage structures already weakened by the first shock, even when their magnitudes are smaller.</li><li>Both foundation conditions and structural condition matter after the main shock. A building that remains standing is not automatically undamaged or safe.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Damage can continue after shaking</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Broken gas lines can feed fires.</li><li>Broken water mains can hinder firefighting.</li><li>Dam failures and land subsidence can cause flooding.</li><li>Underwater displacement can generate a tsunami.</li><li>Hazard maps, stronger construction, base isolation, secured utilities, and emergency planning can reduce losses. They cannot make an area completely free of earthquake risk.</li><li>Current safety guidance<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Drop, Cover, and Hold On under sturdy nearby furniture when possible.</li></ul></li><li>The supplied course material includes doorway advice.</li><li>Current USGS guidance rejects the doorway as the preferred refuge.</li><li>Avoid open flames when gas lines may be damaged.</li></ul></section><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><a href=\"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/earthquake-hazards/earthquake-facts-earthquake-fantasy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Current USGS guidance</a><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><a href=\"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/earthquake-hazards/earthquake-facts-earthquake-fantasy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Drop, Cover, and Hold On</a>.</li></ul></li></ul>"},"g12-8-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare tectonic mechanisms</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Alaska in 1964 involved subduction-related thrusting and major tsunami damage. San Francisco in 1906 involved the San Andreas strike-slip system, with shaking and extensive fires.</li><li>The 1994 Northridge event involved a buried thrust fault. A damaging fault can remain hidden below the surface; absence of an obvious exposed trace is not proof of safety.</li><li>The 1959 Hebgen Lake event near Yellowstone involved normal faulting. The Madison Canyon landslide buried part of the valley and formed Earthquake Lake; do not relabel the fault mechanism as a volcanic eruption.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Local geology changes the result</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The 1985 Mexico earthquake originated in a coastal subduction setting. Mexico City suffered severe damage far from the source because lake sediments and basin geometry amplified the shaking.</li><li>The event source and the damaged city are different locations. A city need not sit directly on the ruptured plate boundary to experience destructive shaking.</li><li>San Francisco’s fires and Mexico City’s sediment response illustrate different pathways from one earthquake to widespread destruction. Magnitude alone does not determine the final losses.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Intraplate earthquakes still matter</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The 1811–1812 New Madrid sequence reactivated an ancient zone of weakness within North America. Modern stress can act on old structures far from a present plate boundary.</li><li>Charleston experienced a damaging earthquake in 1886. The supplied chapter treats its exact tectonic cause as debated; do not turn that uncertainty into a settled fault mechanism.</li><li>Use the historical examples to connect setting, ground conditions, and damage. Death tolls, estimated magnitudes, and rupture lengths differ among records; the lab prioritizes the supported mechanisms over disputed exact totals.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare tectonic mechanisms</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Alaska in 1964 involved subduction-related thrusting and major tsunami damage. San Francisco in 1906 involved the San Andreas strike-slip system, with shaking and extensive fires.</li><li>The 1994 Northridge event involved a buried thrust fault.</li><li>A damaging fault can remain hidden below the surface.</li><li>Absence of an obvious exposed trace is not proof of safety.</li><li>The 1959 Hebgen Lake event near Yellowstone involved normal faulting.</li><li>The Madison Canyon landslide buried part of the valley and formed Earthquake Lake.</li><li>Do not relabel the fault mechanism as a volcanic eruption.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Local geology changes the result</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The 1985 Mexico earthquake originated in a coastal subduction setting. Mexico City suffered severe damage far from the source because lake sediments and basin geometry amplified the shaking.</li><li>The event source and the damaged city are different locations. A city need not sit directly on the ruptured plate boundary to experience destructive shaking.</li><li>San Francisco’s fires and Mexico City’s sediment response illustrate different pathways from one earthquake to widespread destruction. Magnitude alone does not determine the final losses.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Intraplate earthquakes still matter</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The 1811–1812 New Madrid sequence reactivated an ancient zone of weakness within North America. Modern stress can act on old structures far from a present plate boundary.</li><li>Charleston experienced a damaging earthquake in 1886. The supplied chapter treats its exact tectonic cause as debated.</li><li>Do not turn that uncertainty into a settled fault mechanism.</li><li>Use the historical examples to connect setting, ground conditions, and damage.</li><li>Death tolls, estimated magnitudes, and rupture lengths differ among records.</li><li>The lab prioritizes the supported mechanisms over disputed exact totals.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-9-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Tohoku: megathrust slip and tsunami</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The March 2011 Tohoku earthquake occurred on the subduction interface offshore of northeastern Japan. A large rupture displaced the seafloor and generated a destructive tsunami.</li><li>The tsunami inundated coastal communities and damaged power and cooling systems at Fukushima. This links a geological event to cascading infrastructure failure.</li><li>Aftershocks outline part of the affected fault region and can damage weakened structures. A tsunami forecast map shows modeled wave amplitudes, not a direct map of earthquake magnitudes.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Haiti and Turkey–Syria: fault networks and vulnerable communities</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The 2010 Haiti earthquake occurred near a complex plate-boundary fault system with strike-slip and thrust motion. Severe building collapse caused major losses; “near a transform boundary” does not prove that every fault slipped purely horizontally.</li><li>The 2023 Turkey–Syria sequence occurred in a region of interacting faults and plates. Major strike-slip ruptures and numerous aftershocks affected a densely settled area.</li><li>Building vulnerability, population exposure, and disrupted services help explain losses. A smaller magnitude can cause catastrophic damage when the rupture is close to vulnerable communities.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate the event from the published estimate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The textbook and lecture use historical estimates of magnitude, deaths, and damage. Those estimates should be tied to their source date rather than presented as a permanent leaderboard.</li><li>The supplied October 27 lecture also compares New Zealand events. The current textbook section instead emphasizes Turkey–Syria; these are different source selections, not grounds to silently rename one event as another.</li><li>For comparison practice, identify the tectonic setting, the immediate physical hazard, and the factors that turn that hazard into losses.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Tohoku: megathrust slip and tsunami</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The March 2011 Tohoku earthquake occurred on the subduction interface offshore of northeastern Japan. A large rupture displaced the seafloor and generated a destructive tsunami.</li><li>The tsunami inundated coastal communities and damaged power and cooling systems at Fukushima. This links a geological event to cascading infrastructure failure.</li><li>Aftershocks outline part of the affected fault region and can damage weakened structures. A tsunami forecast map shows modeled wave amplitudes, not a direct map of earthquake magnitudes.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Haiti and Turkey–Syria: fault networks and vulnerable communities</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The 2010 Haiti earthquake occurred near a complex plate-boundary fault system with strike-slip and thrust motion.</li><li>Severe building collapse caused major losses.</li><li>“Near a transform boundary” does not prove that every fault slipped purely horizontally.</li><li>The 2023 Turkey–Syria sequence occurred in a region of interacting faults and plates. Major strike-slip ruptures and numerous aftershocks affected a densely settled area.</li><li>Building vulnerability, population exposure, and disrupted services help explain losses. A smaller magnitude can cause catastrophic damage when the rupture is close to vulnerable communities.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate the event from the published estimate</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The textbook and lecture use historical estimates of magnitude, deaths, and damage. Those estimates should be tied to their source date rather than presented as a permanent leaderboard.</li><li>The supplied October 27 lecture also compares New Zealand events.</li><li>The current textbook section instead emphasizes Turkey–Syria.</li><li>These are different source selections, not grounds to silently rename one event as another.</li><li>For comparison practice, identify the tectonic setting, the immediate physical hazard, and the factors that turn that hazard into losses.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-10-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Displace the water column</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A tsunami begins when a disturbance rapidly moves a large volume of water. Vertical seafloor displacement during megathrust rupture is a major mechanism.</li><li>Before rupture, a locked interface lets the overriding plate deform. When it slips, the seafloor can change shape abruptly and displace the overlying water.</li><li>Landslides entering water or moving underwater can also generate tsunamis. Volcanic explosions and collapse, as in the Krakatau example, can displace water without requiring a megathrust.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow the wave from deep ocean to shore</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In deep water, a tsunami can travel very fast with a long wavelength and modest height. It affects a much deeper water column than an ordinary wind wave.</li><li>In shallow water it slows, its wavelength shortens, and its height can increase. Coastal shape and bathymetry influence the eventual runup and flooding.</li><li>A tsunami is often a series of surges and strong currents, not one picturesque breaking wave. An initial withdrawal of the sea can occur, but not every tsunami begins with visible withdrawal.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Damage and warning depend on time and place</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The 1960 Chile event produced a tsunami that crossed the Pacific and damaged Hawaii and Japan. A community far from the earthquake can still face a tsunami.</li><li>Coastal inundation can destroy buildings, move boats inland, and carry debris. Large landslide-generated local waves can also arrive very quickly.</li><li>Warning systems combine earthquake information with sea-level and ocean-bottom measurements. Detection and forecasting support warnings; they do not predict the exact earthquake before it begins.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the historical tsunami examples</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chile, 1960: Local South American tsunami soon after the great earthquake.</li><li>Hawaii, 1960: Distant arrival across the Pacific from the Chile earthquake.</li><li>Hokkaido, 1993: Rapid local tsunami after a Japanese offshore earthquake.</li><li>Papua New Guinea, 1998: Earthquake-associated submarine landslides generated destructive waves.</li><li>Travel time depends on distance and water depth. A near-source tsunami may arrive before a distant warning system can help.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the 2004 Sumatra example</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The December 2004 Sumatra earthquake ruptured a subduction boundary. Seafloor displacement initiated a tsunami that spread across the Indian Ocean.</li><li>Separate the local source process from the distant consequence: traveling water waves can damage shores far beyond the strongest earthquake shaking.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Displace the water column</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A tsunami begins when a disturbance rapidly moves a large volume of water. Vertical seafloor displacement during megathrust rupture is a major mechanism.</li><li>Before rupture, a locked interface lets the overriding plate deform. When it slips, the seafloor can change shape abruptly and displace the overlying water.</li><li>Landslides entering water or moving underwater can also generate tsunamis. Volcanic explosions and collapse, as in the Krakatau example, can displace water without requiring a megathrust.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow the wave from deep ocean to shore</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In deep water, a tsunami can travel very fast with a long wavelength and modest height. It affects a much deeper water column than an ordinary wind wave.</li><li>In shallow water it slows, its wavelength shortens, and its height can increase. Coastal shape and bathymetry influence the eventual runup and flooding.</li><li>A tsunami is often a series of surges and strong currents, not one picturesque breaking wave. An initial withdrawal of the sea can occur, but not every tsunami begins with visible withdrawal.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Damage and warning depend on time and place</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The 1960 Chile event produced a tsunami that crossed the Pacific and damaged Hawaii and Japan. A community far from the earthquake can still face a tsunami.</li><li>Coastal inundation can destroy buildings, move boats inland, and carry debris. Large landslide-generated local waves can also arrive very quickly.</li><li>Warning systems combine earthquake information with sea-level and ocean-bottom measurements.</li><li>Detection and forecasting support warnings.</li><li>They do not predict the exact earthquake before it begins.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the historical tsunami examples</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chile, 1960<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Local South American tsunami soon after the great earthquake.</li></ul></li><li>Hawaii, 1960<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Distant arrival across the Pacific from the Chile earthquake.</li></ul></li><li>Hokkaido, 1993<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Rapid local tsunami after a Japanese offshore earthquake.</li></ul></li><li>Papua New Guinea, 1998<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Earthquake-associated submarine landslides generated destructive waves.</li></ul></li><li>Travel time depends on distance and water depth. A near-source tsunami may arrive before a distant warning system can help.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the 2004 Sumatra example</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The December 2004 Sumatra earthquake ruptured a subduction boundary. Seafloor displacement initiated a tsunami that spread across the Indian Ocean.</li><li>Separate the local source process from the distant consequence<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Traveling water waves can damage shores far beyond the strongest earthquake shaking.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"g12-11-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Measure the surface record</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Map the location, orientation, and extent of a rupture. Measure displaced streams, roads, fences, and rock units to determine movement direction and amount.</li><li>A single-event offset differs from cumulative displacement across many earthquakes. Match the same feature across the fault before calculating its displacement.</li><li>Scarps, linear valleys, ridges, ponds, and diverted drainage can reveal faults. An isolated linear feature is a clue that needs corroboration, not proof by itself.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct events before written records</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A trench across a fault exposes displaced sediment and soil layers. Older layers can preserve several events while younger layers record fewer.</li><li>Dated charcoal or peat can bracket deformation. Date the relevant material and interpret its relationship to the fault; flowing stream water cannot date the last fault movement.</li><li>The North Anatolian example distinguishes a 1.59 m component of 1999 scarp displacement from a larger cumulative scarp. Much motion was horizontal, so scarp height alone is not total slip.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe deformation remotely and at depth</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Before-and-after satellite radar measurements can form an interferogram. Colored fringes represent measured ground displacement along the satellite’s viewing direction; they are not rock colors or a direct stress map.</li><li>Laser surveying and repeated surface measurements detect deformation. Instruments in a borehole through an active fault can record conditions at depth.</li><li>Combining field relations, dating, and geophysical measurements constrains history and recurrence. It does not establish the exact time of the next earthquake.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Measure the surface record</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Map the location, orientation, and extent of a rupture. Measure displaced streams, roads, fences, and rock units to determine movement direction and amount.</li><li>A single-event offset differs from cumulative displacement across many earthquakes. Match the same feature across the fault before calculating its displacement.</li><li>Scarps, linear valleys, ridges, ponds, and diverted drainage can reveal faults. An isolated linear feature is a clue that needs corroboration, not proof by itself.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct events before written records</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A trench across a fault exposes displaced sediment and soil layers. Older layers can preserve several events while younger layers record fewer.</li><li>Dated charcoal or peat can bracket deformation.</li><li>Date the relevant material and interpret its relationship to the fault.</li><li>Flowing stream water cannot date the last fault movement.</li><li>The North Anatolian example distinguishes a 1.59 m component of 1999 scarp displacement from a larger cumulative scarp. Much motion was horizontal, so scarp height alone is not total slip.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Observe deformation remotely and at depth</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Before-and-after satellite radar measurements can form an interferogram.</li><li>Colored fringes represent measured ground displacement along the satellite’s viewing direction.</li><li>They are not rock colors or a direct stress map.</li><li>Laser surveying and repeated surface measurements detect deformation. Instruments in a borehole through an active fault can record conditions at depth.</li><li>Combining field relations, dating, and geophysical measurements constrains history and recurrence. It does not establish the exact time of the next earthquake.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-12-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Forecast from several kinds of evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Long-term forecasts combine historical earthquake records, prehistoric fault evidence, and tectonic setting. Recurrence and potential event sizes help estimate hazard over an interval.</li><li>Hazard is generally high near many active plate boundaries, including subduction zones and collision belts. New Madrid and Charleston illustrate that some intraplate areas also have important hazard.</li><li>A hazard map describes an expected shaking measure or probability over a defined time. Read its legend and date; a historical forecast map is not a newly issued forecast.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Interpret seismic gaps cautiously</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A seismic gap is a segment with less earthquake activity than neighboring segments or than expected from its history. In the chapter example, some quiet segments were interpreted as locked and accumulating strain.</li><li>Quiet does not automatically mean safe. Nor does a gap establish exactly when, where, and how large the next earthquake will be.</li><li>A creeping segment and a locked segment can behave differently. Combine deformation measurements with earthquake history rather than treating a lack of dots as a complete explanation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish a forecast, prediction, and warning</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Parkfield exercise used repeated events to anticipate another earthquake during a specified window; the eventual 2004 event occurred later. An average interval is not a clock.</li><li>Monitoring small motions, tilt, or possible foreshocks can inform research, but there is no reliable method for predicting a specific earthquake’s exact time, location, and magnitude.</li><li>An early warning detects an earthquake that has already begun and can alert places before stronger shaking arrives. It is different from predicting the event before rupture starts.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the country examples</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chile: Oceanic subduction beneath South America.</li><li>Iran: Collision involving the Arabian plate.</li><li>Nepal: India–Asia continental collision.</li><li>Papua New Guinea: Complex active plate boundaries.</li><li>Different boundary geometries produce hazard; national borders are not fault boundaries.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Forecast from several kinds of evidence</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Long-term forecasts combine historical earthquake records, prehistoric fault evidence, and tectonic setting. Recurrence and potential event sizes help estimate hazard over an interval.</li><li>Hazard is generally high near many active plate boundaries, including subduction zones and collision belts. New Madrid and Charleston illustrate that some intraplate areas also have important hazard.</li><li>A hazard map describes an expected shaking measure or probability over a defined time.</li><li>Read its legend and date.</li><li>A historical forecast map is not a newly issued forecast.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Interpret seismic gaps cautiously</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A seismic gap is a segment with less earthquake activity than neighboring segments or than expected from its history. In the chapter example, some quiet segments were interpreted as locked and accumulating strain.</li><li>Quiet does not automatically mean safe. Nor does a gap establish exactly when, where, and how large the next earthquake will be.</li><li>A creeping segment and a locked segment can behave differently. Combine deformation measurements with earthquake history rather than treating a lack of dots as a complete explanation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish a forecast, prediction, and warning</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Parkfield exercise used repeated events to anticipate another earthquake during a specified window.</li><li>The eventual 2004 event occurred later.</li><li>An average interval is not a clock.</li><li>Monitoring small motions, tilt, or possible foreshocks can inform research, but there is no reliable method for predicting a specific earthquake’s exact time, location, and magnitude.</li><li>An early warning detects an earthquake that has already begun and can alert places before stronger shaking arrives. It is different from predicting the event before rupture starts.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the country examples</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Chile<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Oceanic subduction beneath South America.</li></ul></li><li>Iran<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Collision involving the Arabian plate.</li></ul></li><li>Nepal<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>India–Asia continental collision.</li></ul></li><li>Papua New Guinea<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Complex active plate boundaries.</li></ul></li><li>Different boundary geometries produce hazard.</li><li>National borders are not fault boundaries.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-13-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">One fault system, different behavior</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The San Andreas system accommodates relative motion between the Pacific and North American plates. Its segments differ in creeping behavior, locking, and earthquake history.</li><li>The northern segment includes the 1906 rupture; the Loma Prieta earthquake affected part of the broader system in 1989. Nearby faults can also produce damaging earthquakes.</li><li>The central creeping segment moves gradually at the surface. Creep does not establish that every depth or adjacent fault is incapable of earthquake rupture.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use history without treating it as a deadline</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Parkfield has been studied because of repeated moderate earthquakes. The southern segment associated with the 1857 Fort Tejon event is an important example of long-term locking.</li><li>Historical probability statements require their original date and time window. The textbook’s rolling “next 30 years” phrase is not a current forecast simply because it appears on this page.</li><li>A restraining bend in the southern system produces compression and thrusting. This helps explain why thrust-fault earthquakes can occur within a region famous for strike-slip motion.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize evidence in the landscape</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Look for offset streams, linear valleys and ridges, scarps, ponds, and drainages aligned with the fault. Match the aerial photograph with the named features in the source illustration.</li><li>Trenches and displaced rock units can reveal movement over different timescales. An abandoned stream channel can preserve an earlier alignment.</li><li>The East California Shear Zone contains related active faults east of the San Andreas. Hazard assessment must include the network, not just the best-known named fault.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">One fault system, different behavior</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The San Andreas system accommodates relative motion between the Pacific and North American plates. Its segments differ in creeping behavior, locking, and earthquake history.</li><li>The northern segment includes the 1906 rupture.</li><li>The Loma Prieta earthquake affected part of the broader system in 1989. Nearby faults can also produce damaging earthquakes.</li><li>The central creeping segment moves gradually at the surface. Creep does not establish that every depth or adjacent fault is incapable of earthquake rupture.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use history without treating it as a deadline</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Parkfield has been studied because of repeated moderate earthquakes. The southern segment associated with the 1857 Fort Tejon event is an important example of long-term locking.</li><li>Historical probability statements require their original date and time window. The textbook’s rolling “next 30 years” phrase is not a current forecast simply because it appears on this page.</li><li>A restraining bend in the southern system produces compression and thrusting. This helps explain why thrust-fault earthquakes can occur within a region famous for strike-slip motion.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize evidence in the landscape</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Look for offset streams, linear valleys and ridges, scarps, ponds, and drainages aligned with the fault. Match the aerial photograph with the named features in the source illustration.</li><li>Trenches and displaced rock units can reveal movement over different timescales. An abandoned stream channel can preserve an earlier alignment.</li><li>The East California Shear Zone contains related active faults east of the San Andreas. Hazard assessment must include the network, not just the best-known named fault.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-14-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Start with samples and exposures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Volcanic inclusions can bring pieces of older rocks toward the surface. Uplift and erosion can expose rocks that once formed at depth.</li><li>Mines expose rocks along excavated workings. Drill cores recover cylindrical samples along boreholes. These are direct observations with limited spatial coverage, not a complete view of Earth’s interior.</li><li>Surface outcrops and mine material can offer accessible evidence of subsurface rock types. Check where a sample came from before using it to reconstruct its original position.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Measure physical properties indirectly</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Seismic-reflection surveys send energy into the ground and record returning signals with geophones. Reflections can reveal boundaries, folds, faults, and layered geometry.</li><li>Magnetic surveys measure variations in magnetic field strength related to rock properties. Gravity surveys measure small gravitational variations related to density contrasts.</li><li>Electrical surveys measure conductivity or resistivity. Water-bearing material and clay can influence electrical response; a conductive anomaly does not uniquely identify one rock type.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Combine methods to test an interpretation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The buried-channel example combines low-density sediment, relatively low magnetism, and a conductivity pattern consistent with wetter material. Each measurement constrains a different property.</li><li>A seismic profile is processed data; a colored geologic cross section is an interpretation based on those data and other evidence. They are not the same kind of observation.</li><li>Several subsurface configurations may fit one data set. Combining samples, geology, and multiple surveys helps reduce ambiguity.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the field-method vocabulary</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Scientists who use physical measurements to study Earth: Geophysicists.</li><li>Instruments detecting returning seismic vibrations: Geophones.</li><li>Instruments measuring small gravity variations: Gravity meters.</li><li>Cylindrical samples recovered by drilling: Drill cores.</li><li>Older fragments enclosed within another rock: Inclusions.</li><li>A sample is direct rock evidence; a geophysical measurement constrains an interpretation.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Start with samples and exposures</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Volcanic inclusions can bring pieces of older rocks toward the surface. Uplift and erosion can expose rocks that once formed at depth.</li><li>Mines expose rocks along excavated workings. Drill cores recover cylindrical samples along boreholes. These are direct observations with limited spatial coverage, not a complete view of Earth’s interior.</li><li>Surface outcrops and mine material can offer accessible evidence of subsurface rock types. Check where a sample came from before using it to reconstruct its original position.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Measure physical properties indirectly</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Seismic-reflection surveys send energy into the ground and record returning signals with geophones. Reflections can reveal boundaries, folds, faults, and layered geometry.</li><li>Magnetic surveys measure variations in magnetic field strength related to rock properties. Gravity surveys measure small gravitational variations related to density contrasts.</li><li>Electrical surveys measure conductivity or resistivity.</li><li>Water-bearing material and clay can influence electrical response.</li><li>A conductive anomaly does not uniquely identify one rock type.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Combine methods to test an interpretation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The buried-channel example combines low-density sediment, relatively low magnetism, and a conductivity pattern consistent with wetter material. Each measurement constrains a different property.</li><li>A seismic profile is processed data.</li><li>A colored geologic cross section is an interpretation based on those data and other evidence.</li><li>They are not the same kind of observation.</li><li>Several subsurface configurations may fit one data set. Combining samples, geology, and multiple surveys helps reduce ambiguity.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the field-method vocabulary</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Scientists who use physical measurements to study Earth<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Geophysicists.</li></ul></li><li>Instruments detecting returning seismic vibrations<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Geophones.</li></ul></li><li>Instruments measuring small gravity variations<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Gravity meters.</li></ul></li><li>Cylindrical samples recovered by drilling<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Drill cores.</li></ul></li><li>Older fragments enclosed within another rock<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Inclusions.</li></ul></li><li>A sample is direct rock evidence.</li><li>A geophysical measurement constrains an interpretation.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-15-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reflect or refract at a boundary</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Reflection sends part of the wave energy back from an interface. Refraction changes the direction of transmitted energy when the wave enters material with a different seismic velocity.</li><li>For the source’s downward-traveling ray, entering faster material bends the ray toward a shallower angle to a horizontal interface. Entering slower material bends it closer to the interface normal.</li><li>Wave speed depends on elastic properties and density together. A general increase in mantle velocity with depth is not a rule that density alone always makes every wave faster.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the short path with the fast path</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Near an earthquake, the shorter path through crust can produce the first arrival. Farther away, a path that enters faster mantle can arrive before the slower crustal path despite its greater length.</li><li>The distance where the first arrival changes constrains crustal thickness when combined with velocities and a model. The Moho is the crust–mantle boundary.</li><li>A reflected travel time includes the trip down and back up. For the textbook’s idealized 6 km/s crust, a 10-second vertical round trip corresponds to 30 km depth, not 60 km.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the shadow zones</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>P-waves refract strongly at the core–mantle boundary, leaving a zone without direct P arrivals. A P-wave shadow is not evidence that P-waves cannot travel through liquid.</li><li>Direct S-waves do not cross the liquid outer core. The broad S-wave shadow, together with other observations, supports a molten outer core.</li><li>Reflected and converted waves can take indirect paths. Keep direct-wave shadow claims separate from the claim that no seismic energy of any kind could ever be detected there.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reflect or refract at a boundary</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Reflection sends part of the wave energy back from an interface. Refraction changes the direction of transmitted energy when the wave enters material with a different seismic velocity.</li><li>For the source’s downward-traveling ray, entering faster material bends the ray toward a shallower angle to a horizontal interface. Entering slower material bends it closer to the interface normal.</li><li>Wave speed depends on elastic properties and density together. A general increase in mantle velocity with depth is not a rule that density alone always makes every wave faster.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the short path with the fast path</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Near an earthquake, the shorter path through crust can produce the first arrival. Farther away, a path that enters faster mantle can arrive before the slower crustal path despite its greater length.</li><li>The distance where the first arrival changes constrains crustal thickness when combined with velocities and a model. The Moho is the crust–mantle boundary.</li><li>A reflected travel time includes the trip down and back up. For the textbook’s idealized 6 km/s crust, a 10-second vertical round trip corresponds to 30 km depth, not 60 km.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the shadow zones</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>P-waves refract strongly at the core–mantle boundary, leaving a zone without direct P arrivals. A P-wave shadow is not evidence that P-waves cannot travel through liquid.</li><li>Direct S-waves do not cross the liquid outer core. The broad S-wave shadow, together with other observations, supports a molten outer core.</li><li>Reflected and converted waves can take indirect paths. Keep direct-wave shadow claims separate from the claim that no seismic energy of any kind could ever be detected there.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-16-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Combine rocks, experiments, and calculations</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Exhumed metamorphic rocks preserve minerals and structures formed at depth. Laboratory experiments test how rock responds to pressure and temperature.</li><li>Numerical models explore possible flow and wave paths under stated physical assumptions. Models are tested with observations; they are not direct photographs of inaccessible depths.</li><li>Together these approaches constrain conditions and processes that a surface exposure alone cannot reveal.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build a velocity image from crossing paths</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Seismic tomography combines many rays passing through a region from different directions. Earlier-than-expected arrivals can indicate faster material; later arrivals can indicate slower material.</li><li>In the supplied diagram, the granite body is a faster region relative to surrounding sedimentary rocks. Rays that sample it can arrive earlier than predicted by a uniform-background model.</li><li>The red and blue colors encode relative velocity in this particular figure. They are not literal rock colors, and they are not the lab’s reading-emphasis colors. Sparse ray coverage limits resolution.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate observations from deep-flow interpretations</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Slower regions can be consistent with hotter material; faster regions can reflect cooler or stiffer material. Composition and other physical properties also matter, so velocity alone is not a unique thermometer.</li><li>The chapter presents models with cool subducted slabs sinking and hot, mostly solid material rising. It discusses the D-double-prime region near the core–mantle boundary and possible links to plumes.</li><li>The mantle’s long-term flow does not require it to be wholly liquid. The source treats some deep-flow interpretations as debated and does not place every mid-ocean ridge above a deep plume.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Combine rocks, experiments, and calculations</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Exhumed metamorphic rocks preserve minerals and structures formed at depth. Laboratory experiments test how rock responds to pressure and temperature.</li><li>Numerical models explore possible flow and wave paths under stated physical assumptions.</li><li>Models are tested with observations.</li><li>They are not direct photographs of inaccessible depths.</li><li>Together these approaches constrain conditions and processes that a surface exposure alone cannot reveal.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build a velocity image from crossing paths</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Seismic tomography combines many rays passing through a region from different directions.</li><li>Earlier-than-expected arrivals can indicate faster material.</li><li>Later arrivals can indicate slower material.</li><li>In the supplied diagram, the granite body is a faster region relative to surrounding sedimentary rocks. Rays that sample it can arrive earlier than predicted by a uniform-background model.</li><li>The red and blue colors encode relative velocity in this particular figure. They are not literal rock colors, and they are not the lab’s reading-emphasis colors. Sparse ray coverage limits resolution.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate observations from deep-flow interpretations</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Slower regions can be consistent with hotter material.</li><li>Faster regions can reflect cooler or stiffer material.</li><li>Composition and other physical properties also matter, so velocity alone is not a unique thermometer.</li><li>The chapter presents models with cool subducted slabs sinking and hot, mostly solid material rising. It discusses the D-double-prime region near the core–mantle boundary and possible links to plumes.</li><li>The mantle’s long-term flow does not require it to be wholly liquid. The source treats some deep-flow interpretations as debated and does not place every mid-ocean ridge above a deep plume.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-17-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the rupture to the plate boundary</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The 1964 Alaska earthquake was a very large subduction-related event, reported as moment magnitude 9.2 in the course sources. It began beneath southern Alaska and ruptured a broad megathrust surface.</li><li>The downgoing Pacific plate moved beneath the overriding North American plate. The textbook cross section shows a gently dipping interface, not a continental rift or a purely strike-slip boundary.</li><li>The aftershock distribution helped constrain the large affected fault area. The felt region and the region of severe property damage were different sizes.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Trace effects on land and in water</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shaking damaged Anchorage and triggered major landslides. Weak ground and slope failure caused buildings and neighborhoods to settle or collapse.</li><li>Some coastal areas rose while others subsided. Uplift exposed marine organisms above their former tide level; subsidence allowed seawater to flood former dry land.</li><li>Seafloor displacement generated a Pacific tsunami, while local submarine landslides produced additional waves. Do not assume every local wave height represents the same source mechanism.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the observations to test a tectonic model</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Surveyors mapped uplift and subsidence rather than assuming a uniform elevation change. The coherent regional pattern supported thrusting at a convergent margin.</li><li>This work helped establish the subduction interpretation during the development of plate tectonics. The nearby volcanic arc provides an additional regional clue to subduction.</li><li>The map’s broad boundary of ground and ice cracking marks an affected region; it must not be read as one continuous fault fissure thousands of kilometers long.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect the rupture to the plate boundary</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The 1964 Alaska earthquake was a very large subduction-related event, reported as moment magnitude 9.2 in the course sources. It began beneath southern Alaska and ruptured a broad megathrust surface.</li><li>The downgoing Pacific plate moved beneath the overriding North American plate. The textbook cross section shows a gently dipping interface, not a continental rift or a purely strike-slip boundary.</li><li>The aftershock distribution helped constrain the large affected fault area. The felt region and the region of severe property damage were different sizes.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Trace effects on land and in water</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Shaking damaged Anchorage and triggered major landslides. Weak ground and slope failure caused buildings and neighborhoods to settle or collapse.</li><li>Some coastal areas rose while others subsided.</li><li>Uplift exposed marine organisms above their former tide level.</li><li>Subsidence allowed seawater to flood former dry land.</li><li>Seafloor displacement generated a Pacific tsunami, while local submarine landslides produced additional waves. Do not assume every local wave height represents the same source mechanism.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the observations to test a tectonic model</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Surveyors mapped uplift and subsidence rather than assuming a uniform elevation change. The coherent regional pattern supported thrusting at a convergent margin.</li><li>This work helped establish the subduction interpretation during the development of plate tectonics. The nearby volcanic arc provides an additional regional clue to subduction.</li><li>The map’s broad boundary of ground and ice cracking marks an affected region.</li><li>It must not be read as one continuous fault fissure thousands of kilometers long.</li></ul></section>"},"g12-18-a":{"source":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the three source records</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The investigation provides P–S intervals of 0.25 s at White Sands, 0.45 s at Hillside, and 0.78 s at Mesaview. White Sands is nearest; Mesaview is farthest.</li><li>Read each interval against this investigation’s own travel-time graph. It gives approximate distances of 2.5 km, 4.5 km, and 7.8 km respectively; these are source-specific graph readings, not a universal conversion.</li><li>Use the map scale to draw those radii around the three named stations. Their common region lies offshore near the trench, supporting the trench-related earthquake interpretation rather than Lava Mountain or Red Mesa.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect terrain and ground conditions to hazards</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sandpoint stands on reclaimed loose coastal material, so tsunami exposure and weak-ground behavior both matter. Riverton is low at the head of a sandy bay with gentle offshore slopes, creating substantial inundation concern.</li><li>Ashton lies on steep volcanic slopes with large blocks nearby; shaking can trigger slope failure. Cascade Village and Cliffside lie near fractured cliffs, and Cliffside also has soft, wet ground.</li><li>Mesaview is farther from the offshore source but sits between steep Red Mesa and Lava Mountain. Greater source distance can reduce shaking relative to nearer sites without eliminating slope hazards.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Make a relative recommendation with reasons</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Hillside occupies a higher inland setting with gentler adjacent slopes. In this scenario those features make it a defensible lower-hazard choice, not a guarantee of no shaking.</li><li>White Sands is behind a reef in the supplied scenario; that is the course’s relative comparison with exposed Sandpoint. A reef is not reliable real-world protection from a tsunami and does not justify staying on the coast.</li><li>Rank towns by the specified event and hazard, then cite elevation, distance, sediment, slope, and coastal geometry. The textbook allows reasoned alternatives for its overall safest/least-safe ranking; a single unlabeled risk score would conceal those assumptions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the investigation towns · 1</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sandpoint: Loose reclaimed coastal fill: shaking, liquefaction, and tsunami exposure.</li><li>Hillside: Higher inland ground with comparatively gentle terrain.</li><li>Ashton: Volcanic flank with blocks indicating slope failure.</li><li>Cascade: Fractured Gray Cliffs above town: rockfall exposure.</li><li>Distance, slope, substrate, and water exposure are separate controls; the least of one hazard is not the least of every hazard.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the investigation towns · 2</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Cliffside: Cliff instability together with soft coastal ground.</li><li>Riverton: Low bay beside a gently sloping seafloor: tsunami exposure.</li><li>White Sands: Reef changes the course comparison but does not guarantee protection.</li><li>Mesaview: Farther from the trench, yet beside steep slopes.</li><li>Distance, slope, substrate, and water exposure are separate controls; the least of one hazard is not the least of every hazard.</li></ul></section>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the three source records</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The investigation provides P–S intervals of 0.25 s at White Sands, 0.45 s at Hillside, and 0.78 s at Mesaview.</li><li>White Sands is nearest.</li><li>Mesaview is farthest.</li><li>Read each interval against this investigation’s own travel-time graph.</li><li>It gives approximate distances of 2.5 km, 4.5 km, and 7.8 km respectively.</li><li>These are source-specific graph readings, not a universal conversion.</li><li>Use the map scale to draw those radii around the three named stations. Their common region lies offshore near the trench, supporting the trench-related earthquake interpretation rather than Lava Mountain or Red Mesa.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect terrain and ground conditions to hazards</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sandpoint stands on reclaimed loose coastal material, so tsunami exposure and weak-ground behavior both matter. Riverton is low at the head of a sandy bay with gentle offshore slopes, creating substantial inundation concern.</li><li>Ashton lies on steep volcanic slopes with large blocks nearby.</li><li>Shaking can trigger slope failure.</li><li>Cascade Village and Cliffside lie near fractured cliffs, and Cliffside also has soft, wet ground.</li><li>Mesaview is farther from the offshore source but sits between steep Red Mesa and Lava Mountain. Greater source distance can reduce shaking relative to nearer sites without eliminating slope hazards.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Make a relative recommendation with reasons</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Hillside occupies a higher inland setting with gentler adjacent slopes. In this scenario those features make it a defensible lower-hazard choice, not a guarantee of no shaking.</li><li>White Sands is behind a reef in the supplied scenario.</li><li>That is the course’s relative comparison with exposed Sandpoint.</li><li>A reef is not reliable real-world protection from a tsunami and does not justify staying on the coast.</li><li>Rank towns by the specified event and hazard, then cite elevation, distance, sediment, slope, and coastal geometry.</li><li>The textbook allows reasoned alternatives for its overall safest/least-safe ranking.</li><li>A single unlabeled risk score would conceal those assumptions.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the investigation towns · 1</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sandpoint<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Loose reclaimed coastal fill</li><li>Shaking, liquefaction, and tsunami exposure.</li></ul></li><li>Hillside<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Higher inland ground with comparatively gentle terrain.</li></ul></li><li>Ashton<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Volcanic flank with blocks indicating slope failure.</li></ul></li><li>Cascade<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Fractured Gray Cliffs above town</li><li>Rockfall exposure.</li></ul></li><li>Distance, slope, substrate, and water exposure are separate controls.</li><li>The least of one hazard is not the least of every hazard.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare the investigation towns · 2</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Cliffside<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Cliff instability together with soft coastal ground.</li></ul></li><li>Riverton<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Low bay beside a gently sloping seafloor</li><li>Tsunami exposure.</li></ul></li><li>White Sands<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Reef changes the course comparison but does not guarantee protection.</li></ul></li><li>Mesaview<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Farther from the trench, yet beside steep slopes.</li></ul></li><li>Distance, slope, substrate, and water exposure are separate controls.</li><li>The least of one hazard is not the least of every hazard.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T1":{"source":"<p><b>Igneous</b> rock forms when magma or lava cools and crystallizes. <b>Sedimentary</b> rock forms from sediment through deposition and lithification (compaction and cementation), or from material precipitated from water. <b>Metamorphic</b> rock is changed by heat, pressure, and fluids while remaining solid.</p><p>Practice the verbs: weathering breaks rock down; erosion removes it; transport moves it; deposition drops it. Burial can compact sediment or lead to metamorphism. Melting produces magma; cooling produces igneous rock. Uplift exposes rock to surface processes.</p><p><b>There is no mandatory starting point or single loop.</b> Granite can become metamorphic directly, or weather into sediment. Sedimentary rock can be buried and eventually melt.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Rock cycle and its many paths</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Igneous</mark> rock forms when magma or lava cools and crystallizes.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Sedimentary</mark> rock forms from sediment through deposition and lithification (compaction and cementation), or from material precipitated from water.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Metamorphic</mark> rock is changed by heat, pressure, and fluids while remaining solid.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Practice the verbs<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Weathering breaks rock down.</li><li>Erosion removes it.</li><li>Transport moves it.</li><li>Deposition drops it.</li></ul></li><li>Burial can compact sediment or lead to metamorphism.</li><li>Melting produces magma.</li><li>Cooling produces igneous rock.</li><li>Uplift exposes rock to surface processes.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>There is no mandatory starting point or single loop.</b></li><li>Granite can become metamorphic directly, or weather into sediment.</li><li>Sedimentary rock can be buried and eventually melt.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T2":{"source":"<p>Oldest → youngest: <b>Precambrian → Paleozoic → Mesozoic → Cenozoic.</b> These are the four broad blocks used in this course; Precambrian spans more than one formal eon. Earth is about <b>4.5 billion years</b> old.</p><p>Attach one landmark to each: most of Earth history; early diverse life on land and sea; dinosaurs; recent mammal-rich time. The review emphasizes the major blocks and their order, without requiring every period name.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Major divisions of geologic time</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Oldest → youngest<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Precambrian → Paleozoic → Mesozoic → Cenozoic.</b></li></ul></li><li>These are the four broad blocks used in this course.</li><li>Precambrian spans more than one formal eon.</li><li>Earth is about <b>4.5 billion years</b> old.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Attach one landmark to each<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Most of Earth history.</li><li>Early diverse life on land and sea.</li><li>Dinosaurs.</li><li>Recent mammal-rich time.</li></ul></li><li>The review emphasizes the major blocks and their order, without requiring every period name.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T3":{"source":"<p>Wegener linked continental fits, fossils, rocks, and past climates, but lacked a convincing driving mechanism. Seafloor spreading supplied a testable process: new basalt forms at a ridge and records magnetic polarity as it cools. Matching reversal patterns on both sides support spreading.</p><p>Away from an undisturbed ridge, oceanic lithosphere is generally <b>older, cooler, denser, thicker, and lower</b>. The mantle part of the lithosphere thickens as it cools. Sediment cover also generally thickens with age.</p><p><b>Keep three thicknesses separate:</b> oceanic crust stays roughly similar in thickness; cooling mantle lithosphere thickens; sediment can accumulate on top.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Drift, spreading, stripes, and oceanic plate aging</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wegener linked continental fits, fossils, rocks, and past climates, but lacked a convincing driving mechanism.</li><li>Seafloor spreading supplied a testable process<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>New basalt forms at a ridge and records magnetic polarity as it cools.</li></ul></li><li>Matching reversal patterns on both sides support spreading.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Away from an undisturbed ridge, oceanic lithosphere is generally <b>older, cooler, denser, thicker, and lower</b>.</li><li>The mantle part of the lithosphere thickens as it cools.</li><li>Sediment cover also generally thickens with age.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><b>Keep three thicknesses separate</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Oceanic crust stays roughly similar in thickness.</li><li>Cooling mantle lithosphere thickens.</li><li>Sediment can accumulate on top.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T4":{"source":"<table><tr><th>Relative motion</th><th>Boundary</th><th>Crustal budget</th></tr><tr><td>Apart</td><td>Divergent / constructive</td><td>Creates oceanic crust at ridges</td></tr><tr><td>Together</td><td>Convergent / destructive in subduction</td><td>Consumes oceanic lithosphere at trenches</td></tr><tr><td>Slide past</td><td>Transform / conservative</td><td>Neither creates nor consumes crust</td></tr></table><p>Count connected plate regions, not arrows, continents, or boundary lines. Relative motion determines boundary type. At a ridge, crust is added; at a subduction zone, oceanic lithosphere is consumed. Practice identifying these processes from the arrows and features shown.</p><p><b>Red Sea:</b> a young opening ocean basin. <b>Atlantic:</b> a mature spreading basin. <b>Pacific:</b> extensive subduction around its margins. This is a teaching comparison, not a single guaranteed future path for all oceans.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read plate maps and boundary types</h4><table><tr><th>Relative motion</th><th>Boundary</th><th>Crustal budget</th></tr><tr><td>Apart</td><td>Divergent / constructive</td><td>Creates oceanic crust at ridges</td></tr><tr><td>Together</td><td>Convergent / destructive in subduction</td><td>Consumes oceanic lithosphere at trenches</td></tr><tr><td>Slide past</td><td>Transform / conservative</td><td>Neither creates nor consumes crust</td></tr></table><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Count connected plate regions, not arrows, continents, or boundary lines.</li><li>Relative motion determines boundary type.</li><li>At a ridge, crust is added.</li><li>At a subduction zone, oceanic lithosphere is consumed.</li><li>Practice identifying these processes from the arrows and features shown.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Red Sea</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A young opening ocean basin.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Atlantic</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A mature spreading basin.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Pacific</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Extensive subduction around its margins.</li></ul></li><li>This is a teaching comparison, not a single guaranteed future path for all oceans.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T5":{"source":"<p><b>Composition:</b> crust, mantle, core. Oceanic crust is more mafic; continental crust is generally more felsic. The core is dominated by iron and nickel; its outer part is liquid and inner part solid.</p><p><b>Mechanical behavior:</b> the lithosphere is rigid crust plus uppermost mantle. The underlying asthenosphere is hotter, weak, and mostly solid; it can flow over geologic time. <b>LAB = lithosphere–asthenosphere boundary.</b> It is not the crust–mantle boundary.</p><p><b>Isostasy:</b> equally dense thicker blocks float higher and extend deeper; equally thick denser blocks sit lower. Thick continental crust helps explain the higher elevation of the Denver region compared with the Gulf Coast. The wooden-block analogy illustrates buoyancy, not a wholly liquid mantle.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Earth layers, the LAB, and isostasy</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Composition</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Crust, mantle, core.</li></ul></li><li>Oceanic crust is more mafic.</li><li>Continental crust is generally more felsic.</li><li>The core is dominated by iron and nickel.</li><li>Its outer part is liquid and inner part solid.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Mechanical behavior</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>The lithosphere is rigid crust plus uppermost mantle.</li></ul></li><li>The underlying asthenosphere is hotter, weak, and mostly solid.</li><li>It can flow over geologic time.</li><li><b>LAB = lithosphere–asthenosphere boundary.</b></li><li>It is not the crust–mantle boundary.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Isostasy</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Equally dense thicker blocks float higher and extend deeper.</li><li>Equally thick denser blocks sit lower.</li></ul></li><li>Thick continental crust helps explain the higher elevation of the Denver region compared with the Gulf Coast.</li><li>The wooden-block analogy illustrates buoyancy, not a wholly liquid mantle.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T6":{"source":"<p>A <b>mantle plume</b> is rising unusually hot mantle, mostly solid before melting. At shallower depths, decompression can produce magma. A moving plate over a relatively stationary hot spot can leave a chain that gets older away from the active center.</p><p><b>Hawaii</b> illustrates an oceanic hot spot and basaltic volcanism. <b>Yellowstone</b> illustrates a continental hot spot; hot mantle-derived magma can transfer heat to continental crust and generate more silica-rich melts.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Hot spots and mantle plumes</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A <mark class=\"pilot-term\">mantle plume</mark> is rising unusually hot mantle, mostly solid before melting.</li><li>At shallower depths, decompression can produce magma.</li><li>A moving plate over a relatively stationary hot spot can leave a chain that gets older away from the active center.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Hawaii</mark> illustrates an oceanic hot spot and basaltic volcanism.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Yellowstone</mark> illustrates a continental hot spot.</li><li>Hot mantle-derived magma can transfer heat to continental crust and generate more silica-rich melts.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T7":{"source":"<p>Read the axes first: temperature increases to the right; on these diagrams pressure/depth increases downward. The <b>solidus</b> marks the beginning of melting. Crossing into the melt-present field does not imply the entire rock is liquid.</p><p>A rightward path heats rock at similar pressure. An upward path reduces pressure at similar temperature. Water moves the solidus to a lower temperature, so melting can begin without heating the rock further.</p><h3>Compare one variable at a time</h3><p>In the A–D figure, A and B share pressure: higher temperature puts B on the liquid side. B and C share temperature: higher pressure keeps C on the solid side. A and C are both solid even though C is hotter, because pressure also increases. Heating does not guarantee melting when pressure changes too.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read a pressure–temperature melting diagram</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Read the axes first<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Temperature increases to the right.</li><li>On these diagrams pressure/depth increases downward.</li></ul></li><li>The <mark class=\"pilot-term\">solidus</mark> marks the beginning of melting.</li><li>Crossing into the melt-present field does not imply the entire rock is liquid.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A rightward path heats rock at similar pressure.</li><li>An upward path reduces pressure at similar temperature.</li><li>Water moves the solidus to a lower temperature, so melting can begin without heating the rock further.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare one variable at a time</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the A–D figure, A and B share pressure<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Higher temperature puts B on the liquid side.</li></ul></li><li>B and C share temperature<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Higher pressure keeps C on the solid side.</li></ul></li><li>A and C are both solid even though C is hotter, because pressure also increases.</li><li>Heating does not guarantee melting when pressure changes too.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T8":{"source":"<p><b>Coarse / phaneritic:</b> visible crystals, slower cooling. <b>Fine / aphanitic:</b> very small crystals, faster cooling. <b>Glassy:</b> cooling so fast crystals fail to grow. <b>Porphyritic:</b> large phenocrysts in a fine groundmass, commonly slow growth followed by faster cooling.</p><p><b>Pegmatitic:</b> exceptionally large crystals. The textbook emphasizes water-rich melt that allows efficient movement of atoms; “simply the slowest cooling” is an incomplete explanation.</p><h3>Use the setting to explain the texture</h3><p>In the original section 5.1B overview, point 5 represents slow cooling at depth and coarse crystals. The path associated with point 8 allows crystals to grow below ground before the remaining melt cools rapidly, producing a porphyritic texture. At point 9, water-rich magma helps atoms move and large pegmatite crystals grow. Name the texture, then give its cause.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Textures reveal cooling history</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Coarse / phaneritic</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Visible crystals, slower cooling.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Fine / aphanitic</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Very small crystals, faster cooling.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Glassy</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Cooling so fast crystals fail to grow.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Porphyritic</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Large phenocrysts in a fine groundmass, commonly slow growth followed by faster cooling.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Pegmatitic</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Exceptionally large crystals.</li></ul></li><li>The textbook emphasizes water-rich melt that allows efficient movement of atoms.</li><li>“Simply the slowest cooling” is an incomplete explanation.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the setting to explain the texture</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the original section 5.1B overview, point 5 represents slow cooling at depth and coarse crystals.</li><li>The path associated with point 8 allows crystals to grow below ground before the remaining melt cools rapidly, producing a porphyritic texture.</li><li>At point 9, water-rich magma helps atoms move and large pegmatite crystals grow.</li><li>Name the texture, then give its cause.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T9":{"source":"<p><b>Vesicles</b> are holes left by gas bubbles as pressure falls and lava solidifies. <b>Pumice</b> is highly vesicular, commonly light-colored, silica-rich volcanic glass; many pieces float. <b>Scoria</b> is commonly dark, mafic to intermediate, with thicker walls and fewer bubbles than pumice.</p><p>Glassy and vesicular describe different properties: a glass can contain vesicles, and a vesicular rock need not be pure glass.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Vesicles, pumice, and scoria</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Vesicles</mark> are holes left by gas bubbles as pressure falls and lava solidifies.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Pumice</mark> is highly vesicular, commonly light-colored, silica-rich volcanic glass.</li><li>Many pieces float.</li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Scoria</mark> is commonly dark, mafic to intermediate, with thicker walls and fewer bubbles than pumice.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Glassy and vesicular describe different properties<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>A glass can contain vesicles, and a vesicular rock need not be pure glass.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T10":{"source":"<p>Choose the composition column, then the texture row. Coarse/fine pairs: <b>granite/rhyolite</b> (felsic), <b>diorite/andesite</b> (intermediate), <b>gabbro/basalt</b> (mafic). <b>Peridotite</b> is coarse and ultramafic.</p><p>Felsic rocks commonly contain quartz and K-feldspar. Mafic rocks commonly contain pyroxene and Ca-rich plagioclase, sometimes olivine. Ultramafic rocks are rich in olivine and pyroxene. These mineral associations constrain the composition; crystal size then constrains the rock name.</p><h3>Read the mineral bands</h3><p>The lower panel shows which minerals occur across the composition groups. Quartz and potassium feldspar favor the felsic side; olivine favors the mafic/ultramafic side. Plagioclase spans a broad range, from sodium-rich toward the felsic side to calcium-rich toward the mafic side. Use the bands to support a classification, then use texture to choose the rock name. Exact percentages are not the goal.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Igneous composition × texture chart</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Choose the composition column, then the texture row.</li><li>Coarse/fine pairs<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Granite/rhyolite</mark> (felsic), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">diorite/andesite</mark> (intermediate), <mark class=\"pilot-term\">gabbro/basalt</mark> (mafic).</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Peridotite</mark> is coarse and ultramafic.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Felsic rocks commonly contain quartz and K-feldspar.</li><li>Mafic rocks commonly contain pyroxene and Ca-rich plagioclase, sometimes olivine.</li><li>Ultramafic rocks are rich in olivine and pyroxene.</li><li>These mineral associations constrain the composition.</li><li>Crystal size then constrains the rock name.</li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the mineral bands</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The lower panel shows which minerals occur across the composition groups.</li><li>Quartz and potassium feldspar favor the felsic side.</li><li>Olivine favors the mafic/ultramafic side.</li><li>Plagioclase spans a broad range, from sodium-rich toward the felsic side to calcium-rich toward the mafic side.</li><li>Use the bands to support a classification, then use texture to choose the rock name.</li><li>Exact percentages are not the goal.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T11":{"source":"<p>The eight common crustal elements: <b>O, Si, Al, Fe, Ca, Na, K, Mg</b>. Oxygen and silicon dominate. A silicate tetrahedron contains <b>one Si surrounded by four O</b>. Tetrahedra can occur independently or link into single chains, double chains, sheets, or frameworks.</p><p>Examples: olivine isolated; pyroxene single chains; amphibole double chains; mica sheets; quartz and feldspar frameworks. Non-silicate groups are named by their principal anion or anion group: <b>sulfide S²⁻, sulfate SO₄²⁻, carbonate CO₃²⁻, oxide O²⁻</b>.</p><p><b>Ionic:</b> attraction between charged ions. <b>Covalent:</b> shared electron pairs. <b>Metallic:</b> mobile shared electrons. <b>Van der Waals / molecular:</b> weaker attractions between units or layers.</p><h3>Structure explains what you can see</h3><p>Silicon–oxygen tetrahedra can share oxygen atoms to form chains, sheets, and frameworks. Mica has strong bonding within sheets and weaker bonds between sheets, so it cleaves into thin layers. Quartz has a well-bonded three-dimensional framework and no preferred cleavage planes. One isolated SiO₄ tetrahedron has a net charge of −4: +4 from silicon and −8 from four oxygen atoms.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Elements, silicates, anions, and bonds</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The eight common crustal elements<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>O, Si, Al, Fe, Ca, Na, K, Mg</b>.</li></ul></li><li>Oxygen and silicon dominate.</li><li>A silicate tetrahedron contains <b>one Si surrounded by four O</b>.</li><li>Tetrahedra can occur independently or link into single chains, double chains, sheets, or frameworks.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Examples<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Olivine isolated.</li><li>Pyroxene single chains.</li><li>Amphibole double chains.</li><li>Mica sheets.</li><li>Quartz and feldspar frameworks.</li></ul></li><li>Non-silicate groups are named by their principal anion or anion group<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Sulfide S²⁻, sulfate SO₄²⁻, carbonate CO₃²⁻, oxide O²⁻</b>.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Ionic</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Attraction between charged ions.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Covalent</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Shared electron pairs.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Metallic</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Mobile shared electrons.</li></ul></li><li><b>Van der Waals / molecular</b><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Weaker attractions between units or layers.</li></ul></li></ul></section><section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Structure explains what you can see</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Silicon–oxygen tetrahedra can share oxygen atoms to form chains, sheets, and frameworks.</li><li>Mica has strong bonding within sheets and weaker bonds between sheets, so it cleaves into thin layers.</li><li>Quartz has a well-bonded three-dimensional framework and no preferred cleavage planes.</li><li>One isolated SiO₄ tetrahedron has a net charge of −4<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>+4 From silicon and −8 from four oxygen atoms.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T12":{"source":"<p>Cooling, high → low temperature: <b>olivine → pyroxene → amphibole → biotite</b> in the discontinuous branch. Plagioclase changes from Ca-rich toward Na-rich in the continuous branch. K-feldspar, muscovite, and quartz are low-temperature minerals.</p><p>For the simplified course model, heating reverses the general order: lower-melting components enter the liquid first. A <b>partial melt is generally more silica-rich than its source</b>. That does not make every partial melt rhyolite: mantle peridotite can yield basalt, which is more silica-rich than its ultramafic source.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bowen’s series and partial melting</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Cooling, high → low temperature<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li><b>Olivine → pyroxene → amphibole → biotite</b> in the discontinuous branch.</li></ul></li><li>Plagioclase changes from Ca-rich toward Na-rich in the continuous branch.</li><li>K-feldspar, muscovite, and quartz are low-temperature minerals.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>For the simplified course model, heating reverses the general order<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Lower-melting components enter the liquid first.</li></ul></li><li>A <b>partial melt is generally more silica-rich than its source</b>.</li><li>That does not make every partial melt rhyolite<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Mantle peridotite can yield basalt, which is more silica-rich than its ultramafic source.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T13":{"source":"<p><b>Add heat:</b> hot magma transfers heat to surrounding rock. <b>Lower pressure:</b> rising mantle undergoes decompression beneath ridges and hot spots. <b>Add water/volatiles:</b> slab-derived water lowers the melting temperature of the overlying mantle in subduction zones.</p><p>Connect setting → process → graph. Ridge: upwelling, pressure decrease. Subduction: water released from minerals in the slab, lower solidus. Continental hot spot: mantle decompression plus heat transfer into crust can both matter.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Three melting mechanisms and tectonic settings</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Add heat</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Hot magma transfers heat to surrounding rock.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Lower pressure</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Rising mantle undergoes decompression beneath ridges and hot spots.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Add water/volatiles</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Slab-derived water lowers the melting temperature of the overlying mantle in subduction zones.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Connect setting → process → graph.</li><li>Ridge<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Upwelling, pressure decrease.</li></ul></li><li>Subduction<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Water released from minerals in the slab, lower solidus.</li></ul></li><li>Continental hot spot<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Mantle decompression plus heat transfer into crust can both matter.</li></ul></li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T14":{"source":"<p><b>Viscosity</b> is resistance to flow. More silica and lower temperature generally increase viscosity. Fluid basalt spreads to build broad <b>shield volcanoes</b>. More viscous magma and alternating eruption products build steeper <b>composite volcanoes</b> such as Mount Fuji and Mount St. Helens.</p><p><b>Cinder/scoria cones</b> are small, steep accumulations of loose fragments around a vent; their steep slopes do not mean the magma must be felsic. Gas escape, composition, temperature, and crystals all influence eruption behavior.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Viscosity and volcano shape</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Viscosity</mark> is resistance to flow.</li><li>More silica and lower temperature generally increase viscosity.</li><li>Fluid basalt spreads to build broad <mark class=\"pilot-term\">shield volcanoes</mark>.</li><li>More viscous magma and alternating eruption products build steeper <mark class=\"pilot-term\">composite volcanoes</mark> such as Mount Fuji and Mount St. Helens.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Cinder/scoria cones</mark> are small, steep accumulations of loose fragments around a vent.</li><li>Their steep slopes do not mean the magma must be felsic.</li><li>Gas escape, composition, temperature, and crystals all influence eruption behavior.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T15":{"source":"<p><b>Convection:</b> heat carried by moving material—warm, less dense material rises and cooler, denser material sinks. The boiling-pot circulation is the review analogy.</p><p><b>Conduction:</b> heat moves through material or across contact without bulk movement—magma heats its wall rock; heat passes through the pot wall. <b>Radiation:</b> heat carried by electromagnetic waves—hot lava emits thermal radiation.</p><p>Plume ascent carries heat by convection; transfer from magma into neighboring crust can occur by conduction. Distinguish heat transfer from the separate pressure decrease that causes decompression melting.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Convection, conduction, and radiation</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Convection</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Heat carried by moving material—warm, less dense material rises and cooler, denser material sinks.</li></ul></li><li>The boiling-pot circulation is the review analogy.</li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Conduction</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Heat moves through material or across contact without bulk movement—magma heats its wall rock.</li><li>Heat passes through the pot wall.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Radiation</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Heat carried by electromagnetic waves—hot lava emits thermal radiation.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Plume ascent carries heat by convection.</li><li>Transfer from magma into neighboring crust can occur by conduction.</li><li>Distinguish heat transfer from the separate pressure decrease that causes decompression melting.</li></ul></section>"},"exam1-T16":{"source":"<p><b>Ocean–ocean:</b> one oceanic plate subducts, commonly the older, colder, denser one; an island arc can form. <b>Ocean–continent:</b> denser oceanic lithosphere subducts beneath buoyant continental crust; a continental volcanic arc can form.</p><p><b>Continent–continent:</b> buoyant continental crust resists deep subduction; collision thickens crust and builds mountains. India–Asia and the Himalaya are the class example.</p>","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h4 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Convergent boundaries and density</h4><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Ocean–ocean</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>One oceanic plate subducts, commonly the older, colder, denser one.</li><li>An island arc can form.</li></ul></li><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Ocean–continent</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Denser oceanic lithosphere subducts beneath buoyant continental crust.</li><li>A continental volcanic arc can form.</li></ul></li></ul><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"pilot-term\">Continent–continent</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Buoyant continental crust resists deep subduction.</li><li>Collision thickens crust and builds mountains.</li></ul></li><li>India–Asia and the Himalaya are the class example.</li></ul></section>"}},"focus":{"1":{"source":"Transgression is the landward advance of marine conditions as relative sea level rises. Regression moves the shoreline seaward as relative sea level falls or sediment builds outward faster than accommodation is created. Track the shoreline relative to the land; do not confuse shoreline movement with the direction a wave travels.","trapSource":"Relative sea level includes movement of the land as well as the water. A vertical facies stack needs a fixed observation site and its original setting.","groups":[{"heading":"Track the shoreline","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Track the shoreline</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Transgression is the landward advance of marine conditions as relative sea level rises.</li><li>Regression moves the shoreline seaward as relative sea level falls or sediment builds outward faster than accommodation is created.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Transgression","Regression"],"definitions":["landward advance of marine conditions as relative sea level rises","moves the shoreline seaward as relative sea level falls or sediment builds outward faster than accommodation is created"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Regression moves","label":"Follow the shoreline"}],"limitPoints":["Relative sea level includes movement of the land as well as the water."]},{"heading":"Keep the reference frame clear","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep the reference frame clear</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Track the shoreline relative to the land.</li><li>Do not confuse shoreline movement with the direction a wave travels.</li></ul></section>","terms":["shoreline movement"],"definitions":["direction a wave travels"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Track the shoreline relative to the land","label":"Keep the reference frame"}],"limitPoints":["A vertical facies stack needs a fixed observation site and its original setting."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Relative sea level includes movement of the land as well as the water.</li><li>A vertical facies stack needs a fixed observation site and its original setting.</li></ul>","terms":["Transgression","Regression","relative sea level"],"summary":{"terms":["transgression/regression","shoreline migration"],"definitions":["sea-level change","shoreline direction","vertical sediment sequence"]}},"2":{"source":"Use size, sorting, roundness and setting together. Poor, moderate and good sorting describe a wide, intermediate or narrow spread of grain sizes. Angular, subangular, subrounded and rounded describe progressively smoother corners. Abrasion and collisions wear clasts; selective transport and deposition change the sizes remaining in motion. Rocky Mountain slope debris is commonly coarse and angular; longer transport toward the Mississippi delta commonly produces smaller, rounder, better-sorted sediment. Rivers rework sand and gravel; quiet lakes receive fines; deltas form at river mouths; the continental slope is offshore.","trapSource":"Sorting is not roundness. Neither a rounded clast nor high transport energy establishes one unique environment; local delta deposits can differ from the regional downstream trend.","groups":[{"heading":"Read grain size, sorting and roundness","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read grain size, sorting and roundness</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Use size, sorting, roundness and setting together.</li><li>Poor, moderate and good sorting describe a wide, intermediate or narrow spread of grain sizes.</li><li>Angular, subangular, subrounded and rounded describe progressively smoother corners.</li></ul></section>","terms":["sorting","Angular, subangular, subrounded and rounded"],"definitions":["wide, intermediate or narrow spread of grain sizes","progressively smoother corners"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Use size, sorting, roundness and setting together","label":"Combine the clues"}],"limitPoints":["Sorting is not roundness."]},{"heading":"Connect wear with transport","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect wear with transport</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Abrasion and collisions wear clasts.</li><li>Selective transport and deposition change the sizes remaining in motion.</li><li>Rocky Mountain slope debris is commonly coarse and angular.</li><li>Longer transport toward the Mississippi delta commonly produces smaller, rounder, better-sorted sediment.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Abrasion and collisions","Selective transport and deposition","Rocky Mountain slope debris","Mississippi delta"],"definitions":["wear clasts","change the sizes remaining in motion","commonly coarse and angular","commonly produces smaller, rounder, better-sorted sediment"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Longer transport toward the Mississippi delta","label":"Follow the transport trend"}],"limitPoints":["Local delta deposits can differ from the regional downstream trend."]},{"heading":"Compare depositional settings","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare depositional settings</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Rivers rework sand and gravel.</li><li>Quiet lakes receive fines.</li><li>Deltas form at river mouths.</li><li>The continental slope is offshore.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Rivers","Quiet lakes","Deltas","continental slope"],"definitions":["rework sand and gravel","receive fines","form at river mouths","offshore"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Deltas form at river mouths","label":"Connect place and process"}],"limitPoints":["Neither a rounded clast nor high transport energy establishes one unique environment."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Sorting is not roundness.</li><li>Neither a rounded clast nor high transport energy establishes one unique environment.</li><li>Local delta deposits can differ from the regional downstream trend.</li></ul>","terms":["clasts","delta","continental slope","Poorly sorted","Moderately sorted","Well-sorted","Angular","Subangular","Subrounded","rounded","abrasion","selective transport","deposition","Mississippi delta"],"summary":{"terms":["poor, moderate and good sorting","downstream change","sediment observations"],"definitions":["grain-size spread","using abrasion and selective transport/deposition","infer transport history cautiously"]}},"3":{"source":"Weathering breaks rock physically or alters/dissolves minerals chemically at its source. Erosion removes material; transport carries it; deposition leaves it behind. Burial can compact sediment, reducing pore space, while minerals precipitating between grains cement it into rock. The source may be igneous, sedimentary or metamorphic. This is a sedimentary pathway through a branching rock cycle; compaction and cementation can overlap.","trapSource":"Weathering does not itself mean downstream movement. Lithification does not require melting.","groups":[{"heading":"Follow the sedimentary pathway","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow the sedimentary pathway</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Weathering breaks rock physically or alters/dissolves minerals chemically at its source.</li><li>Erosion removes material.</li><li>Transport carries it.</li><li>Deposition leaves it behind.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Weathering","Erosion","Transport","Deposition"],"definitions":["breaks rock physically or alters/dissolves minerals chemically at its source","removes material","carries it","leaves it behind"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Weathering breaks rock","label":"Start at the source"}],"limitPoints":["Weathering does not itself mean downstream movement."]},{"heading":"Turn sediment into rock","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Turn sediment into rock</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Burial can compact sediment, reducing pore space, while minerals precipitating between grains cement it into rock.</li><li>The source may be igneous, sedimentary or metamorphic.</li><li>This is a sedimentary pathway through a branching rock cycle.</li><li>Compaction and cementation can overlap.</li></ul></section>","terms":["compact sediment","cement it into rock","Compaction and cementation"],"definitions":["reducing pore space","minerals precipitating between grains","can overlap"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Burial can compact sediment","label":"Follow lithification"}],"limitPoints":["Lithification does not require melting."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Weathering does not itself mean downstream movement.</li><li>Lithification does not require melting.</li></ul>","terms":["Weathering","Erosion","Compaction","cementation","lithification","Physical weathering","chemical weathering","deposition","burial"],"summary":{"terms":["lithification mechanisms","weathering, erosion, transport, deposition and burial"],"definitions":["buried sediment sample","Trace"]}},"6":{"source":"Gravel-sized clasts form conglomerate when rounded and breccia when angular. Sand forms sandstone; quartz sandstone is quartz rich, arkose contains abundant feldspar, and graywacke contains a mixed mineral and rock-fragment population. Silt forms siltstone. Clay-rich, finely splitting mudrock is shale; mudstone is the broader massive, nonfissile muddy rock description. Size is the primary clastic classification axis, with shape, composition and splitting refining the name.","trapSource":"Do not name every fine-grained rock shale or every coarse rock conglomerate.","groups":[{"heading":"Classify gravel and sand","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Classify gravel and sand</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Gravel-sized clasts form conglomerate when rounded and breccia when angular.</li><li>Sand forms sandstone.</li><li>Quartz sandstone is quartz rich, arkose contains abundant feldspar, and graywacke contains a mixed mineral and rock-fragment population.</li></ul></section>","terms":["conglomerate","breccia","sandstone","Quartz sandstone","arkose","graywacke"],"definitions":["Gravel-sized clasts","when rounded","when angular","Sand forms","quartz rich","contains abundant feldspar","contains a mixed mineral and rock-fragment population"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Gravel-sized clasts form conglomerate","label":"Use size and shape"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Distinguish the finer rocks","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish the finer rocks</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Silt forms siltstone.</li><li>Clay-rich, finely splitting mudrock is shale.</li><li>Mudstone is the broader massive, nonfissile muddy rock description.</li></ul></section>","terms":["siltstone","shale","Mudstone"],"definitions":["Silt forms","Clay-rich, finely splitting mudrock","broader massive, nonfissile muddy rock description"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Clay-rich, finely splitting mudrock is shale","label":"Check how it splits"}],"limitPoints":["Do not name every fine-grained rock shale or every coarse rock conglomerate."]},{"heading":"Choose the classification evidence","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Choose the classification evidence</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Size is the primary clastic classification axis, with shape, composition and splitting refining the name.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Size","shape, composition and splitting"],"definitions":["primary clastic classification axis","refining the name"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Size is the primary clastic classification axis","label":"Choose the evidence"}],"limitPoints":[]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Do not name every fine-grained rock shale or every coarse rock conglomerate.</li></ul>","terms":["conglomerate","breccia","sandstone","siltstone","shale","mudstone"],"summary":{"terms":["shale","mudstone","gravel, sand and silt"],"definitions":["fissile","nonfissile","rock names"]}},"7":{"source":"Cross-bedding consists of inclined internal layers left by migrating dunes or ripples. Both wind and flowing water can produce it: channel gravel and river geometry favor a water interpretation, while an arid dune setting supports wind. Oxidized iron minerals can coat grains or cement and give sedimentary rocks red/brown colors. Combine these observations with texture, fossils and the surrounding setting.","trapSource":"Cross-bedding alone does not prove a desert; red sandstone alone does not identify one depositional environment.","groups":[{"heading":"Interpret cross-bedding","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Interpret cross-bedding</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Cross-bedding consists of inclined internal layers left by migrating dunes or ripples.</li><li>Both wind and flowing water can produce it<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>Channel gravel and river geometry favor a water interpretation, while an arid dune setting supports wind.</li></ul></li></ul></section>","terms":["Cross-bedding","wind and flowing water"],"definitions":["inclined internal layers left by migrating dunes or ripples","can produce it"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Channel gravel and river geometry","label":"Use the surrounding setting"}],"limitPoints":["Cross-bedding alone does not prove a desert."]},{"heading":"Use color with other evidence","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use color with other evidence</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Oxidized iron minerals can coat grains or cement and give sedimentary rocks red/brown colors.</li><li>Combine these observations with texture, fossils and the surrounding setting.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Oxidized iron minerals"],"definitions":["coat grains or cement and give sedimentary rocks red/brown colors"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Combine these observations with texture, fossils and the surrounding setting","label":"Combine the evidence"}],"limitPoints":["Red sandstone alone does not identify one depositional environment."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Cross-bedding alone does not prove a desert.</li><li>Red sandstone alone does not identify one depositional environment.</li></ul>","terms":["Cross-bedding","bedform","lee side","Oxidation","iron oxides"],"summary":{"terms":["cross-bedding","red/brown iron-oxide color"],"definitions":["wind or water","interpret it using context","without overclaiming environment"]}},"11":{"source":"Many limestones form from calcium-carbonate skeletal material and carbonate sediment produced in warm, shallow marine environments. Reefs and carbonate platforms around the Bahamas and Florida Keys are useful examples. Organisms produce carbonate that can accumulate and later lithify into limestone. Calcium carbonate is the safe compositional description for the reef example; modern stony corals are not universally calcite.","trapSource":"A biochemical carbonate rock is not classified by the gravel/sand/mud scheme used for clastic rocks.","groups":[{"heading":"Produce and accumulate carbonate","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Produce and accumulate carbonate</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Many limestones form from calcium-carbonate skeletal material and carbonate sediment produced in warm, shallow marine environments.</li><li>Reefs and carbonate platforms around the Bahamas and Florida Keys are useful examples.</li><li>Organisms produce carbonate that can accumulate and later lithify into limestone.</li></ul></section>","terms":["limestones","Bahamas and Florida Keys","Organisms"],"definitions":["calcium-carbonate skeletal material and carbonate sediment produced in warm, shallow marine environments","Reefs and carbonate platforms","produce carbonate that can accumulate and later lithify into limestone"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Organisms produce carbonate","label":"Follow carbonate formation"}],"limitPoints":["A biochemical carbonate rock is not classified by the gravel/sand/mud scheme used for clastic rocks."]},{"heading":"Keep composition precise","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Keep composition precise</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Calcium carbonate is the safe compositional description for the reef example.</li><li>Modern stony corals are not universally calcite.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Calcium carbonate","Modern stony corals"],"definitions":["safe compositional description for the reef example","not universally calcite"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Modern stony corals are not universally calcite","label":"Keep composition precise"}],"limitPoints":[]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A biochemical carbonate rock is not classified by the gravel/sand/mud scheme used for clastic rocks.</li></ul>","terms":["limestone","calcium carbonate","carbonate platform"],"summary":{"terms":["biochemical carbonate production","calcium-carbonate skeletal sediment"],"definitions":["warm shallow marine settings","limestone precursor"]}},"12":{"source":"Metamorphism changes minerals or texture mainly in the solid state. Temperature, pressure, chemically active fluids and time influence new-mineral growth and recrystallization. Igneous, sedimentary and older metamorphic rocks can all be protoliths. Exhumation brings deeply formed rocks toward the surface as erosion removes overburden, often assisted by uplift and isostatic rebound.","trapSource":"A metamorphic rock exposed at the surface did not necessarily form there. Metamorphism does not require melting or a sedimentary parent.","groups":[{"heading":"Change rocks in the solid state","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Change rocks in the solid state</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Metamorphism changes minerals or texture mainly in the solid state.</li><li>Temperature, pressure, chemically active fluids and time influence new-mineral growth and recrystallization.</li><li>Igneous, sedimentary and older metamorphic rocks can all be protoliths.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Metamorphism","Temperature, pressure, chemically active fluids and time","protoliths"],"definitions":["changes minerals or texture mainly in the solid state","influence new-mineral growth and recrystallization","Igneous, sedimentary and older metamorphic rocks"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Igneous, sedimentary and older metamorphic rocks","label":"Recognize the possible parents"}],"limitPoints":["Metamorphism does not require melting or a sedimentary parent."]},{"heading":"Bring deeply formed rocks toward the surface","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Bring deeply formed rocks toward the surface</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Exhumation brings deeply formed rocks toward the surface as erosion removes overburden, often assisted by uplift and isostatic rebound.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Exhumation"],"definitions":["brings deeply formed rocks toward the surface as erosion removes overburden, often assisted by uplift and isostatic rebound"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Exhumation brings deeply formed rocks","label":"Follow the return to the surface"}],"limitPoints":["A metamorphic rock exposed at the surface did not necessarily form there."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A metamorphic rock exposed at the surface did not necessarily form there.</li><li>Metamorphism does not require melting or a sedimentary parent.</li></ul>","terms":["Metamorphism","Foliation","lineation","Exhumation","Temperature","pressure","fluids","time","protolith","recrystallize"],"summary":{"terms":["temperature, pressure, fluids and time","protoliths","exposure of deeply formed rocks"],"definitions":["metamorphic agents","solid-state mineral changes","through erosion and uplift/rebound"]}},"13":{"source":"The hypocenter (focus) is where rupture begins at depth; the epicenter is directly above it at the surface. On each seismogram identify P and S, then calculate tS − tP. Read the matching distance from the supplied travel-time graph. A longer interval means greater distance for the applicable paths. Center each distance circle on its station and use three circles to constrain the epicenter, the course method called triangulation. The lecture example places ISCO nearest, then DUG, then WUAZ.","trapSource":"Use the graph, not a universal seconds-to-kilometers multiplier. The schematic gives roughly 65, 540 and 670 km for ISCO, DUG and WUAZ. These surface circles alone do not determine focal depth.","groups":[{"heading":"Locate the rupture and surface point","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Locate the rupture and surface point</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The hypocenter (focus) is where rupture begins at depth.</li><li>The epicenter is directly above it at the surface.</li></ul></section>","terms":["hypocenter (focus)","epicenter"],"definitions":["where rupture begins at depth","directly above it at the surface"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"The hypocenter (focus)","label":"Distinguish depth and surface"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Read each seismogram","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read each seismogram</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On each seismogram identify P and S, then calculate tS − tP.</li><li>Read the matching distance from the supplied travel-time graph.</li><li>A longer interval means greater distance for the applicable paths.</li></ul></section>","terms":["seismogram","travel-time graph","longer interval"],"definitions":["identify P and S, then calculate tS − tP","Read the matching distance","greater distance for the applicable paths"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"A longer interval means","label":"Connect interval and distance"}],"limitPoints":["Use the graph, not a universal seconds-to-kilometers multiplier."]},{"heading":"Use the station circles","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the station circles</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Center each distance circle on its station and use three circles to constrain the epicenter, the course method called triangulation.</li><li>The lecture example places ISCO nearest, then DUG, then WUAZ.</li></ul></section>","terms":["triangulation","ISCO","DUG","WUAZ"],"definitions":["Center each distance circle on its station and use three circles to constrain the epicenter","nearest, then"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Center each distance circle on its station","label":"Use all three stations"}],"limitPoints":["The schematic gives roughly 65, 540 and 670 km for ISCO, DUG and WUAZ.","These surface circles alone do not determine focal depth."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Use the graph, not a universal seconds-to-kilometers multiplier.</li><li>The schematic gives roughly 65, 540 and 670 km for ISCO, DUG and WUAZ.</li><li>These surface circles alone do not determine focal depth.</li></ul>","terms":["seismogram","P wave","S wave","P–S interval","hypocenter","focus","epicenter","triangulation","radius","travel-time graph"],"summary":{"terms":["hypocenter","epicenter","S-arrival minus P-arrival interval","travel-time graph","station-centered distance circles"],"definitions":["subsurface","surface","positive","interval ordering to infer distance","constrain the epicenter"]}},"14":{"source":"Large earthquakes are less frequent than smaller earthquakes in a population of events. In a recognized sequence, the mainshock is the largest event; foreshocks precede it and aftershocks follow it. The 2004 Indonesia earthquake is the review’s large-event context. “Frequency” here means how often events occur, not the oscillation frequency of a seismic wave.","trapSource":"Foreshocks are identified retrospectively; the statement does not mean every mainshock has a reliable warning sequence. The exact magnitude–frequency graph was not located in the uploaded decks; do not substitute the Richter nomogram for this relationship.","groups":[{"heading":"Compare earthquake sizes and occurrence","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Compare earthquake sizes and occurrence</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Large earthquakes are less frequent than smaller earthquakes in a population of events.</li><li>In a recognized sequence, the mainshock is the largest event.</li><li>Foreshocks precede it and aftershocks follow it.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Large earthquakes","mainshock","Foreshocks","aftershocks"],"definitions":["less frequent than smaller earthquakes in a population of events","largest event","precede it","follow it","identified retrospectively","does not mean every mainshock has a reliable warning sequence"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Large earthquakes are less frequent","label":"Compare occurrence"},{"phrase":"Foreshocks precede it and aftershocks follow it","label":"Read the sequence"}],"limitPoints":["Foreshocks are identified retrospectively.","The statement does not mean every mainshock has a reliable warning sequence."]},{"heading":"Interpret the review example","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Interpret the review example</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The 2004 Indonesia earthquake is the review’s large-event context.</li><li>“Frequency” here means how often events occur, not the oscillation frequency of a seismic wave.</li></ul></section>","terms":["2004 Indonesia earthquake","Frequency","magnitude–frequency graph","Richter nomogram"],"definitions":["review’s large-event context","how often events occur, not the oscillation frequency of a seismic wave","was not located in the uploaded decks","Do not substitute"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"“Frequency” here means","label":"Keep the meaning of frequency"}],"limitPoints":["The exact magnitude–frequency graph was not located in the uploaded decks.","Do not substitute the Richter nomogram for this relationship."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Foreshocks are identified retrospectively.</li><li>The statement does not mean every mainshock has a reliable warning sequence.</li><li>The exact magnitude–frequency graph was not located in the uploaded decks.</li><li>Do not substitute the Richter nomogram for this relationship.</li></ul>","terms":["mainshock","foreshocks","aftershocks"],"summary":{"terms":["small events","large events","foreshock, mainshock and aftershock"],"definitions":["common","rarer","recognized sequence","without predicting a mainshock"]}},"15":{"source":"Elastic strain is recoverable deformation that stores energy. A locked fault can load while surrounding rock bends; sudden failure/slip releases some stored energy as seismic waves. On the lecture graph stress is vertical and time horizontal. Gradual rising ramps represent loading; sudden drops mark slip and energy release. Some energy also goes into heat and fracture work.","trapSource":"This is a stress–time model, not stress–strain. Its repeated teeth are not an exact recurrence forecast; energy release does not require melting.","groups":[{"heading":"Store and release elastic energy","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Store and release elastic energy</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Elastic strain is recoverable deformation that stores energy.</li><li>A locked fault can load while surrounding rock bends.</li><li>Sudden failure/slip releases some stored energy as seismic waves.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Elastic strain","locked fault","Sudden failure/slip"],"definitions":["recoverable deformation that stores energy","can load while surrounding rock bends","releases some stored energy as seismic waves"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"A locked fault can load","label":"Follow energy storage"}],"limitPoints":["Energy release does not require melting."]},{"heading":"Read the stress–time graph","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the stress–time graph</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On the lecture graph stress is vertical and time horizontal.</li><li>Gradual rising ramps represent loading.</li><li>Sudden drops mark slip and energy release.</li><li>Some energy also goes into heat and fracture work.</li></ul></section>","terms":["stress","time","Gradual rising ramps","Sudden drops"],"definitions":["vertical","horizontal","represent loading","mark slip and energy release"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Some energy also goes into heat and fracture work","label":"Account for the released energy"}],"limitPoints":["This is a stress–time model, not stress–strain.","Its repeated teeth are not an exact recurrence forecast."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>This is a stress–time model, not stress–strain.</li><li>Its repeated teeth are not an exact recurrence forecast.</li><li>Energy release does not require melting.</li></ul>","terms":["elastic deformation","elastic energy","seismic waves","stress","time","loading","fault slip","stress drop"],"summary":{"terms":["elastic strain energy","stress and time","gradual loading"],"definitions":["stored","release during fault slip","supplied graph","abrupt slip/stress release"]}},"16":{"source":"A Wadati–Benioff zone is an inclined distribution of earthquake hypocenters in a subducting slab. The course legend separates shallow (0–70 km), intermediate (70–300 km) and deep (over 300 km) events. Starting at the trench, follow increasing depth into the overriding side. The Nazca slab descends eastward beneath South America; the Pacific slab near Japan deepens generally westward.","trapSource":"Map dots mark epicenters at the surface while colors encode hypocenter depth. Read each region’s legend; do not memorize one universal subduction direction.","groups":[{"heading":"Recognize a dipping earthquake zone","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize a dipping earthquake zone</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A Wadati–Benioff zone is an inclined distribution of earthquake hypocenters in a subducting slab.</li><li>The course legend separates shallow (0–70 km), intermediate (70–300 km) and deep (over 300 km) events.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Wadati–Benioff zone","shallow","intermediate","deep"],"definitions":["inclined distribution of earthquake hypocenters in a subducting slab","0–70 km","70–300 km","over 300 km"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"The course legend separates shallow","label":"Read the depth categories"}],"limitPoints":["Map dots mark epicenters at the surface while colors encode hypocenter depth.","Read each region’s legend."]},{"heading":"Infer slab direction from depth","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Infer slab direction from depth</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Starting at the trench, follow increasing depth into the overriding side.</li><li>The Nazca slab descends eastward beneath South America.</li><li>The Pacific slab near Japan deepens generally westward.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Nazca slab","Pacific slab near Japan"],"definitions":["descends eastward beneath South America","deepens generally westward"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Starting at the trench, follow increasing depth into the overriding side","label":"Infer the slab direction"}],"limitPoints":["Do not memorize one universal subduction direction."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Map dots mark epicenters at the surface while colors encode hypocenter depth.</li><li>Read each region’s legend.</li><li>Do not memorize one universal subduction direction.</li></ul>","terms":["hypocenters","subduction","descending slab","Wadati–Benioff zone","slab dip","intermediate","deep"],"summary":{"terms":["earthquake depth colors","Wadati–Benioff seismic zone"],"definitions":["infer slab descent in the western Pacific","inclined"]}},"19":{"source":"Body waves travel through Earth: P waves involve compression/expansion; S waves involve shear. Love surface waves move particles horizontally across the propagation direction; Rayleigh waves produce rolling motion. P travels faster than S along comparable paths and arrives first; surface waves generally follow in the course record. Material and path affect the speeds.","trapSource":"Wave propagation and particle motion are different. Do not use a universal P:S speed ratio or fixed Love-versus-Rayleigh order.","groups":[{"heading":"Body waves","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Body waves</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Body waves travel through Earth<ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>P waves involve compression/expansion.</li><li>S waves involve shear.</li></ul></li></ul></section>","terms":["Body waves","P waves","S waves"],"definitions":["travel through Earth","involve compression/expansion","involve shear"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"P waves involve compression/expansion","label":"Compare particle motion"}],"limitPoints":["Wave propagation and particle motion are different."]},{"heading":"Surface waves","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Surface waves</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Love surface waves move particles horizontally across the propagation direction.</li><li>Rayleigh waves produce rolling motion.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Love surface waves","Rayleigh waves"],"definitions":["move particles horizontally across the propagation direction","produce rolling motion"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Love surface waves move particles","label":"Compare surface-wave motion"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Arrival order","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Arrival order</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>P travels faster than S along comparable paths and arrives first.</li><li>Surface waves generally follow in the course record.</li><li>Material and path affect the speeds.</li></ul></section>","terms":["P","S","Surface waves"],"definitions":["travels faster than S along comparable paths and arrives first","generally follow in the course record"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Material and path affect the speeds","label":"Keep the path conditions"}],"limitPoints":["Do not use a universal P:S speed ratio or fixed Love-versus-Rayleigh order."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Wave propagation and particle motion are different.</li><li>Do not use a universal P:S speed ratio or fixed Love-versus-Rayleigh order.</li></ul>","terms":["P waves","S waves","body waves","surface waves","Body waves","Love","Rayleigh"],"summary":{"terms":["P/S","Love/Rayleigh","P arrives before S"],"definitions":["body waves","surface waves","without assuming a fixed speed ratio"]}},"20":{"source":"Divergence commonly involves tension and normal faults; convergence compression with reverse/thrust faults or folds; transforms shear and strike-slip motion. Elastic strain is recoverable; brittle deformation breaks/slips; ductile deformation changes shape permanently without a discrete break. The hanging wall lies above an inclined fault plane, the footwall below. Hanging wall down is normal; up is reverse, with low-angle reverse called thrust. Horsts are relatively high and grabens relatively low fault-bounded blocks; both can occur in an extending region.","trapSource":"Use the plane and relative movement, not screen-left/right or topographic height. Temperature, pressure, material and deformation rate help determine brittle versus ductile behavior.","groups":[{"heading":"Connect stress to plate setting","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect stress to plate setting</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Divergence commonly involves tension and normal faults.</li><li>Convergence compression with reverse/thrust faults or folds.</li><li>Transforms shear and strike-slip motion.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Divergence","Convergence","Transforms"],"definitions":["commonly involves tension and normal faults","compression with reverse/thrust faults or folds","shear and strike-slip motion"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Divergence commonly involves tension","label":"Connect setting and motion"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Distinguish deformation behaviors","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish deformation behaviors</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Elastic strain is recoverable.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"October 1 and 6 lectures · textbook 8.1–8.2\">Brittle deformation breaks/slips</mark>.</li><li><mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"October 1 and 6 lectures · textbook 8.1–8.2\">Ductile deformation changes shape permanently without a discrete break</mark>.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Elastic strain","Brittle deformation","Ductile deformation"],"definitions":["recoverable","breaks/slips","changes shape permanently without a discrete break"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Elastic strain is recoverable","label":"Compare deformation behavior"}],"limitPoints":["Temperature, pressure, material and deformation rate help determine brittle versus ductile behavior."]},{"heading":"Identify fault blocks and movement","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Identify fault blocks and movement</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The hanging wall lies above an inclined fault plane, the footwall below.</li><li>Hanging wall down is normal.</li><li>Up is reverse, with low-angle reverse called thrust.</li><li>Horsts are relatively high and grabens relatively low fault-bounded blocks.</li><li>Both can occur in an extending region.</li></ul></section>","terms":["hanging wall","footwall","normal","reverse","thrust","Horsts","grabens"],"definitions":["above an inclined fault plane","below","Hanging wall down","Up is","low-angle reverse","relatively high","relatively low fault-bounded blocks"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Both can occur in an extending region","label":"Keep the tectonic setting"}],"limitPoints":["Use the plane and relative movement, not screen-left/right or topographic height."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Use the plane and relative movement, not screen-left/right or topographic height.</li><li>Temperature, pressure, material and deformation rate help determine brittle versus ductile behavior.</li></ul>","terms":["tension","compression","shear","normal faults","thrust faults","strike-slip","normal fault","thrust","graben","horst","Basin and Range","Brittle deformation","ductile deformation","hanging wall","footwall"],"summary":{"terms":["divergent, convergent and transform boundaries","elastic","ductile deformation","horsts/grabens"],"definitions":["dominant stresses","recoverable","permanent","extension despite local mountain relief"]}},"21":{"source":"In the typical shale-derived sequence, increasing grade gives slate → phyllite → schist → gneiss. Slate has fine dull cleavage; phyllite has a silky sheen; schist has visible aligned minerals; gneiss has compositional bands. Foliation is planar; lineation is linear. On the lecture chart, temperature increases rightward and pressure/depth downward. Shallow contact metamorphism can be hot at low pressure; cold subduction can reach high pressure without comparable heating.","trapSource":"This sequence depends on protolith. High pressure is not automatically high temperature or high grade; do not invent numerical grade boundaries.","groups":[{"heading":"Recognize grade and fabric","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize grade and fabric</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In the typical shale-derived sequence, increasing grade gives slate → phyllite → schist → gneiss.</li><li>Slate has fine dull cleavage.</li><li>Phyllite has a silky sheen.</li><li>Schist has visible aligned minerals.</li><li>Gneiss has compositional bands.</li><li>Foliation is planar.</li><li>Lineation is linear.</li></ul></section>","terms":["slate","phyllite","schist","gneiss","Foliation","Lineation"],"definitions":["fine dull cleavage","silky sheen","visible aligned minerals","compositional bands","planar","linear"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"In the typical shale-derived sequence","label":"Follow the grade sequence"}],"limitPoints":["This sequence depends on protolith."]},{"heading":"Read pressure and temperature","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read pressure and temperature</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>On the lecture chart, temperature increases rightward and pressure/depth downward.</li><li>Shallow contact metamorphism can be hot at low pressure.</li><li>Cold subduction can reach high pressure without comparable heating.</li></ul></section>","terms":["temperature","pressure/depth","Shallow contact metamorphism","Cold subduction"],"definitions":["increases rightward","downward","can be hot at low pressure","can reach high pressure without comparable heating"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"On the lecture chart","label":"Read both axes"}],"limitPoints":["High pressure is not automatically high temperature or high grade.","Do not invent numerical grade boundaries."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>This sequence depends on protolith.</li><li>High pressure is not automatically high temperature or high grade.</li><li>Do not invent numerical grade boundaries.</li></ul>","terms":["pressure","temperature","contact metamorphism","regional metamorphism","slate","phyllite","schist","gneiss"],"summary":{"terms":["slate, phyllite, schist and gneiss","foliation","lineation","P–T diagram"],"definitions":["grade in the shale-derived series","planar","linear","contact, regional and cold-subduction settings"]}},"27":{"source":"In an upright, non-overturned sequence an anticline arches and commonly exposes older rocks toward its core; a syncline forms a trough with younger rocks toward its core. A monocline is a step-like bend in otherwise more nearly horizontal layers. Folds commonly record shortening and ductile deformation. Erosion can reverse the topographic expression of an anticline or syncline.","trapSource":"An anticline need not be a modern hill and a syncline need not be a modern valley.","groups":[{"heading":"Distinguish fold shapes","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish fold shapes</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In an upright, non-overturned sequence an anticline arches and commonly exposes older rocks toward its core.</li><li>A syncline forms a trough with younger rocks toward its core.</li><li>A monocline is a step-like bend in otherwise more nearly horizontal layers.</li></ul></section>","terms":["anticline","syncline","monocline"],"definitions":["arches and commonly exposes older rocks toward its core","forms a trough with younger rocks toward its core","step-like bend in otherwise more nearly horizontal layers"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"In an upright, non-overturned sequence","label":"Check the sequence orientation"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Separate deformation from present topography","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate deformation from present topography</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Folds commonly record shortening and ductile deformation.</li><li>Erosion can reverse the topographic expression of an anticline or syncline.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Folds","Erosion"],"definitions":["commonly record shortening and ductile deformation","can reverse the topographic expression of an anticline or syncline"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Erosion can reverse","label":"Separate structure and landscape"}],"limitPoints":["An anticline need not be a modern hill and a syncline need not be a modern valley."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An anticline need not be a modern hill and a syncline need not be a modern valley.</li></ul>","terms":["anticline","syncline","monocline"],"summary":{"terms":["bed geometry and age relationships"],"definitions":["instead of present-day topography"]}},"28":{"source":"The assigned source gives Earth an age of about 4.55 billion years. Isotopic ages of meteorites, Moon samples and old Earth materials converge on an early solar-system history. Meteorites help constrain formation because Earth’s oldest record has been partly recycled by erosion, metamorphism and melting. The oldest surviving whole rock need not be as old as the planet.","trapSource":"Do not use a sediment’s depositional age or the age of a galaxy as Earth’s formation age.","groups":[{"heading":"Establish the age of Earth","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Establish the age of Earth</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The assigned source gives Earth an age of about 4.55 billion years.</li><li>Isotopic ages of meteorites, Moon samples and old Earth materials converge on an early solar-system history.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Earth","Isotopic ages"],"definitions":["about 4.55 billion years","meteorites, Moon samples and old Earth materials converge on an early solar-system history"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Isotopic ages of meteorites","label":"Combine independent age evidence"}],"limitPoints":["Do not use a sediment’s depositional age or the age of a galaxy as Earth’s formation age."]},{"heading":"Understand the surviving record","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Understand the surviving record</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Meteorites help constrain formation because Earth’s oldest record has been partly recycled by erosion, metamorphism and melting.</li><li>The oldest surviving whole rock need not be as old as the planet.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Meteorites","Earth’s oldest record","oldest surviving whole rock"],"definitions":["help constrain formation","partly recycled by erosion, metamorphism and melting","need not be as old as the planet"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"The oldest surviving whole rock","label":"Distinguish rock age and planet age"}],"limitPoints":[]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Do not use a sediment’s depositional age or the age of a galaxy as Earth’s formation age.</li></ul>","terms":["isotopic ages","meteorites","Moon","4.55 billion"],"summary":{"terms":["solar-system isotopic ages","rock-forming event"],"definitions":["age of Earth","Earth’s formation"]}},"29":{"source":"An unconformity represents missing geologic time from erosion, nondeposition or both. An angular unconformity has younger strata across tilted or folded older strata. A disconformity separates broadly parallel sedimentary strata. A nonconformity puts sedimentary rocks over eroded igneous or metamorphic basement. The geometry and material, not a wavy line alone, decide the category.","trapSource":"A fault cutting layers is a different relationship from a depositional surface over an eroded basement.","groups":[{"heading":"Recognize missing geologic time","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize missing geologic time</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An unconformity represents missing geologic time from erosion, nondeposition or both.</li></ul></section>","terms":["unconformity"],"definitions":["missing geologic time from erosion, nondeposition or both"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"An unconformity represents missing geologic time","label":"Read the missing record"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Distinguish the three contacts","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish the three contacts</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An angular unconformity has younger strata across tilted or folded older strata.</li><li>A disconformity separates broadly parallel sedimentary strata.</li><li>A nonconformity puts sedimentary rocks over eroded igneous or metamorphic basement.</li></ul></section>","terms":["angular unconformity","disconformity","nonconformity"],"definitions":["younger strata across tilted or folded older strata","broadly parallel sedimentary strata","sedimentary rocks over eroded igneous or metamorphic basement"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"A nonconformity puts sedimentary rocks","label":"Check the basement rock"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Use geometry and rock type","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use geometry and rock type</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The geometry and material, not a wavy line alone, decide the category.</li></ul></section>","terms":["geometry and material"],"definitions":["not a wavy line alone"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"The geometry and material","label":"Choose the diagnostic evidence"}],"limitPoints":["A fault cutting layers is a different relationship from a depositional surface over an eroded basement."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A fault cutting layers is a different relationship from a depositional surface over an eroded basement.</li></ul>","terms":["angular unconformity","disconformity","nonconformity"],"summary":{"terms":["disconformity","angular unconformity"],"definitions":["despite parallel beds","tilting and erosion"]}},"30":{"source":"In a non-overturned deposited sequence, lower layers are older. A fault or intrusion is younger than units it cuts; fragments are older than the rock enclosing them; baked material existed before the hot rock heated it. A younger unbroken layer can seal an older fault or erosion surface. Combine these observations to reconstruct each diagram independently. Relative dating orders events; numerical dating assigns age estimates that can bracket a layer between dated units.","trapSource":"An included grain’s crystallization age is not automatically the enclosing rock’s depositional age. Tilting is not necessarily overturning, and letter order from one panel does not transfer to another.","groups":[{"heading":"Order events from rock relationships","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Order events from rock relationships</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>In a non-overturned deposited sequence, lower layers are older.</li><li>A fault or intrusion is younger than units it cuts.</li><li>Fragments are older than the rock enclosing them.</li><li>Baked material existed before the hot rock heated it.</li></ul></section>","terms":["non-overturned deposited sequence","fault or intrusion","Fragments","Baked material"],"definitions":["lower layers are older","younger than units it cuts","older than the rock enclosing them","existed before the hot rock heated it"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"In a non-overturned deposited sequence","label":"Check the sequence orientation"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Reconstruct each diagram","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Reconstruct each diagram</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A younger unbroken layer can seal an older fault or erosion surface.</li><li>Combine these observations to reconstruct each diagram independently.</li></ul></section>","terms":["younger unbroken layer"],"definitions":["can seal an older fault or erosion surface"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Combine these observations to reconstruct each diagram independently","label":"Rebuild each sequence"}],"limitPoints":["Tilting is not necessarily overturning, and letter order from one panel does not transfer to another."]},{"heading":"Separate relative and numerical dating","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Separate relative and numerical dating</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Relative dating orders events.</li><li>Numerical dating assigns age estimates that can bracket a layer between dated units.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Relative dating","Numerical dating"],"definitions":["orders events","assigns age estimates that can bracket a layer between dated units"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Numerical dating assigns age estimates","label":"Distinguish order from age"}],"limitPoints":["An included grain’s crystallization age is not automatically the enclosing rock’s depositional age."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>An included grain’s crystallization age is not automatically the enclosing rock’s depositional age.</li><li>Tilting is not necessarily overturning, and letter order from one panel does not transfer to another.</li></ul>","terms":["cross-cutting","inclusion","baked contact","superposition","Relative dating","Numerical dating","radioactive dating","non-overturned","Cross-cutting","truncation","sealed","stratigraphic column","relative age"],"summary":{"terms":["superposition","inclusions and baked contacts","sedimentary age"],"definitions":["non-overturned sequence","order rock formation","between dated layers"]}},"32":{"source":"A half-life removes half the parent still remaining. In a closed system initially containing parent and no daughter, one, two and three half-lives leave parent fractions 1/2, 1/4 and 1/8 and produce daughter fractions 1/2, 3/4 and 7/8. Infer elapsed half-lives from the parent fraction, then multiply by the supplied half-life to calculate age.","trapSource":"Daughter fraction is different from daughter:parent ratio. The simple calculation assumes no initial daughter and no later gain or loss.","groups":[{"heading":"Track parent and daughter fractions","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Track parent and daughter fractions</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A half-life removes half the parent still remaining.</li><li>In a closed system initially containing parent and no daughter, one, two and three half-lives leave parent fractions 1/2, 1/4 and 1/8 and produce daughter fractions 1/2, 3/4 and 7/8.</li></ul></section>","terms":["half-life","parent fractions","daughter fractions"],"definitions":["removes half the parent still remaining","1/2, 1/4 and 1/8","1/2, 3/4 and 7/8"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"In a closed system initially containing parent and no daughter","label":"Keep the starting conditions"}],"limitPoints":["Daughter fraction is different from daughter:parent ratio."]},{"heading":"Calculate an age from the supplied half-life","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Calculate an age from the supplied half-life</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Infer elapsed half-lives from the parent fraction, then multiply by the supplied half-life to calculate age.</li></ul></section>","terms":["elapsed half-lives","supplied half-life"],"definitions":["from the parent fraction","multiply by the supplied half-life to calculate age"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Infer elapsed half-lives from the parent fraction","label":"Follow the calculation"}],"limitPoints":["The simple calculation assumes no initial daughter and no later gain or loss."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Daughter fraction is different from daughter:parent ratio.</li><li>The simple calculation assumes no initial daughter and no later gain or loss.</li></ul>","terms":["half-life","parent","daughter","closed system","daughter products","half-lives","numerical age"],"summary":{"terms":["parent/daughter fractions","elapsed half-lives"],"definitions":["after one, two and three half-lives","into a numerical age"]}},"35":{"source":"The Gulf Coast record includes thick evaporites deposited as restricted shallow seawater evaporated during early basin opening associated with Pangaea breakup. Burial followed; salt can flow ductilely and rise into domes, deforming surrounding layers. Oil and gas can accumulate in porous reservoir rocks beside salt where geometry and seals trap migrating fluids. Louisiana and Texas are the review’s examples.","trapSource":"Oil is not simply stored inside a solid mass of salt; deposition of salt and later dome deformation are different stages.","groups":[{"heading":"Deposit evaporites and form salt domes","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Deposit evaporites and form salt domes</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The Gulf Coast record includes thick evaporites deposited as restricted shallow seawater evaporated during early basin opening associated with Pangaea breakup.</li><li>Burial followed.</li><li>Salt can flow ductilely and rise into domes, deforming surrounding layers.</li></ul></section>","terms":["evaporites","Salt"],"definitions":["deposited as restricted shallow seawater evaporated during early basin opening associated with Pangaea breakup","can flow ductilely and rise into domes, deforming surrounding layers"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Burial followed","label":"Keep the stage order"}],"limitPoints":["Deposition of salt and later dome deformation are different stages."]},{"heading":"Trap migrating oil and gas","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Trap migrating oil and gas</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Oil and gas can accumulate in porous reservoir rocks beside salt where geometry and seals trap migrating fluids.</li><li>Louisiana and Texas are the review’s examples.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Oil and gas","Louisiana and Texas"],"definitions":["can accumulate in porous reservoir rocks beside salt where geometry and seals trap migrating fluids","review’s examples"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Oil and gas can accumulate","label":"Locate the reservoir and seal"}],"limitPoints":["Oil is not simply stored inside a solid mass of salt."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Oil is not simply stored inside a solid mass of salt.</li><li>Deposition of salt and later dome deformation are different stages.</li></ul>","terms":["evaporites","salt domes","reservoir","seal"],"summary":{"terms":["salt movement","salt rise","petroleum"],"definitions":["restricted evaporation, burial and later","solid-state ductile","sealed adjacent reservoir rock"]}},"38":{"source":"The assigned six-panel set includes Passive Margin, Continental Rift, Normal Fault Blocks, Reverse and Thrust Faults, Strike-Slip Faults, and Regional Subsidence. The East Coast is a passive margin, inherited from rifting and subsequent subsidence. Extension can downfault blocks; thrust loading flexes a foreland basin downward; cooling contracts lithosphere and promotes subsidence. Some bends/steps in strike-slip systems allow local pull-apart basins. The six-panel lecture montage distinguishes passive margin, continental rift, normal-fault basin, foreland basin, strike-slip basin and regional subsidence. Identify the mechanism, not merely the presence of a low area.","trapSource":"Thermal contraction is cooling and volume reduction, not a synonym for plate-boundary compression.","groups":[{"heading":"Recognize the assigned basin set","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the assigned basin set</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The assigned six-panel set includes Passive Margin, Continental Rift, Normal Fault Blocks, Reverse and Thrust Faults, Strike-Slip Faults, and Regional Subsidence.</li></ul></section>","terms":["six-panel set"],"definitions":["Passive Margin, Continental Rift, Normal Fault Blocks, Reverse and Thrust Faults, Strike-Slip Faults, and Regional Subsidence"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"The assigned six-panel set includes","label":"Recall the complete assigned set"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Connect each basin to its mechanism","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Connect each basin to its mechanism</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The East Coast is a passive margin, inherited from rifting and subsequent subsidence.</li><li>Extension can downfault blocks.</li><li>Thrust loading flexes a foreland basin downward.</li><li>Cooling contracts lithosphere and promotes subsidence.</li><li>Some bends/steps in strike-slip systems allow local pull-apart basins.</li></ul></section>","terms":["East Coast","Extension","Thrust loading","Cooling","bends/steps in strike-slip systems"],"definitions":["passive margin, inherited from rifting and subsequent subsidence","can downfault blocks","flexes a foreland basin downward","contracts lithosphere and promotes subsidence","allow local pull-apart basins"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Cooling contracts lithosphere","label":"Explain how the basin forms"}],"limitPoints":["Thermal contraction is cooling and volume reduction, not a synonym for plate-boundary compression."]},{"heading":"Use the lecture montage","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Use the lecture montage</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The six-panel lecture montage distinguishes passive margin, continental rift, normal-fault basin, foreland basin, strike-slip basin and regional subsidence.</li><li>Identify the mechanism, not merely the presence of a low area.</li></ul></section>","terms":["six-panel lecture montage"],"definitions":["passive margin, continental rift, normal-fault basin, foreland basin, strike-slip basin and regional subsidence"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Identify the mechanism, not merely the presence of a low area","label":"Use the mechanism"}],"limitPoints":[]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Thermal contraction is cooling and volume reduction, not a synonym for plate-boundary compression.</li></ul>","terms":["Passive Margin","Continental Rift","Normal Fault Blocks","Regional Subsidence","thermal contraction"],"summary":{"terms":["normal-fault subsidence","strike-slip pull-apart","passive-margin development"],"definitions":["foreland loading","cooling/subsidence settings","crust/lithosphere distinction"]}},"39":{"source":"Isostasy describes buoyant/gravitational balance of crust and lithosphere supported by the denser underlying mantle. Thick continental crust commonly supports greater elevation. The assigned textbook’s rule of thumb gives about 1 km added elevation per 6 km added crust: 12 km additional crust corresponds to about 2 km elevation. Phoenix and Flagstaff have about 28 and 40 km of crust. Their quoted elevation difference of 1800 m is rounded to about 2 km in that example. Lithospheric temperature and density also matter.","trapSource":"The rule is approximate, not a universal exact law; Basin and Range elevation also reflects thin/hot lithosphere.","groups":[{"heading":"Explain isostatic support","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Explain isostatic support</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Isostasy describes buoyant/gravitational balance of crust and lithosphere supported by the denser underlying mantle.</li><li>Thick continental crust commonly supports greater elevation.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Isostasy","Thick continental crust"],"definitions":["buoyant/gravitational balance of crust and lithosphere supported by the denser underlying mantle","commonly supports greater elevation"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Thick continental crust commonly supports greater elevation","label":"Connect crust and elevation"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Apply the approximate crust–elevation relation","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Apply the approximate crust–elevation relation</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The assigned textbook’s rule of thumb gives <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"October 22 lecture · textbook 11.2\">about 1 km added elevation per 6 km added crust</mark><ul class=\"study-subpoints\"><li>12 Km additional crust corresponds to about 2 km elevation.</li></ul></li></ul></section>","terms":["rule of thumb","12 Km additional crust"],"definitions":["about 1 km added elevation per 6 km added crust","corresponds to about 2 km elevation"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"12 Km additional crust","label":"Apply the approximate relation"}],"limitPoints":["The rule is approximate, not a universal exact law."]},{"heading":"Check the example and its limits","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Check the example and its limits</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Phoenix and Flagstaff have about 28 and 40 km of crust.</li><li>Their quoted elevation difference of 1800 m is rounded to about 2 km in that example.</li><li>Lithospheric temperature and density also matter.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Phoenix and Flagstaff","elevation difference of 1800 m","Lithospheric temperature and density"],"definitions":["about 28 and 40 km of crust","rounded to about 2 km in that example","also matter"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Lithospheric temperature and density also matter","label":"Keep the other controls"}],"limitPoints":["Basin and Range elevation also reflects thin/hot lithosphere."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>The rule is approximate, not a universal exact law.</li><li>Basin and Range elevation also reflects thin/hot lithosphere.</li></ul>","terms":["Isostasy","crust","lithosphere","rule of thumb"],"summary":{"terms":["extra-crust"],"definitions":["extra-elevation relationship"]}},"42":{"source":"Hot fluids circulate through fractures, dissolve or mobilize chemical components, and precipitate minerals as conditions change. Mineralized veins are fracture fillings; economically concentrated deposits can be ores. At mid-ocean ridges, heated seawater exits vents, cools/mixes and precipitates sulfide-rich particles and chimneys. Many metal-rich nodules grow by precipitation from seawater or pore waters on the deep seafloor; they are not the same deposit as a vent chimney. EEZ law and mining rights are excluded from scored work.","trapSource":"A black smoker’s dark plume is suspended mineral material, not combustion, and nodules are not universally ridge deposits.","groups":[{"heading":"Form hydrothermal veins","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Form hydrothermal veins</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Hot fluids circulate through fractures, dissolve or mobilize chemical components, and precipitate minerals as conditions change.</li><li>Mineralized veins are fracture fillings.</li><li>Economically concentrated deposits can be ores.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Hot fluids","Mineralized veins","ores"],"definitions":["circulate through fractures, dissolve or mobilize chemical components, and precipitate minerals as conditions change","fracture fillings","Economically concentrated deposits"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Hot fluids circulate through fractures","label":"Follow mineral concentration"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Build vent deposits","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Build vent deposits</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>At mid-ocean ridges, <mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"Lecture 15 slide 9 · textbook 10.2\">heated seawater exits vents, cools/mixes and precipitates sulfide-rich particles and chimneys</mark>.</li></ul></section>","terms":["mid-ocean ridges","heated seawater"],"definitions":["exits vents, cools/mixes and precipitates sulfide-rich particles and chimneys"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"At mid-ocean ridges","label":"Follow vent precipitation"}],"limitPoints":["A black smoker’s dark plume is suspended mineral material, not combustion, and nodules are not universally ridge deposits."]},{"heading":"Distinguish nodules from vent chimneys","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Distinguish nodules from vent chimneys</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Many metal-rich nodules grow by precipitation from seawater or pore waters on the deep seafloor.</li><li>They are not the same deposit as a vent chimney.</li><li>EEZ law and mining rights are excluded from scored work.</li></ul></section>","terms":["metal-rich nodules","vent chimney"],"definitions":["grow by precipitation from seawater or pore waters on the deep seafloor","not the same deposit"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"They are not the same deposit as a vent chimney","label":"Distinguish the deposits"}],"limitPoints":[]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A black smoker’s dark plume is suspended mineral material, not combustion, and nodules are not universally ridge deposits.</li></ul>","terms":["Hydrothermal","veins","black smokers","sulfide","nodules"],"summary":{"terms":["mineral precipitation","ridge vents","vent sulfides"],"definitions":["along a fluid pathway","heating, leaching and precipitation","seafloor nodules"]}},"43":{"source":"A typical passive margin has a relatively gentle shelf, steep slope and sediment apron at the continental rise. Turbidity currents are sediment-laden density flows that move downslope, often through submarine canyons. As flow wanes, coarser grains settle before finer grains, producing normal graded bedding. Repeated deposition can build a deep-water fan. Keep this margin model separate from ridge hydrothermal settings.","trapSource":"A turbidity current is not an ordinary surface wind-driven current; a submarine canyon is a pathway and a fan is a deposit.","groups":[{"heading":"Read the continental margin","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Read the continental margin</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A typical passive margin has a relatively gentle shelf, steep slope and sediment apron at the continental rise.</li></ul></section>","terms":["shelf","slope","continental rise"],"definitions":["relatively gentle","steep","sediment apron"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"A typical passive margin","label":"Read the margin profile"}],"limitPoints":[]},{"heading":"Follow a turbidity current","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Follow a turbidity current</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li><mark class=\"e2-study-emphasis\" title=\"Lecture 15 slides 4 and 6 · textbook 10.9\">Turbidity currents are sediment-laden density flows that move downslope, often through submarine canyons</mark>.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Turbidity currents"],"definitions":["sediment-laden density flows that move downslope, often through submarine canyons"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Turbidity currents are sediment-laden density flows","label":"Follow the sediment-laden flow"}],"limitPoints":["A turbidity current is not an ordinary surface wind-driven current."]},{"heading":"Recognize the deposit","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Recognize the deposit</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>As flow wanes, coarser grains settle before finer grains, producing normal graded bedding.</li><li>Repeated deposition can build a deep-water fan.</li><li>Keep this margin model separate from ridge hydrothermal settings.</li></ul></section>","terms":["normal graded bedding","deep-water fan"],"definitions":["coarser grains settle before finer grains","Repeated deposition can build"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"As flow wanes, coarser grains settle before finer grains","label":"Read the settling sequence"}],"limitPoints":["A submarine canyon is a pathway and a fan is a deposit."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>A turbidity current is not an ordinary surface wind-driven current.</li><li>A submarine canyon is a pathway and a fan is a deposit.</li></ul>","terms":["shelf","slope","rise","Turbidity currents","graded bedding"],"summary":{"terms":["shelf, slope and rise","turbidity currents"],"definitions":["along the margin","canyons/fans and coarse-to-fine deposition"]}},"44":{"source":"From older to younger, the broad course chapters are Precambrian, Paleozoic, Mesozoic and Cenozoic. Precambrian is a broad informal interval; Paleozoic, Mesozoic and Cenozoic are eras within the Phanerozoic Eon. A period is a subdivision of an era. Early life and later multicellular organisms precede the Cambrian expansion of hard-shelled animals. Dinosaurs and the first mammals occur in the Mesozoic; mammals diversify greatly in the Cenozoic after the end-Cretaceous extinction. The assigned source places early mammals in the Triassic.","trapSource":"Paleozoic is not synonymous with Phanerozoic, and Cenozoic diversification is not the first appearance of mammals. Exact garbled review wording remains uncertain.","groups":[{"heading":"Organize the geologic time divisions","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Organize the geologic time divisions</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>From older to younger, the broad course chapters are Precambrian, Paleozoic, Mesozoic and Cenozoic.</li><li>Precambrian is a broad informal interval.</li><li>Paleozoic, Mesozoic and Cenozoic are eras within the Phanerozoic Eon.</li><li>A period is a subdivision of an era.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Precambrian","Paleozoic, Mesozoic and Cenozoic","period"],"definitions":["broad informal interval","eras within the Phanerozoic Eon","subdivision of an era"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"From older to younger","label":"Read the time order"}],"limitPoints":["Paleozoic is not synonymous with Phanerozoic, and Cenozoic diversification is not the first appearance of mammals."]},{"heading":"Place characteristic life in time","html":"<section class=\"e2-note-group\"><h2 class=\"e2-note-heading\">Place characteristic life in time</h2><ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Early life and later multicellular organisms precede the Cambrian expansion of hard-shelled animals.</li><li>Dinosaurs and the first mammals occur in the Mesozoic.</li><li>Mammals diversify greatly in the Cenozoic after the end-Cretaceous extinction.</li><li>The assigned source places early mammals in the Triassic.</li></ul></section>","terms":["Mesozoic","Cenozoic","Triassic"],"definitions":["Dinosaurs and the first mammals","Mammals diversify greatly","early mammals"],"checkpoints":[{"phrase":"Mammals diversify greatly in the Cenozoic","label":"Distinguish appearance and diversification"}],"limitPoints":["Exact garbled review wording remains uncertain."]}],"trapHTML":"<ul class=\"e2-note-points\"><li>Paleozoic is not synonymous with Phanerozoic, and Cenozoic diversification is not the first appearance of mammals.</li><li>Exact garbled review wording remains uncertain.</li></ul>","terms":["Precambrian","Paleozoic","Mesozoic","Cenozoic","Phanerozoic","Triassic"],"summary":{"terms":["eon, era and period","major time intervals","mammal origin"],"definitions":["Phanerozoic eras","characteristic life","later diversification"]}}}};
